{"version": "2.0", "head_chars": 5000, "group": "ls-LS17-S14-0000", "entries": [{"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |1", "t": "Purchase of Housing Units by Central Excise Department", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *1 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, 04th DECEMBER, 2023 / \nAGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA)  \n \nPURCHASE OF HOUSING UNITS BY CENTRAL EXCISE DEPARTMENT \n*1. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA: \nWill the Minister of Finance be pleased to state: \na) whether the Central Excise Department has purchased dwelling Units from \nRajasthan Housing Board in Jaipur for their officers and employees; \n \nb) if so, the details thereof indicating the number of such housing units purchased \nand the amount of funds spent thereon; \n \nc) the number of such housing units currently lying vacant along with the reasons \ntherefor; \n \nd) whether any written complaint have been received by the Government \nregarding the dilapidated conditions of the  said housing units and conduct of \nanti-social activities in such houses lying vacant; and \n \ne) if so, the details thereof and the action taken or likely to be taken by the \nGovernment on such complaints? \n \nANSWER \nFINANCE MINISTER  \n(SMT NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *1 FOR ANSWER ON 04.12.2023 RAISED BY SHRI KANAKMAL \nKATARA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING “PURCHASE OF \nHOUSING UNITS BY CENTRAL EXCISE DEPARTMENT”. \n(a) Yes. \n(b) A total of 12 dwelling units were purchased at Jaipur for an amount of Rs. 1.02 \ncrore.  \n(c) One (1) dwelling unit out of 12 units is vacant at present as no one has applied. \n(d)  No complaint has been received about these dwelling units in Jaipur.  \n(e) Question does not arise in view of (d) above. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |10", "t": "Setting up of Science Museums in Madhya Pradesh", "head": "58,501 women beneficiaries have been assisted under others schemes during the last \nthree years including current year upto 28.11.2023. \n \n�������� The following are the eligibility criteria for availing financial assistance \nfrom NSTFDC including tribal women:  \n \n�� All applicant(s)/ member(s) should belong to the Scheduled Tribes Community. \n�� Annual family income of the applicant(s) should not exceed ₹3.00 lakh p.a. \nboth for rural and urban areas.  \n \n�������� From time to time, NSTFDC in collaboration with the implementing agencies \nconduct awareness camps for disseminating information about its schemes. Further, \nthe Corporation also participate in the events/ programmes of other organisations/ \ndepartments. \n������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |100", "t": "Promotion of Medical Device Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 100 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08th DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nPromotion of Medical Device Industry  \n \n \n*100.  SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH:  \nSHRI D.K. SURESH:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the present status of growth of Medical Device Industry in the country;  \n(b) the details of the initiatives taken/proposed to be taken by the Government for promotion \nof Medical Device Industry;  \n(c) whether the Government has identified potential hubs in various States for the \ndevelopment of Medical Device Industry in the country; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken thereon? \n  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (Dr. MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)  \n \n(a)  to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to the LOK SABHA STARRED Q. NO. 100 (20 th \nPOSITION) for answer on 08.12.2023 raised by SHRIMATI SUMALATHA \nAMBAREESH and SHRI D.K. SURESH regarding “Promotion of Medical Device \nIndustry” \n  \n(a): It is informed that the market size of medical device industry in the country was estimated \nat USD 11 billion in 2020 and is expected to grow rapidly. India is the 4th largest Asian medical \ndevices market after Japan, China and South Korea and among t he top 20 global medical \ndevices markets in the world. \n \n(b): The Government of India has taken several measures to encourage domestic manufacturing \nof Medical Devices. The steps taken to support Medical Devices Industries are as under: \n  \ni. Production Linked Incentive Scheme for Promoting Domestic Manufacturing of \nMedical Devices (PLI MD) with total financial outlay of Rs. 3,420 crore and tenure \nfrom FY 2020-2021 to FY 2027 -28. The financial incentive is to be given to selected \ncompanies at the rate of 5% on incremental sales of medical devices manufactured in \nIndia and covered under the target segments of the scheme, for a period of five years. \n26 participants have been approved under the scheme and manufacturing of 39 products \nhas started.  \nii. Production Linked Incentive Scheme for Pharmaceuticals has been launched with total \nfinancial outlay of Rs. 15,000 crore and tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2028-29. The \nscheme covers In-Vitro Diagnostic (IVD) Device. A total of 55 participants have been \napproved under the scheme, out of which 5 participants were selected for In -Vitro \nDiagnostic (IVD) devices.   \niii. The scheme Promotion of Medical Device s Parks, with a total financial outlay of Rs. \n400 crore and tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2024-2025, provides for the maximum \nfinancial assistance of Rs. 100 crore each to four selected States/Union Territories for \ncreation of Common Infrastructure Facilities in the upcoming Medical Devices Parks. \nUnder the scheme, final approval for fin ancial assistance of Rs. 100 crore each, has \nbeen given to the States of Himachal Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Tamil Nadu and Uttar \nPradesh. First installment of Rs 30 crore each has been released to all the States. \niv. The Department has also notified National Me dical Devices Po licy (NMD), 2023 on \n26.04.2023. Policy envisions to place the Indian medical devices sector on an \naccelerated growth path with a patient-centric approach to meet the evolving healthcare \nneeds of the country.  \nv. Scheme for Promotion of Research and Innovation in Pharma MedTech Sector (PRIP) \nin specified priority areas with an outlay of Rs. 5000 cr. for a period of 5 years i.e., \n2023-24 to 2027-28 was notified on 17.08.2023. Under this scheme, i t is proposed to \nestablish a Centre of Excellence (CoE ) with specialization in Medical Devices  at \nNational Institutes of Pharmaceutical Education & Research (NIPER), Ahmedabad. \n  \n(c) & (d) : As per the study conducted through Centre for Market Research & Social \nDevelopment, th ere are 21 Medical Devic e (MD) Clusters in the country. The \nscheme Assistance to Medical Device Clusters for Common Facilities (AMD-CF) provides for \nfinancial incentive to Medical devices clusters to develop common infrastructure facilities like \nMedical devices testing labs, E-waste treatment facility, logistic centers etc. The scheme also \nprovides for financial assistance to National or State Level Government or Private Institutions \ninterested in establishing or strengthening testing facilities for medical  devices. The financial \noutlay of the Scheme is Rs 300 cr and the tenure of the scheme is FY 2023-24 to FY 2026-27.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |101", "t": "Retail Inflation", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *101 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nRetail Inflation \n*101. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR RINKU: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken note that the country’s retail inflation is increasing \nsteeply; \n(b) if so, the details of the inflation at the current rate; \n(c) whether the Government has conducted any study to find out the reasons for the increase in \ninflation;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to bring down the level of inflation in the \ncountry? \nANSWER \n \nTHE FINANCE MINISTER \n(SMT NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 101 RAISED BY SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR RINKU  FOR 11 th \nDECEMBER, 2023 ON “RETAIL INFLATION” \n(a) & (b): India’s retail inflation has declined from an average of 7.1 per cent in April-October \n2022 to 5.4 per cent in the corresponding period of 2023. The retail inflation is now stable and \nwithin the notified tolerance band of 2 per cent to 6 per cent. A steady decline in core inflation, \nestimated after removing volatile food and fuel items from retail inflation , has been critical in \nweakening the inflationary pressure in the Indian economy. The core inflation has declined \nfrom 5.1 per cent in April 2023 to 4.3 per cent in October 2023.  \n(c) & (d): After the notification of the inflation tolerance band in 2016, retail inflation in India \nhas been mostly within acceptable limits. Temporary increases in inflation on a few occasions  \nare caused by  demand-supply misma tches arising out of global shocks and adverse weather \nconditions. Proactive supply side initiatives by the Government and effective demand \nstabilization measures by the Reserve Bank of India  have helped resolve the demand-supply \nmismatches and to rein in inflation. \n(e): The measures taken by the Government to restrain inflation include strengthening of \nbuffers of key food items and making periodic open market releases , easing imports of \nessential food items through trade policy measures , preventing hoarding through \nimposition/revision of stock limits, and channelling supplies through designated retail outlets. \nTo ensure food security to the poor , the Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana, providing \nfree food grains to about 81.35 crore beneficiaries, has been extended for a period of five years \nwith effect from 1 st January 2024.  In October 2023, Government also increased the subsidy \nunder Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana from Rs. 200 to Rs.300 per 14.2 kg cylinder. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |102", "t": "Small Scale Farmers", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 102 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nMIGRANT CHILD LABOUR \n \n102.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)the number of migrant child labour in the country as a whole and \nparticularly in Kerala State during the period 2019-22; and  \n(b)the details of child labour (inter -State and intra-State) rescued in \neach State by the Labour Department during the years 2019-22? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Government has enacted the Child Labour (Prohibition \n& Regulation) Act, 1986 which was amended in 2016.  The amended \nAct is called the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation)  Act, 1986.  The Act provides for complete prohibition of \nwork or employment of children below 14 years in any occupation and \nprocess and adolescents in the age group of 14 to 18 years in \nhazardous occupations and processes. As per “Crime in India” a \npublication of National Crime Records Bureau, 772, 476 and 613 \nnumber of cases were registered during calendar year  2019,2020 and \n2021 respectively under Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation) Act, 1986 in the country. State-wise details are annexed. \n \n*                                                     ***** \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) & (b)  OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 102 FOR 04.12 .2023 RAISED BY DR. SHASHI \nTHAROOR REGARDING MIGRANT CHILD LABOUR. \n \nNumber of cases registered under Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation) Act, 1986, State / Union Territories -wise: \nSl State/UT 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 2 37 12 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 1 0 \n3 Assam 68 40 78 \n4 Bihar 15 3 14 \n5 Chhattisgarh 2 0 0 \n6 Gujarat 64 39 40 \n7 Haryana 11 1 12 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 0 1 0 \n9 Jharkhand 18 27 5 \n10 Karnataka 83 54 58 \n11 Kerala 2 0 3 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 4 1 5 \n13 Maharashtra 53 29 57 \n14 Meghalaya 2 0 0 \n15 Odisha 0 0 6 \n16 Punjab 8 11 8 \n17 Rajasthan 48 30 19 \n18 Tamil Nadu 3 2 26 \n19 Telangana 314 147 224 \n20 Tripura 0 1 0 \n21 Uttar Pradesh 9 1 1 \n22 Uttarakhand 27 41 25 \n23 West Bengal 7 3 2 \n24 Chandigarh 0 1 7 \n25 D&N Haveli and Daman \n& Diu+ 2 0 0 \n26 Delhi 30 6 11 \n  TOTAL  772 476 613 \n Source: Crime in India, National Crime Records Bureau*** \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |103", "t": "Education Projects in Jharkhand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 103 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nEDUCATION PROJECTS IN JHARKHAND \n \n*103. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \na) the details of educational projects being run in Jharkhand, district-wise; \n(b) the details of funds allocated/released for these projects in Jharkhand during the last four years and \nthe current year; \n(c) the total amount of funds spent on Higher Education projects in Jharkhand during the aforesaid \nperiod, district-wise; and \n(d) the steps taken/being taken by the Government for effective implementation of such educational \nprojects in Jharkhand? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House \n**** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 103 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 ASKED BY SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH \nCHOUDHARY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING EDUCATION PROJECTS \nIN JHARKHAND \n(a) to (c) Under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA), \nfinancial support for various higher education projects is provided to States/UTs including Jharkhand. \nThe district-wise details of projects approved in Jharkhand are Annexed. As against the approved total \ncentral share of Rs. 380.6 crores, since inception of the scheme an amount of Rs. 259.28 crores has been \nreleased out of which an amount of Rs. 103.86 crores has been released to the State since 2019-20 (last \nfour years and current year, till date) . As on 06.12.2023, the State has an unspent balance of Rs. 40.88 \ncrores in its Single Nodal  Agency Account. Further releases shall be subject to utilisation of unspent \nbalance and compliance to the scheme guidelines. \nIn addition , Central government provides fund for other Centrally funded Higher Education \nInstitutions under the Department of Higher Education in the State of Jharkhand and funds released \nduring the last four years and the current year for such institutions are as below: \nName of \nInstitutions \nName of \nDistrict where \nlocated  \nFunds released (in Rs. crores) \n  2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \n(as on date) \nIndian Institute of \nTechnology (Indian \nSchool of Mines) \nDhanbad 308.99 319.77 264.84 316.51 271.05 \nNational Institute of \nTechnology Jamshedpur 94.30 80.19 98.07 120.82 100.20 \nIndian Institute of \nManagement Ranchi 25.00 51.00 116.68 - - \nIndian Institute of \nInformation \nTechnology (PPP) \nRanchi 5.12 6.24 11.51 18.95 25.00 \nCentral University of \nJharkhand Ranchi 51.54 71.80 68.12 93.01 72.80 \nNational Institute of \nAdvanced \nManufacturing \nTechnology \nRanchi 47.37 43.21 47.97 54.53 - \n \n  \n\n(d) Education being in the Concurrent List,  implementation and monitoring of educational projects is \nthe responsibility of both the Central and State Governments.  The Government is monitoring for \neffective implementation of these education projects through regular online/physical review meetings \nwith stakeholders. \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 103 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 ASKED BY SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH \nCHOUDHARY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING EDUCATION PROJECTS \nIN JHARKHAND \nDistrict-wise details of projects approved in Jharkhand under the scheme of Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha \nAbhiyan (RUSA) \nS. No. \n \nDistrict Names Number of \nUnits Approved \nTotal Amount \nApproved (in Rs. crore) \nCentral Share \nApproved (in Rs. \ncrore) \n1.  Bokaro 8 42.0 25.2 \n2.  Chatra 3 18.0 10.8 \n3.  Deoghar 4 8.0 4.8 \n4.  Dhanbad 7 14.0 8.4 \n5.  Dumka 6 42.0 25.2 \n6.  East Singhbhum 9 97.0 58.2 \n7.  Garhwa 2 14.0 8.4 \n8.  Giridih 3 18.0 10.8 \n9.  Godda 4 34.0 20.4 \n10.  Gumla 2 14.0 8.4 \n11.  Hazaribagh 7 36.0 21.6 \n12.  Koderma 2 28.0 16.8 \n13.  Latehar 1 12.0 7.2 \n14.  Lohardaga 1 2.0 1.2 \n15.  Pakur 2 16.0 9.6 \n16.  Palamu 3 32.0 19.2 \n17.  Ramgarh 4 20.0 12.0 \n18.  Ranchi 14 104.0 62.4 \n19.  Sahibganj 4 20.0 12.0 \n20.  Seraikela kharsawan 1 2.0 1.2 \n21.  Simdega 1 12.0 7.2 \n22.  West Singhbhum 4 28.0 16.8 \n23.  State as a Unit 3 17.2 12.8 \n Total 95 630.2 380.6 \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |104", "t": "Rewriting of Agama Shastras", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 104 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nForest Fires  \n \n104. SHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether any steps have been taken to prevent and address forest fires in the country; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)    whether the Government is facing any challenge in implementing its National Forest    \n        Certification Scheme, particularly in addressing the needs and challenges faced by   \n        small-scale producers in developing countries; \n(d)   if so, the details thereof, if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e)  whether the Government has taken any initiatives to ensure the exchange of traditional \nknowledge, technology innovation and community engagement practices in the context \nof forest fire prevention and sustainable forest management; and \n (f)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n (SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) The Protection and Management of forest and Wildlife including prevention and \ncontrol of forest fire is primarily the responsibility of concerned State Governments /UT \nAdministrations. The Ministry supports their efforts by providing financial assistance under the \nCentrally Sponsored Forest Fire Prevention and Management Scheme, CAMPA and under \nDevelopment of Wildlife Habitat schemes. These schemes provide support in various activities \nto the state forest departments towards forest fire prevention and mitigation activities including, \ncreation and maintenance of fire lines, water conservation structures, procurement of \nfirefighting equipment, awareness creation etc. \n \nBesides, Forest Survey of India (FSI), generate and disseminates satellite based information, \nwhich is “Weekly Early-Warning Alerts” of forest fire and also “Large Forest Fire Monitoring” \nalerts, to the filed forest officials who are registered with FSI. These fire alerts aim at improving \n\ntactical and strategic response to large forest fires by the respective States/UTs Administrations. \nCurrently the number of registered users is around 2.14 lakh across the country. Further, Van \nAgni Geo Portal is an app developed by FSI monitors large forest fires in various States/UTs \nthroughout the fire season and accordingly alerted the State Forest Departments.  \n  \n (c) & (d) The Ministry has released Indian Forest Management Standard as a part of National \nWorking Plan Code – 2023. This standard is a basis for monitoring of sustainable forest \nmanagement in terms of a broad framework comprising criteria, indicators and verifiers which \nprovides the platform for Indian Forest and Wood Certification Scheme to address the \nchallenges of small scale producers.     \n \n(e) & (f) The Implementation of Forest Fire Prevention and Management Scheme is done \nthrough Joint Forest Management Committee (JFMc) and Village Eco-Development \nCommittees by involving active participation of local Community.  \n \nFurther, this Ministry has formulated the National Action Plan on Forest Fire (NAPFF) in the \nyear 2018 to minimize forest fires by informing, enabling and empowering forest fringe \ncommunities and incentivizing them to work in tandem with the State Forest Departments. The \nMinistry has also constituted a Central Monitoring Committee (CMC) which reviews the \nimplementation of NAPFF and preparedness of States/UTs regarding Forest Fire prevention and \nmanagement in the country. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |105", "t": "Basic Amenities in Tribal Regions", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 105 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nCENTRAL SECTOR SCHEME FOR REHABILITATION OF BONDED \nLABOURERS IN ODISHA \n \n105.  SHRI BASANTA KUMAR PANDA:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)the details of Central Sector Schemes for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer; \n(b)the number of beneficiaries in Odisha including Kalahandi and \nNuapada, districts; \n(c)the details of fund al located for this scheme under the Odisha \nState, district-wise; and \n(d)the details of facilities being provided to labourers for \nrehabilitation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d): The Ministry of Labour & Employment has been \nimplementing a Central Sector Scheme viz. Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer for identification and rehabilitation of bonded labourers. \nThe Scheme was last revamped in January, 2022 and henceforth is \nknown as Cen tral Sector Scheme for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer-2021. The salient features of the scheme are as follows: \n• Under the Scheme, the Government of India provides \nimmediate financial assistance amounting to Rs.30,000/- for \neach case of rehabilitation. \n• Rehabilitation assistance to the tune of Rs.1.00 lakh, \nRs.2.00 lakh and Rs.3.00 lakh is provided to rescued bonded \nlabourers based on their category and level of exploitation \nupon proof of bondage. \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n• The Scheme also provides for financial as sistance of Rs. \n4.50 lakh per district to the States for conducting Survey \nonce in every three years per sensitive district, Rs. 1.50 lakh \nfor evaluatory studies (maximum of five Evaluatory Studies \nper year) and Rs. 10 lakhs per State per annum for \nawareness generation. \n• The Scheme provides for creation of a Bonded Labour \nRehabilitation Fund at District level by each State with a \npermanent corpus of at least Rs. 10  lakh at the disposal of \nthe District Magistrate which would be renewable for \nextending immediate help to the released bonded labourers. \nThe Central Sector Scheme for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer is a demand driven scheme where funds are re-imbursed to \nconcerned State/UTs Governments on receipt of proposal from them. \nDuring last three financial  years i.e. FY 2020 -21, FY 2021 -22 & FY \n2022-23, the State Government has not sent any  proposal and \nMinistry of Labour & Employment has not released any funds for         \nre-imbursement of financial assistance to the State Government of \nOdisha including districts Kalahandi and Nuapada. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |106", "t": "National Career Service (NCS) Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*106 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL CAREER SERVICE (NCS) PROJECT \n \n*106.     SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n             SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the objectives of National Career Service (NCS) Project; \n(b) the number of Employment Exchanges and Model Career \nCentres (MCCs) in the country, State/district-wise; \n(c) the number of Employment Exchanges upgraded to Model Career \nCentres (MCCs) in the country , State-wise and district -wise in \nRajasthan and Uttar Pradesh; \n(d) the status and details of Pali, Jhansi, Deoria, Pratapgarh \nEmployment Exchange and the number of benefic iaries/job \nseekers/ employers registered on NCS por tal in these \nConstituencies;  \n(e) whether the Government has plans for  setting up more \nEmployment Exchange/MCCs and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) the details of steps taken by the Government to get more \nemployers registered   on the NCS portal? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *106 DUE FOR REPLY ON 11.12.202 3 \nREGARDING “NATIONAL CAREER SERVICE  (NCS) PROJECT” BY SHRI \nP.P. CHAUDHARY AND SHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \n(a) to (f) :   Ministry of Labour  and Employment is implementing the \nNational Career Service (NCS) project to provide a variety of career \nrelated services like job search and matching, career counselling, \nvocational guidance, information on skill development courses etc. \nthrough a digital platform [www.ncs.gov.in].  \n \n          Establishment/Expansion of Employment Exchanges is under the \npurview of the State/UT Governments. As on 30.11.2023 there are 1005 \nEmployment Exchanges in the country. Under the NCS project, the \nGovernment has targeted to establish 400 Model Career Centres \n(MCCs) and all 400 MCCs have been approved including 370 in \nEmployment Exchanges. State -wise details of Employment Exchanges \nand Model Career Centres are provided at Annexure I and Location -\nwise employment exchanges of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh where \nModel Career Centres have been approved are provided at Annexure-II.  \n \nThe number of beneficiaries/Jobseekers/Employers register ed on \nNCS portal of Pali, Jhansi, Deoria and Pratapgargh districts as on 30th \nNovember 2023 are given below: \n \n \nSl. No. Districts Jobseeker Employer \n1 Pali 1,28,835 7,804 \n2 Jhansi 1,01,710 2,172 \n3 Deoria 70,445 2,072 \n4 Pratapgarh 1,46,898 2,741 \n\n           In order to increase the registration of employers on the NCS \nportal, the Government has taken the following initiatives:  \ni. NCS portal has  been integrated with Udyam portal of Ministry of \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises  (MSME) for seamless \nregistration of Udyam registered MSMEs as employers on the NCS \nportal. More than 16.81 lakhs Udyam registered MSMEs are \nregistered as employers on NCS portal. \nii. Ministry of Labour and Employment has signed Memorandum of \nUnderstandings (MoUs)/ partnerships with  various job portals/ \nprivate organizations  like Monster.com, Freshersworld, Quess \nCorp Limited, HireMee, TCS -iON, UNICEF YuWaah etc to enhance \njob opportunities on NCS for the job seekers.   \niii. NCS portal is also interlinked with EPFO and ESIC portals for \nconnecting with the employers.   \niv. Further, to popularize NCS and for connecting with job seekers \nand employers, Information Education and Communication (IEC) \nactivities and outreach activities are regularly organized. \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY (a) TO (f) TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *106 DUE FOR REPLY ON 11.12.2023 \n \nSl. \nNo \nState/UT No. of \nEmployme\nnt \nExchange \nNo. of \nEmployment \nExchanges \nupgraded to \nMCCs  \nNo. of MCCs \napproved at \ninstitutions \nother than \nEmployment \nExchanges \nTotal \nNo. of \nMCCs \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 12 6 - 6 \n2 Assam 52 12 1 13 \n3 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1 1 - 1 \n4 Andhra Pradesh 17 29 1 30 \n5 Bihar 47 20 - 20 \n6 Chhattisgarh 26 12 - 12 \n7 Delhi 3 1 1 2 \n8 Goa 1 1 - 1 \n9 Gujarat 46 13 - 13 \n10 Haryana 65 3 1 4 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 15 2 - 2 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 20 18 1 19 \n13 Ladakh 2 2 1 3 \n14 Jharkhand 42 20 - 20 \n15 Karnataka 40 13 1 14 \n16 Kerala 89 13 1 14 \n17 Lakshadweep 1 1 1 2 \n18 Maharashtra 36 26 2 28 \n19 Meghalaya 12 4 - 4 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 49 11 2 13 \n21 Manipur 11 1 1 2 \n22 Mizoram 3 4 - 4 \n23 Nagaland 8 7 - 7 \n24 Odisha 40 30 2 32 \n25 Puducherry 1 4 - 4 \n26 Punjab 47 12 - 12 \n27 Rajasthan 38 16 - 16 \n28 Sikkim - - 6 6 \n29 Telangana 34 12 - 12 \n30 Tripura 6 3 1 4 \n31 Tamil Nadu 35 19 1 20 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 100 35 4 39 \n33 Uttarakhand 24 4 1 5 \n34 West Bengal 77 15 1 16 \n35 Chandigarh  2 - - - \n36 D. &N. Haveli Daman & Diu 3 - - - \nTotal 1005 370 30 400 \nThe State-wise number of Employment Exchanges and Model Career Centr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |107", "t": "Reservation of Tribal Traditions", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO- †*107 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 11/12/2023 \n \nRESERVATION OF TRIBAL TRADITIONS \n  \n  \n†*107. SHRI SANJAY SETH: \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any steps have been taken by the Government to preserve tribal traditions, culture and \ncustoms; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether due to conversion there has been an adverse impact on tribal traditions, culture and customs; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof including steps taken by the Government to stop conversion;  \n \n(e) whether any steps have been taken by the Government to stop conversion of tribal community in the \ncountry particularly in Jharkhand as conversion is a big issue in tribal dominated States; \n  \n(f)  whether the Government has any data of tribals converted in Jharkhand during the last five years; \nand  \n  \n(g) if so, the details thereof? \n  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI ARJUN MUNDA) \n  \n(a) to (g):        A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n  \nStatement referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. †*107 for answer on \n11.12.2023 \n(a) & (b): Yes, Sir. Ministry of Tribal Affairs has taken various steps to preserve tribal traditions, culture \nand customs through Centrally Sponsored Schemes of “Support to Tribal Research Institute” and Central \nSector Scheme “Tribal Research, Information, Education, Communication and Events” under which \nvarious activities are undertaken to conserve and promote tribal culture, archives, artifacts, customs and \ntraditions of the tribal communities. Under the scheme “Support to Tribal Research Institutes (TRIs)”, \nMinistry has sanctioned various projects to the Tribal Research Institutes of the States/UTs towards \nvarious activities for socio-cultural and economic development of the tribal communities including \npreservation of their cultural heritage and protection of the indigenous faith, practices, unique ideas and \nknowledge and of tribal people in diverse form. There are 28 Tribal Research Institutes in States/UTs \nand National Tribal Research Institute in Delhi. Some of the initiatives worth mentioning are as under: \ni. To acknowledge the heroic and patriotic deeds of tribal people and to exhibit rich tribal cultural \nheritage of the region, Ministry has sanctioned 10 Tribal Freedom Fighters Museums. \nii. The Ethnographic Museum under the States’ TRIs have been sanctioned to preserve and \nshowcase rare artifacts related to life and culture of various tribes.  \niii. Tribal Research Institutes have organized different events like National Tribal Craft Mela, \nNational/State Tribal Dance Festival, Art Competition, Workshop – cum – Exhibition on Tribal \npaintings and state level tribal poet and writers meet. For promoting the unique tribal heritage, \nMinistry has sanctioned fund to organize the tribal fairs & festivals, festival like \"Medaram \nJathara\" undertaken by the Koya Tribe of Telangana, Hornbill Festival of Nagaland, Sarhul \nFestival of Jharkhand, Lokotsav of Goa and Pawl Kut festival of Mizoram, etc. \niv. Ministry has developed searchable digital repository where all research \npapers, books, reports and documents, folks songs, photos/videos are uploaded. The repository \ncurrently has more than 10,000 photographs, videos and publications which are mostly done by \nTribal Research Institutes.    \nv. Under the scheme “Tribal Research, Information, Education, Communication and Events (TRI-\nECE)”, reputed research institutes/organizations/universities have undertaken various research \nstudies/publication of books/documentation including audio visual documentaries to fill the gap \nof research studies on tribal issues and Promotion of rich tribal cultural, traditions and customs \nas well as capacity building of tribal persons/institutions associated with tribal affairs, \ndissemination of information and creation of awareness. Tribal cultural exchange programmes \nwas also sanctioned. Research and documentation of indigenous practices by tribal healers and \nmedicinal plants, tribal languages, agriculture system, dances and paintings, organization of \nliterary festivals, publication of books written by tribal writers/ authors, translation works and \nliterature competitions, were undertaken under the scheme. Military Tattoo and Tribal Dance \nFestival was organized in January 2023 to exhibit the various dance performances by \ntribal    communities showcasing the beauty of India’s diverse tribal culture from across India.  \nvi. Government of India has declared 15th November as Janjatiya Gaurav Divas to honour all the \ntribal freedom fighters and for remembering and acknowledging their contribution to the \nfreedom struggle and cultural heritage, and to re-energize the efforts for the socio-economic \n\ndevelopment of the tribal regions. The Ministry of Tribal Affairs along with others central \nministries, state governments and other"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |108", "t": "Tribal Development", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-108 \nANSWERED ON-04/12/2023 \n \nMUSLIM COMMUNITY LAGGING BEHIND IN HEIs \n \n108. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the All India Survey on Education conducted by the Ministry reveals that the \nMuslim community is lagging behind all communities in the Higher Education painting a \ndismal picture of the community; \n \n(b) if so, the details of All India Survey on Education 2020 -21 State -wise/year \nwise/community- wise; and \n \n(c) the proposal of the Government to increase the admission from the communities which \nare lagging behind? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) &(b) As per the All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE), enrolment of students of \nMuslim community in Higher Educational Institutions (HEIs) has increased to 21 .08 Lakh in \n2021-22(Provisional) from 19.22 Lakh in 2020-21, and 15.34 Lakh in 2014 -15. So increase \nin the enrolment of students of Muslim community is around 37.5%, whereas the overall \nStudent enrolment in Higher Education has increased by 26.5% since 2014 -15.  Hence, the \ngrowth in enrolment of Muslim Students is higher the overall student growth.  The details of \nEnrolment of students of Muslim Minority and total Students, State -wise, as per AISHE,  is \navailable at https://www.education.gov.in/parl_ques . \n \n(c) The various initiatives of the Government in encouraging students from the Minority \ncommunity as well as students from SC/ST/OBC communities has resulted in higher growth \nin enrolment of these communities over the years. Some of these initiatives are as under :-  \nI Ministry of Minority Affairs implements the Post Metric and Merit -cum-Means based \nScholarship Schemes for educational empowerment of students belonging to 6 notified \nMinority communities, namely, Buddhist, Christian, Jain, Muslim, Sikh and Zoroastrian  \n(Parsi).  These are implemented with the objective to uplift the socio -economic conditions of \nthe minority communities, increase their rate of attainment in higher education and enhance \ntheir employability and to enable the poor and meritorious students pursue professional and \ntechnical courses.   \n \n \n\nII.  The Department of Higher Education is implementing the “Pradhan Mantri Uchchatar \nShiksha Protsahan [PM-USP] Yojana”, with three component schemes namely :- \n \n• Central Sector Scheme of Scholarship for College and University Students;  \n• Special Scholarship Scheme for Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh aimed specifically at \nyouth of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh to take advantage of the educational \ninstitutions outside these UTs  and interact with their counterparts from rest of \ncountry]; \n• Central Sector Interest Subsidy Scheme.  \nAll categories of students in the country including students from Muslim \nCommunity are eligible to get financial assistance as per the schematic norms.  \n[The details of these schemes are available in the Ministry’s website: \nhttps://www.education.gov.in/en/pm-usp-scholarships-education-loan].  \nIII.  Besides, various fellowships and scholarships are being implemented  University Grants \nCommission, All India Council for Technical Education, Ministry of Social Justice & \nEmpowerment and Ministry of Tribal Affairs.  \nIV. Examinations like Joint Entrance Examination (JEE), National Eligibility cum Entrance \nTest (NEET) etc. are conducted in 13 Indian languages, to facilitate the students of \nSC/ST/OBC/rural/vernacular background. \nV. Education and Skill Textbooks in Indian languages are being brought ou t. All India \nCouncil of Higher Education (AICTE) has released entire course books for 1 st Year of \nEngineering and Polytechnic subjects in Indian Languages. These would facilitate students, \nparticularly from vernacular background and marginalised communities in pursuing Higher \nStudies.  \nVI. The University Grants Commission is implementing the following Schemes for weaker \nsections, including minorities : \ni. Remedial Coaching for SC/ST/OBC (non -creamy layer), and  Minority Community \nStudents \nii. Coaching  Classes  for  Entry  into Services for SC/ST/OBC (Non Creamy Layer) and \nMinority Community Students \niii. Coaching For National Eligibility Test or State Eligibility Test for SC/ST/OBC (Non-\nCreams Layer), and Minority Community Students.  \niv. Certain relaxations in eligibility conditions in UGC-Junior Research Fellowship    for \nSC/ST students are provided. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |109", "t": "Promotion of Wildlife and Forest Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*109 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF WILDLIFE AND FOREST TOURISM \n \n*109.  DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \n  SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \n  \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any scheme has been formulated for promotion of wildlife \nand forest tourism in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the scope of the same in \nvarious States/UTs including Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu;  \n(c) whether there is a big potent ial for wildlife and forest tourism in \nthe country;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken to tap the potential and for promotion of the \nwildlife and forest tourism in the notified Tiger Reserves, National \nParks and Wildlife Sanctuaries within the country including \nMaharashtra State;  \n(e) whether any scheme has been put in place to involve local \ncommunities as stakeholders in the development of sustainable \nwildlife and forest tourism; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the funds provided by the \nGovernment for pro moting sustainable wildlife and forest tourism \nduring the last five years, State/UT -wise including Maharashtra \nand Tamil Nadu State?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM         (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (f): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a)  TO (f) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION No.*109 ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 REGARDING PROMOTION \nOF WILDLIFE AND FOREST TOURISM BY DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE \nAND SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI. \n \n(a) to (f): The Ministry of Tourism under  its scheme of Swadesh \nDarshan identified Wildlife Circuit and Eco Circuit as among 15 thematic \ncircuits for development.  The above circuits cover National Parks, \nWildlife Sanctuaries and Reserves in the country.  The details of projects \nsanctioned under the Wildlife and Eco Circuits of Swadesh Darshan \nscheme in various States/UTs is at Annexure.  No project has been \nsanctioned in the State s of Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu under the \nabove two circuits of the scheme. \n \nMinistry of Tourism has now revamped its Swadesh Darshan scheme as \nSwadesh Darshan 2.0 (SD 2.0) with the objective to develop sustainable \nand responsible destinations following a tourist and destination centric \napproach.  Sindhudurg in Maharashtra and  Mamallapuram and The \nNilgirs in Tamil Nadu have been identified for development under the \nSwadesh Darshan 2.0 scheme.  \n \nApart from the above, Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change \n(MoEFCC) has conveyed the following:- \n \n(i) The Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972, empowers the States/UTs, \nincluding Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, for declaration of an \narea as Sanctuary and National Park, if it considers such area is \nof adequate ecological, faunal, floral, geo -morphological, \nnatural or zoological significance. The management of such \ndesignated areas  (National Parks, Sanctuaries, Tiger Reserve) \nis within the ambit of the respective States/UTs. Such \n\nmanagement interventions may also include Eco -Tourism \nactivities in these areas. The Ministry has issued guidelines for \npreparation of management plans for these areas in accordance \nwith the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 on 30.11.2023. \n(ii) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change has \nissued ‘Guidelines for Sustainable Ecotourism in Forest and \nWildlife Areas-2021’ on 29.10.2021. It promotes engagement of \nlocal communities in nature tourism in a manner that enriches \nlocal economy and encourages sustainable use of indigenous \nmaterials through financially viable values chains thereby \nhelping such local communities become “Atma Nirbhar”.  The \nGuidelines also includes potential sites of Eco Tourism in the \ncountry, including Sanjay Gandhi (Borivilli), Lonar and Thane \nCreek Flamingo in Maharashtra and Gulf of Mannar Marine, \nVedanthangal and Point Calimere in Tamil Nadu. \n(iii) Further, MoEFCC has also circulated guidelines for preparation \nof management plans for protected areas which also includes \ntourism activities.  \n(iv) The National Tiger Conservation Authority has also notified the \n‘Normative Standards for tourism activities and Project Tiger \nguidelines, 2012’. \n \nIn addition to above, Ministry of Tourism has formulated National \nStrategy for Sustainable Tourism.  Following strategic pillars have been \nidentified for development of sustainable tourism: \n \n(i) Promoting Environmental Sustainability \n(ii) Protecting Biodiversity  \n(iii) Promoting Economic Sustainability  \n(iv) Promoting Socio-Cultural Sustainability  \n\n(v) Scheme for Certification of Sustainable Tourism \n(vi) IEC and Capacity Building  \n(vii) Governance  \n \nAlso, as part of its on -going activities, the Ministry of Tourism  regularly \npromotes India as a holistic tourism destination including wildlife and \nforest tourism  through various mediums including social media and \nthrough its website www.incredibleindia.org. \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS ("}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |11", "t": "Permission to Grade-1 Diabetic Students in Classroom & Examination", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.11 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nPERMISSION TO GRADE-1 DIABETIC STUDENTS IN CLASSROOM & \nEXAMINATION \n \n*11. SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the decision taken by the Maharashtra Education \nDepartment recently to permit students suffering from Type -1 Diabetes in grades I to XII \nto consume snacks and take medication in the classroom and also during examinations, \nconsidering them as part of the special needs (disabled) category, if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) whether the Government has information on students being allowed to use devices such \nas Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM), Flash Glucose Monitoring (FGM) or insulin \npumps during exams as communicated by the Education Department; \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government endorses the provision that if a smartphone is being used for \nglucose monitoring, it should be kept with the teacher or invigilator only; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO ( E) OF THE LOK \nSABHA ADMITTED STARRED QUESTION NO. 11 FOR REPLY ON 04.12.2023 \nASKED BY HON’BLE MP SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL REGARDING ‘PERMISSION \nTO GRADE-1 DIABETIC STUDENTS IN CLASSROOM & EXAMINATION’.  \n \n(a) to (e) Education is in the concurrent list of the Constitution and majority of the schools are \nunder the domain of respective State and UT Government s. State and UT Governments are the \nappropriate Governments under the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) \nAct, 2009.  \n The Government of Maharashtra vide Government Resolution dated 26.10.2023 \npermitted students suffering from Type-1 Diabetes in grades I to XII to consume snacks and take \nmedication in the  classroom and also during examinations.  The students suffering from Type -1 \nDiabetes can avail these facilities on demand. \n Para 22.24 of Resolution dated 26.10.2023 of Government of Maharashtra mentions that, \nif smartphone is being used for glucose monitoring then it must be kept with the teacher or \ninvigilator.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |110", "t": "Comparative Analysis of Employment in Tourism Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*110 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nCOMPARATIVE ANALYSIS OF EMPLOYMENT IN TOURISM SECTOR \n \n*110.SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:   \n                                            \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of comparative analysis of the current employment \nscenario in the tourism sector in India over the last three years, \nincluding the total number of jobs directly generated and the \nmanner in which this has evolved over the abovementioned period;  \n(b) the comparative overview of its contribution to the Gross Domestic \nProduct (GDP) of India over the past three years; and \n(c) the trends or changes noticed during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n****** \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.* 110 ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 REGARDING \nCOMPARATIVE ANALYSIS OF EMPLOYMENT IN TOURISM SECTOR BY \nSHRI. JAGDAMBIKA PAL \n \n(a) to  (c): The employment scenario in the tourism sector and the \ncontribution of tourism to the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) in India, \nare as follows: \n \nIndicator 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nTotal Tourism GDP (Rs. crore) 1041747.04 297432.99 415689.03 \nTotal Tourism share in GDP \n(%) \n5.18 1.50* 1.77 \nTotal Tourism Jobs (million) 69.44 68.07 70.04 \nShare in employment 13.50 12.91* 12.66 \n \nSource: National Council of Applied Economic Research (NCAER)  \ncomputations using National Acc ount Statistics 2023, Third Tourism \nSatellite Account - 2015-16 and respective rounds of Periodic Labour \nForce Survey data. \n*The decline in the share of tourism GDP and tourism employment is due \nto the COVID-19 pandemic. \n \nThe share of tourism in the GDP de clined in the year 2020 -21 to 1.50 % \nfrom 5.18% in the year 2019 -20 due to the Covid pandemic. However, in \nthe post-pandemic year 2021 -22, the share of tourism in the total GDP \nincreased to 1.77 %. The share of Tourism emplo yment in the total \nemployment of the economy declined in the year 2020-21 to 12.91% from \n13.50% in the year 2019 -20. In the post -pandemic year 2021 -22, the \nshare of Tourism employment was 12.66%. \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |111", "t": "Green Credit Programme", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial services \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 111 \nANSWERED ON DECEMBER 4th, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA) \nPRADHAN MANTRI MUDRA YOJANA (PMMY) \n \n111.  SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n  