������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |201", "t": "Tiger Attacks", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 201 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, DECEMBER 4, 2023/AGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA)  \nLIFE INSURANCE PENETRATION AND DENSITY \n201. SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware that life insurance penetration and insurance density in \nIndia is among the lowest in the world;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has taken or plans to ta ke any measures to address these issues; \nand  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) and (b) : Since opening up of the insurance sector in India in the year 2000 the insurance \npenetration (the percentage of insurance premiums to GDP) of life insurance sector has gone up \nfrom 2.15% in FY 2001 -02 to 3.20% in FY 2021 -22 while the global average for l ife insurance \npenetration for the year 2021 -22 is 2.8%. Life insurance density (the ratio of premiums to \npopulation) has gone up from $ 9.1 in FY 2001- 02 to $ 69.0 in FY 2021-22. \nFurther, as per information provided by IRDAI, the life insurance sector in India has grown from \nRs 0.56 lakh crores in the FY 2001-02 to Rs 6.93 lakh crores in FY 2021-2022.  \n(c) and (d): For creating universal social security system for all Indians, especially the poor and \nthe under-privileged Government of India launched two Social Security Schemes in the Insurance \nsector; namely the Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY) and the Pra dhan Mantri \nJeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana (PMJJBY) on pan India basis on the 9th of May, 2015. \nPMJJBY offers risk cover of Rs. 2 lakhs in case of death of the insured, due to any reason, at an \nannual premium of Rs. 436/ - to people in the age group of 18 to 50 y ears having a bank / Post \noffice account. 18.33 crores subscribers have been enrolled under the scheme up to 25.10.2023.   \nPMSBY offers risk cover of Rs 2 lakhs in case of accidental death or total permanent disability \nand Rs. 1 lakh for partial permanent disability; due to accident at a premium of Rs. 20 per annum \nto people in the age group 18 to 70 years with a bank / Post office account. 40.63 crore \nsubscribers have been enrolled under the scheme up to 25.10.2023. \nGovernment runs various awareness/enrolm ent campaigns up to Gram Panchayat level for \ncreating awareness about the micro insurance schemes.  These campaigns are conducted with \nactive involvement of the banks and the local administration. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |202", "t": "Development of Tourist Destinations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  202 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nINDUSTRIAL TRAINING INSTITUTES \n†202:   Shri Nayab Singh   \nShri Sanjay Bhatia  \n \nWill the Minister of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government aims to increase the young workforce through Industrial \nTraining Institutes (ITIs) and transform the said ITIs into Technology Centers; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to achieve the said objectives in the Parliamentary \nConstituencies of Kurukshetra and Karnal and if so, the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a & b) Sir, Under the Govt of India Skill India Mission (SIM), the Ministry of Skill Development \nand Entrepreneurship (MSDE) provides skilling, re-skilling and up-skilling to all young Indians \nthrough a comprehensive network of Centres / Institutes across the country under various \nschemes viz. Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY),  Scheme of Support to Jan \nShikshan Santhans (JSS), National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS), and Craftsmen \nTraining Schems (CTS) through Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs). \nAt present, there are 15,016 ITIs established in the country out of which 3,289 ITIs are \nGovernment ITIs and 11,727 ITIs are Private ITIs. Establishment and day to day administration \nof ITIs comes under the jurisdiction of the State Government, while the policies such as setting \nthe criterion for affiliation, conducting the examination along with certification and designing \ncurriculum are the responsibilities of the Central Government. \n \nUnder CTS scheme, skill training is imparted in 152 National Skill Qualification Framework \n(NSQF) aligned courses (trades). These courses are designed to provide basic skills and \nknowledge in that trade, so as to prepare the trainees for wage employment and/ or self-\nemployment. The course curriculum is updated regularly to incorporate the latest technological \nequipment and industry requirements. To cater the demand of futuristic job roles in emerging \ntechnologies, DGT has developed 23 New-Age courses in areas such as Drone Technologies, \nArtificial Intelligence, and Internet of Things (IoT). \n\nIn addition, the Dual System of Training (DST) and Flexi MoU Schemes have been introduced \nwherein a large portion of training is ‘hands on’ and is provided in the industry itself. In the DST \nframework, On-the-Job Training (OJT) for a period of 6-12 months is conducted within the \nindustries for courses of a total duration of 1 or 2 years. This immersive approach integrates \ntheoretical learning from ITIs with practical hands-on experience gained in the industry. \nIn Flexi MoU scheme, industries offer training in courses similar to CTS courses, but customized \nand tailored to industry’s needs to ensure that the training aligns precisely with the skill sets \nrequired within the industry, enhancing workforce competence and productivity significantly.  \nThere are no centres by the name of Technology Centers under MSDE. However, several state \ngovernments have collaborated with industries to transform the Government ITIs into \ntechnology centers / hubs with modern infrastructure and training facilities to meet the industry \n4.0 standards. These collaborations aim to equip Government ITIs with cutting-edge technology \nand industry-aligned curriculum to bridge the skill gap and prepare students for the demands of \nthe evolving industrial landscape. \n(c) The State of Haryana has also collaborated with industries for infrastructure \ndevelopment, course curriculum development, training of Master Trainers, and upgradation of \nmachinery and equipment in Government ITIs of the State. In addition to this, Haryana is also \nimplementing the DST in collaboration with local industries in its Government ITIs.  \n \nIn the parliament constituency of Kurukshetra, there are 42 ITIs established in the districts of \nKurukshetra and Kaithal. Out of these 42 ITIs, 14 are Government ITIs and 28 are Private ITIs. \n \nIn the parliament constituency of Karnal, at present, there are 18 ITIs out of which 6 are \nGovernment ITIs and 12 are Private ITIs. One Government ITI in Kurukshetra district and two \nGovernment ITIs in Karnal district have implemented DST mode in collaboration with local \nindustry. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |203", "t": "Deputation/Direct Recruitment in Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 203 \nANSWERED ON 18.12.2023 \n \nDEPUTATION/DIRECT RECRUITMENT IN  \nKENDRIYA VIDYALAYA SANGATHAN \n \n*203. DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has issued instructions \nrestraining the forwarding of application of employees for outside \nemployment on deputation/direct recruitment;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether grievances are being received by Employees/Teacher Associations \nregarding deliberate delay in processing of such applications at School, \nRegional and Head quarter level resulting in failure in submission of \napplication before the last date, which adversely hampers the future career \nprospects of employees;  \n(d)  if so, the details of applications of KVS employees for deputation elsewhere \nreceived/ forwarded/declined and still pending during the last three years, \nregion-wise;  \n(e)  whether the Government has any mechanism for grievance redressal in the \nKVS to check discrimination against SC/ST/OBC employees in such matters \nand if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f)  the other steps being taken by the KVS in adhering to DoPT guidelines in \nthis regard? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (f) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n****** \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 203 RAISED BY DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY, \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT ANSWERED ON 18/12/2023 REGARDING \nDEPUTATION/DIRECT RECRUITMENT IN KENDRIYA VIDYALAYA SANGATHAN.  \n  \n(a) & (b) As per information received from Kendriya Vidyalayas Sangathan (KVS) , \nthe matter of forwarding of application of employees for deputation/direct recruitment \nare dealt at three levels i.e. Vidyalaya level, Regional Office level and Headquarters \nlevel. During COVID pandemic years, in order to continue teaching -learning due to \nthe vacancies at that time, an advisory was issued by KVS to exercise abundant \ncaution while forwarding applications of the employees for deputation  / direct \nrecruitment. Once recruitment process was notified  by KVS , the matters for \ndeputation / direct recruitment are forwarded , if he  / she meets the requisite \nconditions/ criteria. \n(c) & (d) As per information received from KVS, no such grievance  received from \nemployees/Teacher Associations of KVS regarding deliberate delay in processing of \nsuch applications at School, Regional and Head quarter level resulting in failure in \nsubmission of application before the last date. The KVS regional office wise details of \napplications of KVS employees for deputation received /forwarded/ declined and still \npending during the last three years are as under:-   \n  \nSl. \nNo. \n  \nKVS Region \nName \n2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nReceived \nForwarded \nDeclined \nReceived \nForwarded \nDeclined \nReceived \nForwarded \nDeclined \n1.  Bhubaneswar 1 1 0 34 34 0 6 6 0 \n2.  Delhi 11 11 0 22 22 0 29 29 0 \n3.  Ernakulam 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n4.  Gurugram 0 0 0 2 0 2 0 0 0 \n5.  Jaipur 1 1 0 2 2 0 0 0 0 \n6.  Ranchi 0 0 0 1 1 0 2 2 0 \n7.  KVS (HQ)  17 17 0 17 17 0 28 28 0 \nTotal 31 30 1 78 76 2 65 65 0 \n \n(e) & (f) The Grievance Redressal Mechanism has been set up in KVS constituting a \nGrievance Cell at the Headquarter of the KVS and each of the 25 Regional Offices \nacross the country to redress grievance of KVS employees including SCs/STs/OBCs \nemployee. The Cell is functional with a Regional Grievance Officer in place in each of \nthe Regional Office and a Cent ral Grievance Officer at KVS (HQ) for monitoring the \nredressal of grievances for early disposal within the stipulated time and as per relevant \nrules. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |204", "t": "Fraudulent Loan Apps", "head": "������������������� �\n�\n������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n����������� �\n������������������������ ��� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n����������� ���������� ���� ��\n�\n������������������������������������ �\n�\n�������� �\n�\n��� ��������������� ���������������� �������� ��\n����������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n����������������������������������� �\n���� ���� �������� ��� ���� �������� ������ ����� ��� ���� ����������� ��� ��������� ����\n������������������������������������������������ �\n�\n������ �\n�\n��������� ��� ������ ������������� �������� ��� ���� ��������� ���\n����������� ���� ���������� ���������������� �� ������� ��� ������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������ �\n�\n�������������������� �\n�\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������� ����������������������\n��� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ��������������������������� ���������������������������������������� ����\n���������� ���������� �