���������\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n������������������������������ ��������������������������������\n���������������� ����������\n���������������������������������������������������� ������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n������������������������������ ��������������������������������\n���������������� ������������������\n���������������� ������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n������������������������������ ������������������������������������������ ��������������������������������\n���������������� ������������ ������������\n�������������������� ������������ ������������\n��\n��\n������������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |24", "t": "Delay in achieving the Targets under PMGSY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 24 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nDELAY IN ACHIEVING THE TARGETS UNDER PMGSY \n \n*24.  DR. PON GAUTHAM SIGAMANI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that construction of more than 24000 km. \nof road length under the first phase of the Pradhan Mantri \nGram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) is still pending and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(b)  the total length of road under the said Yojana that has been \ncompleted/constructed as on date; \n(c)  whether it is also a fact that under the second phase of the \nsaid Yojana, construction of 1562 km. of road length is still \nincomplete and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether it is a fact that there has been inordinate delay in \nachieving the targets under these two phases; and \n(e)  if so, the reasons therefor along with the corrective steps \nproposed for construction/completion of remaining length of \nroads under these two phases? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e)   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha \nStarred Question No. 24  due for answer on 05.12.2023 \n  \n(a) & (b)   No, Sir. Since inception of Pradhan Mantri Gram \nSadakYojana (PMGSY-I) till 29 th November, 2023, a total of 6,45,111 \nkm road length has been sanctioned under the programme, out of \nwhich 6,22,744 km road length has been completed. The balance \nlength to be constructed under PMGSY -I is 6,930 Km as on \n29.11.23.  \n  \nThe balance road length is less than the difference of sanctioned \nand completed length due to reduction in curve length, change in \nalignment, construction of part length by other agencies, etc.  \n   \nThe State/ UT -wise balance road length under PMGSY -I is given at \nAnnexure-I.  \n  \n(c)   No, Sir. Since inception of PMGSY -II till 29 th November, 2023, a \ntotal of 49,856 km has been sanctioned under the programme, out of \nwhich 48,685 km road length has been completed. The balance \nlength to be constructed under PMGSY-II is 652 Km.  \n  \nThe balance road length is less than the difference of sanctioned \nand completed length due to reduction in curve length, change in \nalignment, construction of part length by other agencies, etc.  \n  \nThe State/ UT -wise balance road length under PMGSY -II is given at \nAnnexure-II.  \n  \n(d) & (e)   The sunset date for completion of PMGSY -I was March, \n2019. The PMGSY -II was targeted for completio n by March, 2020. \nHowever, since several projects sanctioned under these \ninterventions were pending mainly in the States of   NE region and \nhill States, on the requests of these States, the  timeline for \ncompletion of PMGSY-I, II was initially extended for PMGSY-I & II till \nSeptember, 22 which has been further extended till March, 2024. \n  \nWhile implementing PMGSY -I & II schemes in the states/UTs, \nseveral challenges like issues of land acquisition, forest clearance, \npoor contracting capacity of the States, lack of response to tenders, \nlaw and order issues, financial capability of states to release funds \non time, execution capacity of States/ State Rural Road \nDevelopment Agencies (SRRDAs) came in the way which impacted \nthe overall progress of the scheme . For No rth-Eastern and hill \n\nStates, some additional issues like adverse climatic conditions, \ntough terrain, short working season etc. also came in the way which \ncompounded the challenges. Covid 19 also impacted the progress of \nworks in some States. \n  \nRegular Revi ew meetings, including Regional Review Meetings \n(RRMs), Performance Review Committee (PRC) Meetings and Pre -\nEmpowered (Pre EC)/ Empowered Committee(EC) Meetings, are \nbeing conducted with the States to expedite the progress of works \nand sort out any bottlen eck coming in the way. Necessary hand -\nholding of the States is also being done through various   workshops \nbeing organized in the States as well as by holding inter -\ndepartmental meetings etc.  \n  \n********* \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \n  \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (b) of Lok SabhaStarred \nQ.No. 24  \ndue for reply on 05.12.2023  \n  \n  \nState/UT-wise details of balance road length under PMGSY-I as on \n29.11.23 \nSr.No. State Name Balance Road Length (in \nKm) \n1 Andaman and Nicobar \nIslands 12 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 89 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 922 \n4 Assam 56 \n5 Bihar 590 \n6 Chhattisgarh 975 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 529 \n8 Jammu and Kashmir 817 \n9 Jharkhand 148 \n10 Kerala 42 \n11 Ladakh 37 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 40 \n13 Maharashtra 65 \n14 Manipur 841 \n15 Meghalaya 391 \n16 Mizoram 77 \n17 Nagaland 83 \n18 Odisha 42 \n19 Sikkim 220 \n20 Telangana 143 \n21 Tripura 158 \n22 Uttar Pradesh 10 \n23 Uttarakhand 485 \n24 West Bengal 159 \nTotal 6,930 \n  \n \n\n \nAnnexure-II \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c) of Lok SabhaStarred Q.No. \n24 due for reply on 05.12.2023 as on 29.11.23 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |240", "t": "Demand for Jobs under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 240 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/12/2023 \n \nDEMAND FOR JOBS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*240. SHRI THIRUMAAVALAVAN THOL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the reasons behind the dipping demand for jobs under \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (MGNREGS) between May till August this year; \n(b)  the details of the steps taken by the Ministry to incorporate \nmore agricultural jobs under the Scheme to boost demand; \n(c)  whether the Ministry monitors the job demand by households \nand individuals under MGNREGS; and \n(d) if so, the details of the same from April to June of the year \n2021-22? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n   \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. \n240 for 19.12.2023. \n  \n(a):    Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) is a demand driven wage employment \nScheme.  \n  \nAs per NREGASoft, a total of 142.98 crore persondays have been \ngenerated during the month of May, 2023 to August, 2023, which is \nmore than 125.76 crore persondays generated during the same period \nof the previous financial year. \n  \n(b):    There are 266 permissible works under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS \nout of which 150 works are related to Agriculture & Allied Activities, \nwhich in-turn supports  the farmers who are eligible for benefit of the \nscheme. As  per para 4(3) of Schedule-I of Mahatma Gandhi National \nRural Employment Guarantee Act, \"works which are non-tangible, not \nmeasurable, repetitive such as, removing grass, pebbles, agricultural \noperations, shall not be taken up\".  \n  \n(c)&(d):   Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven wage \nemployment scheme. This Ministry regularly reviews the performance \nof the implementation of Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in States/UTs \nthrough various fora viz., Mid-Term Review, Labour Budget meetings, \nLabour Budget Revision meetings, Programme Review meetings. \nCentral Employment Guarantee Council and State Employment \nGuarantee Councils periodically monitor implementation of the \nprogramme. State Technical Cell are constituted at various level in the \nStates to regularly monitor the progress of works.  \n  \nOut of total demand of wage employment, more than 99.82% \nhouseholds are getting offer of employment. In case employment is not \nprovided as per the provision of the Act, the households is entitled for \nunemployment allowances according to the Act.  \n \nAs per NREGASoft, Details of households availed employment \nunder Mahatma Gandhi NREGS during the month of April, May and June \nin the financial year 2021-22 is as under: \n  \nPeriod  During the month of \nApril, 2021 \nDuring the month of \nMay, 2021 \nDuring the month of \nJune, 2021 \nHouseholds \navailed \nemployment  \n(Figure in \ncrore) \n2.13 2.22 2.93 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|STARRED   |241", "t": "Computerization of PDS Shops", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 241 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNON-AVAILABILITY OF MARKET FOR AGRICULTURAL PRODUCE \n \n241. SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether farmers have to destroy their produceof tomato, onion etc. due to non-\navailability ofmarket on a large scale across the country, especiallyin the State of \nMaharashtra; \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof particularly for theState of Maharashtra; \n \n(c) whether efforts have been made to providerelief to the farmers by bringing them under \ntheambit of Food Plaza and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  the names of places where these food plazashave been established in the country, \nparticularly inthe State of Maharashtra; and \n \n(e) the other measures proposed to be taken bythe Government in this regard so that the \nfarmers donot destroy their produce? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b):  Agricultural marketing is a state subject and wholesale markets are set up \nunder the provisions of respective State Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) \nAct. However, in order to reduce supply chain losses, promote competition and encourage \nprivate sector investment in development of value chain and marketing infrastructure of \nperishable horticultural produce, Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare (DA&FW), \nhas been pursuing with the States to de-regulate marketing of fruits and vegetables \noutside the market yards. Responding to pursuance, most of the State Governments have \nderegulated fruits and vegetables.  \n \nAs per the information received from Maharashtra State Agricultural Marketing \nBoard, Pune, “In order to reduce the price gap between producers and consumers through \nreduction in intermediaries Maharashtra State has deregulated fruits and vegetables \noutside the Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) yards in July, 2016. To \novercome forced sale of onion, chawl capacity of about 29.75 lakh MT has been created. \nFor tomatoes the current rates are in range of Rs.2084 to Rs.2508 per Qtl. However, the \ndestroy of produce has not been recorded”. The arrival & modal price of onions for last 03 \n\nyears in the APMCs of Maharashtra is at Annexure-I. The arrival & modal price for \ntomatoes for last 03 years in the APMCs of Maharashtra is at Annexure-II. \n \n(c) to (e):  As per the information received from Ministry of Food Processing Industries \n(MoFPI), MoFPI has been implementing Mega Food Park Scheme (MFPS), a component \nscheme under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana (PMKSY), to create modern \ninfrastructure for the food processing sector along the value chain from farm to market. \nMinistry does not set up Mega Food Parks on its own. The scheme is demand driven and \nproposals are invited through Expression of Interest. Under the scheme, Ministry had \napproved 41 Mega Food Parks (MFPs) in the country. State-wise details of the 41 MFPs is \nat Annexure-III. This scheme has been discontinued by the Government with effect from \n01.04.2021 with provision for committed liabilities for ongoing projects.  \n \nAs per the information received from Maharashtra State Agricultural Marketing \nBoard, Pune, “Recently in the year 2020-21 the Maharashtra Agribusiness Network Project \n(MAGNET) is started with support of Asian Development Bank for value chain \ndevelopment of Horticultural Crops. With objective to Increase farmers income while \npromoting commercially attractive private investment in selected value chains namely \nPomegranate, Banana, Custard apple, Guava, Sapota, Orange, Sweet orange, \nStrawberry, Okra, Chilly (red and green),Flowers etc.” \n \nGovernment of India is implementing Market Intervention Scheme (MIS)for \nprocurement of agricultural and horticultural commodities which are perishable in nature \nand are not covered under the Price Support Scheme (PSS). The objective of intervention \nis to protect the growers of these commodities from making distress sale in the event 