�������������������������������\n��\n�������������������������������� ���������� ������������ ������������������ ���������������� ������������������ ��������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� ���������������������� �������� �������� ������ ���������������� �������� ������ ����������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |26", "t": "Discrimination against Dalits and Adivasis in Private Schools", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.26 \nANSWERED ON-04/12/2023 \n \nDiscrimination against Dalits and Adivasis in Private Schools  \n26. SHRI A. RAJA: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of instances of discrimination against Dalits and Adivasis in private \nschools reported in the last three years; \n \n(b) the specific regulatory measures issued by the Government or CBSE to address \ndiscrimination against Dalits and Adivasis in private schools in the last three years; and  \n \n(c) the details of the Government circulars, guidelines/orders sent to States to address \nthe problems of students from marginalized communities and ensure safety, equality and \ninclusiveness in private schools? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a):  Education is a subject in the Concurrent List of the Constitution and schools, other \nthan those owned / funded by Central Government, are under the jurisdiction of the State \nGovernments. Thus, the matters relating to discrimination against students belonging to \nSC & ST communities  in private schools and related matter s are regulated in terms of \nRules and Instructions of the respective State Government concerned.  \n \n(b) and (c):   Under Section 12(1)(c) of the RT E Act, private unaided schools have to \nreserve 25 percent of their seats for children belonging to economically weaker sections \nand disadvantaged group. Sections 8(c) and 9(c) of RTE Act, 2009 makes it obligatory \nfor schools to ensure that the child belongi ng to weaker section and the child belonging \nto disadvantaged groups are not discriminated against and prevented from pursuing and \ncompleting elementary education on any grounds.  \n \nNational Education Policy (NEP), 2020 provides that special attention will be given to \nreduce disparities in the educational development of schedule Castes and Scheduled \nTribes. \n \nThe NEP, 2020 further mandates that students will be sensitized through this new school \nculture, brought in by teachers, trained social workers and counsellors as well as through \ncorresponding changes to bring in an inclusive school curriculum. The school  \n\n \ncurriculum will include, early on, material on human values such as respect for all \npersons, empathy, tolerance, human rights, gender equality, non -violence, global \ncitizenship, inclusion, and equity. It would also include more detailed knowledge of \nvarious cultures, religions, languages, gender identities, etc. to sensitize and develop \nrespect for diversity. Any biases and stereotypes in school curriculum will be removed, \nand more material will be included that is relevant and relatable to all communities. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |260", "t": "Agricultural R&D Institutes in Karnataka", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 260 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nAGRICULTURAL R&D INSTITUTES IN KARNATAKA \n \n260. DR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Agricultural Research and Development Institutes are working for agricultural \ndevelopment in Karnataka; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, location-wise; and  \n(c) the details of agriculture related works done by the said Agricultural R&D Institutes during \nthe last four years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE         \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h                               (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b) : Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) has established 6 research \ninstitutes in the State of Karnataka. ICAR has also established 74 Centres of All India \nCoordinated Research Projects (AICRPs)/All India Network Projects (AINPs), Outreach \nProgrammes and Consortia Research Platforms (CRPs) operating in the Agricultural \nUniversities and ICAR Research Institutes located in the State. These institutes are catering \nto the agricultural technology needs of the State of Karnataka besides other parts of the \ncountry. In addition, at district level, 33 Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) have also been \nestablished in Karnataka for training and demonstration of the technologies developed.  \n \n The list of the Research Institutes and Regional Stations located in the State of Karnataka \nis attached as Annexure-1&2 \n(c): Agricultural research institutes/centres located in Karnataka have undertaken research \nfor the development of various field crops, pulses, oilseeds, fibres, horticultural crops, climate \nresilient varieties; diseases diagnosis and development of vaccines for animals, poultry and \nfisheries sectors; ergonomically improved tools & equipment and farmer friendly tools  and \nmachineries for farm operations; demonstration, training and skill development programmes \nfor farmers and stakeholders etc.   \n\n During the last four years (2019 -2022) and current year (2023), a total of 26 4 field \ncrop varieties were developed by ICAR for Karnataka, these include 86 varieties of cereals; \n45 of oilseeds; 32 of pulses; 65 of fibre; 24 of forages; 10 of sugarcane and 2 of other crops. \nSimilarly, ICAR varieties developed fruits 6; vegetables 27;   Medicinal crops 6; arecanut 1; \ncocoa 3 and coconut 1 varieties are widely adopted by the farmers of Karnataka. The \nNational Animal Disease Referral Expert System (NADRES_v2) of ICAR - National Institute \nof Veterinary Epidemiology and Disease Informatics  (NIVEDI) is an early warning system \nwhich provided more than 1875 predictions for major livestock diseases . These  were \ncommunicated to the State Animal Husbandry Department for necessary preparedness \nduring last four year. Further, Indirect ELISA Kit for Avion Influenza virus antibody detection \nin chicken and Mab based blocking ELISA Kit for Foot and Mouth Disease (FMD)  was also \ndeveloped. Also 2691 On Farm Trials, 16069 Frontline Demonstrations; training of 4.57 lakhs \nfarmers and 37011 Extension personals were taken up. 10972 quintals of seeds; 58.451 \nlakhs planting materials and 3.959 lakhs of livestock strains and fingerlings  has been \nproduced during last four year. National Surveillance Programme for Aquatic Animal \nDiseases Marine Fisheries (NSPAAD) Poli cy has been implemented ; Established \nminimum Legal Size (MLS) for capture of 72 commercially exploited marine finfish/shellfish; \nDeveloped about 500 open -sea cages and Established seabass hatchery of 5.0 lakh \nfingerlings capacity for regular seed supplying in coastal cages. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n[Part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha USQ No. 260 dated 05TH DECEMEBER, 2023] \n \nList of Agriculture Research Institutes located in the State of Karnataka \n1.  ICAR-National Bureau of Agricultural Insect Resources, Bengaluru \n2.  ICAR-Indian Institute of Horticulture Research, Bengaluru \n3.  ICAR-Directorate of  Cashew Research, Puttur \n4.  ICAR-National Institute of Animal Nutrition and Physiology, Bengaluru \n5.  ICAR-National Institute of Veterinary Epidemiology and Disease \nInformatics(NIVEDI), Bengaluru  \n6.  ICAR-Agricultural Technology Application Research Institute, Zone XI, \nBangaluru \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II \n[Part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha USQ No. 260 dated 05TH DECEMEBER, 2023] \n \nList of ICAR Regional Stations located in the State of Karnataka \n  \n \n \n \n1.  ICAR- Central Institute for Research on Cotton Technology   (Circot), \nQuality Evaluation Unit, Agricultural Research Station, Dharwad Farm \nB.P.O., Dharwad - 580007, Karnataka \n2.  ICAR- Indian Veterinary Research Institute  (IVRI) Regional Station, IVRI \nCampus, Hebbal, Bangal"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |261", "t": "Complaints on Mobile App Meri Sadak", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 261 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nELEVATED ROAD ON NH-48  \n  \n*261.  SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA:  \n  \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has announced the construction  of an \n elevated road on the National Highway (NH) No. 48 at Khairwara \n town located in Udaipur district and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the funds allocated and spent for construction of the said  road; \n and \n(c) the time by which the construction of the said road is likely to be \n completed? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (c)        A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 261 ANSWERED ON 21 ST DECEMBER, \n2023 ASKED BY SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA, HON’BLE MP(LS) REGARDING \n‘ELEVATED ROAD ON NH-48’ \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n(a) The proposals for construction of 2 Nos. of six lane flyovers in \nKhairwara Town was sanctioned as a part of six laning of th is section \nof NH-48. However, due to demand of public/public representatives for \nconstruction of elevated highway in Khairwara town, construction of \nthese flyovers was de-scoped from the work.  \n(b) & (c) does not arise.  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |262", "t": "Gram Manchitra Application", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 262 \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nGram Manchitra Application \n262. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to State: \na. the ways in which Gram Manchitra application enable real -time tracking and \nmonitoring of activities, bridging the gap between planning and execution at the Gram \nPanchayat level; \nb. whether the Gram Manchitra application align with broader goals of transparency, \naccountability and efficient governance at the grassroots level; \nc. if so, the details thereof; and \nd. the manner in which this application utilizes Geographic Information System (GIS) \nmaps and technology for preparing Gram Panchayat Development Plans? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) In order to encourage the Spatial Planning by the Gram Panchayat, Ministry of \nPanchayati Raj had launched the Geographic Information System (GIS) application “Gram \nManchitra” (https://grammanchitra.gov.in). This application facilitates and supports Gram \nPanchayats to perform planning at Gram Panchayat level using geo -spatial technology. It \nprovides a single/ unified Geo Spatial platform to better visualize the various developmental \nworks to be taken up across the different sectors and provide a decision sup port system for \nGram Panchayat Development Plan (GPDP).  \n\nFurther, Ministry has launched mActionSoft, a mobile based solution to help in \ncapturing photos with Geo-Tags (i.e. GPS Coordinates) for the works which have asset as an \noutput. Geo-tagging of the assets is done in all three stages viz. (i) before start of the work, \n(ii) during the work and (iii) on completion of work.  This would provide a repository of \ninformation on all works and assets related to natural resource management, water harvesting, \ndrought proofing, sanitation, agriculture, check dams and irrigation channels etc. Assets geo-\ntagged using the m-ActionSoft application are available on Gram Manchitra, enhancing the \nvisualization of various developmental works in the Gram Panchayats. \n(b) to (d) The assets created under the finance commission funds are geotagged with the \nphotographs of assets by the Panchayats. The GIS data of assets geotagged on the map of \nPanchayat can be visualized on the Gram Manchitra application. Gram Manchitra provides \nseveral planning tools that use GIS technology to help Gram Panchayat officials develop \nrealistic and achievable development plans. These tools provide a decision support system in \nthe preparation of development plans viz. tools for identifying potential sites for development \nprojects, asset tracking, estimating the costs of projects, and assessing the impact of projects. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |263", "t": "Empowering of CBC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 263\n(To be answered on the 21st December 2023)\n \nINTERNATIONAL FLIGHT FROM LUCKNOW AIRPORT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether any action plan has been prepared to improve the international\nair connectivity from Lucknow Airport to ensure direct connectivity to\ncountries such as US, UK etc.;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the Government also proposes to expand runways and taxiways in\norder to accommodate aircrafts for directly connecting Lucknow to countries\nlike the US and UK; and\n(d) if so, the details of tenders issued and progress achieved in land acquisition\nalongwith the expected timeline for completion of the project?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n*****\n*263. SHRI RITESH PANDEY\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question\nNo. 263 regarding \"International Flights From Lucknow Airport\" to be\nanswered on 21.12.2023\n \n(a) & (b): Presently, Lucknow is directly connected to countries such as UAE,\nSaudi Arabia, Oman and Thailand. The international operations of airlines\nare governed by the bilateral Air Services Agreement (ASA) between India\nand the respective country. In order to improve international air connectivity\nfrom Lucknow, it has been included in the list of 18 tourists destinations\nwhich are available to designated carriers of ASEAN and SAARC countries\n(except Pakistan & Afghanistan) for unlimited operations. Lucknow is also\navailable as a point of call for designated carriers of USA and UK. However,\nstart of direct international flights from any point in India is purely a\ncommercial decision of scheduled airlines on the basis of passenger demand,\navailability of slots, economic viability of the route and other associated\nfactors.\n \n(c) & (d):  Upgradation/expansion of airports is a continuous process and is\nundertaken by Airports Authority of India (AAI) and other Airport\nOperators from time to time depending on the availability of land,\ncommercial viability, socio-economic considerations, traffic demand /\nwillingness of airlines to operate to/from such airports. AAI has leased out\nLucknow airport under Public Private Partnership (PPP) for operation,\nmanagement and development on long term lease basis.  The PPP partner has\nembarked upon the airport expansion plan which also includes extension of\nrunway and construction of taxiways.  However, the timeline for completion\nof the airport projects depends upon many factors such as land acquisition,\navailability of mandatory clearances, financial closure etc. \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |264", "t": "Corporate Funds for Promotion of Sports", "head": "������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������\n������������������ ���� �������� ������������������ �������� �������������������� ������ ���������������������� ���������� ������\n�������� ���������������������� ������ �������������� ������������������������ ������ ���������� �������� ���� ���������������������� ����������\n���������������� ������ �������������������������� �������������� �������� �������������������� �������� ������������������������ ������ ����������\n���������� �������������������� �������������� ���������������� �������� ���������������� ���������������� ������������ ��������\n�������������������� �������� ������������������ ������ �������� �������� ���������� �������� ���������������������� �������� ��������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���� ���������� ������������������ ������������ ���� ������������ ������������������ �������� ������������������������������ ��������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ������ �������������� ���� �������������������� ������ ������������ ���������������� ���������������� ������������������������������\n���������������������������� ������������ �������������������� ���������� �������� ������������ ������������������������ ������ ���������� ������\n�������� ������������������ �������������� ������������������������������ ���������� �������������� ������������ ����������������������\n��������������������������������\n������������������ ������������ �������� �������� ���������������������� ������ ���������� �������������������� �������� ��������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� �������� ������������������ ������ �������������������� ������������������ ������ ������������������ ������������������ ������������������\n���������������� ���������������������� �������������� ���������������������� �������� �������������������� �������� ����������������\n�������� �������������� ���������������� ���������� ������������ �������������� ���� ���� ������������������ �������� ������������ ��������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������� �������������� �������������� ���������������������� �������������� �������������������� �������� ��������������\n���������������� �������������������� �������� ���������������� ������ �������������� ������ �������������������� ���������������� ��������\n����������������������������������������\n������������\n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |265", "t": "Kisan e-Mitra", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 265 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 21, 2023 \n \nCIVIC AMENITIES IN SLUMS  \n \nNO. 265. SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n  DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY:   \n    \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of programmes being implemented by the Government \nto assist States/UTs in providing housing and basic civic amenities \nto the urban poor including slum dwellers of the urban areas;  \n(b) the details and the current status of each of the above projects;  \n(c) whether the Government has al located funds to provide civic \namenities in the slums and development of slums in the country \nduring the last three years and the current year; \n(d) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n(e) whether the Government proposes to conduct a survey regarding \nslums in the urban areas and to identify  them by adopting Remote \nSensing and Geographical Information System, if so, the details \nthereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(f) whether the Government proposes to take any c oncrete st eps to \nprovide basic amenities such as healthcare to the people living in \nthe slums in the Metropolitan cities of the country  and if so, the \ndetails thereof?\n \n \n           \n    ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (f): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION  \nNO. 265* FOR 21.12.2023 REGARDING ‘CIVIC AMENITIES IN SLUMS’ \n \n \n(a)  to (d): ‘Land’ and ‘Colonisation’ are State subjects. Schemes related \nto development of the slums including providing civic amenities are  \nimplemented by States/Union Territories (UTs). However, Central  \nGovernment is supplementing the effor ts of S tates/UTs to provide \nhousing and civic amenities in urban areas including slums through \nvarious Missions, such as Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urban \n(PMAY-U), Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation \n(AMRUT 2.0), Swachh Bharat Mission - Urban (SBM-U 2.0). \n \nUnder PMAY-U, Central Assista nce is provided to States/UT s for  \nconstruction of houses for all eligible families/beneficiaries in urban  \nareas including slum dwellers. As on 04.12.2023, a total of  118.63 \nlakh houses have been sanctioned  based on the project proposals \nsubmitted by States/UTs under PMAY -U, out of which 113.42 lakh \nhave been grounded and 78.27 lakh are completed/delivered to the \nbeneficiaries. The State/UT-wise details of Central Assistance  \nreleased during the last t hree years and the curre nt year are at \nAnnexure-I.  \n \nUnder SBM-U, funds are released to States/UTs for construction of  \nCommunity/Public Toilets  ( CT/PT) and Individual Household Latrines  \n(IHHL) on need basis along with Solid Waste Management  (SWM) \ncomponent in urban areas including slums. The State/UT -wise details \nof Central Share f unds released under SBM -U during the last t hree \nyears and the current year are at Annexure-II.  \n \nUnder AMRUT Scheme, State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs)  amounting \nto ₹77,640 crore including committed Central  Assistance of ₹ 35,990 \ncrore have been approved for projects in all  States/UTs for the entire \nMission Period. So far, Central Assistance amounting to ₹33,913 crore \nhas been released to States/UTs for AMRUT projects for water supply, \nsewerage & septage management, storm water drainage, green \nspaces & parks and non-motorized urban transport. The State/UT-wise \ndetails of Central  funds released under AMRUT during the last t hree \nyears and the current year are at Annexure-III. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nFurther, AMRUT 2.0 and SBM 2.0 have been launched to make cities \ngarbage free and water secure during the mission period. Under \nAMRUT 2.0, so far , State Water Action Plans (SWAP) have been \napproved by MoHUA for 6,894 projects wort h ₹1,53,573.75 crore \n(including O&M) submitted by States/UTs.  Under SBM 2.0, Mission \nallocation is  ₹32,541.11 crore against which, ₹ 3,938.06 crore have \nbeen released. \n \n(e): Census of India conducts enumeration of population including \nslums on decadal basi s i n the country. The last census  was \nconducted in 2011, according to which, a total of 6.54 crore people \nwere living in 1,08,227 slums across the country.  At present, there is \nno proposal with MoH UA to conduct any survey o f slum areas usin g \nRemote Sensing and Geographic Information System.   \n \n(f): Apart from PMAY-U, SBM-U and AMRUT, to provide civic amenities, \nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare under National Health Mission \nprovides financial and technical support to States/UTs to strengthen \nthe health car e systems to provide affordable healthcare to all the \ncitizens including slum areas. Under Ayushman Bharat - Pradhan \nMantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB -PMJAY), health coverage up to ₹5 lakh \nper family per year is provided to all  eligible households including \nslum dwellers. Further, support i"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |266", "t": "Seed Cooperative Society", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 266 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  21st December 2023 )  \nKRISHI UDAN SCHEME \n266.    DR. G. RANJITH REDDY                    \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) the details of airports which have been included under Krishi UDAN \nScheme in the country; \n \n(b) the details of facilities and incentives given for movement of agri -\nproduce by air transport; \n \n(c) whether it is true that Telangana has not been inc luded in the \nabove scheme for transport of perishable fruits, vegetables and other \nagri products and if so, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) the efforts being made to include some of the airports from \nTelangana under Krishi UDAN Scheme? \nANSWER \nMinister of CI VIL AVIATION      (Shri Jyotiraditya M&#46; \nScindia) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 266 FOR 21.12.2023 \nREGARDING KRISHI UDAN SCHEME   (a) The Stat e-wise list \nof 58 Airports covered under the Krishi Udan Scheme is at \n\nAnnexure.;   (b) Krishi Udan Scheme seeks to ensure \nseamless, cost effective, time bound air transportation and \nassociated logistics for all agri produce originating \nespecially from Nort h East, hilly and tribal regions of the \ncountry.  In order to facilitate and incentivise movement of \nagri-produce by air transportation, the scheme envisages \nwaiver of Landing charges, Parking charges, etc. provided \nfor Indian freighters by Airports Author ity of India (AAI) and \nMinistry of Defence at selected airports under the scheme.;  \n(c) & (d) All agri and perishable commodities are covered \nunder Krishi Udan Scheme in the country at selected 58 \nairports, which includes horticulture, fishery, livestock a nd \nprocessed products. Krishi Udan is an ongoing Scheme and \nis reviewed from time to time in consultation with \nstakeholders. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NUMBER \n266 FOR ANSWER ON 21.12.2023 \nAnnexure \nState-wise List of Airports Covered Under Krishi Udan Scheme \nSl. No. State/Union Territory Airport \n1 Andaman & Nicobar  1. Port Blair  \n2 Andhra Pradesh  2. Visakhapatnam  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  3. Tezu  \n4 Assam  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n4. Dibrugarh  \n5. Jorhat  \n6. Lilabari  \n7. Rupsi  \n8. Silchar  \n9. Tezpur \n5 Bihar  10. Patna  \n11. Darbhanga \n6 Chandigarh  12. Chandigarh  \n7 Chhattisgarh  13. Raipur  \n8 Goa  14. Goa  \n9 Gujarat  15. Bhuj  \n16. Jamnagar  \n17. Rajkot  \n10 Himachal Pradesh  \n  \n  \n18. Bhuntar  \n19. Gaggal  \n20. Shimla  \n11 Jammu & Kashmir  \n  \n21. Jammu  \n22. Srinagar  \n12 Jharkhand  23. Ranchi  \n13 Karnataka 24. Belagavi \n14 Kerala  25. Thiruvananthapuram  \n15 Ladakh  26. Leh  \n16 Lakshadweep  27. Agatti  \n17 Madhya Pradesh  28. Indore  \n29. Bhopal \n30. Jabalpur \n18 Maharashtra  31. Nashik  \n32. Pune  \n19 Manipur  33. Imphal  \n20 Meghalaya  34. Shillong  \n21 Mizoram  35. Lengpui  \n22 Nagaland  36. Dimapur  \n\n23 Odisha 37. Jharsuguda \n24 Punjab 38. Adampur  \n39. Amritsar  \n40. Pathankot \n25 Rajasthan  41. Jaisalmer  \n 42. Jodhpur  \n26 Sikkim  43. Pakyong  \n27 Tamil Nadu  44. Coimbatore  \n 45. Tiruchirappalli  \n28 Tripura  46. Agartala  \n29 Uttar Pradesh  47. Agra  \n 48. Bareli  \n 49. Gorakhpur  \n 50. Hindon  \n 51. Kanpur  \n 52. Prayagraj  \n 53. Varanasi  \n30 Uttarakhand  \n  \n  \n54. Dehradun  \n55. Pantnagar  \n56. Pithoragarh  \n31 West Bengal  57. Bagdogra  \n 58. Kolkata  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |267", "t": "Penalty under PMAY-G", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 267 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 21, 2023 \nONLINE PERMISSION FOR BUILDINGS \n \nNO. 267.  SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY:  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state :- \n(a) whether the Municipalities and the Municipal Corporations in \nthe country have taken positive steps towards seeking online \npermission for buildings; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Municipalities and Municipal Corporations in the \ncountry, particularly the Municipal Corporation of Greater \nMumbai (MCGM), have authorised the Architects for approval of \nbuilding constructions under the Ease of Doing Business \ninitiative of the Government; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 267* FOR ANSWER  ON DECEMBER 21, 2023 \nREGARDING ‘ONLINE PERMISSION FOR BUILDINGS’. \n \n(a) and (b):    Urban Planning is a State subject. As per the 12 th \nschedule of the Constitution, urban planning is the function of Urban \nLocal Bodies (ULBs)/ Urban Development Authorities subject to the \ntransfer of the functions by the State Government. Government of \nIndia has only an advisory role in the matter. Ministry of Housing and \nUrban Affairs (MoHUA) provides schematic and programmatic \nsupport for urban development in the country. Under Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT), one of the reform \ncomponents is implementation of Online Building Permission System. \nAs reported by the States , 3599 ULBs including 449 AMRUT cities \nhave Online Building Permission System (OBPS). \n \n(c)  and (d): As per the response received from Municipal Corporation \nof Greater Mumbai (MCGM) / (Brihanmumbai Municipal Corporation  \n(BMC)), Architects registered with the Council of Architecture and, \nLicensed Surveyors and Engineers registered with the Planning \nAuthority of BMC have been empowered to grant provisional \napproval to the building proposal plan categorised as Low Risk and \nModerate Risk. Buildings on vacant plot area upto 150 s q.mt. are \nconsidered Low Risk and buildings having vacant plot area from 151 \nsq.mt. to 200 sq.mt. are considered Moderate Risk. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |268", "t": "Funds Granted for PMMSY", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO *268\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2023\n \n \nPUBLIC PROCUREMENT POLICY FOR MSMEs\n \n \n*268.              \n \nSHRI MITESH RAMESHBHAI PATEL (BAKABHAI):\n \nSHRI DURGA DAS \n(D.D.) UIKEY:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nthe significance of Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs);\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe\n \nmanner in which it impacts procurement from MSEs by Central \nMinistries/Departments/Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs);\n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details regarding total procurement from MSEs by Central Ministries/Departments/CPSEs;\n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government has taken steps to boost/promote the MSME sector through \ncollaboration/tie\n-\nups with e\n-\nCommerce companies;\n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, the details thereof and the steps taken in this regard during the last three years and the \ncurrent year;\n \n \n(f) \n \nthe steps \ntaken by the Government to promote trade in the MSME sector across the country, \nState/UT\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(g) \n \nwhether the Government has launched any scheme within public procurement to provide relief \nto MSMEs affected due to Covid\n-\n19 pandemic, if so, the details a\nnd the outcome thereof; and\n \n \n(h) \n \nthe number of cases in which relief has been provided so far along with the value of such \nrelief?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) \nto (h):  \n \nA statement is laid on the \nTable of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENTS REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (h) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *268 FOR ANSWER ON 21.12.2023.\n \n \n(a)\n  \n&\n \n(b)\n:\n \nWith the objective of promoting the growth and development of Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterprises \n(MSME) by improving their market access and linkages, Ministry of MSME \nnotified the Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises from 1\nst\n \nApril, 2012, as \namended subsequently. The Policy is mandatory as notified under the MSMED Act 2006. The P\nolicy \ntarget is an overall procurement of minimum 25% of total annual purchases of products produced/ \nservices rendered by Micro and Small Enterprises (MSE) by the Central Ministries, Government \nDepartments and Central Public Sector Undertakings (CPSUs). T\nhe Policy has also specified a sub \ntarget of 4% procurement of goods and services from MSEs owned by SC/ ST entrepreneurs and\n \n3% \nfrom\n \nMSEs owned by women entrepreneurs.\n \n \n \nBenefits available to the MSEs under the Policy are:\n \n \n \n(i) \n \nPurchase Preference\n-\n \nMini\nmum 25% annual procurement from MSEs by Central Ministries/ \nDepartments/ CPSUs.\n \n \n \n(ii) \n \nPrice match\n-\nmaking facility\n-\n \nIf the L1 price by a non\n-\nMSE is the lowest, participating MSEs \nquoting a price within a band of L1+15% will be allowed to bring down their \nprice to match L1 \nprice. Such MSEs are allowed to supply at least 25% of total tendered value.\n \n \n \n(iii) MSEs are to be provided tender sets free of cost, and are exempted from payment of earnest \nmoney.\n \n \n \n(iv) \n \n358 items are reserved for procurement from MSE\ns. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \n \nImplementation of Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises is monitored \nthrough ‘MSME SAMBANDH’ portal. As per the portal, during the FY 2023\n-\n24, as on 18.12.2023, \nout of total procurement of Rs. 1,03,139.37 crore\n \nreported by 110 CPSUs and one Department, total \nprocurement from MSEs is Rs. 35,671.42 crore (34.59% of total procurement) bene\nﬁtting 1,23,853 \nMSEs.\n \n \n(d) \n&\n \n(e): \n \nAn initiative of Open Network for Digital Commerce (ONDC) for empowering \nMSMEs to transact go\nods and services over digital/electronic network has been taken to facilitate \nMSMEs. ONDC is actively working with Ministry of MSME to onboard MSMEs to the network \nthrough existing seller applications and also working to integrate MSME Mart which has over \n2 lakh \nMSMEs, with ONDC. \nONDC is working actively with States/UTs.\n \n \n(f)\n:\n \n \nPromotion, growth and development of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\n \n(MSMEs) sector \nis a continuous process and Ministry of MSME is constantly working in this direction. \nThe \nsche\nmes/programmes inter alia include Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme \n(PMEGP), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro & Small Enterprises, Micro & Small Enterprises \nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), MSME Champions Scheme, Entrepreneurship Sk\nill \nDevelopment Programme (ESDP), Procurement and Marketing Support Scheme (PMSS), etc.\n \nThe \ncomponents of PMSS, which inter alia include Participation of Individual MSEs in Trade Fairs / \nExhibitions, Vendor Development Program, Adoption of Modern Packaging\n \nTechniques, Adoption of \nBar Code, Adoption of e\n-\nCommerce Platform, etc. to promote trade of MSME Sector.\n \n\n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \nAdditionally, the Public Procurement (Preference to Make in India) Order 2017 (PPP\n-\nMII \nOrder) was issued on 15th June, 2017 pursuant"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |269", "t": "Agroforestry Practices", "head": "districts \n182 Khordha District Odisha 14 \n183 Cuttack District Odisha 9 \n184 Puducherry District Puducherry 2 \n185 Yanam Puducherry 0 \n186 Karaikal & Nagapattinam Districts Puducherry & Tamil Nadu 8 \n187 Jalandhar* Punjab 3 \n188 Ludhiana* Punjab 3 \n189 Amritsar  District Punjab 19 \n190 Bhatinda  District Punjab 19 \n191 Rupnagar District Punjab 19 \n192 Fatehgarh Sahib District Punjab 10 \n193 SAS Nagar District (EAAA), Patiala & Sangrur \nDistricts \nPunjab 26 \n194 Ludhiana District (EAAA), Barnala & Moga \nDistricts \nPunjab 22 \n195 Jalandhar District (EAAA), Kapurthala & SBS \nNagar Districts \nPunjab 19 \n196 Ferozepur, Faridkot and Sri Muktsar Sahib \nDistricts \nPunjab 27 \n197 Hoshiarpur and Gurdaspur Districts Punjab 40 \n198 Pathankot district Punjab 3 \n199 Tarn Taran district Punjab 3 \n200 Fazilka (except area already authorized), \nGanganagar and Hanumangarh districts \nPunjab & Rajasthan 12 \n201 Kota Rajasthan 9 \n202 Barmer, Jaisalmer & Jodhpur Districts Rajasthan 37 \n203 Alwar (Other than Bhiwadi) & Jaipur Districts Rajasthan 48 \n204 Kota District (EAAA), Baran & Chittorgarh \n(Only Rawatbhata Taluka) Districts \nRajasthan 15 \n205 Bhilwara & Bundi Districts Rajasthan 17 \n206 Chittorgarh (Other than Rawatbhata Taluka) & \nUdaipur Districts \nRajasthan 37 \n207 Dholpur District Rajasthan 8 \n208 Ajmer, Pali and Rajsamand Districts Rajasthan 56 \n209 Jalore and Sirohi Districts Rajasthan 28 \n210 Bikaner and Churu districts Rajasthan 3 \n211 Jhunjhunu, Sikar and Nagaur districts Rajasthan 6 \n212 Dausa, Karauli, Sawai Madhopur and Tonk \ndistricts \nRajasthan 0 \n213 Bhiwadi (in Alwar District) Rajasthan 3 \n214 Kanchipuram District Tamil Nadu 34 \n215 Chennai & Tiruvallur Districts Tamil Nadu 67 \n216 Coimbatore District Tamil Nadu 26 \