at District Uttar Pradesh 13 \n252 Bulandshahr District (EAAA), Aligarh & Hathras \nDistricts \nUttar Pradesh 19 \n253 Allahabad District (EAAA), Bhadohi & \nKaushambi Districts \nUttar Pradesh 15 \n254 Amethi, Pratapgarh & Raebareli Districts Uttar Pradesh 27 \n255 Auraiya, Kanpur Dehat & Etawah Districts Uttar Pradesh 29 \n256 Faizabad & Sultanpur Districts Uttar Pradesh 9 \n257 Gorakhpur, Sant Kabir Nagar & Kushinagar \nDistricts \nUttar Pradesh 27 \n258 Meerut District (EAAA), Muzaffarnagar & \nShamli Districts \nUttar Pradesh 28 \n259 Moradabad (EAAA) District Uttar Pradesh 18 \n260 Unnao (EAAA) District Uttar Pradesh 2 \n261 Azamgarh, Mau and Ballia Districts Uttar Pradesh 24 \n262 Bareilly (EAAA) District, Pilibhit and Rampur \nDistricts \nUttar Pradesh 45 \n263 Basti and Ambedkarnagar Districts Uttar Pradesh 13 \n264 Farrukhabad, Etah and Hardoi Districts Uttar Pradesh 39 \n265 Gonda and Barabanki Districts Uttar Pradesh 23 \n266 Jaunpur and Ghazipur Districts Uttar Pradesh 12 \n267 Kanpur (EAAA) District, Fatehpur and Hamirpur \nDistricts \nUttar Pradesh 21 \n268 Mainpuri and Kannauj Districts Uttar Pradesh 21 \n269 Mirzapur, Chandauli and Sonbhadra Districts Uttar Pradesh 19 \n270 Shahjahanpur and Budaun Districts Uttar Pradesh 34 \n\n271 Amroha (except area already authorized) & \nSambhal (except area already aut"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |27", "t": "PARAKH", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 27 \nANSWERED ON - 04/12/2023 \n \nPARAKH \n \n27. SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR:  \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH:  \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether  the Government has proposed to set up Performance Assessment, Review and \nAnalysis of Knowledge for Holistic Development (PARAKH), to oversee the process and \nconduct periodic tests like the National Achievement Survey (NAS) and State Achievement \nSurveys;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the PARAKH has been launched under the National Education Policy (NEP) -2020 \nto develop a standard -setting body to advise school boards regarding new assessment patterns \nand latest research, and promote collaborations between them and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the ETS, which conducts key tests such as TOEFL and GRE, have been chosen by \nthe Government for setting up the regulatory platform;  \n(e) if so, the details thereof;  \n(f) whether PARAKH will be able to bring “uniformity” in assessment across the boards in 36 \nStates and UTs despite the diverse school education system due to geographic differences and \nmultiple languages; and  \n(g) the other steps being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) to (g):National Education Policy (NEP), 2020 under Para 4.41 envisions “to set up a National \nAssessment Centre, PARAKH (Performance Assessment, Review, and Analysis of Knowledge \nfor Holistic Develop ment),  as a standard-setting body under M inistry of Education (MoE)  that \nfulfils the basic objectives of setting norms, standards, and guidelines for student assessment and \nevaluation for all recognized school boards of India, guiding the State Achievement Survey \n(SAS) and undertaking the National Achievement Survey (NAS), monitoring achievement of \n\nlearning outcomes in the country, and encouraging and helping school boards to shift their \nassessment patterns towards meeting the skill requirements of the 21st century in consonance \nwith the stated objectives of this Policy. This Centre will also advise school boards regarding \nnew assessment patterns and latest researches, promoting collaborations between school boards. \nIt will also become an instrument for the sharing of best practices among school boards, and for \nensuring equivalence of academic standards among learners across all school boards.” National \nAssessment Centre, PARAKH (Performance Assessment, Review and Analysis of Knowledge \nfor Holistic Development) was set up in NCERT as an independent constituent unit vide \nNotification no. 1 -4/2012-EC/ 101 - 164 of NCERT dated 8th Feb, 2023, to fulfil the basic \nobjectives of setting norms, standards, guidelines and implement activities related to student \nassessment along with other tasks as mandated by Para 4.41 of NEP, 2020. Educational Testing \nServices (ETS)  has been hired by NCERT to assist in establishing and operationalizing \nPARAKH.  \nPARAKH has undertaken State Educational Achievement Survey 2023 , a study on equivalence \nacross the boards and is actively involved in development of Holistic Progress Card for all four \nstages outlined in the NEP 2020: Foundational, Preparatory, Middle and Secondary stages. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |270", "t": "Funds for Modernization of AIR and DD Kendras", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 270 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023) \nFUNDS FOR MODERNIZATION OF AIR AND DD KENDRAS \n270.  SHRI JUAL ORAM:  \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased \nto state:  \n(a)  whether any funds have been earmarked or sanctioned by the \nGovernment for the upgradation and modernization of the All \nIndia Radio and Doordarshan Kendras in Odisha and other \nStates during the last three financial years;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the details of steps taken by the Government in this regard in \nthe State of Odisha and particularly in Sundargarh District? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n  \n\n(a) & (b):  The modernization and strengthening of infrastructure and \nnetwork of Prasar Bharati in respect of All India Radio (AIR) and \nDoordarshan (DD) at different stations across the country is a \n���������������������� ���������������� ������������ �������� ���������������������������� ������������������������������ ��������\n���������������� �������������������������� �������������� ���������������� ���������� �������������� �������� ����������\nfurther continued for the period 2021 -26 with an outlay of Rs. \n2539.61 crores for various components. \n(c): Following projects have been undertaken under BIND Scheme \nin the state of Odisha (including Sundargarh district): \n(i) Setting up of  100W FM Relay Station at Sundargarh. \n(ii) Approval for setting up of 1kW FM transmitter at Bonaigarh \nin Sundargarh district. \n(iii) Modernization and upgradation of DDK Bhubaneswar  & \nDDK Sambalpur . \n(iv) Modernization of MW Transmitters at Jeypore, Sambalpur \nand Bhawanipatna. \n(v) Modernization and upgradation of Regional training Centre \nat Bhubaneswar. \n(vi) Commissioning of 100 W FM Relay stations at 7 locations \n(Malkangiri, Nabrangpur, Phulbani, Bargarh, Kendrapara, \nKoraput and Mohana). \n(vii) Setting up of 5 kW FM transmitter at Balasore.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |271", "t": "Review of Schemes for Doubling the Income of Farmers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 271 \nANSWERED ON 21/12/2023 \nPROMOTION OF SOLAR ENERGY \n*271. SHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR \nWill the Minister of New and Renewable Energy be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to promote the use of solar energy in the country including \nWest Bengal and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the status of installation of Solar Power Plants/Solar Power Panels including rooftops and \nsolar water heating systems in West Bengal during the last four years; and \n(c) the details of the total installed capacity of solar power in the State? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION No. \n271 for ANSWER ON 21-12-2023 \n \n(a) The Government has set a target of achieving 500 GW of installed power generation capacity \nfrom non-fossil fuels, including solar energy, by 2030 in the country, including in the State of West \nBengal. Solar projects of aggregate capacity of 72.31 GW have been installed in the country as on \n30.11.2023. \n(b) The details of solar power projects, including Roof-Top solar systems, installed in West Bengal \nduring the last four years are given at Annexure. \nSolar Water Heating Systems (SWHS) are being installed in the  market mode in the country.  \nHence, State-wise data of installation of SWHS is not maintained. \n(c) Total installed capacity of solar power in West Bengal is 194.06 MW as on 30.11.2023. \n \n***** \n\nANNEXURE REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.271 FOR 21.12.2023 \n \nS.N. Year Cumulative Solar Capacity \nInstalled in West Bengal (MW) \n1 2019-20 127.24 \n2 2020-21 162.65 \n3 2021-22 166.00 \n4 2022-23 179.97 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |272", "t": "Rashtriya Gokul Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 272 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER 2023 \n \nRASHTRIYA GOKUL MISSION \n272. SHRI RATANSINH MAGANSINH RATHOD: \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \n be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Rashtriya Gokul Mission has been implemented in Gujarat;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the details of steps taken by the Government to enhance productivity of bovines and \nincrease milk production; and  \n(d) the funds allocated for the Rashtriya Gokul Mission in Gujarat for last three years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n (SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) (b) & (d) Yes,  Sir. Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying is implementing \nRashtriya Gokul Mission for development and conservation of indigenous bovine breeds and \ngenetic upgradation of bovine population since December 2014 throughout the country \nincluding Gujarat.  An amount of Rs 8559.38 lakh has been released to the State during the \nlast 3 years and current year for the implementation of Rashtriya Gokul Mission. \n \n(c) Following steps are undertaken by the Department to enhance productivity of bovine  \npopulation and increase milk production:  \n \n(i) Implementation of Nationwide Artificial Insemination Programme  for extension of \n Artificial Insemination coverage \n(ii) Progeny testing and pedigree selection for production of high genetic merit bulls of \n indigenous breeds. \n(iii) Establishment of Breed Multiplication Farms preferably of indigenous breeds \n(iv) Promotion of In-vitro Fertilization (IVF) technology for faster genetic upgradation of \n indigenous breeds \n(v) Implementation of genomic selection using genomic chip. \n(vi) Accelerated Breed Improvement Programme using Sex sorted semen  \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |273", "t": "Suicide by Agricultural Labourers and Daily Wage Workers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*273  \nANSWERED ON-21.12.2023 \n \nAssistance to States under SBM-G \n \n†*273. SHRIMATI NAVNEET RAVI RANA: \nSHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of financial and technical assistance  provided by the Government to the States of  \nMaharashtra, Jharkhand, Bihar and Jammu and  Kashmir for disposal of garbage/waste collected \nunder the Swachh Bharat Mission-Grameen (SBM-G), district-wise; \n \n(b) whether the garbage/waste collected under  the SBM-G is being disposed of in a traditional  \nmanner and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the district-wise number of modern plants set up for disposal of garbage/waste in Maharashtra \nparticularly in Amravati district, Haryana particularly  in Sonipat district, Jharkhand, Bihar and \nJammu and Kashmir during the last five years? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) in respect of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo.*273 for reply on 21.12.2023 regarding Assistance to States under SBM-G \n \n(a) & (b)  Swachh Bharat Mission-Grameen (SBM-G) being a Centrally Sponsored Scheme is \nimplemented through the State /UT Governments. Government of India provides financial and \ntechnical support to States/UTs for implementation of Swachh Bharat Mission-Grameen. \n \nFor management of garbage/waste being generated in the rural areas, the Operational Guidelines \nof Swachh Bharat Mission-Grameen (SBM-G) Phase-II provides various technological options to \nthe States/UTs.  For biodegradable waste, a s far as possible, and especia lly for smaller Gram \nPanchayats, decentralized household level composting is preferred. For large GPs/peri-urban areas, \nor in areas where household level processing is not feasible, community level processing may be \nconsidered. Implementation of Plastic Wa ste Management involves creation of storage and \nsegregation facility at Village level and Material Recovery Facility/ Plastic Waste Management \nUnit at District / Block level. SBM(G) Phase -II Operational Guidelines prescribe for at least one \nPlastic Waste M anagement Unit (PWMU) in each block if no clustering is possible. States/UTs \nhave also been advised to use the urban facilities for Plastic Waste Management. Further, this \ndepartment has issued Manuals and toolkits for Solid Waste Management (SWM) and Plas tic \nWaste Management (PWM) to all the States/UTs. However, States have the flexibility to choose \nthe technology option best suited for local conditions.  Besides, capacity strengthening of various \nstakeholders at State/Districts/Block/GP levels are being done on technical components of SWM. \n \nUnder SBM(G), the funds are released to States/UTs  on sharing basis between centr e and states \n(60:40 for Non- NE States, 90:10 for NE & Himalayan and J&K States and 100% for UTs). The \ncentre share of fund released from 2014-15 to 2023-24 under SBM(G) to States of Maharashtra, \nJharkhand, Bihar and Jammu and Kashmir are as under: - \nRs. in crore \nSr. No. State Central share released from \n2014-15 to 2023-24 \n1 Maharashtra 4594.48 \n2 Jharkhand 2724.43 \n3 Bihar 7192.66 \n4 Jammu & Kashmir 1167.70 \n \n \n(c) State-wise no. of Community Compost Pits, Community Biogas Plants, Storage & Segregation \nsheds and PWMUs constructed under SBM(G) Phase-II are as under: - \nSr. \nNo. \nState No. of \nCommunity \nCompost Pits \nNo. of \ncompleted \ncommunity \nbiogas \nplants \nNo. of storage \n& segregation \nsheds \nNo. of PWMU \n1 Maharashtra 33481 12 19085 5 \n2 Haryana 4449 7 3391 20 \n3 Jharkhand 47266 12 4445 30 \n4 Bihar 8679 14 3921 128 \n5 Jammu & Kashmir 12325 3 6542 61 \n \nSource: Data reported by  the States on Integrated Management Information System of SBM(G) - \nhttps://sbm.gov.in/sbmphase2/Secure/Entry/UserMenu.aspx  \nDistrict-wise details are at Annexure. \n*****\n\nAnnexure \nStatement referred to in part (c) of the reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No.273 due for \nreply on 21-12-2023 \nDistrict-wise no. of Community Compost Pits, Community bio gas plants, Storage & Segregation sheds and \nPWMU constructed under SBM(G) Phase-II as on 15.12.23 \nSr. \nNo. \nState District No. of \nCommunity \nCompost \nPits \nNo. of \ncompleted \ncommunity \nbiogas \nplants \nNo. of Storage \n& \nSegregation \nSheds \nNo. of \nPWMU \n1 Maharashtra Ahmednagar 3644 1 1237 0 \n2 Maharashtra Akola 912 0 198 0 \n3 Maharashtra Amravati 1244 1 891 0 \n4 Maharashtra Aurangabad 478 1 363 0 \n5 Maharashtra Beed 442 0 111 0 \n6 Maharashtra Bhandara 637 0 252 0 \n7 Maharashtra Buldhana 701 1 170 0 \n8 Maharashtra Chandrapur 535 0 138 0 \n9 Maharashtra Dhule 681 0 400 0 \n10 Maharashtra Gadchiroli 1834 0 268 0 \n11 Maharashtra Jalgaon 1768 1 1339 0 \n12 Maharashtra Jalna 224 0 130 0 \n13 Maharashtra Kolhapur 73 1 53 0 \n14 Maharashtra Latur 475 1 189 0"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |274", "t": "Reservation for OBCs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.274 \nANSWERED ON 21.12.2023 \n \nENERGY CONSERVATION PROGRAMMES \n \n†*274. SHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA: \n \nWill the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the performance of t he country with  regard to energy \nconservation is not satisfactory as  compared to other countries of the \nworld; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  the details of energy conservation programmes  being implemented \nby the Government; and \n \n(d)  the extent to which success has been achieved  in meeting the set \ntargets? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) IN RESPECT OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 274 FOR REPLY ON  21.12.2023 REGARDING \nENERGY CONSERVATION PROGRAMMES  ASKED BY SHRI MANSUKHBHAI \nDHANJIBHAI VASAVA: \n********* \n \n(a) & (b) :  The performance of India in respect to energy conservation has been \none of the best in the world.  India’s average annual energy intensity \nimprovement rate has been around 2.4% during the period from 2015 to 2020, \nwhereas the global average rate of energy intensity improvement wa s only 1.4% \nover the same period in terms of Mega Joule/USD (2015 Purchasing Power \nParity), as per the report of International Energy Agency.   \n \n(c) & (d) :  The major programmes on Energy Conservation being implemented \nby the Government are Perform, Achie ve and Trade, Standards and Labelling \nscheme of appliances.  Unnat Jyoti by Affordable LEDs for All (UJALA) scheme , \nEnergy Conservation  Building Code and adoption of electric mobility etc. Details \nof these programme are at Annexure. \n \nThe implementation of  various energy efficiency schemes/ \nprogrammes have led to the overall energy savings of 50.81 Million ton nes of Oil \nequivalent (MTOE) which is about 6.87% of the total primary energy supply of \nthe country for the year 2022 -23, reflecting the monetary savin gs of around              \nRs. 1,88,000 Crores and CO 2 emissions reduction of 307 million Tonnes (approx.) \nduring the year 2022-23. \n  \n**************** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN PART S (c) &  (d) OF THE STATEMENT LAID IN \nREPLY TO STARRED QUESTION N O. 274 ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON \n21.12.2023 REGARDING ENERGY CONSERVATION PROGRAMMES \n*********** \nThe major programmes on Energy Conservation being implemented are as under -  \n \ni. Perform, Achieve and Trade (PAT): In respect of large energy intensive sectors , the \nGovernment is implementing a flagship program called Perform, Achieve and Trade \n(PAT). The scheme covers 1333 units from 13 sectors as on October, 2023, that \nhas resulted in annual energy savings of about 25.9 million Tonnes of Oil \nEquivalent (MTOE) translating into avoiding of about 110.67 million tonne of CO2 \nemissions annually. \n \nii. Standards and Labelling (S&L) scheme : In the appliances sector, Standards and \nLabelling (S&L) scheme of Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) prescribes minimum \nenergy perform ance norms for appliances / equipment. Under the program, the \nmajor energy consuming appliances are given star rating from 1 to 5 with 5 star as \nmost efficient appliance. Based on star label, the consumer is facilitated for \nmaking informed choice regarding  purchase of energy efficient appliances thereby \nsaving electricity consumption. As on October, 2023, the scheme covers 35 \nappliances under the S&L programme. In the year 2022 -23, this scheme has \nresulted in savings of 81.6 billion units of electricity and  cost savings of Rs 49,017 \nCrores. \n \niii. Unnat Jyoti by Affordable LEDs for All (UJALA) : Under this scheme the total \nquantity of energy efficient and affordable LEDs Bulbs and LED Tube lights \ndistributed by EESL since its inception is 36.87 Crores and 72.19 la khs \nrespectively.  The UJALA scheme has resulted in large -scale transformation in the \nLED industry and has enabled market creation of approximately 70 crores LED bulb \nannually. This has led to cumulative distribution of 419 crore LED bulbs and 151 \ncrore LED Tube lights as on March, 2023 since the inception of UJALA scheme in \n2015. This initiative has resulted in estimated savings of 176.19 billion units of \nelectricity per annum and cost savings of INR 70,477 crore per annum.   \n \niv. The buildings sector is broad ly categorized into Commercial and Residential \nbuildings in order to promote energy efficiency. The updated Energy Conservation \nBuilding Code (ECBC) was launched in 2017 for the commercial buildings. Eco -\nNiwas Samhita (ENS) pertains to residential sector, and is taken up on priority. \nECBC has been published by Central Government. Till October 2023, there are 24 \nStates/UTs, which have notified the ECBC based on their regional climatic \nconditions.  \n \nv. In the transport sector, the activitie"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |275", "t": "Rankings to Research Stations of ICAR", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 275 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n \nRANKINGS TO RESEARCH STATIONS OF ICAR \n \n \n275.  SHRI PARBHUBHAI NAGARBHAI VASAVA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nक ृ षि��और��ककसान ��कल्याण��मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of steps that have been taken by the Government for granting rankings for \nvarious research stations of ICAR based on working parameter such as transforming its \nresearch in the field by visible results by increasing farming outputs; and  \n(b) the details of steps that have been taken by the Government for demarcation of boundary \nand removing encroachments from land of various research stations of ICAR? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nक ृ षि��और��ककसान ��कल्याण��मंत्री                             (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) The ranking of the Research Institutes of Indian Council of Agricultural Research \n(ICAR) has been carried out on the basis of various activities taken up by the institutes having \ndifferent weightage marks like i) Institute profile (5 mark), ii) Institute performance (30 mark), \niii) Recognitions and awards for faculty (10 mark), iv) Research outcome (38 mark), v) \nResearch impact (12 mark) and vi) Vision and future readiness of the institute (5 mark). \n \n(b) As per land records furnished by ICAR Institutes, there is no discrepancy in the Land \nrecord/documents and its physical possession. All land parcels/pieces of ICAR Institutes are \nprotected by boundary wall or fencing. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |276", "t": "Micro ATM by Cooperative Societies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 276 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 21, 2023 \n \n                       Redevelopment of Government Colonies \n \nNO. 276.  DR. SATYAPAL SINGH:  \n        \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state : \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes for redevelopment of some \nmore Central Government colonies; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the total estimated cost of redevelopment of these colonies; \n(d)  whether the Government has commercialized a part of the land \nafter redevelopment of some Central Government colonies; and \n(e)     if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n               THE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (e) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. * 276 ( 16th POSITION) FOR \nANSWER ON 21.12.2023 REGARDING REDEVELOPMENT OF  \nGOVERNMENT COLONIES \n \n(a) to (e ) : Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs ( MoHUA) has re -\ndeveloped Government colonies of New Moti Bagh & East Kidwai \nNagar in Delhi. Government through National Building Construction \nCorporation (NBCC) and Central Public Works Department (CPWD) is  \nredeveloping 07 (seven)  more General Pool Residential \nAccommodation (GPRA) colonies namely Kasturaba Nagar, \nMohammadpur, Sriniwaspuri, Thyagraj Nagar, Nauroji Nagar, Netaji \nNagar and Sarojini Nagar  in Delhi. Two of these colonies  \nMohammadpur and Thyagraj Nagar have been re- developed and \nallotted.  These projects are being executed on self -sustainable \nfinance model wherein the cost of the construction is to be met from \nthe sale of commercial built-up area (BUA) at Nauroji Nagar and parts \nof Sarojini Nagar. The total estimated cost of redevelopment of these \n07 GPRA colonies is Rs. 32,835 crores.   There are a large number of \nGPRAs in Delhi. Some of these colonies have old houses and offer \ngood potential for re-development on self sustainable finance model.               \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |277", "t": "Cancellation of Fake Job Cards under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 277 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nCANCELLATION OF FAKE JOB CARDS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n277.  SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV): \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of fake job cards of the Mahatma Gandhi National \nRural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) cancelled in \nthe country during each of the last two years, State-wise; and \n(b)  the steps taken to prevent fake job card issue in future? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a): State/UT-wise details of Job Card deleted with reason 'Fake Job \nCard' under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) in the last two financial year 2021-\n22 and 2022-23 is given at Annexure. \n(b):    Job Card deletion/updation is a continuous process and this \nexercise is being conducted by the States on a regular basis. As per \nSection 25 of the Act, whoever contravenes the provisions of this Act \nshall on conviction be liable to a fine which may extend to one \nthousand rupees. Also, Aadhaar seeding has been made mandatory for \nthe de-duplication of the beneficiaries data base to prevent fake Job \nCard issuance.  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 277 dated 05.12.2023. \n  \nState/UT-wise details of Job Card deleted with reason 'Fake Job Card' \nunder Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) in the last two financial year 2021-\n22 and 2022-23. \nSl. No. State/UT 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 Andaman And Nicobar 15  0 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1833  46662 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 573  656 \n4 Assam 3156  7988 \n5 Bihar 27062  80203 \n6 Chhattisgarh 7292  14626 \n7 Goa 0  0 \n8 Gujarat 2394  3814 \n9 Haryana 170  129 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 464  585 \n11 Jammu And Kashmir 2108  1664 \n12 Jharkhand 23528  70673 \n13 Karnataka 1457  4108 \n14 Kerala 831  1548 \n15 Ladakh 11  44 \n16 Lakshadweep 0  1 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 95209  27859 \n18 Maharashtra 1362  4106 \n\n19 Manipur 62  21 \n20 Meghalaya 279  124 \n21 Mizoram 38  56 \n22 Nagaland 9  35 \n23 Odisha 50817  114333 \n24 Puducherry 3  25 \n25 Punjab 787  3818 \n26 Rajasthan 14782  45646 \n27 Sikkim 14  15 \n28 Tamil Nadu 3365  5831 \n29 Telangana 93  5036 \n30 Tripura 162  253 \n31 Uttar Pradesh 67937  296464 \n32 Uttarakhand 743  1871 \n33 West Bengal 388  5263 \nTotal  3,06,944  7,43,457 \n  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |278", "t": "Women Sportspersons  under TOP Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 278 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nREPAIR OF ANKALESHWAR-BURHANPUR NATIONAL \nHIGHWAY \n*278. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has awarded the job  of repairing the \nNational Highways (NHs) which  have been damaged heavily and \nrequire immediate repairs including the Ankaleshwar-Burhanpur NH \nin Raver Parliamentary Constituency; \n(b) if so, the details of NHs proposed and the  funds allocated for the \nsaid purpose; \n(c) whether the work of repairs for Ankaleshwar -Burhanpur NH is \ndelayed and is progressing very slowly and if so, the reasons therefor; \nand \n(d) whether the Government has directed/informed  the concerned \ncontractor to complete the awarded  work of repairs immediately as \nthis NH is observing  loaded transportation and accidents are \noccurring very frequently and if so, the details thereof?   \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 278 ANSWERED ON 21.12 .2023 \nASKED BY SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE REGARDING REPAIR \nOF ANKALESHWAR-BURHANPUR NATIONAL HIGHWAY  \n(a) to (d) The Ministry is primarily responsible for development and \nmaintenance of National Highways (NHs). Development and \nmaintenance of NHs is a continuous process. The works on NHs are \naccordingly taken up from time to time as per traffic density, inter-se \npriority and availability of funds to keep the NH s in traffic worthy \ncondition. \nExpenditure incurred by the Ministry  for development and \nmaintenance of NHs  during the last three years and current year, \nseparately for the country and the State of Maharashtra, includ ing \nRaver Parliamentary Constituency, are as under:-  \nAmount in Rs. Crore \nParticulars 2020-21 2021-22  2022-23  2023-24 (Till \n31.10.2023) \nAlloc. Expd. Alloc. Expd. Alloc. Expd. Alloc. Expd. \nCountry  1,68,988 1,65,812 2,13,537 2,01,827 2,34,226 2,20,100 2,85,290 1,67,143 \nMaharashtra  21,530 20,783 18,826 18,646 18,290 18,335 9,741 8,670 \nThe Maintenance  and Repair work  of Taloda to Burhanpur \nsection of NH -753BE in the State of Maharashtra having length of \n232.2 Km (part of Ankaleshwar-Burhanpur stretch) passing through  \nRaver Parliamentary Constituency has been awarded and work order \nissued on 06.07.2023 amounting to Rs. 61.14 Crore with scheduled \ncompletion by June, 2024. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |279", "t": "Fishermen in Coastal Areas", "head": "�\n�\n������������������� �\n�������������������� �������������������� ������������� �\n�������������������� ����\n�\n��������� �\n�\n����������������������� ����\n������������������� �� ��������� �������\n�\n���������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������� ��\n�\n����� ���� ��������� �������������� ������� ���������� ���� ���������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������ ���������������������������������������� �\n�\n� � ������������������������ �\n�\n�������� �������� ���� ���� ������� ����������� ������ ��� ���������� ������ ����������� ��� �������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������\n������\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |28", "t": "Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003", "head": "Government of India  \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Economic Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 28  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, DECEMBER 4, 2023/AGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nFISCAL RESPONSIBILITY AND BUDGET MANAGEMENT ACT, 2003 \n28. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has formed any committee to review the Fiscal Responsibility and \nBudget Management Act, 2003;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the suggestions of the committee; \n(c)  whether there is a need to amend the said Act especially in the light of COVID-19 Pandemic; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  the steps taken/being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE (SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) & (b). The Government of India constituted the FRBM Review Committee on 17th May, 2016. The \nFRBM review committee submitted its report to the Government. The FRBM review committee \nrecommended Debt to GDP ratio of 60 percent of GDP for General Government by FY23, consisting \nof 40 percent for Central Government; adopting fiscal deficit as key operational target, etc. The report \nof the FRBM review committee is also available on \nhttps://dea.gov.in/sites/default/files/Volume%201%20FRBM%20Review%20Committee%20Report.\npdf. \n(c) to (e): Post Covid-19, the Central Government announced a glide path of fiscal consolidation to \nachieve fiscal deficit to GDP ratio lower than 4.5 percent of GDP by 2025-26. Since then, the \nGovernment has been following the glide path of fiscal consolidation. \n*****   "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |280", "t": "Fishing Harbour in Kerala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOKSABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.280  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n \nFishing Harbour in Kerala \n \n280.  ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपालन, \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of safety measures recommended by the central ly deputed technical team after \ninspecting Muthalapozhi fishing harbour in Kerala; \n \n(b)  whether the recommended safety measures have been taken to avert the continuous \naccidents in the harbour, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has received any request from the State Government of Kerala for \ntechnical / financial assistance to address the issues in Muthalapozhi fishing harbour; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying had on \n17th July, 2023 deputed a three member Technical Team for inspection of  Muthalapozhi fishing \nharbour. The team based on  detailed site inspection and local stakeholder consultation s, \nrecommended short term and long-term measures mainly to mitigate the  navigational difficulties \nfaced by the fishermen at the fishing harbour . The short-term measures recommended by the \nTechnical Team primarily include dredging and removal of rock boulders in the channel  and the \nlong-term measures include stabilizing the navigational channel and mouth to ensure safe \nmaneuvering of fishing vessels. \n \n(b) : The Government of Kerala has reported that its executing agency  has been advised to \nmaintain depth in channel and mouth of Fishing Ha rbour as per MoU entered into between the State \nGovernment and  its executing agency  in connection with construction of l oad out facility at \nMuthalapozhi Fishing Harbour, for transporting stones for the construction of breakwaters at \nVizhinjam International Sea Port. It is reported that executing agency has also been directed by the \nGovernment of Kerala  to remove the scatter ed stones lying in the estuary and channel  and \naccordingly, the work of removing scattered stones and tetrapodes lying in the estuary and channel  \nusing excavator and long boom crane is progress ing and dredger reached at site for carrying out \ndredging at Muthalapozhi Fishing Harbour by its executing agency. The Government of Kerala has \nfurther reported that more life guards have been deputed, an ambulance has been allocated for rescue \noperations and high mast lights are installed at breakwater for smooth operations at Muthalapozhi \nFishing Harbour. \n \n\n(c) and (d) : The  Government of Kerala has submitted a proposal for the upgradation of \nMuthalapozhi Fishing Harbour at a cost Rs 50 crore and sought central financial assistance under the \nPradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY). This proposal primarily envisages  upgradation \nof internal road & parking area, construction of new drain, overhead water tank, workers rest shed, \nshops, dormitory, gate, gate house, auction hall, loading area, new toilet block, col d storage room, \nextension of wharf and auction hall, deepening of basin and berthing area, landscaping/beautification \nworks, replenishment of breakwater and providing road facility over breakwater, refurbishment of \nexisting structures, installation of ETP,  Electrification & yard lighting, installation of surveillance \nsystem, power backup facilities , mechanical conveyor system and automation .After scrutiny of the \nproposal, the Government of Kerala has been advised in October, 2023 to submit a comprehensive \nproposal after incorporating suggestions of the studies carried out by the State  Government through \nthe Central Water and Power Research Station (CWPRS), Pune.  \n \n       ***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |281", "t": "Revision of Budget for PMGSY", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 281 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nREVISION OF BUDGET FOR PMGSY \n \n281.  DR. M.P. ABDUSSAMAD SAMADANI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to revise the budget for \nPradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) considering the \nsuggestions from the public and peoples’ representatives to \ninclude more roads in the project; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has received any proposals from the \nState of Kerala to include more roads in the PMGSY; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b): No Sir.  There is no proposal to revise the Pradhan Mantri \nGram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) budget since the present allocation is \nsufficient to meet the cost of the projects.  \n  \n(c) & (d):  The state of Kerala was allocated the target of 1,425 km \nunder PMGSY-III, out of which, 1,281  Km has already been sanctioned. \nThe State Government has submitted proposal for balance length \nwhich has been examined in Pre-Empowered Committee meeting. The \nState Government has been requested to send the compliance report \non the observations of the Pre-Empowered Committee meeting. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |282", "t": "Taxation on Farmers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE  \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE  \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 282 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023  \n \nTAXATION ON FARMERS \n \n282. DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:  \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR.S:  \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE:  \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n \na. Whether in the year 2022 the Indian farmers experienced implicit taxation totaling $ \n169 billion; \n \nb. If so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor and the corrective, steps taken in this \nregard, \n \nc. Whether the farmers in the country are not able to sell their crops and are not getting \nremunerative price which is 50% than the input costs and value of production as per \nthe Swaminathan Report as promised by the Government; \n \nd. If so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government; \n \ne. Whether the Government has raised the farm credit target to Rs. 20 lakh crore for 2023-\n24 and if so, the details for the proposal and plan; \n \nf. Whether it is a fact that the agricultural sector faces several issues such as low growth, \nhigh incidence of indebtedness among farmers, high cost of inputs, fragmented land \nholdings and a lack of capital investments; and  \n \ng. If so, the action taken by the Government for utilization of 100 percent funds for the \nwelfare scheme therefor? \n \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b):  The OECD in its report titled ‘Agricultural Policy Monitoring and \nEvaluation 2023’, has estimated the net support to agriculture, using specific \nmethodologies and assumptions. The estimated support to agriculture is OECD’s \nindependent estimate.  \n\n \nThe Government has adopted and implemented several policies, \ndevelopmental programmes, schemes and reforms for achieving higher incomes for \nthe farmers, which are as follow: \ni. Supplementary income transfers under PM-KISAN of Rs. 6000/- per year in \nthreeequal instalments to eligible beneficiaries. \nii. Increase in Minimum Support Price (MSPs) for all Kharif& Rabi crops ensuring \na minimum of 50 percent of profit margin on the cost of production. \niii. Crop insurance under Pradhan Mantri FasalBimaYojna (PMFBY). \niv. Better access to irrigation under Pradhan MantriKrishiSinchayeeYojana \n(PMKSY). \nv. Special attention for creation of infrastructure through Agri Infrastructure Fund \n(AIF) with a size of Rs. 100,000 crores. \nvi. New procurement policy under PM-AASHA in addition to FCI operations. \nvii. Kisan Credit Cards (KCC) offering production loan to even dairy & fishery \nfarmers besides agricultural crops. \nviii. Formation and promotion of 10,000 Farmer Producer Organisations (FPO). \nix. National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA), which aims to evolve and \nimplement strategies to make Indian agriculture more resilient to the changing \nclimate. \nx. Adoption of drone technologies in agriculture which has potential to \nrevolutionize the Indian Agriculture. \nxi. Benefits accruing under Bee-Keeping, RashtriyaGokul Mission, Blue \nRevolution, Interest Subvention Scheme, Agro-forestry, Restructured Bamboo \nMission, implementation of new generation watershed guidelines, etc. \nxii. Focus on application of digital technology at all stages of agricultural value \nchain. \nxiii. Supply of fertilizer to farmer at subsidized price so as to reduce input cost. \nxiv. Production and availability of quality seeds to farmers. \nxv. Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme(NREGS) \nhas resulted in the enhancement of livelihoodsecurity. \n \n(c) & (d):  The Government fixes Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) for 22 \nmandated agricultural crops and Fair & Remunerative Price (FRP) for sugarcane on \nthe basis of the recommendations of the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices \n(CACP), after considering the views of the State Governments and Central \nMinistries/Departments concerned.  While recommending MSPs for crops, CACP \nfactors in the input cost incurred by the farmers. National Commission on Farmers \n(NCF), constituted under the Chairmanship of Prof. M.S. Swaminathan in 2004, inter \nalia, recommended that, “MSP should be at least 50 percent more than the weighted \naverage cost of production”. To give effect to this recommendation, Government, in its \nUnion Budget for 2018-19, had made an announcement to keep MSP at a level of one \nand half times of the cost of production as a pre-determined principle. Accordingly, \nMSPs for all mandated Kharif, Rabi and other commercial crops have been fixed with \na margin of at least 50 per cent over all India weighted average cost of production \nsince the year 2018-19.  \n \nFurther, to realise the objective of MSP policy, Government extends price support for \npaddy and wheat through the Food Corporation of India (FCI) and State Agencies."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |283", "t": "Construction of Youth Hostels", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs and Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.283  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nConstruction of Youth Hostels \n \n \n283.  DR. A. CHELLAKUMAR: \nSHRI BENNY BEHANAN: \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the total funds allocated and utilised for construction of Youth Hostels, \nyear-wise; \n \n(b)  the details of steps taken by the Government to guarantee quality \nstandards, hygiene and infrastructure improvements in Youth Hostels; \n \n(c)  the details of measures taken by the Government to facilitate a \ncooperative effort between the volunteers of Nehru Yuv a Kendra Sangathan \n(NYKS) and Common Service Centres (CSCs) aimed at enhancing digital and \nfinancial literacy among rural populations under the Pradhan Mantri Gramin \nDigital Saksharta Mission (PMGDISHA); and \n \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to raise t he remuneration for \nNational Youth Volunteers from Rs.5000 and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) The details of funds allocated and utilized for construction of Youth \nHostels for last two years and current year are given below: \n(Rs. in crore) \nYear BE RE Expenditure \n2021-22 3.00 2.50 0.50 \n2022-23 5.32 2.01 2.00 \n2023-24 \n(Till date) \n5.00 5.00 0.12 \n\n \n(b) As per Youth Hostel Manual, Manager of Youth Hostel is responsible to \nmaintain the Hostel building and fixtures, its equipment and stores in good \nworking condition and to ensure a high standard of hygiene and cleanliness \nin the kitchen, dormitories, lawns, social areas and Hostel surroundi ngs. A \nneed based, o ne-time grant/financial assistance upto Rs.20 lakhs can be \nreleased for renovation /up-gradation of Youth Ho stels, subject to viability of \nthe proposal  and availability of funds.  Further with a view to ensure  the \nquality of service delivery to the visitors,  currently, 19 Youth Hostels are ISO \n9001:2015 certified. \n \n(c) Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan ( NYKS) is not engaged with Pradhan \nMantri Gramin Digital Saksharta Mission . However, in order to enhance \nfinancial literacy a mong rural population, NYKS , in collaboration with \nMinistry of Corporate Affairs, conducted 150 programmes in 2022 -23 and \nimparted financial literacy among 12,377 youth.  \n \n(d) There is no such proposal under consideration. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |284", "t": "Programmes Launched under PMMSY", "head": " \nO.I.H. \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 284 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nProgrammes Launched under PMMSY \n \n284. SHRI ARUN SAO: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the present status of the various programmes launched under Pradhan Mantri Matsya \nSampada Yojana (PMMSY); \n \n(b)  whether the Government has taken any steps to amend the critical gaps in the fisheries \nvalue chain and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the details of new steps taken by the Government to establish a robust fisheries \nmanagement framework and ensure socio -economic welfare of fishermen and fish \nfarmers? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n \n \n(SHRI PARS0HOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and \nDairying is implementing a flagship scheme namely Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana \n(PMMSY) with an investment of ₹20050 crore in all the States and Union Territories (UTs) \nfor a period of 5 years with effect from the financial year 2020 -21 to FY 2024-25 for holistic \ndevelopment of the fisheries sector in the country. Under this scheme d uring the last thr ee \nfinancial years (FY 2020 -21 to  2022-23), and current financial year (2023 -24), fisheries \ndevelopmental projects of various State Governments , Union Territories and other \nimplementing agencies amounting to of Rs. 17118.62 crore have been approved for  overall \ndevelopment of fisheries and aquaculture sector in the country. \n \n \n \n\n \nThe Pr adhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) is designed and implemented to \naddress critical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, post -harvest \ninfrastructure and management and  modernisation a nd strengthening of value chain , \ntraceability and quality improvement . To modernize and strengthen fisheries value chain , \nPMMSY supports creation of post-harvest infrastructure such as fishing harbours/fish landing \ncentres, cold storages and ice plants, fish transportation vehicles including refrigerated and \ninsulated vehicles, ice flaking and ice crushing units, ice/fish holding boxes, etc. with \nmotorcycles, bicycles and autorickshaws,  value addition enterprise units as well as modern \nhygienic markets such as w holesale fish markets including supermarkets, retail fish markets \nand outlets, mobile fish and live fish markets.  So far, u nder PMMSY investments worth Rs. \n4005.96 crore have been approved for the above listed activities during the last three financial \nyears (FY 2020-21 to 2022-23), and current financial year (2023-24).  \nPMMSY provides for establishment of a robust fisheries management framework and provides \nneed-based support to States/UTs for formulation and implementation of fisheries management \nplans. Besides, the PMMSY promotes aquaculture, mariculture and post-harvest management \nand also various economic activities in fisheries, aiming towards  enhancement of income and \nimprove socio-economic condition of fishers and other stakeholders  associated with fisheries \nsector. Under PMMSY financial assistance for livelihood  and nutritional support are being \nprovided to 6.0 lakh annually for traditional and socio-economically backward, eligible active \nmarine and inland fishermen families during the fishing ban period, vis-à-vis PMMSY supports \ninsurance coverage to 33.20 lakh fishers and also supports interest subvention scheme for \nfishing vessels . Additionally under PMMSY 20823.40 hectares of pond area for inland \naquaculture, 3942 Biofloc units,  11927 Nos o f Re-circulatory Aquaculture Syst ems (RAS), \n44,408 reservoir cages and 543.7 hectares pens in reservoirs, 1,11,110 units of seaweed rafts \nand monoline units, 1489 bivalve cultivation units, 562 cold storages, 6542 fish kiosks, 108 \nvalue added enterprise units and  25,193 post harvest transportation units have been approved \nfor promotion of aquaculture, mariculture and post-harvest management activities. \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |285", "t": "Cold Chain and Storage for Mega Food Parks", "head": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 285 \nANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nCOLD CHAIN AND STORAGE FOR MEGA FOOD PARKS \n \n285.  SHRI RANJEETSINGH NAIK NIMBALKAR: \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \nDR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of schemes launched by the Government for Food Processing Industry  (FPI) during \neach of the last three years for preservation and processing of vegetables, fruits, milk products etc. in \norder to increase the income of farmers in the country; \n(b) the number of cold supply chain and cold storage for Mega Food Park (MFP) established under \nthe above schemes, State-wise and location-wise, including Jharkhand and NCT of Delhi; \n(c) whether the number of cold supply chain and cold storage established/approved so far for \nMFP are sufficient as per the demand; \n(d) if not, whether there is any proposal to increase their number; and \n(e)     if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) : For overall growth and development of food processing sector including preservation and \nprocessing of vegetables, fruits, milk products etc. in the country,  Ministry of Food Processing \nIndustries (MoFPI) is implementing Centrally Sponsored PM  Formalisation of Micro Food \nProcessing Enterprises Scheme (PMFME) since 2020-21 and Central Sector Production Linked \nIncentive Scheme (PLISFPI) for Food Processing Industry (PLISFPI) since 2021-22 across the country \nand thus helping increase the income of the farmers in the country. \n(b)      : MoFPI is also implementing a Central Sector Umbrella scheme - Pradhan Mantri  Kisan \nSampada Yojana (PMKSY) since 2016 -17. The state-wise details of Mega Food Parks and Integrated \nCold Chains projects approved under corresponding component schemes of PMKSY since 2020 -21 is \nplaced at Annexure. \n(c) to (e):  The number of Food Processing projects assisted  through PMKSY is progressively \nincreasing as MoFPI seeks such proposals through Expression of Interest from time to time and provides \ncredit linked capital subsidy to eligible projects. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 285 ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 REGARDING COLD \nCHAIN AND STORAGE FOR MEGA FOOD PARKS \n \nState-wise details of Mega Food Parks and Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition \nInfrastructure under PMKSY since 2020-21 \n(In Rs Cr) \nComponent Scheme-Mega Food Park Scheme \nS. \nNo. State Project Name District Project \nCost \nApproved \nGrant-in-aid \n1 Bihar \nBihar Industrial Area Development \nAuthority (BIADA) Khagaria 180.57 45.4 \n2 Meghalaya Meghalaya Industrial Development \nCorporation Ltd. (MIDC) \nNorth Garo \nHills \n65.86 48.3 \n3 Rajasthan Shree Ram Mega Food Park Pvt. Ltd. Bikaner 132.81 50 \n4 Tamil Nadu \nTamil Nadu State Agricultural Marketing \nBoard Tirunelveli 77.024 23.64 \nComponent Scheme- Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure \nScheme \n5 Andhra Pradesh Srichakra Milk Products LLP Anantpur 22.05 6.95 \n6 Andhra Pradesh Veerabhadra Exports Pvt. Ltd. East Godavari 36.38 9.88 \n7 Andhra Pradesh Naturade Foods Private Limited Visakhapatnam 15.57 3.79 \n8 Andhra Pradesh Jaya Lakshmi Sea Foods Pvt Ltd Krishna 74.23 10 \n9 Andhra Pradesh Castlerock Fisheries Private Limited Nellore 40.85 10 \n10 Andhra Pradesh Coastal Corporation Limited East Godavari 71.16 9.95 \n11 Andhra Pradesh Varma Marine West Godavari 26.44 9.83 \n12 Andhra Pradesh Aparna Marine Exports Guntur 40.67 9.53 \n13 Andhra Pradesh Mekworld Marines and Exports Pvt Ltd West Godavari 28.22 10 \n14 Andhra Pradesh Vaisakhi Bio Marine Pvt. Ltd. Visakhapatnam 44.16 10 \n \n \n15 \n \nAndhra Pradesh \nThe Krishna District Milk Producers’ \nMutually Aided Co- operative Union \nLimited \n \n \nKrishna \n \n \n80.48 \n \n \n10 \n16 Andhra Pradesh Sukshma Gamma LLP Krishna 24.77 8.38 \n17 Andhra Pradesh Mulpuri Aqua Processors Krishna 37.07 10 \n18 Andhra Pradesh Krishna Coastal Foods Pvt. Ltd Krishna 6.65 1.57 \n19 Andhra Pradesh Gayatri Marine Products Nellore 31.58 8.4 \n20 Andhra Pradesh Aadhya Sea Foods Pvt Ltd  39.72 10 \n21 Andhra Pradesh SAM Aqua Exports LLP Krishna 43.49 10 \n22 Andhra Pradesh GSR Nirman Guntur 44.3345 10 \n23 Andhra Pradesh Crest Foods Chittooor 30.2 6.5128 \n24 Andaman & \nNicobar \nFirstfish Seafood Processing Pvt. Ltd. Port Blair 19.94 10 \n25 Bihar Saran Dairy Producer Company Limited Gopalganj 11.66 3.05 \n26 Gujarat Lions Foods Pvt Ltd Gir Somnath 22.45 6.47 \n27 Gujarat MCFROST India Surat 27.68 8.67 \n\n \n \n \n28 Gujarat Foods and Inns Limited Valsad 23.66 8.89 \n29 Gujarat Funwave Foods LLP Morbi 30.9 9.8 \n30 Gujarat Bizz Corporation Morbi 43.2 10 \n31 Gujarat PEP Foods Mehsana 44.7116 8.4007 \n32 Gujarat Jackwin Foods Private Limited Mehsana 40.4339 6.878 \n33 Haryana Jivo Wellness Private Limited Sonepat 48.33 10 \n34 Haryana Zanders Resorts Private"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |286", "t": "Bamboo Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 286 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nBAMBOO PRODUCTION  \n \n286: SHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the absence of incentives for producers and other stakeholders hindered \nsignificant growth in bamboo production and if so, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(b) if not, the details thereof along with present Bamboo production in the country, State-\nwise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any national policy centred towards developing \nBamboo related industries and if so, the outcome of the policy so far; and  \n \n(d) whether there are any plans to create and promote Bamboo clusters and also for \nspreading awareness regarding the bamboo products through digital and physical platforms \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी    (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b) : The Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare is implementing \nRestructured National Bamboo Mission (NBM) since 2018-19 for the development of \ncomplete value chain of bamboo sector to link growers with consumers starting from \nplanting material, plantation, creation of facilities for collection, aggregation, processing, \nmarketing, micro, small & medium enterprises, skill development and brand building \ninitiative in a cluster approach mode. Bamboo plantation under NBM is being carried out in \nnon-forest Government land and private farmer’s field.  Presently, NBM is implemented in \nthe States namely Assam, Arunachal Pradesh, Manipur, Mizoram, Meghalaya, Nagaland, \nSikkim, Tripura, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Chhattisgarh, Odisha, Karnataka, \nUttarakhand, Bihar, Jharkhand, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, \nUttar Pradesh, Himachal Pradesh and Union Territory of Jammu & Kashmir. The State-wise \ndetails of bamboo bearing area under forest is at Annexure-I and the plantation taken up in \nnon-forest area under NBM is at Annexure-II.  \n\n(c): The restructured National Bamboo Mission supports bamboo industries for \ndevelopment of bamboo value chain including promotion of bamboo treatment, seasoning \nand preservation, establishment of primary processing units, value addition and production \nunits such as bamboo based cottage industry and MSME etc. The detail of physical \nachievements of bamboo treatment and processing units under NBM is at Annexure-III.  \nMinistry of MSME is also implementing a major credit-linked subsidy programme \nnamed Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP) for generating \nemployment in the country by setting up of micro-enterprises in the non-farm sector \nincluding Bamboo industry. \n(d):  Under the NBM, 22 Bamboo clusters and 40 Farmers Producer Organizations \n(FPOs) have been formed by the State Bamboo Missions. Besides, Ministry of MSME is \nimplementing Scheme of Fund for Regeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), to \norganize traditional industries and artisans into collectives and make them competitive, \nprovide sustained employment and enhance marketability of products. The scheme \nsupports creation of Common Facility Centers (CFCs), procurement of new machineries, \nproduction infrastructure, setting up raw material banks, skill development and training, \nmarket promotion initiatives, etc. Since 2014-15 till date, 41 bamboo clusters have been \napproved with Government of India assistance of Rs. 98.64 cr. benefitting 9197 artisans \nunder this scheme. \n Further, for popularizing use of bamboo and bamboo products 214 seminars, \nconferences, awareness campaign etc. at National, State and District levels, and capacity \nbuilding programs for 14656 participants including farmers/artisans and field functionaries \nhas been undertaken so far under the NBM. Awareness is also created through print and \nelectronic media and participation of NBM in fairs and other events. A dedicated window for \nmarketing bamboo products has also been created on GeM portal.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure I \nAnnexure to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 286 due for answer on 05.12.2023 \nState-wise details of Bamboo bearing area in Recorded Forest Area (as per India \nState of Forest Report (ISFR) 2021) \nSr. No. State/UT \nBamboo bearing area*(in \nha) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 610400 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 1573900  \n3 Assam 1065900 \n4 Bihar 110300 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1046700 \n6 Goa 28800 \n7 Gujarat 354700 \n8 Haryana 3900 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 102700 \n11 Jharkhand 371700 \n12 Karnataka 862400 \n13 Kerala 240400 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 1839400 \n15 Maharashtra  1352600 \n16 Manipur 837700 \n17 Meghalaya 500700 \n18 Mizoram 456100 \n19 Nagaland 394700 \n20 Odisha 1119900 \n21 Punjab 28000 \n22 Rajasthan 155500 \n23 Sikkim 99400 \n24 Tamil Nadu 400100 \n25 Telangana 453500 \n26 Tripura 420100 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 183"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |287", "t": "Sale of Quality Seeds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 287 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 05th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSALE OF QUALITY SEEDS \n \n287.  SHRI  GNANATHIRAVIAMS. \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has noticed the increasing trend of sub-standard seeds \nbeing sold in the open market;  \n(b) if so, whether it is being ensured by the enforcement agencies that genuine varieties \nare grown to produce quality seeds and the same are packed and sold as seeds \nonly; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS’ WELFARE  \nक ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a):  As reported by the Department of Agriculture of various States, there is no \nincreasing trend of sale of sub-standard seeds in the market.  In order to ensure the \nsupply of quality seeds to the farming community Government makes all efforts as per \nextant provision of Seeds laws. \n(b) & (c):  The seed production, processing, labelling and sale are extensively \nmonitored by concerned state seed law enforcement agencies. Besides, the allocation of \nbreeder seed of notified varieties is being made by the Government of India to the \nindenting States and other seed producing agencies for production and to ensure \navailability of genuine varieties to the farmers.   \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |288", "t": "Sustainable Farming", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 288 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSUSTAINABLE FARMING \n \n288. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR RINKU: \n   \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to promote sustainable farming, \norganic farming and reduce the use of pesticides inputs in Punjab and if so, the details \nthereof; \n  \n(b)  whether the Government has initiated any policies in this regard and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government has taken any measures for better market access and fair \nprices for agricultural produce in Punjab and if so, the details thereof; and \n  \n(d)  whether any proposal has been or is being taken by the Government to enhance the \nincome and livelihoods of farmers in Punjab and if so, the details thereof, State/district-\nwise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (d): For promotion of organic farming, Government is implementing scheme of \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY) since 2015-16. PKVY is being implemented in \nall the States across the country including the state of Punjab,                                                                                                                        \nother-than North Eastern (NE) States. Where a separate scheme of Mission Organic Value \nChain Development for North Eastern Region (MOVCDNER).  Under the PKVY & \nMOVCDNER schemes, farmers are encouraged to adopt organic cultivation and are \nprovided end to end support from production to value addition, certification and marketing \nof organic produce.  \n \nThe Government of Punjab has informed that the state of Punjab is promoting PKVY \nscheme specifically for promotion of Organic farming. Punjab Agri Export Corporation \nLimited, which is a Government Institution of Punjab State, has been appointed as Nodal \nagency for Promotion of Organic farming in the state of Punjab since 2015. In the state of \nPunjab, 2000 farmers practice Organic farming in 5000 acre under the supervision of \nPunjab Agri Export Corporation Limited. \n\n \n \nUnder PKVY assistance is provided @ Rs.15.0 lakh / 1000 ha cluster for 3 years to \nfacilitate farmers for direct marketing of their organic products to the buyers. In addition, \nassistance is provided @ Rs.53.0 lakh /1000 ha clusters for 3 years for branding, publicity, \nexhibition, trade fairs and other marketing initiatives to support direct buying of organic \nproducts from farmers. A dedicated web portal- www.Jaivikkheti.in/  has been created as \nonline marketing platform for direct marketing of organic produce by the farmers to get a \nbetter price.  \n \nGovernment is also implementing the 'Sub-Mission on Plant Protection and Plant \nQuarantine' through 35 Central Integrated Pest Management Centers located in 28 States \nand 2 UTs  with a mandate to popularize adoption of Integrated Pest Management (IPM) \nthrough training and demonstration in crops, inter-alia promotion of biological control \napproaches in crop protection technology with major emphasis on judicious use of chemical \npesticide as a last resort, safety in use of pesticides, alternate tools for pest management \nviz; cultural, physical, mechanical methods of pest control as well as use of bio-pesticides \nand bio-control agents. \n \nOrganic farming creates livelihood opportunities for farmers and enhances return. \nHowever, the Government of Punjab has informed that for marketing organic products of \nfarmers, Punjab Agro buys organic products at premium prices above MSP. Organic \nproducts of Punjab Agro are also sold and exported under the brand “FIVE RIVERS”. With \nregard to marketing of organic products, Punjab Agri Export Corporation Limited is \nproviding market facilities to organic farmers for selling organic produce like fruits and \nvegetables and other organic products etc. at various places in the state of Punjab. Other \nthan that Punjab Agri Export Corporation Limited has established the Organic huts for the \nmarketing of organic products of farmers in various districts of Punjab. \n \n****** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |289", "t": "Sub-schemes of PMMSY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.289 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSub-schemes of PMMSY  \n \n289. SHRI V.K. SREEKANDAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total area expanded for fisheries to non -traditional areas in the country during the last \nthree years under Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY);  \n(b)  whether the allocated funds of Rs. 20,050 have been utilised during the year 2020 and if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c)  whether it is also true that the Government has announced any sub schemes of PMMSY and \nmade investments therein; \n (d)  if so, the objective of the said sub-schemes; and  \n(e)  whether it is also true that the fishermen in the country are facing hardship that they never \nexperienced a decade ago because of the high cost of diesel and due to oth er moves by the \nGovernment? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a): Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying is \nimplementing flagship scheme namely “Pradhan Mantri  Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY)” \nwith a vision to bring Blue Revolution through sustainable and responsible development of \nFisheries sector in India with highest ever investment of ₹20050 crore in fisheries sector for a \nperiod of 5 years with effect from the financial year 2020-21 to FY 2024-25. During the last three \nfinancial years (F Y 2020 -21, 2021 -22 and 2022 -23) and current financial year (2023 -24), \nPMMSY has supported expansion of aquaculture area through activities such as construction of \nfreshwater ponds for 20823.40 hectares, saline/alkaline ponds for 2855.59 hectares and brackish \nwater aquaculture ponds for 1370.86 hectares and high density aquaculture activities like \nconstruction of biofloc ponds for 3703.5 hectares. \n(b): Under PMMSY during 2020 -21 an investment of Rs. 2876.33 cr was approved ( Rs. \n1084.72 cr central share).  A total of Rs. 699.72 crore was released to various State \nGovernments/Union Territories and other implementing agencies, out of which it has been \nreported that an amount of Rs. 620.38 crore has been utilised. \n\n (c) and (d):  Yes Sir. In the Union Budget 2023 -24, the Government has announced a new sub -\nscheme of Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana with targeted investment of Rs. 6,000 crore to \nfurther enable activities of fishermen, fish vendors and micro & small enterprises, improve value \nchain efficiencies and expand the market. The Sub -scheme intends to gradually formalize the \nfisheries sector and facilitate access to institutional financ ing in the sector, incentivize value chain \nplayers such as micro enterprises and/or small enterprises for improving value chain efficiencies, \nquality assurance and supply of safe fish and fishery products; and for creation and maintenance \nof jobs with focus on jobs for women in the fisheries sector. The Sub -scheme also intends to help \nin addressing associated risks in aquaculture system. \n \n(e):  No Sir. No such report received in the Ministry of Fisheries Animal Husbandry and \nDairying. \n \n \n********** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |29", "t": "Overcrowding at Popular Tourist Destinations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.29 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nOVERCROWDING AT POPULAR TOURIST DESTINATIONS \n \n29. DR. A. CHELLAKUMAR: \nSHRI BENNY BEHANAN: \nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \nSHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of crises caused by \novercrowding in popular tourist destinations; \n(b) if so, the reasons for overcrowding; \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps to improve \ninfrastructure and overcome overcrowding; and  \n(d) whether the funds allocated to the Ministry include funding for \neducational campaigns and information dissemination initiatives \naimed at promoting responsible tourism behaviour and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM       (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (d) : Yes, Sir. The Government has taken various steps in \nimproving infrastructure and overcome overcrowding through Swadesh \nDarshan, PRASHAD and Assistance to Central Agencies Schemes. The \nMinistry of T ourism is also devel oping new destination and lesser \nknown  destination of India. \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism has formulated National Strategy for \nSustainable Tourism . To assist the Ministry in implementation of the \nstrategy, Ministry has designated Indian In stitute of Tourism & Travel \nManagement (IITTM) as the Central Nodal Agency – Sustainable \nTourism (CNA-ST).  \n\n \nIn line with the National Strategy for Sustainable Tourism, Ministry of \nTourism has launched the Travel for LiFE Initiative. Travel for LiFE aims \nto promote sustainable tourism in the country, through mindful and \ndeliberate actions mobilized toward tourists and tourism businesses in \nthe consumption of tourism resources.  \n \nThe Ministry is funding such initiatives under the Domestic Promotion \nand Publicity including Hospitality (DPPH) scheme. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |290", "t": "Construction of Residential Units under PMAY", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 290 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF RESIDENTIAL UNITS UNDER PMAY \n \n290.  SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR: \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of residential units constructed under the \nPradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY) particularly in Maharashtra \nduring each of the last five years, State and district-wise; \n(b)  the details of housing units allotted so far in Maharashtra, \ndistrict-wise; \n(c)  the details of the budget sanctioned, earmarked, allocated and \nutilized during the said period, State and district-wise including \nMumbai, Maharashtra; \n(d)  the details of the budget sanctioned, earmarked, allocated and \nutilized particularly for Ramtek districts of Nagpur in \nMaharashtra during the said period since inception of PMAY; \n(e)  whether the amount allocated by the Union Government has \nbeen fully utilized during the said period, State-wise including \nRamtek district of Nagpur in Maharashtra; and \n(f)  if not, the reasons therefor along with the steps taken by the \nUnion Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Rural Development is implementing the \nPradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana- Gramin (PMAY-G) from 1st April, 2016 \nto provide assistance to eligible rural households for construction of \n2.95 crore Pucca houses with basic amenities to achieve the objective \nof “Housing For All” in rural areas by March, 2024. The year-wise and \nState-wise details of total number of housing units constructed under \n\nthe PMAY-G, during each of the last five years is given at Annexure-I. \nThe District level data of all States/UTs related to houses allocated, \nsanctioned and completed can be seen at programme website at \nwww.pmayg.nic.in---->AwaasSoft---->Reports---->Houses progress \nagainst the target financial year. The number of houses allotted during \neach of the last five years district-wise in respect of Maharashtra is \ngiven at Annexure II.  \n  \nPradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana- Urban (PMAY-U): ‘Land’ and \n‘Colonisation’ are State subjects. The Ministry of Housing and Urban \nAffairs (MoHUA) is implementing Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Urban \n(PMAY-U) under “Housing for All” Mission since June 2015 by providing \nCentral assistance to supplement efforts of States/ Union Territories \n(UTs) for providing all weather ‘pucca’ houses with basic civic \nservices to all eligible urban beneficiaries. The scheme is being \nimplemented through four verticals, i.e., Beneficiary Led Construction \n(BLC); Affordable Housing in Partnership (AHP); In-Situ Slum \nRedevelopment (ISSR) and Credit Linked Subsidy Scheme (CLSS) \nbased on eligibility criteria as per scheme guidelines.  The State/UT \nwise houses sanctioned, grounded for construction and \ncompleted/delivered under PMAY-U is annexed as Annexure-I. Further, \nthe details of houses sanctioned, constructed during each of last five \nyears, district-wise for the State of Maharashtra is annexed as \nAnnexure II. \n  \n(c) to (f): Under Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G), a \ntotal amount of Rs. 7,568.63 cr. has been released and an amount of \nRs. 12,672.51 cr. (which includes State share) has been utilized during \nthe period of last 5 years i.e. Financial Years 2018-19 to 2022-23) in \nthe State of Maharashtra. The Central Assistance under PMAY-G is \nreleased directly to the State considering State as a unit. Further \nrelease of these funds to beneficiaries in various districts is done by \nthe respective State/UT Governments. Therefore, the district level \ndata related to funds sanctioned, allocated and utilized is not \nmaintained at the Ministry level. The following steps are taken by the \nGovernment of India for utilization of funds of by States under PMAY-G: \ni. Regular review of progress at the level of Ministry. \n\nii. Launch of PMAY-G Dashboard for monitoring and supervision of \nthe scheme. \niii. Timely allocation of targets to the States/UTs and release of \nadequate funds. \niv. Regular follow ups with the State to ensure release of central \nand state share of funds and provision of land to landless \nbeneficiaries in rural areas. \nv. Awards to the best performing States/UTs, districts and blocks \nbased on performance index dashboard, thereby creating healthy \ncompetition and motivation among the States/UTs for achieving \nthe set targets. \n  \nPradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-Urban (PMAY-U): PMAY-U, which was \nearlier up-to 31.03.2022, has since been extended upto 31.12.2024, \nexcept CLSS vertical, to complete all the sanctioned houses without \nchanging the funding pattern and implementation methodology . Under \nPMAY-U, Central Assistance of ₹ 23,337.41 crore was sanctioned and \n₹16,397.10 crore was released during last five years. During the period \n₹15,857.44 crore was utiliz"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |291", "t": "Farming and Forestry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 291 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFARMING AND FORESTRY \n \n291.  SHRI K. NAVASKANI  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान \nकʞ ाण मंũी be pleased to state: \n(a) Whether the Government is cognizant that blending of farming and forestry \nhas a number of advantages, including higher yields from staple food crops, \nimproved farmer livelihoods through wealth creation, increased biodiversity, \nenhanced soil structure and health, limited erosion, and carbon sequestration; and \n(b) If so, the details of the steps that are proposed to be taken by the Government \nto improve the agricultural system’s functioning and sustainability keeping in mind \nthat Agroforestry could provide a variety of ecosystem services that can help to \nensure long-term agricultural sustainability? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a)& (b): The Government is cognizant that blending of farming and forestry has \nnumber of advantages.  Therefore, keeping in view the importance of Agroforestry, a \nland use system that integrates trees and shrubs on farmlands and rural landscapes \nto enhance productivity, profitability, diversity and ecosystem sustainability, natural \nresource management system through integration of trees on farms and in the \nagricultural landscape, diversifies and sustains production and builds social \ninstitutions; the Government has formulated the National Agroforestry Policy-2014. \nThe policy envisages encouraging and expanding tree plantation in complementarity \nand integrated manner with crops and livestock to improve productivity, employment, \nincome and livelihoods of rural households, especially the smallholder farmers. It \nalso focuses on protecting and stabilizing ecosystems and promoting resilient \ncropping and farming systems to minimize the risk during extreme climatic events.  \n \n As recommended in the National Agroforestry Policy; Sub-Mission on \nAgroforestry (SMAF) was launched in the year 2016-17 to encourage tree plantation \non farm land with the aim of “Har Medh Par Ped”, along with crops/ cropping system \n\nto help the farmers get additional income. Sub-Mission on Agroforestry (SMAF) was \nimplemented from 2016-17 to 2021-22. Presently, the restructured Agroforestry \nComponent is being implemented under the Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY) \nand focuses on Quality Planting Material. Agroforestry is known to have the potential   \nto mitigate the climate change effects through microclimate moderation, conservation \nof natural resources and creation of an additional source of livelihood and income \nopportunities. The scheme is implemented with objectives to encourage and expand \ntree plantation in a complementary and integrated manner with crops to improve \nproductivity, additional income generation and improved livelihoods of rural \nhouseholds, especially the small farmers. The details of cost norms of assistance \nbeing provided are shown at Annexure-I \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 291 due for answer on \n05.12.2023 \n \nDetails of Cost Norms of Assistance being provided under the Agroforestry \nComponent of RKVY \n \nS.No. Name of interventions Unit Cost \n(Upper Limit) \n(Rs. In Lakh) \nRemarks \n1. Establishment of new nurseries \n(a) Hi-Tech ( 2 ha) 50.00 100% assistance to \nGovernment \nagencies for taking \nup the work on \nGovernment land \nand 50% assistance  \nto Private Agency/ \nindividual \nentrepreneur \n(b) Big (1 ha) 16.00 \n(c) Small (0.5 ha) 10.00 \n2. Raising saplings in the existing \nnursery \nUpto Rs. 5.00 \nLakh/nursery \n3. Establishment of Tissue Culture Units \n (a) Strengthening of existing \ntissue culture units \n20.00 100% assistance to \nGovernment \nagencies and 50% \nback ended credit \nlinked assistance for \nprivate partners \n (b) Establishment of new tissue \nculture units \n200.00 100% assistance to \nGovernment \nagencies and 50% \nback ended credit \nlinked assistance for \nprivate partners \n \n \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |292", "t": "Promoting the Production of Millets", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 292 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023   \nPROMOTING THE PRODUCTION OF MILLETS \n292. SHRIMATI SARMISTHA SETHI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the United Nations Organisation (UNO) has declared the year 2023 as \nInternational Year of Millets (IYOM);  \n(b) the details of the total production of millets in the country, State-wise and district-\nwise for Odisha for the last year; \n(c)  whether the Government has taken any steps to promote the production of millets \nin the year 2023-24 and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the details of funds allocated, sanctioned, released and utilized for each of the \nabove schemes during the said period, scheme/State/district-wise for Odisha? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a) : Yes Sir. The United Nation’s General Assembly (UNGA) declared 2023 as \nInternational Year of Millets.  \n \n(b) :  The total production of Millets (Shree Anna) in the country during 2022-23 is \n17.32 million tonnes. The State/UT wise production of Millets (Shree Anna) during \n2022-23 is given at Annexure-I.  \n \n(c) : The Government of India is implementing a multi stakeholder approach towards \ncelebration of International Year of Millets (IYM) 2023 to achieve the objectives of IYM \n2023 and promote Millets (Shree Anna). In order to increase production and \nproductivity of Millets (Shree Anna), the Department of Agriculture and Farmers \n\nWelfare (DA&FW) is implementing a Sub-Mission on Nutri-Cereals under National \nFood Security Mission (NFSM) in all districts of 28 States & 2 Union Territories viz. \nJammu & Kashmir and Ladakh. Under NFSM–Nutri Cereals, the incentives are \nprovided to the farmers, through the States/UTs, on crop production and protection \ntechnologies, cropping system based demonstrations, production & distribution of \ncertified seeds of newly released varieties/hybrids, Integrated Nutrient and Pest \nManagement techniques, improved farm implements/tools/resource conservation \nmachineries, water saving devices, capacity building of farmers through trainings \nduring cropping season, organizing events/workshops, distribution of seed minikits, \npublicity through print and electronic media etc. To make India a global hub for 'Shree \nAnna', the Indian Institute of Millets Research (IIMR), Hyderabad has been declared \nas the Centre of Excellence for sharing best practices, research and technologies at \nthe national and international level.  \n \n(d) :     The State/UT wise allocation, release and expenditure of funds (Central Share) \nunder NFSM-Nutri Cereals during 2022-23 is given at Annexure-II. \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState /UT wise production of Millets (Shree Anna) during 2022-23  \n(Thousand Tonnes) \nState/ UT Jowar Bajra Ragi Small Millets Total Millets \nAndhra Pradesh 283.78 50.68 32.00 9.46 375.92 \nArunachal Pradesh 0.00 0.00 28.45 0.00 28.45 \nAssam 0.00 0.00 0.00 4.79 4.79 \nBihar 0.98 2.68 2.36 1.16 7.18 \nChhattisgarh 1.10 0.05 0.86 21.53 23.54 \nDadra and Nagar Haveli 0.00 0.75 1.40 0.00 2.15 \nDelhi 3.00 4.65 0.00 0.00 7.65 \nGujarat 46.12 1293.68 8.30 15.99 1364.09 \nHaryana 14.12 1199.85 0.00 0.00 1213.97 \nHimachal Pradesh 0.00 0.26 0.98 1.10 2.34 \nJammu and Kashmir 0.00 10.32 0.12 0.17 10.61 \nJharkhand 0.76 0.14 12.72 0.00 13.62 \nKarnataka 681.68 177.40 1148.17 25.35 2032.60 \nKerala 0.00 0.01 0.21 0.05 0.27 \nMadhya Pradesh 169.46 943.44 0.00 140.65 1253.55 \nMaharashtra 1312.25 467.93 91.03 27.37 1898.58 \nMeghalaya 0.00 0.00 0.00 2.83 2.83 \nNagaland 0.30 2.21 0.37 11.12 14.00 \nOdisha 5.58 1.14 37.40 32.22 76.34 \nPuducherry 0.01 0.07 0.17 0.00 0.25 \nPunjab 0.00 0.41 0.00 0.00 0.41 \nRajasthan 567.18 5105.02 0.00 1.41 5673.61 \nSikkim 0.00 0.00 0.30 0.00 0.30 \nTamil Nadu 292.81 113.38 206.50 17.52 630.21 \nTelangana 119.54 11.81 0.00 1.05 132.40 \nTripura 0.00 0.00 0.00 1.04 1.04 \nUttar Pradesh 315.41 2045.52 0.00 7.64 2368.57 \nUttarakhand 0.00 0.00 114.23 61.54 175.77 \nWest Bengal 0.09 0.03 5.78 0.28 6.18 \nAll India 3814.18 11431.42 1691.37 384.26 17321.23 \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \nState /UT wise allocation, release and expenditure of funds (Central Share) \nunder NFSM- Nutri Cereals during 2022-23   \n(Rs. in crores) \nState/UT Nutri Cereals   \nAllocation Release Expenditure  \nAndhra Pradesh 8.700 0.000 0.000 \nArunachal Pradesh    1.500 0.380 0.380 \nAssam                        1.500 1.130 1.500 \nChhattisgarh 3.310 2.100 0.810 \nGujarat 7.470 5.600 1.300 \nHaryana 16.140 4.035 3.707 \nHimachal Pradesh   0.965 0.370 0.070 \nUT of J & K                      0.615 0.154 0.000 \nKarnataka                60.430 45.326 51.260 \nUT of Ladakh 0.360 0.000 0.000 \nMadhya Pradesh 17.385 4.350 2.180 \nMaharashtra            61.935 30.967 43.420 \nManipur 1.500 0.000 0.000 \nMeghalaya               1.500 0.3"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |293", "t": "Modernization of AIR and DD Kendras", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 293 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023) \nMODERNIZATION OF AIR AND DD KENDRAS \n293.  ADV. A.M. ARIFF:  \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased \nto state:  \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any measures for the \nmodernization of Akashvani and Doordarshan Kendras in the \ncountry including those from Kerala to meet the changing \ndemands and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether it is true that several All India Radio (AIR) stations are \nstill functioning with old vacuum tube transformers which \nresult in consumption of more power and resulting in higher \noperational costs, if so, the details thereof including along \nwith the list of such AIR stations, State and UT-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to replace the old vacuum \ntube transformers by Digital Radio Mondiale (DRM) \ntransmitters to ensure smooth functioning and reduce \noperational costs in a time bound manner;  \n\n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(e)  the details of the amounts sanctioned, released and utilized \nfor each of the AIR stations in Kerala in Financial Year 2023-24 \nas on date? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n(a) to (d):  The modernization and strengthening of infrastructure and \nnetwork of Prasar Bharati in respect of Akashvani and Doordarshan  \n(DD) at different stations across the country is a continuous process \nunder the “Broadcasting Infrastructure and Network Development” \n(BIND) Scheme. This scheme has been further continued for the \nperiod 2021 -26 with an outlay of Rs. 2539.61 crores for var ious \ncomponents. \nPresently, only 25 Akashvani stations are functioning with vacuum \ntube transmitters. State/UT- wise details are at Annexure-I. \n(e):  The details of funds sanctioned, released and utilised for each \nof the AIR stations in Kerala are at Annexure -II.  \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED  TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 293 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nSTATE-WISE DETAILS OF STATIONS HAVING VACUUM TUBE \nTRANSMITTERS BASED MW TRANSMITTERS \n  \nS. No. State District Location Power \n1. Arunachal Pradesh Lohit Tezu 10 KW \n2. Assam Kamrup \nMetropolitan \n  \n  \nGuwahati 100 KW \n3. Assam Kokrajhar Kokrajhar 20 kW \n4. Assam Sonitpur Tezpur 20 KW \n5. Bihar Bhagalpur Bhagalpur 20 KW \n6. Goa North Goa Panaji 20 KW \n7. Karnataka Shivamogga Bhadrawati 20 KW \n8. Karnataka Gulbarga Gulbarga 20 KW \n9. Kerala Alappuzha Alappuzha 200 kW \n10. Kerala Kozhikode Kozhikode \n(Calicut) \n100 KW \n11. Kerala Thrissur Thrissur 100 KW \n12. Madhya Pradesh Bhopal Bhopal 10 KW \n13. Madhya Pradesh Gwalior Gwalior 20 KW \n14. Maharashtra Parbhani Parbhani 20 KW \n15. Maharashtra Sangli Sangli 20 KW \n16. Rajasthan Udaipur Udaipur 20 KW \n17. Tamil Nadu Coimbatore Coimbatore 20 KW \n18. Tamil Nadu Madurai Madurai 20 KW \n19. Tamil Nadu Tuticorin Tuticorin 200 KW \n20. Tripura West Tripura Agartala 20 KW \n21. UT (Puducherry) Puducherry Puducherry 20 KW \n22. Uttar Pradesh Agra Agra 20 KW \n23. Uttar Pradesh Gorakhpur Gorakhpur 10 KW \n(Mobile) \n24. Uttar Pradesh Prayagraj Prayagraj 20 KW \n25. Uttar Pradesh Rampur Rampur 20 KW \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED  TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 293 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nDETAILS OF SANCTIONED BUDGET GRANT(SBG)/ RELEASED &  \nEXPENDITURE OF THE AKASHVANI STATIONS IN KERALA  \n(Amount in Rs. thousands) \nS.  No. \n  \nName of the Stations Other Admn. Exp. (Internal and \nExtra Budgetary Resources) \nSBG \n2023-24 \nReleased Exp. \nUpto Oct. \n2023 \n1 Akashvani, Cochin 17870 12527 11100 \n2 Main Channel, Akashvani, Cochin 3228 1691 1127 \n3 Akashvani, Calicut 22151 15294 12080 \n4 Akashvani, Manjeri  4580 3260 2517 \n5 Akashvani, Trichur 16650 10692 9568 \n6 High Power Transmitter, Akashvani, \nAlleppey \n17100 10641 8298 \n7 Akashvani, Cannanore 5490 3537 3026 \n8 Akashvani, Devikulam 3625 2524 1946 \n9 Akashvani, Thiruvananthapuram 40510 27495 19559 \n10 Akashvani, Thiruvananthapuram, \n5kW \n375 266 36 \n11 Commercial Broadcasting Station, \nAkashvani, Thiruvananthapuram \n2135 1581 752 \n  Total 133714 89508 70009 \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |294", "t": "Availability of Computer System in Panchayats", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 294 \nANSWERED ON 5/12/2023  \n \n \nAvailability of Computer System in Panchayats \n \n \n294. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is having any data on the availability of computer system in each \npanchayat to meet the challenge of digital India; \n(b) if so, the details of number of panchayats available in the country and number of village \npanchayats yet to be provided with computer system to meet the digital requirements; \n(c) the details of fund provision made and utilised so far; \n(d) whether any timeline has been fixed for providing computer system in all village \npanchayats; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL)  \n(a) & (b) Yes Sir. As per the information available, out of 269073 Gram Panchayats (GPs)/ \nTraditional Local Bodies (TLBs), 226063 GPs have been computerized and 43010 GPs are yet to \nbe computerized. \n \n(c) to (e) Panchayat being State subject, providing computers for Gram Panchayats (GPs) is \nprimarily the responsibility of States. However, the revamped Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan \n(RGSA) scheme approved for implementation from 01.04.2022 to 31.03.2026, is supplementing \nthe efforts of all States/UTs with focus on NE States, towards supporting functioning of GPs by \nproviding computers amounting to Rs. 50,000/- per GP as proposed/demand by States/UTs in their \nAnnual Action Plan and approved by Central Empowered Committee on a limited scale.  \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |295", "t": "Schemes for Deaf and Dumb", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF EMPOWERMENT OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES                                                             \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION No. 295 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 05.12.2023 \nSCHEMES FOR DEAF AND DUMB \n295.   SHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the Government schemes available for deaf and dumb boys and girls separately \nin the country; and \n(b) the other steps taken by the Government for deaf and dumb? \nANSWER \n(MINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT) \nSUSHRI PRATIMA BHOUMIK \n \n(a) and (b) Relief to the persons with disabilities is a State subject by virtue of entry ‘9’ of the \nState List of the Constitution of India. However, the Central Government supplements the efforts \nof the State Governments for empowerment of persons with disabilities. The Department has \nenacted the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act, 2016, which came into force on \n19.04.2017. Chapter III of the said Act provides for duties of educational institutions and specific \nmeasures to promote and facilitate inclusive education for children with disabilities. Further, \nSection 31 of the said Act mandates the appropriate Government to ensure that Persons with \nbenchmark disabilities (PwBD), i.e. disability of 40% or more, has access to free education in an \nappropriate environment till he attains the age of 18 years. Section 32 of the said Act provides \nfor reservation of not less than 5% seats in Govt./Govt. aided higher educational institutions for \nPwBD. \n  \nDepartment of School Education & Literacy, Ministry of Education has launched an \noverarching programme for the school education sector - Samagra Shiksha Scheme. Under \nSamagra Shiksha, there is a dedicated component for Inclusive Education for Children with \nSpecial Needs (CwSN), to ensure full equity and inclusion so that all children with special needs \nare able to fully participate in schools. The scheme aims at education of CwSN in a continuum \nfrom pre-school to class XII. The scheme covers all CwSN with one or more disabilities. The \nSamagra Shiksha Scheme is being implemented through State Governments/ UT Administrations \nand the Central Government provides necessary financial support for the  Inclusive Education for \nCwSN component. Various provisions are made available for CwSN such as identification and \n\nassessment camps (at block level), student specific interventions @ Rs.3,500/- per CwSN per \nannum for support, such as provision of aids, appliances, assistive devices, teaching-learning \nmaterials, Braille books, large print books, transportation, escort, scribe allowances as well as \nhome based education for children with severe & multiple disabilities who are unable to attend \nschool. In addition, to encourage girl children with disabilities to attend schools, stipend for girls \nwith special needs @ Rs.200/- per month for 10 months (Rs.2,000/- per annum) through Direct \nBenefit Transfer is provided. Further, individualized support is also provided through resource \ncentres at block level to cater to the unique learning needs of CwSN.  \n  \nThe Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities (DEPwD) has the \nfollowing major provisions/schemes to encourage the persons with disabilities including the deaf \nand dumb students: \n  \n(I) The Department is implementing Scheme of ‘Assistance to Disabled Persons for \nPurchase/Fitting of Aids/Appliances (ADIP)’ under which funds are released to various \nImplementing Agencies for distribution of aids and assistive devices to eligible persons with \ndisabilities including deaf and dumb, boys and girls throughout the country. Under the ADIP \nScheme, there is a provision for Cochlear Implant Surgery for children with hearing impairment.  \n  \n(II) For the welfare of deaf and dumb boys and girls, the scheme namely Financial Assistance to \nthe existing deaf colleges in the country envisages financial assistance to colleges affiliated to the \nUGC approved State/ Central/ UT administered colleges in each of the following five regions of \nthe country: Rural Development and Management college for the Deaf in North Zone of India, \nCollege for Deaf in West Zone, College for deaf in South Zone, College for Deaf Central Zone, \nCollege for Deaf in East Zone. \n  \n(III) An umbrella Scheme titled ‘Scholarships for Students with Disabilities (Divyangjan) \n(including for deaf and dumb boys and girls) in the country is implemented. The six components \nof the Scholarship Scheme are : Pre-matric (Classes IX & X), Post-matric (Classes XI to Post \nGraduate Degree/Diploma), Top Class Education (Graduate and Post Graduate Degree/Diploma \nin notified Institutes of excellence in education), National Fellowship (M. Phil/Ph.D in Indian \nUniversities), National Overseas Scholarship (Master’s Degree and Ph.D in Foreign Universities) \nand Free Coaching (for competitive exams in G"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |296", "t": "Encouraging Sports among Young", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 296 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \nEncouraging Sports among Young \n296.   SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n DR. UMESH G. JADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the Government has taken any initiatives to \nencourage sporting habits among young minds particularly in rural \nareas in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n(a) to (c): ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the responsibility to promote \nand develop sports in the country, including rural areas, rests primarily \nwith the respective State/Union Territory Governments. However, the \nUnion Government supplements their efforts in this regard through its \nfollowing schemes:  \n1. Khelo India;  \n2. Assistance to National Sports Federations;  \n3. Special Awards to Winn ers in International sports events and their \nCoaches;  \n4. National Sports Awards,  \n\n5. Pension to Meritorious Sports Persons;  \n6. Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay National Welfar e Fund for \nSportspersons; National Sports Development Fund; and  \n7. Running sports training centres through Sports Authority of \nIndia(SAI).  \n \nAll these schemes cater to the entire country, including rural  areas . \nDetails of these schemes are available in the public domain on the \nwebsites of this Ministry and the Sports Auth ority of India  \n(www.yas.nic.in/sports; www.sportsauthorityofindia.nic.in).  \nFurther, one of the components of the Khelo India Scheme - “Promotion \nof rural and indigenous/tribal games” , is specifically dedicated towards \npromotion of sports in rural areas.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |298", "t": "Development of Fishermen Community", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 298 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05th  DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nDevelopment of Fishermen Community \n \n298.  DR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \n         SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI: \n         SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY:  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन \nऔर डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has chalked out any comprehensive welfare schemes for the \ndevelopment of the fishermen community in the country during the last five years particularly \nin Andhra Pradesh; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and funds allocated and disbursed under various welfare schemes in \nAndhra Pradesh during the last five years; \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(d)  the details of the steps taken by the Government to provide adequate skill training, provision \nof State of the art technology gadgets for fishing and storage and support for the creation of \nvalue added products? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a):  Yes Sir. \n(b):  The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying  \n(MoFAH&D) has been implementing various schemes for development of fisheries in the country \nincluding in the State of Andhra Pradesh  for development of fisheries and aquaculture including \nwelfare of fishermen is at the core of the all these schemes. The schemes implemented by Department \nof Fisheries, MoFAH&D include; (i) Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) on Blue Revolution: Integrated \nDevelopment and Management of Fisheries at a total central outlay of Rs 3000 crore implemented for \na period of 5 years from 2015-16 to 2019-20, (ii) Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development \nFund (FIDF) with a total fund size of Rs 7522.48 crore for a period of 5 years from 2018 -19 to 2023-\n24 for providing concessional finance, (iii) issuance of Kisan Credit Cards (KCC) to fis hers and fish \nfarmers since 2018-19 for fish farming and fishing in estuary and sea, fish fanning/mariculture activities \nin estuaries and open sea and any other State specific fisheries and allied activities (iv) Pradhan Mantri \nMatsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY ) under Atmanirbhar Bharat Package with an investment of \nRs.20,050 crore for a period of 5 years from 2020-21 to 2024-25.  \n\n \n During last five years  and current year , the proposals of Government of Andhra Pradesh \napproved at a total cost of Rs.2385.17 crore with central share of Rs.694.20 crore under PMMSY and \nCSS on Blue R evolution for development of Fisheries in the state including welfare of fishermen . \nCentral share of Rs.379.70 crore released to Government of Andhra Pradesh during last five years \nbased o n the utilization of central funds by the State Government.  Under FIDF,  proposals of \nGovernment of Andhra Pradesh approved at a total cost of Rs.1291.40 crore for construction of fishing \nharbor in the State. 14918 KCC issued to fishers and fish farmers of Andhra Pradesh.      \nVarious activities approved to Andhra Pradesh under PMMSY for welfare of fishermen such \nas 2191 unit of replacement of boat and net, 1300 unit of bio toilet for installation in fishing vessels, \n800 unit of sea cage, 41,200 monoline/tubenet and 26000 rafts for seaweed cultivation,  75 unit of \nvalue added enterprises, construction of 6 fish landing centre and 3 fishing harbour, 50 unit of bivalve \ncultivation, 20 u nit of ice plants/cold storage, Livelihood and nutritional support for 98360 socio-\neconomically backward active traditional fishers families annually at the rate of Rs.4500/family/ during \n61 days fishing ban/lean period . Besides there is provision under PMMSY  insurance to Fishers : (i) \nRs.5.00 lakh is provided against accidental dea th or permanent total disability, Rs.2.50 lakh against \naccidental permanent partial disability and Rs. 25,000 against accidental hospitalization (ii) Insurance \npremium subvention for fishing vessels. All these activities add to fisher’s income and their welfare.   \n(c):  Does not arise \n \n(d):  PMMSY inter -alia lays special focus on training, skill development, skill upgradation and \ncapacity building through trainings, awareness programmes and exposure visits to various \nstakeholders especially fishers, fish fa rmers, fish workers, fish vendors, entrepreneurs, officials, \nfisheries Cooperatives and members of Fish Farmer Producer Organizations for various fishing  \ntechnology, aquaculture and post-harvest related activities. The Training, Awareness, Exposure and \nCapacity Building programmes are carried out through the National Fisheries Development Board  \n(NFDB), ICAR institutes, Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs), other organizations and State/UT Fisheries \nDepartments.  \nUnder PMMSY, 14484 unit of transponders for safety of fishermen at sea and 50 deep sea \nfishing vessels for better catch approved to Andh"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |299", "t": "Community Radio Stations", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 299 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023) \n \nCOMMUNITY RADIO STATIONS \n \n299. DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: SHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the salient features of Community Radio Stations (CRSs);  \n \n(b) the details of funds sanctioned, allocated and utilized so far for setting up of these \nstations in the country, State/UT-wise including Tamil Nadu and Rajasthan;  \n \n(c) the number of CRSs established in the country, State/UT-wise including Tamil Nadu \nand Rajasthan;  \n \n(d) the number of CRSs being operated so far in the country, State/UT -wise including \nTamil Nadu and Rajasthan; \n \n(e) whether the Government has fixed any target for setting up of these stations \nespecially in remote/ far flung areas of the country; and  \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof, State/UT -wise including Tamil Nadu and Rajasthan along \nwith the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; \nAND MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n(a) The Salient feature of Community Radio Stations (CRSs) are as under:- \n(i) Community Radio Stations are low power FM Radio stations, hav ing a \nbroadcasting coverage in the range of 5-10 Kms. \n(ii) Permission to establish Community Radio Station (CRS) in India , is granted to \nnot-for-profit organisations. These include NGOs, educational institutions, Krishi \nVigyan Kendras (KVKs), etc. \n(iii)The C ommunity Radio stations provide a platform to the c ommunity for \nbroadcasting programmes  on local issues concerning health, nutrition, education, \nagriculture, etc.  \n(iv) Community Radio stations are required to generate at least 50% of content with \nthe participation of the local community.  \n\n \n \n(b) The Community Radio stations are set  up by the permission holders using their own \nfunds. However, the Government provides financial assistance amounting upto Rs. 10.00 \nlakh (upto Rs. 12 lakh  for CRS in North Eastern States)  to Permission holders towards \nsetting up of infrastructure, once they setup and operationalise the station for at least 3 \nmonths. So far , a total of 119 CRSs have received financial assistance  amounting to Rs. \n8.86 crores towards Infrastructure for CRS. The  State/ UT  wise details of CRS ar e as \nunder:- \nSr. No. Name of the State /UT Number of Stations received \nFinancial Assistance \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 1 \n2 Assam 1 \n3 Bihar 2 \n4 Chhattisgarh 2 \n5 Delhi 2 \n6 Gujarat 3 \n7 Haryana 6 \n8 Himanchal Pradesh 1 \n9 J&K 1 \n10 Karnataka 10 \n11 Kerala 6 \n12 Ladakh 1 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 11 \n14 Maharashtra 12 \n15 Manipur 4 \n16 Nagaland 1 \n17 Odisha 6 \n18 Puducherry 1 \n19 Punjab 2 \n20 Rajasthan 7 \n21 Sikkim 1 \n22 Tamil Nadu 10 \n23 Telangana 2 \n24 Tripura 1 \n25 Uttar Pradesh 22 \n26 Uttarakhand 3 \n Total 119 \n \n  \n\n(c) and (d)   As on November, 2023 a total of 474 Community Radio S tations are \nestablished/operational in the country. Out of the total 474 CRSs, 72 CRSs are \noperational in Tamil Nadu and Rajasthan. List of Operational Community Radio Stations \nin the country is as under:-  \n \n \n \n \nList of Community Radio Stations established/operated in the country, State/UT-wise \nS.No. Name of States/UTs No. of Operational Community Radio Station \n1. Andaman and Nicobar Islands 1 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 11 \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 2 \n4. Assam 5 \n5. Bihar 13 \n6. Chandigarh 5 \n7. Chhattisgarh 13 \n8. Delhi 5 \n9. Gujarat 15 \n10. Haryana 22 \n11. Himachal Pradesh 7 \n12. Jammu and Kashmir 7 \n13. Jharkhand 3 \n14. Karnataka 25 \n15. Kerala 21 \n16. Ladakh 1 \n17. Madhya Pradesh 40 \n18. Maharashtra 50 \n19. Manipur 4 \n20. Meghalaya 1 \n21. Nagaland 1 \n22. Odisha 40 \n23. Puducherry 2 \n24. Punjab 9 \n25. Rajasthan 27 \n26. Sikkim 1 \n27. Tamil Nadu 45 \n28. Telangana 10 \n29. Tripura 1 \n30. Uttar Pradesh 58 \n31. Uttarakhand 15 \n32. West Bengal 14 \n                  Total 474 \n\n(e) and (f)  The setting up of Community Radio is a voluntary activity by eligible \norganisations. To increase the number of CRS in the country, the Government has taken \nseveral measures including provisioning of Financial Assistance for infrastructure and \nawareness workshops especially in Remote and far flung areas of the country. As a result, \nthe number of CRS has grown from 140 in 2014 to 474 as of the November 2023  which \nis a growth of more than 300% in the sector. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |3", "t": "Selling of Attached Assets", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 3 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nNEW KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS  \nIN JABALPUR \n \n†*3. DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) opened during the last five \nyears along with the details thereof, region-wise;  \n(b)  the details of the new KVs opened or proposed to be opened in \nJabalpur, Madhya Pradesh during the last five years and the time \nby which the proposed Vidyalayas are likely to start;  \n(c)  whether there is a proposal to open Kendriya Vidyalayas in Barghat \nof Seoni district and Tirodi of Balaghat district in Balaghat \nParliamentary Constituency in Jabalpur;  \n(d)  if so, whether the formalities like issuing tenders etc. by CPWD have \nbeen completed; and  \n(e)  the time by which the said Vidyalayas are likely to start? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n****** \n \n  \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 3 RAISED BY DR. DHAL \nSINGH BISEN, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT ANSWERED ON \n04/12/2023 REGARDING NEW KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS IN \nJABALPUR.  \n  \n(a) & (b) As per information received from Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan \n(KVS), 73 new Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) under Civil / Defence / Institute \nof Higher Learning / Project Sector, including 03 KVs in Jabalpur Region, \nhave been opened throughout the country during the last five years  (i.e. \n2018-19, 2019-20, 2020-21, 2021-22, 2022-23 and current year 2023 -\n24). State / UT and KVS Region-wise details are annexed. \n(c) to (e) Opening of new KVs is a continuous process. KVs are opened \nprimarily to cater to the educational needs of the wards of transferable \nCentral Government Employees including Defence & Para -military \npersonnel, Central Autonomous Bodies, Central Public Sector \nUndertakings (PSUs) and  Central Institute of Higher Learning (IHL) by \nproviding a common programme of education throughout the country. \nProposals for opening of new KVs may be sponsored by Ministries or \nDepartments of the Government of India / State Governments / Union \nTerritories (UTs) Administrations, with commitment to provide requisite \nresources including land and temporary accommodation for setting up a \nnew KV as per norms. As per information received from KVS, proposals \nfor opening of new KVs at Barghat, Seoni district and Tirodi, Balaghat \ndistrict have been received.  These proposals for establishment of new \nKVs are subject to approval of the competent authority  as per extant \nprocedures. \n***** \n \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 3 RAISED BY DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN, HON’BLE MEMBER \nOF PARLIAMENT ANSWERED ON 04/12/2023 REGARDING NEW KENDRIYA \nVIDYALAYAS IN JABALPUR.  \n \nSTATE/UT WISE DETAILS OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS  \nOPENED IN LAST FIVE YEAR AND CURRENT YEAR \n \n \nYear S. No. State/UT Region \nS. \nNo. Name of KV \n2018-19 \n1 \nKarnataka \nBengaluru 1 Virupapura village, Gangavathi Taluk, \nDistt. Koppal \n2 Bengaluru 2 Gauribidanur, Distt. Chikkaballapur \n3 Bengaluru 3 BRBNMPL, Mysuru, Karnataka \n4 Kerala  Ernakulum 4 Konni, Distt Pathanamthitta \n5 Madhya Pradesh Jabalpur 5 Lakhnadon, Distt. Seoni \n6 Jabalpur 6 Harisingh Gaur University \n7 Rajasthan Jaipur 7 Tivri, Distt. Jodhpur \n8 Jaipur 8 Pali, Distt. Pali \n9 Telangana Hyderabad 9 Nizamabad, Distt. Nizamabad \n10 Hyderabad 10 Siddipet, Distt. Siddipet \n11 Bihar Patna 11 NPGCL Nabinagar, Distt. Aurangabad \n12 Uttar Pradesh Agra 12 Baoli, distt. Baghpat \n13 Agra 13 SSG, CISF Camp, Greater Noida \n14 Manipur Tinsukia 14 Chakpikarong, Distt. Chandel \n15 Maharashtra Mumbai 15 Washim, Distt. Washim \n16 Mumbai 16 Parbhani, Distt. Parbhani \n2019-20 \n1 \nUttar Pradesh \nVaransi 17 Bhadohi, Distt. Bhadohi \n2 Varansi 18 Mirzapur, Distt. Mirzapur \n3 Varansi 19 Kauhar, Gauriganj, Distt. Amethi \n4 Varanasi 20 Aayer, Distt. Varanasi \n5 Agra 21 Railway Idgah, Distt. Agra \n6 Lucknow 22 39th Bn SSB Gadnia Palia Kalan Distt. \nLakhimpur Khiri \n7 Lucknow 23 59th Bn. SSB Nanpara, Distt. Behraich \n8 Uttarakhand Dehradun 24 Khatima, Distt. Udham Singh Nagar \n9 Chhattisgarh Raipur 25 GC CRPF Bilaspur, Distt. Bilaspur \n10 Raipur 26 Kondagaon, Distt. Kondagaon \n11 J&K Jammu 27 Jodhpur, Distt. Doda \n12 Delhi Delhi 28 Dr. Rajendra Prasad Kendriya \nVidyalaya, President Estate, New Delhi. \n13 Odisha Bhubaneswar 29 Gunupur, distt. Rayagada \n14 Bhubaneswar 30 Anandpur, Distt. Keonjhar \n15 Assam Guwahati 31 210 Cobra, CRPF BN Dalgaon \n16 Guwahati 32 N.F.Railway Rangapara, Distt. Sonitpur \n17 Haryana Gurugram 33 GC, CRPF, Sonepat, Haryana \n18 Gurugram 34 CRPF Kadarpur, Gurgaon \n\n \n \n19 Andhra Pradesh Hyderabad 35 Kandukur Town, Distt. Prakasam \n20 Hyderabad 36 Irlapadu Village, Nadendla, Distt. Guntur \n21 \nMadhya Pradesh \nJabalpur 37 An"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |30", "t": "Development of Scheduled Tribes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nREDUCTION IN ACTIVE WORKERS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*30.  SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that there is a reduction in the number of \nactive workers under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) from 15.49 crore \nin the previous fiscal year to 14.33 crore as per the data \navailable till 06 October, 2023; \n(b)  whether it is also a fact that while the MGNREGS workforce is \nshrinking, there is an increased demand for work under the \nScheme and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether a net deletion of 80 lakh workers in the ongoing \nfinancial year paints a bleak picture for the MGNREGS and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to identify the factors \ncontributing to this decline and formulate strategies for \nrevitalizing the programme’s participation; and \n(e)  if so, the steps taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n   \n  \n  \n \n  \n\nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha  Starred Question \nNo. 30 for 05.12.2023. \n  \n(a): Currently, the beneficiary who has worked during the last \nthree financial years is called an active beneficiary. Mahatma \nGandhi NREGA is a demand driven scheme. If an adult member of a \nhousehold, of the rural area, does not demand for the work during \nthe last three previous financial years, that member will not be in \nthe list of active beneficiary. The change in number of active \nbeneficiary is a continuous process. Currently, there are a total of \n14.37 crore active beneficiaries under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA. \n(b): Mahatma Gandhi NREGA is a demand driven scheme. A total of \n221.60 crore persondays has been generated so far (as on \n30.11.2023), which is more than the persondays generated during \nthe same period in the last financial year i.e. 208.74 crore \npersondays (as on 30.11.2022). \n(c)to(e): Updation/deletion of Job Cards is a regular \nexercise conducted by the States/UTs. The reasons of deletion of \nJob card are listed below: \n(i)      fake job card (incorrect job card),  \n(ii)     duplicate job card,  \n(iii)    not willing to work,  \n(iv)    family shifted from Gram Panchayat permanently and  \n(v)     single person in job card and that person is expired. \n  \n******* \n  \n   \n  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |300", "t": "PMAY-G in Tamil Nadu", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 300 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nPMAY-G IN TAMIL NADU \n \n300.  SHRI SELVAM G.: \nSHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G) is \nbeing implemented in Tamil Nadu; \n(b)  if so, the target set for construction of houses under the said \nYojana; \n(c)  the number of houses sanctioned and those for which funds have \nbeen released; and \n(d)  the measures being taken to approve the remaining requests/ \napplications for release of funds to complete the construction of \nhouses already sanctioned/to be sanctioned under the said \nYojana in Tamil Nadu? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (c): To achieve “Housing for All” in rural areas, the Ministry of \nRural Development is implementing Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana- \nGramin (PMAY-G) w.e.f. 1st April 2016. The target is to construct 2.95 \ncrore pucca houses with basic amenities by March 2024. As on \n29.11.2023, a target of 2.95 crore houses has been allocated to \nStates/UTs, of which, more than 2.94 crore houses have been \nsanctioned to the beneficiaries by the States/UTs and 2.50 crore \nhouses have been completed. \n\nFurther, as on 29.11.2023, the number of the houses targeted, \nsanctioned, instalments released and completed in the State of Tamil \nNadu is given as under: \n Target Houses \nSanction\ned \nby the \nstate  \nRelease \nof 1st \nInstallme\nnt \nRelease of \n2nd  Installme\nnt \nRelease \nof 3rd \nInstallme\nnt \nRelease \nof 4th \nInstallme\nnt \nHouses \ncomplet\ned \n7,79,85\n1 \n7,59,184 7,43,778 6,34,145 5,84,390 5,63,664 5,82,264 \n  \n(d): Under the PMAY-G, an amount of Rs. 5321.64 crore has been \nreleased to the state of Tamil Nadu since inception of the scheme. The \nstate has utilized  amount of Rs. 7627.08 crore including state share. \nThere is an unspent balance of Rs. 1247.27 crore available in the in \nthe State Nodal Account (SNA) of the Scheme. The Central Govt could \nnot release any programme funds in the current financial year owing to \nhuge unspent balance in the SNA and non-submission of the proposal \nby the state.  All the States/UTs including the State of Tamil Nadu \nhave been requested to ensure spending and reduce the SNA balance \nand then submit complete proposals for release of funds. As on date, \nno proposal for release of programme funds have been received from \nthe state in this financial year.  \n******** \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |301", "t": "One District One Product Approach", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION No. 301 \nANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023  \n \nONE DISTRICT ONE PRODUCT APPROACH  \n \n†301.  SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n  \n            Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the manner in which One District One Product approach has been adopted to reap the benefits \nof scale for product marketing and availing services under the PM Formalisation of Micro Food \nProcessing Enterprises (PM-FME) Scheme;  \n \n(b) the details of the above particularly in Karnal Parliamentary Constituency and Haryana;  \n \n(c)  whether any officer has been appointed to identify the food products of a district of the State in \nview of availability of raw materials; and  \n \n(d)  the steps being taken by the Government to provide general infrastructure for products and \nendorsement of brand and marketing thereof so as to promote them and make the enterprises \nself-reliant?  \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a). Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro Food Processing Enterprises (PMFME) Scheme adopts One \nDistrict One Product (ODOP) approach to reap the benefit of scale in terms of procurement of inputs, \navailing common services and marketing of products. It provides the framework for value chain \ndevelopment and alignment of suppo rt infrastructure. The scheme also envisages strengthening \nbackward and forward linkages, provision of common facilities, incubation centres, training, marketing \n& branding primarily for ODOP. \n   \n(b). The district-wise list of ODOP approved in Haryana incl uding Karnal Parliamentary Constituency \nis placed at Annexure-I.  \n(c).  District Nodal Officers (DNOs) have been nominated in  the districts of Haryana for coordination \nand implementation of PMFME scheme. Further, District Level Committee, chaired by Distri ct \nCollector, has been constituted for review and approval of projects under the scheme.   The details of \nDNOs for the districts of Karnal and Panipat under Karnal Parliamentary Constituency is placed at \nAnnexure-II. \n \n(d). Support for setting up of Food Pr ocessing line along with Common Infrastructure is provided to \nFarmer Producer Organizations (FPOs)/Cooperatives/Self Help Groups (SHGs) and its \nFederations/Government agencies under PMFME. Substantial capacity of common infrastructure as \nwell as processing  line assisted under the scheme should be available for the use by other units and \npublic on hiring basis.  \n  \nUnder Branding and Marketing component of the scheme, support is provided to \nFPOs/SHGs/Cooperatives or an SPV of micro food processing enterprises  for developing a common \npackaging & branding with provision for quality control, standardization, and adhering to food safety \nparameters for consumer retail sale.   \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN  REPLY TO PART  (b) OF THE LOK SABHA UNS TARRED \nQUESTION NO. 301  FOR ANSWER ON 05.12.2023 REGARDING “ONE DISTRICT ONE \nPRODUCT APPROACH” \nDistrict-wise list of ODOP approved for the State of Haryana \nSl. No. District ODOP \n1 Ambala Milk Based Product \n2 Bhiwani Citrus Fruits \n3 Charkhi Dadri Milk Based Product \n4 Faridabad Bakery Products \n5 Fatehbad Citrus Fruits \n6 Gurugram Amla \n7 Hisar Milk based products \n8 Jhajjar Milk Based Product \n9 Jind Poultry Meat based products \n10 Kaithal Milk based products \n11 Karnal Milk Based Product \n12 Kurukshetra Bakery products \n13 Mewat/Nuh Tomato \n14 Narnaul/ Mahendragarh Spices (chilies etc.) \n15 Palwal Milk Based Product \n16 Panchkula Ginger \n17 Panipat Carrot \n18 Rewari Mustard based Products \n19 Rohtak Bakery Products \n20 Sirsa Milk Based Product \n21 Sonipat Mushroom based products \n22 Yamuna Nagar Bakery Products \n \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN  REPLY TO PART  (c) OF THE LOK SABHA UNS TARRED \nQUESTION NO. 301  FOR ANSWER ON 05.12.2023 REGARDING “ONE DISTRICT ONE \nPRODUCT APPROACH” \nDetails of DNOs in the districts of Karnal and Panipat of Haryana \n  \nSl. \nNo. \nState/District Nodal Officer Contact Details \n1 Karnal District Nodal \nOfficer \nAssistant Director, \nMSME Department \nEmail: ad-krl.msme@hry.gov.in \n2 Panipat District Nodal \nOfficer \nAssistant Director, \nMSME Department \nEmail: ad-pnp.msme@hry.gov.in \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |302", "t": "Programmes under PMMSY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 302 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nProgrammes under PMMSY \n302.    SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARA SHRANGARE: \n           SHRI DILIP SAIKIA: \n           SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n           SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the present status of the various programmes launched under Pradhan Mantri Matsya   \nSampada Yojana (PMMSY); \n \n(b)  whether the Government has taken any steps to address the critical gaps in the \nfisheries value chain and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the details of new steps taken by the Government to establish a robust fisheries \nmanagement framework and ensure socio -economic welfare of fishermen and fish \nfarmers? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (c): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and \nDairying is implementing a flagship scheme namely Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada \nYojana (PMMSY) with an investment of ₹20050 crore in all the States and Union \nTerritories (UTs) for a period of 5 years with effect from the financial year 2020-21 to FY \n2024-25 for holistic development of the fisheries sector in the country. Under this scheme \nduring the last three financial years (FY 2020 -21 to 2022 -23), and current financial y ear \n(2023-24), fisheries developmental projects of various State Governments, Union \nTerritories and other implementing agencies amounting to of Rs. 17118.62 crore have been \napproved for overall development of fisheries and aquaculture sector in the country. \n\nThe Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) is designed and implemented to \naddress critical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, post -harvest \ninfrastructure and management and modernisation and strengthening of value chain,  \ntraceability and quality improvement. To modernize and strengthen fisheries value chain, \nPMMSY supports creation of post -harvest infrastructure such as fishing harbours/fish \nlanding centres, cold storages and ice plants, fish transportation vehicles inclu ding \nrefrigerated and insulated vehicles, ice flaking and ice crushing units, ice/fish holding \nboxes, etc. with motorcycles, bicycles and autorickshaws, value addition enterprise units \nas well as modern hygienic markets such as wholesale fish markets including supermarkets, \nretail fish markets and outlets, mobile fish and live fish markets. So far, under PMMSY \ninvestments worth Rs. 4005.96 crore have been approved for the above listed activities \nduring the last three financial years (FY 2020 -21 to 2022 -23), and current financial year \n(2023-24).  \nPMMSY provides for establishment of a robust fisheries management framework and \nprovides need-based support to States/UTs for formulation and implementation of fisheries \nmanagement plans. Besides, the PMMSY promotes aquaculture, mariculture and post -\nharvest management and also various economic activities in fisheries, aiming towards \nenhancement of income and improve socio -economic condition of fishers and other \nstakeholders associated with fisheries sector. Under PMMS Y financial assistance for \nlivelihood and nutritional support are being provided to 6.0 lakh annually for traditional \nand socio-economically backward, eligible active marine and inland fishermen families \nduring the fishing ban period, vis-à-vis PMMSY  supports insurance coverage to 33.20 lakh \nfishers and also supports interest subvention scheme for fishing vessels. Additionally under \nPMMSY 20823.40 hectares of pond area for inland aquaculture, 3942 Biofloc units, 11927 \nNos of Re -circulatory Aquaculture Syst ems (RAS), 44,408 reservoir cages and 543.7 \nhectares pens in reservoirs, 1,11,110 units of seaweed rafts and monoline units, 1489 \nbivalve cultivation units, 562 cold storages, 6542 fish kiosks, 108 value added enterprise \nunits and  25,193 post harvest tran sportation units have been approved for promotion of \naquaculture, mariculture and post-harvest management activities. \n \n \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |303", "t": "Private Security Agencies", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 303 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 14, 1945 \n(SAKA) \n \nPRIVATE SECURITY AGENCIES \n \n†303.     SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of security agencies in the country, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Government has taken steps with the view to make Private \nSecurity Agencies more professional, reliable and responsible; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a): Private Security Agencies in the country are regulated as per \nprovisions of the Private Security Agencies (Regulation) Act, 2005 [PSAR \nAct]. Further, as per the Act, Controlling Authorities appointed by State/UT \nGovernments have been assigned the duties of issuance of licence and \nregulation of private security agencies in a State/UT. The data pertaining \nto the number of private security agencies in the country is not maintained \ncentrally. This data is maintained at the level of State/UT. However, in the \nyear 2019, the Central Government has developed a ‘Private Security \nAgency Licensing Portal’ to facilitate the States/UTs for online issue of \nlicence  to  private  security  agencies.   As  per  the  web  portal, there are \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 303 FOR 05.12.2023 \n \n 21835 licenses of Private Security Agencies as on 30.11.2023.          \nThe State-wise list of licenses of Private Security Agencies is at Annexure.  \n \n(b) & (c): Yes, Sir. In order to make Private Security Agencies more \nprofessional and responsible, the Government of India has notified two \nmodel rules under the PSAR Act, 2005, namely, the Private Security \nAgencies Central Model Rules, 2020 in supersession of earlier Central \nModel Rules of 2006 and the Private Security Agencies (Private Security to \nCash Transportation Activities) Rules, 2018. \n \nThe new Model Rules, 2020 accommodate changes in the ecosystem \nover the years and are aligned with the key visions of ‘Digital India’ and ‘e \nGovernance’. The licensee of private security agency has to successfully \nundergo a training relating to the private security as prescribed by the \nControlling Authority. The new model rules provide syllabus for training of \nlicensee to bring uniformity in licensee’s training across the States. The \ntraining shall be for a minimum period of six working days which shall \nbroadly include the following subjects, namely, (i) Present security \nscenario, (ii) Role and Functioning of Private Security Agencies, (iii) Legal \nprovisions, (iv) Management of Security Agencies, (v) Interface with public, \nPolice and other departments and (vi) Private Security Personnel – DO’s \nand DON’Ts (Conduct Rules). \n****** \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n (Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.303 for 05.12.2023) \n \nNumbers of private security agencies in the country, State/UT-wise: \n(as per the database on the web portal psara.gov.in as on 30.11.2023)* \n \n \nS. No. Name of State/UT No. of licence \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 26 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 390 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 22 \n4 Assam 312 \n5 Bihar 483 \n6 Chandigarh 123 \n7 Chhattisgarh 244 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 129 \n9 Delhi 1380 \n10 Goa 112 \n11 Gujarat 2555 \n12 Haryana 1416 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 274 \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 104 \n15 Jharkhand 322 \n16 Karnataka 937 \n17 Kerala 597 \n18 Ladakh 0 \n19 Lakshadweep 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 1029 \n21 Maharashtra 3790 \n22 Manipur 21 \n23 Meghalaya 26 \n24 Mizoram 2 \n25 Nagaland 2 \n26 Odisha 577 \n27 Puducherry 101 \n28 Punjab 948 \n29 Rajasthan 1871 \n30 Sikkim 27 \n31 Tamil Nadu 1012 \n32 Telangana 729 \n33 Tripura 88 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 1658 \n35 Uttarakhand 393 \n36 West Bengal 135 \n All States and UTs 21835  \n*As the data is dynamic and maintained at the level of State/UT Governments, the actual figures may \ndiffer from information registered on the portal. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |304", "t": "Monitoring of Doping and Age Fudging", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 304 \nTO BE ANSWERD ON 05.12.2023 \n \nMonitoring of Doping and Age Fudging \n \n304. SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA:  \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a)  the details of measures taken by the Government to monitor and \nprevent the incidence of doping among athletes participating in age \ngroup level sports tournaments in the country; \n(b)  whether the Government is aware of any recent cases of age \nfudging in age group level sports tournaments and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c)  the details of steps being implemented by the Government to \nenhance the integrity and fairness of age group level sports \ncompetitions specifically in relation to preventing age \nmisrepresentation; \n(d)  the details of educational programmes or initiatives which are \nin place to inform young athletes, coaches and sports administrators \nabout the legal and health consequences of doping and age fudging; \nand \n(e)  the extent to which the Ministry is collaborating with national \nsports federations and other relevant bodies to address these issues \neffectively? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n(a):  The Government is committed to eradicate doping/drug abuse \nby sportspersons. The National Anti-Doping Agency (NADA), an \nautonomous body under this Ministry, conducts Anti-Doping \n\neducation awareness programmes in association with Sports \nAuthority of India (SAI) and National Sports Federations (NSFs), for \nall stakeholders, including athletes participating in age-group level \nsports tournaments in the country.  \nThe details of the various measures taken by the Government \nare as: \n Under the Target Olympic Podium Scheme (TOPS), athlete \nworkshops are organized by SAI towards creating awareness \namong athletes with regard to prevention of Drug abuse. \n Awareness-cum-education material has been prepared by NADA \nin association with the NSFs. Mass awareness programmes \nhave been introduced for athletes across the country regarding \nthe Anti-Doping Rules.  \n Doping Control handbooks are disseminated among the \nsportspersons in different regional languages during the various \nawareness programmes. \n Anti-doping programme is implemented during various Khelo \nIndia games where athletes are engaged in workshops, \ninteractive kiosks, games, quizzes and digital content. \n Mobile application namely ‘Know Your Medicine’ has been \nlaunched to reveal the presence of banned/prohibited \nsubstances in medicines to avoid inadvertent usage of banned \nsubstances. \n SAI Call Centre facility is operational where which trainees can \ncontact and lodge their grievances.  Further, the trainees are \nalso contacted on random basis to get their feedback and \ngrievance. \n(b) & (c):  Various National and State Sports Federations typically \nhave rules/regulations in place to ensure fair play and maintain \nintegrity in the sports tournaments. These federations themselves \nlook after the matter of age fudging in age-group level sports \ntournaments.  \n(d): An event-based anti-doping education model has been \nimplemented at various events including Khelo India University \nGames, Khelo India Youth Games, National Games and North East \n\nOlympic Games. Interactive tools like anti-doping pledge, awareness \nmobile game, digital quiz, awareness kiosks, mobile van and \nworkshops is a part of the overall activities. The model has enabled \nanti-doping awareness at major national sports events. A total of 95 \nsuch Anti-Doping awareness and education sessions/workshops were \norganised by NADA in 2023. \n(e): Workshops are hosted by NADA and NSFs to sensitize them \nagainst doping in sport and disseminate the message of clean sport \namong athletes and athlete support personnel. More than 40 NSFs \nalong with stakeholders from SAI, Indian Olympic Association and \nParalympic Committee of India participate in these workshops. \nRegular Training Programmes on Anti-Doping Awareness for Coaches \nand Athletes are also organized by SAI. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |305", "t": "Disable Friendly Infrastructure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF EMPOWERMENT OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 305 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n  \nDISABLE FRIENDLY INFRASTRUCTURE \n  \n305.  SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \n            \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:-     \n  \n(a) whether all the Government buildings have no facility of wheelchair ramps and disabled \nfriendly; \n  \n(b) if so, the total percentage of the Government buildings that have wheelchair ramps installed, \nState/UT-wise; \n  \n(c) the total percentage of the Government buildings that have functional disabled friendly \nwashroom, State/UT-wise; and \n  \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to have disabled friendly infrastructure in Government \nbuildings upto District level? \n  \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n(SUSHRI PRATIMA BHOUMIK) \n(a) to (d) : The Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has informed that the  retrofitting of all \nthe 211 Central Government buildings across the country owned by that ministry for \naccessibility as targeted under Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan (Accessible India Campaign ) has been \ncompleted by Central Public Works Department (CPWD). Further, retrofitting works of 889 \nCentral Government buildings belonging to other Ministries/Departments maintained by the \nCPWD has also been completed. \n‘Works, lands and buildings vested in or in the possession of the State’ is a State subject. The \ndetails of buildings of States/Union Territories made accessible are not being maintained \ncentrally. For guidance of the States and Union Territories, Ministry of Housing and Urban \nAffairs has formulated ‘Harmonized Guidelines & Standards for Universal Accessibility in \nIndia, 2021’ containing accessibility guidelines. \nIn respect of State/UT Government owned buildings funded under target 1.1 of Accessible India \nCampaign, 21 State/UT Governments have reported completion of retrofitting work in 623 \nbuildings.  \n\nIn the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016, provisions have been made to ensure \naccessibility in public infrastructure. In addition, the Department of Empowerment of Persons \nwith Disabilities (Divyangjan) has been implementing a sub-scheme namely Creation of Barrier \nFree Environment under the Central Sector Scheme for Implementation of Rights of Persons \nwith Disabilities Act, 2016 to provide financial assistance to State/UT Governments to provide \nbarrier free environment for the persons with disabilities which include access to built \nenvironment in schools, colleges, academic and training institutions, offices and public \nbuildings, recreational areas, health centres/hospitals etc. This would include provision for \nramps, rails, lifts, adaptation of toilets for wheelchair users, brail signages and auditory signals, \ntactile flooring, causing curb cuts and slopes to be made in pavement for the easy access of \nwheelchair users, engraving on the surface of zebra crossing for the blind or for persons with low \nvision, engraving on the edges of railway platforms for the blind or for low vision and devising \nappropriate symbols of disability, etc. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |306", "t": "ST Beneficiaries of PMFBY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 306 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nST BENEFICIARIES OF PMFBY \n \n306. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी  \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n \n (a)  the number of Scheduled Tribe (ST) farmers who are the beneficiaries of the Pradhan \nMantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), State-wise; and \n (b)  the details of crops insured under this Yojana? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी    (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a)  : As per data available on National Crop Insurance Portal (NCIP), State-wise details of \nScheduled Tribe (ST) farmer applications enrolled during 2022-23 under Pradhan Mantri \nFasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) :  The PMFBY envisages coverage of all food crops  (cereals, millets and pulses), \noilseeds and commercial/horticultural crops subject to availability of past yield data of \nrequisite number of years based on Crop Cutting Experiments  (CCEs)  as well as capacity \nof the State Government to conduct requisite number of CCEs to assess the yield of the \ncrop to calculate claims.  However, specific crop is notified by the concerned State \nGovernment keeping in view the aforesaid provision.   For the crops not meeting the \naforesaid conditions, the concerned State Government is free to notify them for coverage \nunder Restructured Weather Based Crop Insurance Scheme (RWBCIS)  under which \nclaims payment are being structured on the basis of weather index parameters.  State-wise \ndetails of crops being notified by the States/UTs under PMFBY/RWBCIS are given in \nAnnexure-II. \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise details of Scheduled Tribe (ST) farmer applications enrolled during 2022-23 under \nPMFBY (as on 30.11.2023) \nState/UT \nNo. of Scheduled Caste \nfarmer applications enrolled \n(in lakhs) \nRAJASTHAN 29.35  \nMADHYA PRADESH 8.06  \nCHHATTISGARH 6.87  \nANDHRA PRADESH 6.44  \nTAMIL NADU 6.22  \nODISHA 6.13  \nMAHARASHTRA 4.73  \nUTTAR PRADESH 3.42  \nTRIPURA 0.49  \nUTTARAKHAND 0.32  \nASSAM 0.18  \nHIMACHAL PRADESH 0.18  \nHARYANA 0.13  \nJAMMU AND KASHMIR 0.06  \nPUDUCHERRY 0.03  \nKERALA 0.03  \nTotal 72.66  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \nState-wise details of crops being notified during Kharif and Rabi Seasons by the States/UTs \nunder PMFBY \nKharif Seasons \nStates/UTs Name of Crops being Notified \nANDAMAN & \nNICOBAR ISLANDS Paddy (Dhan), Paddy High Yielding Variety \nASSAM Paddy - Sali \nCHHATTISGARH \nBanana, Black Gram (Urad), Brinjal, Chillies, Ginger, Green Gram (Moong), \nGroundnut (Pea Nut), Guava, Maize (Makka), Paddy - IR, Paddy - RF (UnIR), \nPapaya, Pigeon Pea (Red Gram/Arhar/Tur), Soybean (Bhat), Tomato \nGOA Groundnut (Pea Nut), Paddy (Dhan), Pulses, Sugarcane - Seasonal/suru/plant \nHARYANA Cotton (Kapas), Maize (Makka), Paddy (Dhan), Pearl Millet (Bajra) \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH \nCabbage, Cauliflower, Ginger, Maize (Makka), Paddy (Dhan), Peas (Field Peas/ \nGarden Peas/Matar), Potato, Tomato \nJAMMU AND \nKASHMIR Maize (Makka), Paddy (Dhan) \nKERALA \nArecanut, Ash Gourd (Petha), Banana, Barnyard Millet (Kundiraivlli/Sawan, \nBhindi(Okra/Ladysfinger), Bitter Gourd, Cardamom, Chillies, Cocoa, Common \nMillet (Panivaragu/Chena/Proso Millet/Hogm, Cucumber, Fingermillet \n(Ragi/Mandika), Ginger, Italian Millet (Thenai/Navane/Foxtail Millet/Kang) - RF, \nKodo Millet (Kodara/Varagu), Little Millet (Samai/Kutki/Kodo-Kutki), Nutmeg, \nPaddy - Autumn, Pearl Millet (Bajra), Pepper, Pineapple, Pumpkin, Snake \nGourd, Sorghum (Jowar/Great Millet), Sugarcane - Adsali, Tapioca, Tomato, \nTurmeric, Yard Long Bean \nMADHYA PRADESH \nBlack Gram (Urad), Cotton (Kapas), Green Gram (Moong), Groundnut (Pea \nNut), Little Millet (Samai/Kutki/Kodo-Kutki), Maize (Makka), Paddy - IR, Paddy - \nRF (UnIR), Pearl Millet (Bajra), Pigeon Pea (Red Gram/Arhar/Tur), Sesame \n(Gingelly/Til)/Sesamum, Sorghum (Jowar/Great Millet), Soybean (Bhat) \nMAHARASHTRA \nBlack Gram (Urad), Cotton (Kapas), Custard Apple, Fingermillet (Ragi/Mandika), \nGrape, Green Gram (Moong), Groundnut (Pea Nut), Guava, Lemon, Maize \n(Makka), Mosambi (sweet orange), Niger (Ramtil), Onion, Orange, Paddy \n(Dhan), Pearl Millet (Bajra), Pigeon Pea (Red Gram/Arhar/Tur), Pomegranate, \nSapota, Sesame (Gingelly/Til)/Sesamum, Sorghum (Jowar/Great Millet), \nSoybean (Bhat) \nMANIPUR Cabbage (Summer), Paddy High Yielding Variety \nODISHA Cotton (Kapas), Fingermillet (Ragi/Mandika), Ginger, Groundnut (Pea Nut), \nMaize (Makka), Paddy (Dhan), Pigeon Pea (Red Gram/Arhar/Tur), Turmeric \nPUDUCHERRY Paddy - I, Paddy - IR \nRAJASTHAN \nBlack Gram (Urad), Castor (Rehri, Rendi, Arandi), Cotton (Kapas), Cowpea, \nGreen Gram (Moong), Groundnut (Pea Nut), Guar, Guava, Kinnow, Maize \n(Makka), Moth Bean (Kidney Bean/ Deww Gram), Onion, Orange, Paddy (Dhan), \nPearl Millet (Bajra), Pigeon Pea (Red Gram/Arhar/Tur), Sesame \n(Gingelly/Til)/Sesamum, Sorghu"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |307", "t": "Use of Chemical Pesticides", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 307 \nANSWERED ON 5TH December, 2023 \n \nUSE OF CHEMICAL PESTICIDES  \n \n307.  SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state:- \n \n(a) whether the consumption/use of chemical pesticides in the country has witnessed \nincrease due to lack of awareness among farmers during the last several years; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government so far to reduce the use of chemical pesticides?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \n कृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c):  State Governments and Union Territories report data related to consumption of \npesticides to Central Government. As per the provided data, the trend of consumption of \nchemical pesticides has varied from year to year and State to State. The state wise details \non consumption of chemical pesticides and bio-pesticides is attached at Annexure-I and II, \nrespectively.    \nUse of Pesticides depends on several factors like area under cultivation, type of crop, \ncropping intensity, agro climatic conditions, soil condition, weed, insect and disease situation \netc. However, with the objective of  educating,  orienting and training of  farming community \nabout the judicious use of pesticides and use as per the prescriptions given in the label and \nleaflets, Central and State Governments through their field agencies ensure that farmers are \n\nprovided right knowledge/ information about recommended pesticide use.  \nFurther, Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare through its Krishi Vigyan Kendras \n(KVKs), ICAR and Central Integrated Pest Management Centers (CIPMCs) located in 28 \nStates and 2 UTs, etc conduct various training programmers such as Farmers Field Schools, \ntwo/five days HRD programmers, Kisan Goshthis, IPM Exhibitions and Seed Treatment \nCampaigns where  awareness is created amongst  farmers, Pesticide dealers/State \nagriculture functionaries on use of bio pesticides, bio control agents and botanical \nformulations as an alternative to chemical pesticides for pest management, safe and \njudicious use of chemical pesticides, usage, dose and label claim instructions as approved \nby RC.  \nMoreover, Government of India has taken various steps to promote the use of bio pesticides. \nSimplified guidelines have been formulated by Registration Committee (RC) for the \nregistration of bio pesticides. For bio-pesticides, provisional registrations are being granted \nunder Section 9(3B) of the Insecticides Act, 1968 alongwith the permission for \ncommercialization during the provisional registration period of two years based on the \nconfirmation of molecular identity of the strain from ICAR-National Bureau of Agriculturally \nImportant Microorganisms (ICAR-NBAIM) and quality verification of the product from Central \nInsecticide Laboratory (CIL). \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nSTATE-WISE CONSUMPTION OF PESTICIDES \nUnit: M.T. (Tech. Grade) \nS. \nNo. States/UTs 2018-19   2019-20 2020-21  2021-22 2022-23 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1689  1559 1559 1759 2001 \n2 Bihar 850  850 995 850 995 \n3 Chhattisgarh 1770 1672 1639 1740 1775 \n4 Goa 25  30 30 32 34 \n5 Gujarat 1608  1784 1573 1869 1750 \n6 Haryana 4015  4200 4050 4066 4066 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 322  881 56 279 470 \n8 Jharkhand 646 681 1161 450 450 \n9 Karnataka 1524  1568 1930 2224 1669 \n10 Kerala 995  656 585 532 504 \n11 Madhya Pradesh 540  540 691 654 598 \n12 Maharashtra 11746  12783 13243 13175 6814 \n13 Orissa 1609 1115 1158 1240 1348 \n14 Punjab 5543  4995 5193 5376 5130 \n15 Rajasthan 2290  2088 2330 2104 1865 \n16 Tamil Nadu 1901  2225 1834 1851 1952 \n17 Telangana 4894  4915 4986 4920 4920 \n18 Uttar Pradesh 11049 12217 11557 11688 11824 \n19 Uttarakhand 195  224 135 213 111 \n20 West Bengal 3190  3630 3630 3630 3321 \n21 Arunachal Pradesh 5  5 2 1 NR \n22 Assam 256  410 420 474 455 \n23 Manipur NR 25 46 NR NR \n24 Meghalaya Organic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \n25 Mizoram 26  27 NR 29  31 \n26 Nagaland 21  19 36 41 NR \n27 Sikkim Organic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \nOrganic \nState \n28 Tripura 349  364 NR NR NR \n29 Andaman & Nicobar NR NR 1  NR NR \n30 Chandigarh NR NR NR NR NR \n31 Dadra & Nagar Haveli & \nDaman & Diu NR NR NR NR NR \n32 Delhi 110  NR NR NR NR \n33 Jammu & Kashmir 2459 2198 3352 4086 384 \n34 Ladakh NR NR NR NR NR \n35 Lakshadweep NR NR NR NR NR \n36 Pondicherry 42  40 NR NR NR \nTotal 59670 61702 62193 63284 52466 \n \nSource: States/UTs Zonal Conferences on Inputs (Plant Protection) for Rabi & Kharif Seasons.  NR: \nNot Reported.  \n \n \n \n\n    Annexure-II \nSTATE-WISE CONSUMPTION OF BIO PESTICIDES \nUnit: M.T. Formulation \nS. No. States/UTs 2018-19 2019-20 2020-\n21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 10 9 9 45 51"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |308", "t": "Assistance under PM-KISAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 308 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nASSISTANCE UNDER PM-KISAN SCHEME \n \n308. SHRI ANTO ANTONY: \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare कृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is giving financial assistance to farmers under Pradhan Mantri \nKisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN);  \n(b) if so, the details of such assistance given to the farmers under the scheme during the \nlast five years, year-wise and State-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government has noticed that nearly 2.40 lakhs farmers enrolled under the \nscheme are not getting the benefits of this scheme because they are unable to link \nAadhaar Card with Bank Accounts;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to ensure that all \nmembers are getting the benefits under the scheme;  \n(e) whether the Government has any proposal to withdraw the decision to link Aadhaar \nand Bank Accounts; and  \n(f) if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nकृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b) The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN) is a Central Sector \nScheme, being implemented w.e.f. 1st December, 2018.  The Scheme aims at providing \n\nfinancial assistance to all landholding farmer families across the country, subject to \ncertain exclusion criteria, to enable them to take care of expenses related to agriculture \nand allied activities as well as domestic needs.  Under the Scheme, an amount of Rs. \n6000/- per year is transferred in three installments each of Rs.2000/- directly into the \nAadhaar seeded bank accounts of the farmers through DBT. \n  \nState-wise and year-wise details of the funds released under PM-KISAN are \nattached at Annexure. \n \n(c) & (d) Under PM-KISAN, the condition of Aadhaar seeded bank account is mandatory \nfrom 13th instalment to ensure that benefits are transferred only to intended beneficiary’s \nbank account. Further, transferring the benefits in the Aadhaar seeded bank accounts of \nbeneficiaries has inherent advantage in their favor as in case of change of bank accounts, \nbeneficiaries are not required to travel back to the Department to update the same. In \norder to saturate the Aadhaar seeding of the bank accounts, the beneficiaries are \ncontacted by the State/UT Governments for completing their Aadhaar seeding with their \nactive bank accounts.  The Central Government also sends one to one messages to such \nfarmers for taking necessary actions so that benefits can be transferred to them without \nany hassle.  \n \nIn addition to the above, the Government of India has also on boarded India Post \nPayments Bank (IPPB) has also been on boarded to complete beneficiaries’ Aadhaar \nseeding with bank accounts at their doorstep.  Under the scheme, 100% benefits are \nbeing transferred to such beneficiaries in the Aadhaar seeding bank accounts from 13th \ninstalment onwards.   \n \nIt is worth to mention that no instalment of the farmers is withheld by the \nGovernment. Once the Aadhaar seeding is done by the beneficiaries, their all accrued \nbenefits are reprocessed and benefits are transferred to them. \n \n(e) & (f) There is no such proposal under consideration. \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \nDetails of number of beneficiaries and amount released under PM-KISAN year-wise and state-wise (as on 22/11/2023) \n 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \nSl. \nNo. States No. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease \n(In Rs. \nCrore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease \n(In Rs. \nCrore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease \n(In Rs. \nCrore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease \n(In Rs. \nCrore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease (In \nRs. Crore) \n1 Andaman and \nNicobar Islands 9,932 2.02 16,408 9.98 16,324 9.73 16,270 9.54 14,992 8.56 13,160 3.40 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 32,82,718 663.37 49,88,519 2,710.38 49,45,891 2,865.02 46,18,979 2,907.30 47,83,549 2,959.81 43,51,698 1,884.82 \n3 Bihar 2,50,253 50.16 61,99,487 2,921.67 77,41,022 4,601.39 82,93,357 5,033.44 83,54,676 4,851.82 78,64,800 3,181.83 \n4 Chandigarh 48 0.01 448 0.27 432 0.25 397 0.24 367 0.20 134 0.03 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1,02,405 22.61 20,51,396 1,043.50 29,82,431 1,732.75 32,56,809 1,920.28 29,47,050 1,616.42 22,20,010 1,023.30 \n6 Delhi - - 13,131 7.32 14,436 8.53 15,041 9.16 14,976 8.44 13,467 4.72 \n7 Goa 2,409 0.49 8,600 4.31 9,630 5.57 9,451 5.73 9,118 4.74 6,080 2.56 \n8 Gujarat 28,06,803 572.01 51,15,505 2,884.98 56,67,576 3,380.65 58,84,358 3,599.52 59,62,338 3,167.84 48,49,357 1,998.57 \n9 Haryana 9,58,226 193.31 16,40,589 912.82 18,77,565 1,152.14 18,85,868 1,146.42 18,62,403 986.20 16,19,188 664.69 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 4,53,087 91.41 8,80,378 525.36 9,18,666 549.42 9,51,392 570.12 9,46,579 469.83 7,87,961 344.44 \n11 Jammu and Kashmir 4,42,014 91"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |309", "t": "Mega Food Park Projects", "head": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.309 \nANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nMEGA FOOD PARK PROJECTS \n \n309.    SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \n       Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of number of approved Mega Food Park Projects, State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of the Mega Food parks that are presently operational, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether it is a fact that for FY 2021-22, for Mega Food park Projects the revised                             \nestimates were Rs. 54.37 crores, but as of 21.01.22, the actual expenditure was \naround    Rs. 31.34 crores only; and \n \n(d)         if so, the reasons for only 57.64% utilisation of the allocated funds? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b) Ministry of Food Processing Industries has been implementing Mega Food Park  \nScheme (MFPS), a component scheme under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana \n(PMKSY), to create modern infrastructure for the food processing sector along the value \nchain from farm to market. Under the scheme, Ministry has approved 41 Mega  Food Park \n(MFP) projects. Out of this, 24 MFP projects are operational and remaining 17 projects \nare under implementation. State wise details of 41 MFP Projects including 24  operational \nprojects along with their status of implementation is at Annexure. \n \n(c) & (d ) Yes sir. However, the Ministry has incurred an expenditure of Rs. 21.25 crore  \nduring 21.01.2022 to 31.03.2022. Thus total expenditure under Mega Food Park scheme  \nduring the ﬁnancial year 2021-22 was Rs. 52.59 crore against the revised estimate of \nRs. 54.37 crore which is 96.72% of the revised allocated funds. \n \nThe Mega Food Park Scheme has, however, been discontinued w.e.f. 01.04.2021 with \nprovision for committed liabilities only. \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO PART (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 309 REGARDING MEGA FOOD PARK PROJECTS TO BE \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \n \n SPV/IA State/UT District Date of Total Amount Amount Status \n Name Name Name Final Project of Grant of Grant  \n    Approval Cost Approved Released  \nS. \nNo\n. \n    (INR in \nCr) \n(INR in \nCr) \n(INR in \nCr) \n \n1 Srini Food \nPark Pvt Ltd \nAndhra \nPradesh Chittoor 30-Mar- \n2009 121.1 49.92 49.92 Operational \n \n2 \nGodavari Mega \nAqua Food Pa