07.12 212.0\n9 \n376.73 1,062.8\n2 \n672.93 \n17 Manipur 263.85 420.66 742 744.98 0.00 487.23 605.7\n7 \n718.36 537.37 240.50 \n18 Meghalay\na \n357 355.29 483.92 405.89 0.00 289.79 473.7\n1 \n536.92 373.72 105.31 \n19 Mizoram 576.06 1.59 74.34 584.2 2.08 285.18 277.3\n2 \n332.86 315.94 135.26 \n20 Nagaland 88.89 72.89 145.31 183.15 56.21 70.8 144.7 125.83 198.65 59.92 \n21 Odisha 798.11 774.29 437.5 1235.8\n8 \n813.5 2489.91 1754.\n13 \n1796.50 2,088.9\n0 \n1015.5\n8 \n22 Punjab 0 0 68.59 231.06 215.1 32.1 2.67 295.14 27.08 399.50 \n23 Puducherr\ny \n0 0 11.65 24.72 0.27 0 0 0 428.72 10.59 \n24 Rajasthan 184.74 237.15 917.50 199.9 134.4\n1 \n289.11 492.1\n3 \n1452.62 373.25 235.48 \n25 Sikkim 4.39 195.5 107.28 263.33 0.84 175.11 178.5177.89 230.34 94.65 \n\n2 \n26 Tamil \nNadu \n308.46 265.38 440 613.7 182.2\n5 \n799.29 626.9\n2 \n1169.56 532.36 522.75 \n27 Telangana 267.38 0 86.63 321.43 131.9\n6 \n289.44 288.5\n9 \n410.8 346.03 237.59 \n28 Tripura 10.64 69.57 73.88 267.59 0.00 95.36 99.13 203.44 152.9 2502.8\n2 \n29 Uttar \nPradesh \n78.07 123.9 1418.55 2068.5\n7 \n1748.\n93 \n356.63 440.1\n9 \n2074.26 3,267.3\n2 \n241.23 \n30 Uttarakha\nnd \n554.9 1536.27 787 1297.1\n6 \n185 1080.48 1493.\n5 \n1218.45 1,350.0\n2 \n241.23 \n31 West \nBengal \n348.25 969.31 49.94 381.03 2.14 1512.52 1471.\n94 \n701.28 394.75 172.20 \n32 Ladakh 0 50 140.785 109.97 37.5 0 514.7\n3 \n109.66 107.81 8.57 \nTotal 13,995.\n87 \n13,651.\n46 \n13,952.\n86 \n18948.\n62 \n6026.\n31 \n21,723.\n90 \n23,93\n5 \n27,842.\n63 \n24,214.\n45 \n11881.\n19 \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |379", "t": "FM Transmitter in Darbhanga", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 379 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023) \nFM TRANSMITTER IN DARBHANGA \n379.  SHRI GOPAL JEE THAKUR:  \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased \nto state:  \n(a)  whether approval has been granted by the Government to set \nup 5 KW capacity Frequency Modulation (FM) transmitter in \nAkashvani Kendra Darbhanga under the Boardcasting \nInfrastructure and Network Development scheme;  \n(b)  if so, when the said approval was granted;  \n(c)  whether the said transmitter has already been set up and the \nFM broadcast service has been started from the Akashvani \nKendra;  \n(d)  if so, when this service was launched and started;  \n(e)  if not, the details of steps taken by the  Government for setting \nup the same;  \n \n\n \n(f)  whether the Government has fixed any timeline for early \nsetting up of the said transmitter and start broadcasting \nservice;  \n(g)  the time by which such transmitter is likely to be set up; and  \n(h)  if not, the reasons therefor alongwith the details thereof?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n(a) to (h): The Government has approved a project to set up 5 kW FM \ntransmitter at Akashvani  Darbhanga under the Central Sector \nScheme (CSS) Broadcasting Infrastructure and Network \nDevelopment (BIND) scheme on 20.10.2023. \nThe projects approved under BIND Scheme have to be completed \nby March, 2026.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |38", "t": "Increasing levles of Pollution", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 38 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nEngagement of Astrologer by AIFF for Team Selection \n \n*38. SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate : \n \n(a) whether it has come to the notice of the Government the case of \nengagement of Astrologer by All India Football Federation (AIFF) for \nselection of team members; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether such acts do not diminish the image of the country; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to advise or has advised AIFF \nnot to have parameters other than fitness and ability of the players; \nand \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n{SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR} \n \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 38 FOR REPLY ON 05.12.2023 \nASKED BY SHRI S. VENKATESAN  REGARDING ENGAGEMENT OF \nASTROLOGER BY AIFF FOR TEAM SELECTION. \n \n(a) to (e): Sir, the issue of engagement of astrologer by  the All India \nFootball Federation ( AIFF) for the selection of national team members, i f \nany, is a part of the forensic audit report that has been independently \nprepared in terms of the orders of the Hon’ble Supreme Court  in SLP No. \n3047-48 of 2022 titled as “All India Football Federation vs. Rahul Mehra \nand Others. \n \nThe said report has been placed for the consideration in a sealed cover  \nbefore the Hon’ble Supreme Court  by the learned Amicus Curiae in the \nmatter. The matter is still sub judice before the Hon’ble Supreme Court. \n \n****** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |380", "t": "Separate Budget for Agriculture", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 380 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSEPARATE BUDGET FOR AGRICULTURE \n \n380. SHRI R.K. SINGH PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has released any separate budget for agriculture with the \nobjective of making law for regulating the use of groundwater to increase the agricultural \nproductivity to safeguard the farmers’ welfare and to protect the interests of farmers and the \ncommon people dependent on groundwater; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the steps being taken by the Government in this regard; \nand \n \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Both agriculture and water are state subjects. However, Government of India \nis actively supporting and supplementing efforts of state governments by implementing a \ncomprehensive set of schemes and programmes for the benefit of farmers across agriculture \nand allied sectors as well as promoting sustainable groundwater management. Apart from \nthe schemes, Government of India has also taken several initiatives to enhance income and \nimprove quality of life of farmers which include unprecedented enhancement in budget \nallocation for the Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare. Total budget allocation for the \nMinistry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare has been increased to Rs. 1,25,035.79 crore \nduring 2023-24.   \nRegarding legislations to regulate ground water use, Ministry of Jal Shakti has \ncirculated a Model Bill to all the States/UTs to enable them to enact suitable ground water \nlegislation for regulation of its development. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |381", "t": "Status of PM-KISAN in Odisha", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 381 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSTATUS OF PM-KISAN IN ODISHA \n \n381. SHRIMATI APARAJITA SARANGI: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the current status of Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN) in terms of \nthe number of beneficiaries and the amount of funds disbursed in the State of Odisha since \nits launch;  \n(b)  the details of number of women beneficiaries under PM-KISAN across the country \nand particularly in the State of Odisha; and  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to address and reach the unbanked and \nunderbanked segment of the population of Odisha? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nकृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a): In the State of Odisha, under PM-KISAN, benefits worth Rs. 9202.5 Crore have been \ntransferred to 42.02 lakh beneficiaries since the inception of the Scheme. \n(b): 3.03 Crore women beneficiary across the nation have received the benefits of the \nPM-KISAN since its inception, out of which approx 10.24 lakh women beneficiaries belong \nto Odisha. \n(c): Aadhar seeded bank account is mandatory for getting benefit of the scheme, as the \nbenefits under the PM-Kisan Scheme is transferred to Aadhar Seeded Bank Account of the \nbeneficiary through Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) mode.  The Government has on boarded \nIndia Post Payments Bank to provide banking services at the doorstep to the eligible \nbeneficiaries across the country including Odisha.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |382", "t": "Agreement with NADA and NCERT", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †382 \nTO BE ANSWERD ON 05.12.2023 \n \nAgreement with NADA and NCERT \n \n†382. SHRI ANIL FIROJIYA:  \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether any agreement has been signed with National Anti-\nDoping Agency and National Council of Educational Research and \nTraining to strengthen value based sports education among school \nchildren and teachers; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any steps have been taken by the Ministry for \ncleanliness campaign; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken for disposal of \npending cases?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n(a) & (b):  Yes, Sir. National Anti-Doping Agency (NADA), an \nAutonomous Body under the Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports, \nsigned a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with National \nCouncil of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) on \n03.03.2023 to strengthen value-based sports education among \nschool children and teachers. This collaboration will leverage the \ndigital infrastructure and outreach capacity of the Department of \nSchool Education & Literacy through Pradhan Mantri (PM) e-Vidya \ninitiative for the benefit of various stakeholders. \n \n\nThe key activities under the MoU include the following: \n Development of educational e-Books, digital courses, \naudios, videos, interactives, and advocacy materials related \nto sports value education and ethics. \n Promoting sports value education through distribution of \nUNESCO toolkits in vernacular languagesamong students, \nteachers and parents. \n Inclusive and accessible content for players in line with \nUniversal Design of Learning (UDL) Principles particularly \nfor the special needs category. \n Planning and development of digital content along with its \ndissemination and telecast/ broadcast via PMe-VIDYA - One \nclass One Channel and DIKSHA portal. \n(c) & (d): This Ministry successfully implemented the Special \nSwachhta Campaign 3.0 for overall cleanliness of the workspace. \nThe various steps taken during the Campaign are as follows: \n Cleanliness drive resulted in freeing up of space, which was \nfurther utilized for productive purposes. \n Weeding out of Physical Files involved digitization of important \nrecords. \n Closure of electronic files after reviewing. \n Disposal of pending grievance matters.  \nThe details of the disposal of pending cases are as follows: \nS.No. Particular Target Achievement  \n1. VIP References 34 34 \n2. PG Cases 78 78 \n3. Physical Files Reviewed/Closed 237 237 \n4. E-Files Reviewed/Closed 400 400 \n5. State Government References 2 2 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |383", "t": "Multi State Cooperative Societies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 383 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nMulti State Cooperatives Societies \n \n383: SHRI. RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of Multi State Co-operative Societies (MSCSs) registered in the country;  \n(b) the number of the Co-operative societies out of the above which are functional along with \nthe details thereof, State-wise;  \n(c) the Co-operative Societies which have been provided grants for their development during \nthe last five years and the current year along with the amount of funds provided in this regard \nyear and State-wise including Odisha; and  \n(d) the sectors in which the Government proposes to promote the Co-operatives in future? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n \n(a) As on 30.10.2023, 1582 Multi State Cooperative Societies (MSCS) are registered under \nMSCS Act in the country. The state-wise details are enclosed herewith. (Annexure – 1) \n(b) Out of 1582 MSCS, 88 societies including banks are under liquidation. The state-wise \ndetails are enclosed herewith. (Annexure – 2)  \n(c) The details of grants disbursed by National Cooperative Development Corporation \n(NCDC) including to the state of Odisha and to cooperative societies is at Annexure – 3. \n(d) In order to realise the vision of “Sahakar se Samriddhi”, the Ministry of Cooperation has \ntaken number of initiatives to strengthen the cooperatives from primary to apex level in \nconsultation with various Ministries / Departments of the Government of India, State \nGovernments / Union Territory Administrations, Statutory and Non- Statutory \norganisations such as National Bank for Agricultural and Rural Development, National \nDairy Development Board, National Cooperative Development Corporation, National \nFisheries Development Board and National Level Federations. \nA list of these initiatives and their progress is enclosed at Annexure – 4. \n  \n\nAnnexure – 1 \n \nTable 1: List of registered Multi State Cooperative Societies in different states in the \ncountry under Multi State Co-operative Societies as on 30th October, 2023. \n \nSl. No. State/UT Name Societies \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 22 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1 \n3 ASSAM 6 \n4 BIHAR 19 \n5 CHANDIGARH 1 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 8 \n7 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI 1 \n8 GOA 1 \n9 GUJARAT 49 \n10 HARYANA 21 \n11 HIMACHAL PRADESH 1 \n12 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 2 \n13 JHARKHAND 9 \n14 KARNATAKA 30 \n15 KERALA 40 \n16 MADHYA PRADESH 29 \n17 MAHARASHTRA 663 \n18 MANIPUR 4 \n19 NAGALAND 1 \n20 NEW DELHI 161 \n21 ODISHA 19 \n22 PONDICHERRY 5 \n23 PUNJAB 25 \n24 RAJASTHAN 74 \n25 SIKKIM 1 \n26 TAMIL NADU 133 \n27 TELANGANA 13 \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 168 \n29 UTTARAKHAND 5 \n30 WEST BENGAL 70 \n  Total 1582 \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-2 \n \nState – wise list of number of Multi-State Cooperative Societies, which are under \nliquidation \n \nName of State No. of MSCS \nAndhra Pradesh 2 \nBihar 1 \nDelhi 11 \nGujarat 2 \nJharkhand 1 \nMaharashtra 13 \nOdisha 11 \nPunjab 1 \nRajasthan 19 \nTamil Nadu 3 \nTelangana 2 \nUttar Pradesh 9 \nUT of Chandigarh 1 \nWest Bengal 5 \nTotal 81 \n \nState - wise list of number of Multi-State Cooperative Banks, which are under liquidation \n \nName of State No of Cooperative Bank \nGoa 1 \nGujarat 2 \nMaharashtra 4 \nTotal 7 \n \nAnnexure – 3 \n\n \nThe details of grants disbursed by National Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC) \nincluding to the state of Odisha and to cooperative societies \n(Amount in Cr.) \nFinancial Year Amount Disbursed Amount disbursed in \nState of Odisha \n2018-19 132.13  0.32 \n2019-20 134.27  0.37 \n2020-21 311.67  0.16 \n2021-22 281.99  0.54 \n2022-23 518.10  1.61 \n2023-24 (as on 29-11-2023) 44.66  1.72 \n \nThe detailed list of Societies year-wise.is attached. \n\nAnnexure-4 \nMinistry of Cooperation \nMinistry of Cooperation, since its inception on 6th July, 2021, has undertaken several initiatives to \nrealize the vision of “Sahakar-se-Samriddhi” and to strengthen & deepen the cooperative movement \nfrom Primary to Apex level Cooperatives in the country. List of initiatives taken and progress made \nso far are as follows: \nA. Making Primary Cooperatives economically vibrant and transparent  \n1. Model Bye-Laws for PACS making them multipurpose, multidimensional and \ntransparent entities: Government, in consultation with all the stakeholders, including \nStates/ UTs, National Level Federations, State Cooperative Banks (StCBs), District Central \nCooperative Banks (DCCBs), etc., has prepared and circulated Model Byelaws for PACS \nto all the States/ UTs, which enable PACS to undertake more than 25 business activities, \nimprove governance, transparency and accountability in their operations. Provisions have \nalso been made to make the membership of PACS more inclusive and broad-based, giving \nadequate representation to women and Scheduled Castes/Schedules Tribes. Model Byelaws \nhave been adopted by 31 States/ UTs so far. \n \n2. Strengthening of PACS through Computerization: In ord"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |384", "t": "Computerisation of Offices of Registrars of the Cooperative Societies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 384 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nComputerisation of Offices of Registrars of the Cooperative Societies \n \n384.    SHRI HARISH DWIVEDI: \n SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to take steps for computerisation of the offices of the Registrars \nof the Cooperative Societies and Multi- State Cooperative Societies (MSCS) across the \nStates/Union Territories;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with its status as on date; and \n(c) the benefits likely to accrue to the members of MSCSs from the said scheme? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n \n(a) to (b): Yes, Sir. The integrated digital portal for the office of Central Registrar of \nCooperative Societies (CRCS) i.e., www.crcs.gov.in has been launched on 6th August, 2023.  \nSeparately, the project for Computerisation of office of Registrars of Cooperative \nSocieties of all States / UTs has been approved as Centrally sponsored project with total \nfinancial outlay of Rs. 94.59 crore under which funds will be provided to States/UTs for \ndevelopment & maintenance of RCS portal (based on concerned State / UT Cooperative \nSociety Act) and for procurement of hardware & cloud infrastructure. The guidelines of the \nproject have already been circulated.  \n(c): The integrated digital CRCS portal for MSCS provides a complete digital ecosystem for \nMSCSs including Registration, Amendment of Bye-laws, Annual return filing, Branch \nOpening, Appointment of Sale Officer, Appeal, Inspection, Inquiry, Liquidation, Management \nof Cooperative Education Fund, Cooperative Rehabilitation Reconstruction & Development \nFund, Elections, etc. This will improve ‘Ease of Doing Business’ and transparency for the \nMSCS and their members as all the processes are digital and paperless.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |385", "t": "Delay in Conduct of Census", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 385 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 14, 1945 \n(SAKA) \n \nDELAY IN CONDUCT OF CENSUS \n \n†385.     SHRI DEEPAK BAIJ: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to conduct a census to get accurate \ndata of all sections of the society; \n \n(b) if so, the time by which such census is likely to be conducted; and \n \n(c) the reasons due to which the Government has been unable to conduct \nthe census on time? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) to (c):  The intent of the Government for conducting Census 2021 was \nnotified in Gazette of India on 28th March, 2019.  However due to outbreak \nof COVID-19 pandemic, the Census 2021 and the related field activities \nhave been postponed. In Census, data of all persons living in the country at \nthe time of Census is collected.  \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |386", "t": "Asian Games 2023", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.386 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nAsian Games 2023 \n \n386.  SHRIMATI SAJDA AHMED: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has assessed the performance of Indian contingents \nwho participated in the Asian Games 2023 in China and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government adopted a reward policy for emerging and high -\nperforming athletes in the Asian Games; \n \n(c) if so, the details of the reward policy adopted by the Government for athletes \nexcelling in the International and National Games; \n \n(d) whether the Government adopted any schemes for providing financial aid to \neminent sportspersons and their families who are facing financial hardships; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f) the details of strategy the Government has adopted to encourage sponsors to \nallocate investments toward athletes participating in swimming and track and \nfield events? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n \n(a)  Yes , Sir. The Gov ernment of India has assessed the performance of Indian \ncontingents who participated in the Asian Games 2023 in China. India has won \n107 medals which includes 28 Gold, 38 Silver and 41 Bronze  and India placed at \n\n4th in Asian Games 2023. It was India’s best performance at Asian Games in terms \nof medals won. \n \n(b) & (c)   Cash Awards to the medal winners in international sports events \nincluding those in Olympics Games, Commonwealth Games and Asian Games are \ngiven under the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports’ scheme ‘Special Awards to \nMedal Winners in International Sports events and their Coaches’. \n \n(d) & (e) The Ministry of Sports an d Youth Affairs  implements the S cheme of \n‘Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay National Welfare Fund for Sportspersons’ (PDUNWFS) \nwith the objectives to provide suitable assistance to outstanding sportspersons \nand their dependents now living in indigent circumstances, to provide suitable \nassistance to outstanding sportspersons injured during the period of their training \nfor competitions and also during the competitions, depending on the nature of the \ninjury, to provide suitable assistance to sportspersons for medical treatment, to  \nprovide suitable assistance to promote welfare of sportspersons generally in order \nto alleviate distress among them and to provide assistance to sportspersons living \nin indigent circumstances for training, procurement for sports equipment, \nparticipation in national and international sports events etc. \n \n(f)  The Government has set up National Sports Development Fund (NSDF) in 1998 \nto mobilize resources from corporates both in public and private sectors for \ndevelopment of sports in the country.  NSDF scheme has the provision which \nallows the donor to indicate a project along with any specific location/ aspect for \nfunding and also an executing agency for execution of the project while making \ndonation to the fund, subject to general policy guideline s and rules, if any, on this \nbehalf, if the amount of contribution is Rs.5 lakh and above.  Donors have the \nchoice to indicate specific sports including swimming and track and field events. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |387", "t": "Financial Assistance to Fisherfolk", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.387 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFinancial Assistance to Fisherfolk \n \n387.SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालनऔर डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the Government proposes to provide the basic facilities to the fisherfolk    \nso as to ensure their livelihood and if so, the details of action taken thereon; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to provide Kerosene to the traditional \nfisherfolk in a subsidized rate, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons \ntherefor; \n(c)   whether it has come to the notice of the Government that the traditional fisherfolk \nare in crisis due to the hike in price of fuel used in fishing boats and if so, the \naction taken to reduce the price; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to provide financial assistance to the   \nfisherfolk so as to ensure their livelihood; \n(e)    if so, the details of action taken thereon; and \n(f)   the details of the project and programmes of the Government for ensuring the \nwelfare of fisherfolk in Kerala? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a): Yes Sir. The Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) implemented  \nby the Department of Fisheries,  inter-alia envisages support towards livelihood \nstrengthening activities such as providing boats and nets to traditional fishermen, \nsupport for communication/tracking devices, providing sea-safety kits, insurance \ncover to fishers, support for acquisition of deep -sea fishing vessels, \nalternative/additional livelihood activities like seaweed cult ure & bivalve cult ure, \ntraining &  skill development, support towards cold -chain and marketing facilities . \nPMMSY also envisages financial assistance towards livelihood and nutritional \nsupport for socio-economically backward active traditional fisher’s families during the \nfishing ban/lean period.  Besides, the scheme also envisages construction of Fishing \nHarbours and Fish Landing Centres ensuring safe landing and berthing of fishing \nboats/vessels.  \n\n \n (b)& (c): Allocation of Kerosene is handled by Ministry of Petroleum and Natural \nGas (MoPNG). The MoPNG has informed that effective from 1 st March, 2020, the \nretail selling price of PDS Kerosene is being maintained at nil under-recovery level on \npan India basis. The MoPNG has further reported that Government makes allocation \nof Public Distribution System (PDS) Kerosene for cooking and lighting purpose only \non quarterly basis to States/UTs. Government vide order dated 21st Aug ust 2012, \nhas empowered States/ UTs to draw an allocation of one month’s quo ta of PDS \nKerosene at Non-Subsidized rates during each financial year for special needs such \nas natural calamities, religious functions, fishermen, various yatras etc. States/UTs \nmay seek further additional allocation after exhausting available quota. \n \n(d) & (e): Under PMMSY, during the last three financial years (FY 2020 -21 to 2022 -\n23), and current financial year (2023 -24), financial assistance for the projects \namounting to Rs. 17118.62 crore has been approved to various State Governments/ \nUnion Territories and other implementing agencies towards developmental of \nfisheries sector and livelihood support of the fisherfolk.  \n \n(f): During last three years (2020 -21 to 2022 -23) and current year (2023 -24), the \nDepartment of Fisheries, Government of India, under PMMSY has accorded \napprovals to the fisheries developmental proposals of Government of Kerala \namounting to Rs.903.32 crore. Fisheries developmental projects and activities \napproved for Government of Kerala towards development of fisheries and welfare of \nfisherfolk include, livelihood and nutritional support to active traditional fishers’ \nfamilies during fish ban, rearing & grow out ponds, hatcheries, ornamental fisheries, \nfish brood bank, fishing habours, fish landing centres deep sea fishing vessels, cages \nin reservoirs, bivalve culture, Re-circulatory Aquaculture System (RAS), biofloc \nculture units and pen culture units, ice -plants/cold storages, post-harvest \ntransportation vehicles, fish vending centres, fish feed mills , retail markets, fish kiosk, \nAquatic Referral Lab, boats and nets to traditional fishermen, Matsya  Seva Kendras, \nSagar Mitras and artificial reef for sustainability of fish stocks.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |388", "t": "Impact of Soil Erosion on Agriculture", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 388 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nIMPACT OF SOIL EROSION ON AGRICULTURE \n \n388. DR. AMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken cognizance of the finding of the UN’s Food and \nAgriculture Organization (UN FAO), which states that soil erosion is depleting the ability of \nsoil to produce food and could lead to a 10% loss in global crop production by 2050; and  \n(b) if so, the details of the initiatives that are proposed to be taken by the Government to \nsave our soil for agriculture? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nकृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b): In general, the extent and impact of soil erosion in the country depleting the ability \nof soil to produce food is being addressed by the Indian Council of Agricultural Research \n(ICAR) through research, technology development and its dissemination. ICAR through \nIndian Institute of Soil and Water Conservation (IISWC) has developed several location \nspecific bio-engineering measures to check soil erosion due to run-off of rainwater. ICAR-\nCentral Arid Zone Research Institute, Jodhpur has also developed sand dune stabilization \nand shelter belt technology to check wind erosion. Further, ICAR provides technical \nbackstopping and organizes regular training courses for field functionaries and farmers on \nparticipatory watershed management. \nSeveral steps have been taken to improve soil health and ecosystem up-gradation \nalong with improving soil fertility status in the country including soil erosion. Under National \nMission on Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA) to improve the soil health across the country, \nSoil Health Card (SHC) / Soil Health Management (SHM) scheme is operational through the \nState Governments under National Project on Management of Soil Health & Fertility. The \n\nmain objective of the scheme is to assist states in promoting Integrated Nutrient \nManagement (INM) for improving soil health and its productivity. Soil Health Card provides \ninformation to farmers on soil nutrient status of their soil and recommendation on appropriate \ndosage of nutrients to be applied for improving soil health and its fertility. NMSA also \naddresses the problem of soil erosion through its schemes namely Rainfed Area \nDevelopment (RAD) and On Farm Water Management (OFWM); Sub Mission on \nAgroforestry (SMAF) and Restructured National Bamboo Mission (NBM). \nThe Department of Land Resources (DoLR) has been implementing Integrated \nWatershed Management Programme (IWMP) since 2009-10 with primary objective to \ndevelop rainfed / degraded lands. IWMP was merged with the umbrella scheme of PMKSY \nin 2015-16 and named as the Watershed Development Component of the Pradhan Mantri \nKrishi Sinchayee Yojana (WDC-PMKSY). A total of 8214 watershed development projects \nwere sanctioned from 2009-10 to 2014-15 under Integrated Watershed Management \nProgramme (IWMP) in 28 States (now 27 States and 2 UTs of the Jammu & Kashmir and \nLadakh). Out of the 6382 projects funded by DoLR, 6376 projects have been completed. \nUnder WDC-PMKSY 1.0, a total of 6.56 lakh water harvesting structures have been created \n/ rejuvenated. An additional area of 14.54 lakh hectares has been brought under protective \nirrigation benefiting 31.94 lakh farmers during the same period. Under WDC-PMKSY2.0, \nDoLR has sanctioned 1149 projects to States/UTs covering an area of 50.55 lakh hectares \nand an amount of Rs. 2858.76 crore as central share of fund has been released to \nStates/UTs. Under WDC-PMKSY 2.0 during FY 2022-23 and FY2023-24 (till 2nd Qtr.), \n77,000 water harvesting structures have been created / rejuvenated. An additional area of \n0.81 lakh hectare has been brought under protective irrigation benefitting 4.30 lakh farmers \nduring the same period.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |389", "t": "Crops having benefits of MSP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.389 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nCROPS HAVING BENEFITS OF MSP  \n \n389. SHRI THIRUMAAVALAVAN THOL: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has the details of crops that are given the benefit of Minimum \nSupport Price/Fair and Remunerative Price and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the details of amount that has been spent on each of these crops during the last five years;  \n(c) the details of the regions which have been benefited through these schemes; and \n(d) the details of number of farmers in different regions who have been benefited by these \nschemes? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a):  The Government of India fixes Minimum Support Price (MSP) for 22 mandated \nagricultural crops viz paddy, jowar, bajra, maize, ragi, arhar, moong, urad, groundnut, \nsoyabean, sunflower, sesamum, nigerseed, cotton, wheat, barley, gram, masur (lentil), \nrapeseed/mustard, safflower, jute, copra and Fair and Remunerative Price for sugarcane, \non the basis of the recommendations of the Commission for Agricultural Costs &Prices \n(CACP), views of State Governments and Central Ministries/Departments concerned.  \n \n(b) to (d):  State-wise and crop-wise details of number of farmers benefitted and the \namount spent on procurement of agricultural crops at MSP during last five years are given at \nAnnexure. \n\nAnnexure  \n \nAnnexure referred to the reply of part (b) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 389 due for reply on 05.12.2023 \nTABLE 1: Number of farmers benefitted and the amount spent on procurement / MSP Value of Paddy during last five years \nStates/UTs \nKMS 2019-20 \n \nKMS 2020-21       KMS 2021-22  \n  \n  KMS 2022-23   KMS 2023-24 (Upto \n23.11.2023)  \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP \nvalue \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nANDHRA \nPRADESH \n679789 15152.16 803945 15967.04 839210 13049.88 678604 8471.77 19235 297.72 \nTELANGANA 1988630 20415.90 2164354 26637.39 1734675 21629.48 2016798 27163.66 119673 1778.57 \nASSAM   26537 578.46 20401 398.55 60025 1108.65 62423 1232.14 0 0.00 \nBIHAR  279402 3672.79 497097 6719.17 642234 8801.21 577064 8661.76 3573 60.37 \nCHANDIGARH 1537 39.91 1575 53.52 1956 53.48 1404 38.81 1714 55.39 \nCHHATTISGARH 1838593 13736.54 2053490 13418.88 2105972 18033.96 2047842 18031.18 165009 1995.04 \nDELHI  0 0.00 0 0.00 0 0.00 0 0.00 0 0.00 \nGUJARAT  5115 39.02 23799 208.14 25081 238.86 28357 364.32 2192 24.81 \nHARYANA  1891622 11796.51 549466 10676.14 310083 10842.82 282725 12227.40 279175 12937.64 \nHIMACHAL PR. 0 0.00 0 0.00 5851 54.15 3407 28.99 4116 45.86 \nJHARKHAND  53305 697.81 104092 1187.67 139359 1476.65 31855 355.36 0 0.00 \nJAMMU &KASHMIR 3143 27.53 7385 71.97 8724 79.42 6549 69.17 3402 38.28 \nKARNATAKA  27123 112.63 54319 389.31 73161 428.61 7646 42.43 0 0.00 \nKERALA  220066 1301.74 252160 1444.10 254072 1466.87 250373 1506.24 6072 34.26 \nMADHYA \nPRADESH \n436399 4765.80 587223 7035.73 661756 8981.92 647699 9537.48 0 0.00 \nMAHARASHTRA  526476 3197.01 624292 3585.30 595604 3590.87 516941 3805.98 789 6.40 \nODISHA   1161796 12948.71 1394647 14599.36 1514727 13923.27 1710773 16307.52 0 0.00 \nPUDUCHERRY  84 0.73 0 0.00 84 0.68 28 0.16 0 0.00 \nPUNJAB  1125238 29787.39 1057674 38293.23 933263 36707.54 903942 37513.74 889254 39537.56 \nNEF (Tripura)  13613 39.32 14434 45.76 27940 113.37 21712 92.21 0 0.00 \nTAMILNADU  585241 5947.57 852152 8477.54 400949 5406.13 599912 6970.18 86781 1190.69 \n UTTAR PRADESH 706549 10380.60 1022286 12619.91 947325 12843.94 940226 13493.65 68862 1074.63 \nUTTRAKHAND  83910 1867.06 78129 2024.23 78798 2264.71 58038 1845.34 31402 1179.56 \nWEST BENGAL  805186 4960.73 949362 5246.87 1318238 6920.23 1102759 6609.51 0 0.00 \nRAJASTHAN   0  0  0  0 563 14.42 0 0.00 0 0.00 \nALL INDIA TOTAL 12459354 141465.94 13112282 169099.84 12679650 168031.12 12497077 174369.01 1681249 60256.77 \nProcurement Sources: DFPD \nNote: Kharif  Marketing Season (KMS) \n \n \n\nTABLE 2: Number of farmers benefitted and the amount spent on procurement / MSP Value of Wheat during last five years \n \n \n \nStates/UTs \n  \nRMS 2019-20  RMS 2020-21 RMS 2021-22 RMS 2022-23   RMS 2023-24  \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In Crore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In Crore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nNo of \nfarmers \nbenefitted \nMSP value \n(Rs. In \nCrore) \nPUNJAB \n830528 23758.90 1049982 24473.84 887521 26113.81 793449 19434.92 834993 25748.14 \nHARYANA \n902937 17148.99 780962 14245.2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |39", "t": "Biological Diversity Act, 2002", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 39 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL MISSION ON SOIL HEALTH CARD \n \n*39.  SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  the total number of Soil Health Cards issued by the Government under the National \nMission on Soil Health Card till date, State/UT-wise especially in the State of Punjab; \n(b)  whether adequate measures are being taken by the Government to facilitate soil \ntesting at the district and block levels; \n(c)    if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d)  whether the Government has a robust monitoring mechanism to ensure regular testing \nof soil and providing information to farmers about proper usage of fertilizers; and  \n(e)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी    (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 39 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 REGARDING \nNATIONAL MISSION ON SOIL HEALTH CARD. \n \n(a): Soil Health Card/ Soil Health Management Schemes under National Project on \nManagement of Soil Health & Fertility are implemented through the State Government since \n2014-15. Now, this scheme has been merged in Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana as Soil \nHealth & Fertility component from year 2022-23. Under the scheme, since, 2014-15, so far \n23.59 crore soil health cards have been issued across the country including Punjab. So far \nas Punjab is concerned, 26.20 lakh soil health cards have been issued in the state.The \nState-wisedetails of number of Soil Health Cards issued under the scheme, including \nPunjab, are at Annexure-I. \n(b) & (c) : The Government is taking adequate measures to facilitate soil testing at the \ndistrict and block levels. Under the scheme, financial assistance of Rs. 75 lakh per lab is \nprovided for setting up static and mobile soil testing labs. There is also provision to provide \nRs. 1.50 lakh for setting up Village Level Soil Testing Labs (VLSTLs) at village / Gram \nPanchayat level.Since, 2014-15, total 8272 Soil Testing Labs (1068 Static, 163 Mobile, \n6376 Mini labs and 665 Village Level labs) have been established in States. In addition, \n1076 Mini Soil Testing labs have also been established in Krishi Vigyan Kendras and other \ninstitutes of Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR). Further to involve other \nstakeholders and to facilitate soil testing at block and village level close to the farmers filed, \nVillage Level Soil Testing Labs (VLSTLs) are set up by entrepreneurs, Self Help Groups \n(SHGs), Farm Producing Organizations (FPO), Primary Agricultural Credit Societies \n(PACS) etc.  \n(d) & (e):  Government is monitoring the scheme implementation through Soil Health \nCard portal (https://www.soilhealth.dac.gov.in ), which is updated on real time basis. A \nmobile app is used which capture geo-tagging to develop a national soil fertility map on a \nGeographic Information System platform. The Soil Health Card Portal has been revamped \nfor uploading test reports directly on portal. The soil health card generated directly \naccessed by farmers through mobile app and sms. Soil testing laboratories are mapped on \nportal with geo-coordinates.   \n\nProgress of the scheme against the target set in Annual Action Plan is reviewed \nregularly through video conferences, physical meetings, State visits etc. \nCrop based fertilizer usage advisories as per soil health cards are issued to farmers \nthrough State Governments, Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) and Agriculture Technology \nManagement Agencies (ATMAs) Network from time to time.  The Demonstrations on \nbalanced use of fertilizers, based on soil health card recommendations and training to the \nfarmers on proper & integrated use of fertilizers are integral part of the scheme. Indian \nCouncil of Agricultural Research (ICAR) also imparts training, organizes front line \ndemonstrations to educate farmers on these aspects. The Rural Awareness Works \nExperience (RAWE) programme is restructured for Agriculture graduates to generate \nawareness among farmers on use of soil health card for addressing soil health. \nSince 2015, around 6.45 lakh demonstrations, 93781 farmer’s trainings and 7425 \nfarmers melas have been organized through the State Governments under the Scheme.   \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise details of number of Soil Health Cards issued under the scheme \nSl. No. States/UTs Total \n1 Andaman & Nicobar  26,397  \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1,46,31,164  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  70,273  \n4 Assam  26,64,958  \n5 Bihar  1,46,69,629  \n6 Chhattisgarh 94,71,122  \n7 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman \n& Diu \n1,57,97 \n8 Goa  54,015  \n9 Gujarat  1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |390", "t": "Digitalization of PMFBY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 390 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nDIGITALIZATION OF PMFBY \n \n390. SHRI BRIJENDRA SINGH:  \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has implemented digitalization systems to streamline the \nadministration and disbursement of benefits under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana \n(PMFBY);  \n \n(b) if so, the details of the measures taken to ensure its user-friendliness and \naccessibility;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has any grievance redressal mechanism for non-payment \nand delayed payments of claims;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(e)  whether there are mechanisms in place to ensure the security and privacy of \nfarmers’ data collected through digitalization systems for the PMFBY; and  \n \n(f) If so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a) & (b) : Yes Sir.  For transparent calculation and settlement of claims by insurance \ncompanies, claims module namely, Digi Claim has been developed and implemented w.e.f. \nKharif 2022 season wherein all the claim s are worked out through National Crop Insurance \nPortal (NCIP) instead of by insurance company  and  paid to farmers accounts using Public \nFinance Management System (PFMS) ,   which can be monitored by Central and State \nGovernment.  Farmers do not operate the DigiClaim module and only GoI & State Govt. \nfunctionaries have access to it. However, on settlement of claims, a SMS with a link is sent \nto the farmer from which farmer can track the status of payment of claims.    \n(c) & (d) : PMFBY operational guidelines provide for stratified grievance redressal \nmechanism. At the initial level, for grievance redressal, each block & district have \n\ndesignated block/district level grievance redressal officer to respond to the grievances of \nFarmers, Banks, Insurance Companies etc. within 7 days of receipt of grievance. In case of \ndissatisfaction at block level , the matter can be brought before District Level Grievance \nRedressal Committee (DGRC) headed by the District Magistrate/Collector. In c ase of \ndisagreement with the decision by any party at district level; the same is represented to the \nState Level Grievance Redressal Committee (SGRC) within 15 days from the decision of \nDGRC.  SGRC is headed by Principal Secretary/Secretary of Nodal Depart ment. The \nSGRC has to dispose the grievance within 15 days time of receipt of grievance. The \ndecision of the Committee shall be accepted by all the parties.  \nA Krishi Rakshak Portal & Helpline  - a single toll-free phone number integrated grievance \nredressal mechanism having digital portal and a call centre has been developed to enable \nfarmers to lodge their grievances/concerns/queries. This Krishi Rakshak Portal & Helpline \nwas launched on pilot basis for Chhattisgarh \n(e) & (f) : National Crop insurance Portal (NCIP) was launched in April,  2018 and \nhenceforth it is being upgraded with new functionalities time to time to develop it as a \ncentralized IT platform to ensure the better administration, coordination, transparency and \ncentralized data source for g etting real time information and monitoring in a phased \nmanner. NCIP is a web -based portal for crop insurance ( https://pmfby.gov.in) that \nfacilitates to digitize  the processes of PMFBY like notification, farmer enrolmen t through \nvarious channel like Banks, CSC, Intermedia ries etc and claim calculation. It also enables \nthe utilities to manage the processes under the IT setup. NCIP captures the entire data \nrelating to insured crops and also serves as the repository for the  data relating to the crop \ninsurance scheme.   \n \n NCIP is hosted over SSL (https) and is accessible through login and password. Also, \nCAPTCHA is implemented to avoid machine intervention.  Request and response payload is \nencrypted at client side.  Under Dig iclaim process, claims are paid in the claim account or \nAadhar based account after Aadhar verification through PFMS platform. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |391", "t": "Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 391 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nSAANSAD ADARSH GRAM YOJANA \n \n391.  SHRI NIHAL CHAND: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the salient features of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY); \n(b)  the number of villages developed under the said Yojana since its \nlaunch across the country, State/UT-wise; \n(c)  whether funds are allocated by the Union Government for the \nsaid Yojana and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  whether necessary guidelines have been issued by the Union \nGovernment for the development of institutional infrastructure of \nthe villages under the said Yojana and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):  Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY) aims to create holistically \ndeveloped model Gram Panchayats  across the country. The salient \nfeatures of SAGY are placed in the Annexure-I. \n  \n(b):  The Hon‟ble Members of Parliament have identified 3,390 Gram \nPanchayats across the country under SAGY since its inception.   The \nState/ UT wise number of GPs identified under SAGY, since its \ninception is given in Annexure-II.  \n  \n(c): Under the Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana framework, the \ndevelopment of Gram Panchayats is envisaged through convergence \nand implementation of existing Government Schemes and Programmes \nunder the administrative control of respective Ministries without \nallocating additional funds. The amount of funds raised through \nvarious sources for the Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana Gram \nPanchayats is not maintained centrally. \n\n  \n(d): The scheme guidelines have been c onveyed to all the \nstakeholders with the view to achieve its intended objectives and also \nfor the development of institutional infrastructure of the villages under \nthe said scheme. As per the Guidelines, the responsibility of \nimplementing the SAGY largely depends on the functionaries \nconcerned at different levels. As the entire programme is implemented \nin a convergence model, the District Collector plays a significant role. \nIn the SAGY guidelines, as per component 10 (d) the District Collector \nis the nodal officer for implementing SAGY. The District collector \nconducts a monthly review meeting with representatives of the \nparticipating line departments. The Members of Parliament concerned \nchair the review meetings. The heads of the GPs concerned are invited \nfor these monthly meetings. The Ministry has provided training on \nSAGY implementation process to the Gram Panchayat heads. The \nMinistry has prepared a compendium on SAGY containing supporting \nmaterials to help various stakeholders in the implementation of SA GY \nand distributed the same amongst the participants of the said training \nprogrammes.The guidelines and the details of schemes for \nconvergence are available on the scheme portal, i.e., \nhttps://saanjhi.gov.in/. \n  \n*********** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \nAnnexure-I referred in point (a) of reply to Lok Sabha unstarred \nquestion No. 391 scheduled for answer on 5th December 2023. \n  \nThe salient features of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY) are as \nfollows:   \n  \n1.   It is a unique scheme of the Ministry of Rural  Development \nwherein, for the first time, the leadership, capacity, commitment and \nenergy of the Members of Parliament are being leveraged directly for \ndevelopment at the Gram Panchayat level.  \n \n2.    The goal of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY) is to tra nslate \nthe comprehensive and organic vision of Mahatma Gandhi on village \ndevelopment into reality. These „Adarsh Grams‟ are desired to serve as \n„nucleus of health, cleanliness, greenery and cordiality‟ within the \nvillage community and become schools of loc al development and \ngovernance, inspiring neighbouring Gram Panchayats.   \n  \n3.   SAGY aims at instilling certain values in the villages and their \npeople so that villages get transformed into models for others. These \nvalues include ensuring the involvement of all sections of society in all \naspects related to the life of the village, especially in decision -making \nrelated to governance, adhering to Antyodaya – enabling the “poorest \nand the weakest person” in the village to achieve well -being, affirming \ngender e quality, ensuring respect for women, guaranteeing social \njustice, instilling dignity of labour and the spirit of community service \nand voluntarism. Besides, promoting a culture of cleanliness, living in \nconsonance with nature – ensuring balance between dev elopment and \necology, preserving and promoting the local cultural heritage, \ninculcating mutual cooperation, self -help and self -reliance, fostering \npeace and harmony in the village community, bringing about \ntransparency, accountability and probity in public  life, nurturing local \nself-governance, adhering to the values enshrined in the Fundamental \nRights and Fundamental "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |392", "t": "Promoting Decarbonisation in Heavy Industries", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 392 \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nPROMOTING DECARBONISATION IN HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n \n392. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is a scheme in place to promote decarbonisation in carbon intensive and \ncarbon specific heavy industries;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether any measures are being taken by the Government to promote deca rbonisation \namong carbon intensive Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs); and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a): At present, Ministry of Heavy Industries (MHI) has no such scheme in place. \n \n(b): Does not arise. \n \n(c): The PSUs under Ministry of Heavy Industries have taken in –house initiatives to reduce \nthe CO2 emissions. \n \n(d): Major achievements are: \n \nBharat Heavy Electrical Ltd. (BHEL) : BHEL has  successfully demonstrate d its indigenous \nsolution for CO2 Capture through a 1.4 TPD CO2 Capture unit, integrated with its 0.25 TPD Coal-\nto-Methanol demonstration plant using amine-based CO2 absorption method. \n \nAdvanced Ultra Supercritical Technology  (AUSC), indigenously developed by BHEL  \nalong with NTPC and IGCAR, provides best -in-class efficiency in coal -based power generation \nby reducing CO2 emissions by about 11% as compared to Super Critical power plants. \n \nBHEL has installed Solar power plants at its m anufacturing units, spread across the \ncountry, totaling to 35 MW (approx.). \n \nCement Corporation of India (CCI) : CCI has installed Fly Ash Feeding System, hot air duct, \nreplacement of old motors to reduce the carbon emissions from its units.  \n \n********* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |393", "t": "Swayam Shakti Sahakar Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 393 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nSwayam Shakti Sahakar Yojana \n \n393. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the objectives of the Swayam Shakti Sahakar Yojana launched by National Cooperative \nDevelopment Cooperation (NCDC); \n(b) whether any financial assistance is provided to the eligible groups under the said yojana; \nand \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n  \n(a): National Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC), a statutory corporation under \nthe administrative control of Ministry of Cooperation, implements Swayam Shakti Sahakar \nYojana for extending financial assistance to eligible Credit Cooperatives for onward lending \nof working capital loan or term loans to women SHGs. \n \nThe objectives of the Scheme are as under: \n Access to the affordable cost-effective reliable financial services to the poor; \n Facilitating women SHGs to access adequate bank credit to undertake the \ncommon/collective socio-economic activities; \n Promotion of sustainable livelihood. \n \n(b)&(c): Yes Sir, NCDC provides financial assistance to the following types of Agricultural \nCredit Cooperatives under the scheme – \n Primary Agricultural Credit Cooperatives (PACS) \n District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs) \n State Cooperative Banks (StCBs) \n SHG Federated Cooperatives/ Cooperative federations. \n \nAs on 23/11/2023, NCDC has sanctioned financial assistance of ₹2000.11 crore and \nreleased financial assistance of ₹1300.11 crore to cooperatives under the Scheme. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |394", "t": "Strengthening of Central Registrar of MCSs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 394 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nStrengthening of Central Registrar of MCSs \n \n† 394.    SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \n  \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has initiated any action towards strengthening of the office of the Central \nRegistrar of Multi State Cooperative Societies (MCSs); \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the benefits likely to accrue to the members of MCSs from such action taken for strengthening \nof the above? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Sir. The Government has taken number of steps for strengthening the office of \nthe Central Registrar of Cooperative Societies (CRCS) including the following:  \n \n(i) The Multi State Cooperative Societies (Amendment) Act & Rules, 2023 have been \nnotified on 03.08.2023 and 04.08.2023 respectively, to strengthen governance, \nenhance transparency, increase accountability and reform electoral process, etc. in \nMulti State Cooperative Societies (MSCS) by supplementing existing legislation and \nincorporating the provisions of Ninety-seventh Constitutional Amendment.  \n(ii) An integrated web portal www.crcs.gov.in for the office of CRCS has been launched \non 06.08.2023 to provide a complete digital ecosystem for MSCSs including their \nRegistration, Amendment of Bye-Laws, Annual Return filing, Branch Opening, \nappointment of Sales officer, Appeal, Inspection, Inquiry, Liquidation, Management \nof Cooperative Education Fund, Cooperative Rehabilitation/ Reconstruction & \nDevelopment Fund, Elections, etc. This will improve ‘Ease of Doing Business’ and \ntransparency for the MSCS and their members as all the processes are digital and \npaperless. \n(iii) A separate State of the art office of CRCS is being established at World Trade Centre, \nNauroji Nagar, New Delhi to overcome the space constraints and enhance overall \nefficiency.  \n\n(iv) A total of 63 posts including 32 Technical and 31 other supporting staff have been \ncreated for strengthening the Office of CRCS. The Recruitments Rules for Technical \nposts have been notified on 01.09.2023 and 5 posts have already been filled on \ndeputation basis. \n \n(c):  The above initiatives will strengthen the cooperative movement amongst the multi state \ncooperative societies and benefit its members by improving governance, increasing \ntransparency, strengthening audit process, conducting free & fair elections, time bound \nredressal of members grievances, filling regulatory gaps and robust monitoring by CRCS \noffice. \n   \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |395", "t": "Funds raised under RKVY", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 395 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFUNDS RAISED UNDER RKVY \n395.   SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n  \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण \nमंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the amount of funds released for the State of Maharashtra during each of the last \nfive years under Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY);  \n \n(b) the details of the programmes being run under this Yojana; \n \n(c) whether the objectives of the said yojana have been achieved; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याणमंत्री   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b):  The following programmes are implemented under Rashtriya  Krishi Vikas \nYojana (RKVY).  \ni. Per Drop More Crop \nii. Sub-Mission on Agriculture Mechanization  \niii. Soil Health and Fertility \niv. Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojna \nv. Rain Fed Area Development \nvi. Agro Forestry \nvii. Crop Diversification Programme \nviii. RKVY- Detailed Project Report (DPR) component \n  \n The year wise status of fund released to the State Government of Maharashtra \nunder various programmes of RKVY during the last five years is given at Annexure-I.  \n\n(c) & (d):  Yes. As per the report submitted by State Government of Maharashtra, the \nfollowing interventions have been done under scheme:  \n(i) Pre and post -harvest facilities like Protected cultivation, Lining of farm pond, \nConstruction of well, Mechanization, Low cost onion storage structure and godowns \nare provided. \n(ii) The state has utilized the flexibility to plan projects as per its need. \n(iii) Public Private Partnership (PPP) projects are implemented for increasing farmers ’ \nincome. \n(iv) Risk of farmers is mitigated th rough, storage structures, post -harvest structures, \nAnimal husbandry projects. \n(v) National priorities are met through projects like Recla mation of problematic soils, \nAccelerated fodder development projects, foot and mouth disease. \nMore than 1160 custom Hiring Centers, 30 High Tech Hubs and more than 192 Village \nFarm Machinery Banks established for making available the machines  and equipment \nto the farmers on rentals. Integrated farming system have been  implemented in 0.233 \nhector area and  Drip and Sprinkler irrigation facilities are installed for more than six \nlakh hector area. More than 11 lakh tree have been planted to encourage and expand \ntree plantation on farm land. \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nStatement annexed to the Lok Sabha Question No.395 due for answer on 05.12.2023 \n \nFunds allocated and released to State Government of Maharashtra under various components rationalized RKVY during 2018-19 to \n2022-23 \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSl.  \nNo. \nName of the \ncomponent \n2018-19 2019-20   2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nAlloc. Rel. Alloc. Rel. Alloc. Rel. Alloc. Rel. Alloc. Rel. \n1 \nPer Drop \nMore Crop \n(PDMC)  \n480.00 360.00 325.00 325.00 400.00 300.00 400.00 200.00 400.00 334.00 \n2 \nSub-Mission \non \nAgricultural \nMechanizatio\nn (SMAM)  \n151.71 145.50 77.92 75.00 77.92 40.57 77.92 91.48 70.00 72.44 \n3 \nSoil Health & \nFertility \n(SHC&M) \n46.89 47.80 8.55 9.90 6.15 3.71 0.00 0.00 6.00 3.94 \n4 \nParamparaga\nt Krishi Vikas \nYojana  \n(PKVY)  \n16.92 0.00 34.11 34.11 7.47 0.20 0.42 0.00 7.46 4.50 \n5 \nRainfed Area \nDevelopment \n(RAD)  \n25.00 25.00 26.00 16.50 19.00 4.75 16.00 4.00 20.00 15.00 \n6 Agro Forestry \n4.00 2.00 1.50 0.75 2.00 1.60 2.00 0.00  This programme \ncomponent not \nimplemented. \n7 \nCrop \nDiversification \nProgramme \n(CDP)  \nNot implementing this component of RKVY scheme \n8 \nRashtriya  \nKrishi Vikas \nYojana \n(RKVY) DPR \nComponent  \n247.78 245.11 261.76 253.57 290.88 199.62 249.23 120.55 229.77 57.45 \n  Total 968.33 823.41 712.23 714.08 801.42 548.85 743.57 416.03 733.23 487.33 \n \n*Short/ nil release was due to non-submission of utilization certificate towards funds released in earlier years. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |396", "t": "Promotion of Electric Vehicles", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 396 \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF ELECTRIC VEHICLES \n \n396. SHRI KHAGEN MURMU:  \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN:  \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to formulate any plan to promote the use of Electric \nVehicles (EVs);  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government for increasing charging stations;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has formulated any policy to provide the facility of changing \nbatteries at charging stations only; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a) & (b):  Sir, following three schemes have been formulated by the Ministry of Heavy \nIndustries to promote the use of electric vehicles in the country: - \n \ni. Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid and Electric Vehicles in India (FAME \nIndia): The Government notified Phase-II of FAME India Scheme initially for a period of \nthree years commencing from 1st  April, 2019 with a total budgetary support of Rs. 10,000 \ncrore.  The Scheme was extended for a further period of 2 years up to 31st March 2024. \nii. Production Linked Inc entive (PLI) Scheme for Automotive Sector: The Government on \n15th Sep., 2021 approved the PLI Scheme for Automotive Sector with a budgetary outlay \nof Rs. 25,938 crores. \niii. PLI Scheme for Advanced Chemistry Cell (ACC): The Government on 12 th May, 2021 \napproved PLI Scheme for manufacturing of ACC in the country with a budgetary outlay \nof Rs. 18,100 crore.  The scheme envisages to establish a competitive ACC battery \nmanufacturing set up in the country for 50 GWh.  Additionally, 5 GWh of niche AC C \ntechnologies is also covered under the Scheme. \n \n(c) to (e):  To facilitate the establishment of Charging Infrastructure for Electric Vehicles in \nthe country, following major actions have been taken by the Government of India: \ni. Under Phase-II of FAME India Scheme Rs. 1000 Crore is earmarked for installation of \nCharging Infrastructure. \nii. Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has issued amendments in Model Building By -\nLaws and Urban and Regional Development Plans to establish charging stations in private \nand commercial buildings. \niii. Ministry of Power issued guidelines and standards for charging infrastructure for electric \nvehicles.  \n*******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |397", "t": "Development Report of PM-KISAN", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 397 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT REPORT OF PM-KISAN \n \n397.  SHRI SUNIL KUMAR MONDAL: \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the development report of the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM -KISAN) \nscheme launched by the Government specially in the State of West Bengal for the F.Y. 2022-23 and \n2023-24; and  \n(b) the details of developmental works along with future initiatives, if any, State and UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री      (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM -KISAN) is a Central Sector Scheme, being \nimplemented w.e.f. 1 st December, 2018.  The Scheme aims at providing financial assistance to all \nlandholding farmer families across the country, subject to certain exclusion criteria, to enable them to \ntake care of expenses related to agriculture and allied activities as well as domestic needs.  Under the \nScheme, an amount of Rs. 6000/ - per year is transferred in three 4 installments each of Rs.2000/- \ndirectly into the Aadhar seeded bank accounts of the farmers through Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT). \nUnder the PM -Kisan Scheme, ov er 2.80 Lakh Crores has been transferred to ov er 11 Cr. farmers \nacross the country including West Bengal. In West Bengal, PM-Kisan Scheme was onboarded in FY \n2021-22 and since then 51,14,346 Farmers have received benefits worth over 7380.46 Cr.  \n \n The details of funds released and number of beneficiaries of PM-KISAN for the State of West \nBengal in FY 2022-23 and FY 2023-24 are as follows: \n \nFinancial Year No. of beneficiaries Fund released \n2022-23 48,69,285 Rs. 2839.1 Crore \n2023-24 46,92,922 Rs. 1913.09 Crore \n \n(b) PM-KISAN Scheme is only concerned with providing financial assistance to all landholding \nfarmer families across the country. No other developmental works are  linked with the PM-KISAN \nScheme. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |398", "t": "FM Radio Malayalam", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 398 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023) \nFM RADIO MALAYALAM  \n398. SHRI RAJMOHAN UNNITHAN: \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is no Frequency Modulation (FM) Radio \nMalayalam Programmes available in the Kasaragod district and if so, \nthe action taken in this regard; \n(b) the details of Kannada FM channel are available in the northern part \nof the Kasaragod district; \n(c) whether the Government has noticed that Malayalam speaking \npeople are more here in Kasaragod district and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to increase the transmission \npower of the nearest Malayalam FM Radio Station in Kannur FM radio to \navail Malayalam FM radio in all parts of the district; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(f) the details of the progress of the New Community Radio Stations in \nKasaragod; and \n(g) the number of applications still pending for approval in this regard? \n  \n\n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND MINISTER \nOF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS (SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n(a) to ( e) Akashvani has a 100 Watt FM Relay Station at Kasargod \ncarrying AIR channel in Malayalam for localised coverage. The \nbroadcast follows line of sight and is not strictly bound by geographical \nboundaries. FM Broadcast from Akashvani FM transmitters at Madikeri \nand Udupi in adjacent districts of Karnataka is also available in some \nparts of Kasargod district. \nThe transmission capacity of FM Radio Station at Kannur has been \nincreased from 6 KW to 10 KW power. \nIn addition, three Private FM radio channels with transmission power of \n3KW (minimum) to 10 KW (maximum) are also operating in Kannur. \n(f) & (g) The Government of India has signed a Grant of Permission \nAgreement with Tellicherry Social Service Society  for setting up of a \ncommunity radio station in Kasar god.  There is no application pending \nfrom any other organizations in Kasargod. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |399", "t": "Dues of Farmers in PM-KISAN Scheme", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 399 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nDUES OF FARMERS IN PM-KISAN SCHEME \n \n399. SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE: \nSHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) whether there are any dues of farmers especially of Maharashtra under the PM-KISAN \nScheme and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the time by which the said payment is likely to be made and the average time by which \nthe beneficiaries receive full benefits;  \n(c) whether any grievance redressal mechanism exists for farmers in case of delay in \npayment and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d) the details of funds provided to the beneficiaries under PM-KISAN scheme, year-wise;  \n(e) the details of quantum of amount paid to ineligible beneficiaries, along with the State-\nwise details thereof; and  \n(f) whether the Government has any plan to recover the amount paid to ineligible \nbeneficiaries and if so, the steps taken to improve the impl ementation of PM -KISAN \nscheme? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b) The PM-KISAN scheme is a central sector scheme launched in February \n2019 to supplement the financial needs of land -holding farmers. Under this scheme, the \n\nfinancial benefit of Rs 6,000/ - per year in three equal instalments every four months is \ntransferred into the bank accounts of farmers' families across the country through Direct \nBenefit Transfer (DBT) mode . A farmer -centric digital infrastructure has ensured the \nbenefits of the scheme reach all the farmers across the country without any involvement \nof the mi ddlemen. Maintaining absolute transparency in registering and verifying \nbeneficiaries, the Government of India has disbursed over Rs 2.80 lakh Cr to more than \n11 Cr farmers. In the Maharashtra State, over Rs. 27,945 Cr has been transferred to more \nthan 1.11 Cr farmers since inception of the scheme. \n \nUnder the PM-KISAN Scheme, the State/UT Governments are mandated to  \nidentify and verify the beneficiaries as per the guidelines of the scheme. Once the farmer’s \ndetails are verified, the benefits of the scheme are released in to their bank accounts. \nThe validation and checks are ongoing processes, as and when the farmers have \ncompleted the mandatory requirements, they receive their accrued benefits of all the \ninstalments. \n \n(c) Under the PM -KISAN scheme, a robust grievance redressal system  is in place . \nThe farmers can lodge their grievances on the PM -KISAN portal and 24x7 IVRS facility \nfor effective and timely resolution. Further, the farmers also have the facility to register  \ntheir grievances, if any through Public Grievances portal.  In addition to the above, the \nGovernment of India  has developed Kisan e -Mitra (AI Chatbot) – Farmers’ digital \nassistance to address their queries in their own languages, thereby empowering the \nfarmers through technolog ical interventions. The Kisan e -Mitra is removing the existing \ntechnological and language barriers of the farmers. \n \n(d)  The year -wise details of funds provided to the beneficiaries under PM -KISAN \nscheme is attached at Annexure. \n \n(e) & (f) As stated above,  the benefits of the s cheme are released after due \nverification by the State/UT Governments. Further, the PM -KISAN portal has also been \nintegrated with the portals of UIDAI for Aadhaar authentication; with PFMS for validation \nof account and Government employees/pensioners data; with Income Tax department for \n\nvalidation of income tax payee status; and with National Payment Corporation of India \n(NPCI) for account validation and Aadhaar based payments  to identify the ineligible \nfarmers as per the guidelines of the scheme before processing their payments. \n \nFurther, Aadhaar seeding with bank accounts, e-KYC, and land-seeding of all the \nbeneficiaries are also being done to verify  their eligibility as per the guidelines of the \nscheme. States/UTs have been asked to expedite the process for saturation of the \nscheme with all the eligible beneficiaries and removal of ineligible beneficiaries , if any \nfound during the process . In addition, States/UTs have been mandated  to recover any \namount transferred to ineligible farmers. So far, more than Rs. 325 Cr has been recovered \nfrom the ineligible farmers. \n  \n\nAnnexure-1 \nDetails of number of beneficiaries and amount released under PM-KISAN year-wise and state-wise (as on 22/11/2023) \n 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \nSl. No. States \nNo. of \nBeneficiari\nes \nAmount  \nRelease \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease (In \nRs. Crore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease (In \nRs. Crore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount  \nRelease (In \nRs. Crore) \nNo. of \nBeneficiaries \nAm"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |4", "t": "Number of HEIs in the Country", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n��� ������ �\n��������������������� ���\n����������� ������������\n�\n����������������������������� �\n�\n���������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�������������������� �\n��������������� �������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ���� ������� ��� ����� ���� ��� ��� ��� ������ ���� ������� �������� ��� ���� �������� ������ ���� ���������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�\n������ �\n�\n���������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������������������ ��\n�\n���� ����������������� ��� ������� ������������ ������������� �������� ���������� ���������������������� ������\n�������������� ��������� ���� ���������������������������������������� �������������� ������ ������� ��� �������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������