��� �������� ��� ���� ��������� ��� ����������� ����\n���������� ���������������� ��������� ��� ������ ������������� �������� ��� ����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n�������������� ���� ������������ ���������������� ������������ ���� ������ �������� (�������� ��������)������������ ������������ ���� ������ �������� (�������� ��������) \n���� �������������� ������������ ���� ������������ ���� ������ �����������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ������������ ���������\n�\n�������������������������������������� � �����������������������������������������\n��� ����� ���������� ����������� ���� ����������� ��� ��������� ���� ��������� ������\n��������� ��������� ��� ��������� �� ��������� ������ ������ ��������� ���� �� �� ����\n������������ ����������� ����������� ������ ������� ������������� ���� ���� ������ �����\n������������������ ������������ ������������������ �������� ���������������������� ������ ���������������������� ������������ ���������������� ������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������� ���������������� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |400", "t": "Farmer Producer Organisations (FPOs)", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 400 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFARMER PRODUCER ORGANISATIONS (FPOS) \n \n400. SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of Farmer Producer Organisations (FPOs) in the country as on date, State-\nwise; \n \n(b) whether the Government has formulated anypolicy for taxation of FPOs; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) the funds allocated for promotion of FPOsduring the current financial year? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a): The Government of India is implementing the Central Sect or Scheme  (CSS) for \n“Formation and Promotion of 10,000 Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs)” to form and \npromote 10,000 new FPOs.  As on 31.10.2023, under the said scheme so far, 7476 FPOs \nhave been registered. The State-wise number of FPOs registered is at Annexure-I.  \n \n(b) & (c):  The existing provisions for taxation of FPOs under the Company Act are as \nfollows: \ni. Under Companies Act: Under section 80PA, FPOs registered as producer companies \nare exempt from income tax upto  Rs.100 crore. FPOs registered as co mpanies need \nto pay Minimum Alternate Tax (MAT) under the provisions of section 115JB (MAT \nprovision).   \nii. Under Cooperative Act: FPOs registered as cooperative societies get the benefit of \nSection 115JC and their total income is reduced by deduction available under section \n80P. Thus FPOs registered as cooperative societies do not have to pay Minimum \nAlternate Tax (MAT) or income tax.   \n \n(d):  For the current financial year 2023 -24, funds of Rs .955 crores is allocated under the \nCSS for formation & promotion of 10,000 FPOs. \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nState-wise number of FPOs registered \n \nSr. No. States/UT No. of FPOs registered \n1  Andaman & Nicobar 7 \n2  Andhra Pradesh 338 \n3  Arunachal Pradesh 106 \n4  Assam 289 \n5  Bihar 465 \n6  Chhattisgarh 158 \n7  Goa 8 \n8  Gujarat 386 \n9  Haryana 146 \n10  Himachal Pradesh 116 \n11  Jammu & Kashmir 199 \n12  Jharkhand 268 \n13  Karnataka 270 \n14  Kerala 120 \n15  Ladakh 3 \n16  Lakshadweep 1 \n17  Madhya Pradesh 561 \n18  Maharashtra 516 \n19  Manipur 69 \n20  Meghalaya 52 \n21  Mizoram 48 \n22  Nagaland 81 \n23  Odisha 405 \n24  Puducherry 4 \n25  Punjab 117 \n26  Rajasthan 470 \n27  Sikkim 13 \n28  Tamil Nadu 388 \n29  Telangana 263 \n30  Tripura 50 \n31  Uttar Pradesh 1137 \n32  Uttarakhand 134 \n33  West Bengal 288 \n Grand Total 7476 \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |401", "t": "Agriculture Technology", "head": " \n \n \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 401 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nAGRICULTURE TECHNOLOGY \n \n401. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री be \nplease to state:     \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking various steps to ensure that the exponential growth in the \nagriculture technology sector is appropriately leveraged for the benefit of the farmers of the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has also constituted any screening Committee in this regard; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृषि एवं कक सान कल्याण मंत्री     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (d): Yes, the government acknowledges the significant potential of the Agri -Tech industry in \ndriving the transformation of food syste ms and enhancing the efficiency and  profitability of small \nfarms. Further, various initiatives and policies have been introduced to promote technological \nadvancements in agriculture, recognizing the role of Agri -Tech in addressing challenges and \nfostering sustainable agricultural practices.  The government has laid the initiatives to establish a \nDigital Public Infrastructure  (DPI) for Agriculture, incorporating standardised Registries, Krishi \nDecision Support System, Agri Data Exchange, Consent Manager, Sand Box and a Unified Farmer \nService Interface (UFSI). This initiative aims to enhance interoperability and convergence of efforts, \nfostering the development of applications in the agricultural sector using emerging digital \ntechnologies. Further, to complement these efforts and create opportun ities for public -private \npartnerships, a Screening Committee has been constituted to invite Expressions of Interest from \nnon-government entities and to recommend suitable entities for collaborative work with government. \nThe copy of the order of constitution of Screening Committee is given at Annexure. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n  \n\n\n \n***** \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |402", "t": "Lifestock Health Care", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nADMITTED UNSTARRED QUESTION No.402 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nLIFESTOCK HEALTH CARE \n402. SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER:  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of steps taken by the Government to implement the recommendation of NK Jain \nCommittee report;  \n(b) whether the Government realise the inadequacy of Lifestock health care; and  \n(c) if so, the details of steps taken by the Government to address such issue? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a). The Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal \nHusbandry and Dairying, Government of India has taken steps to make the use of Aadhar number \nin various programmes/schemes like National Livestock Mission (NLM), Animal Husbandr y \nInfrastructure Development Fund (AHIDF), Rastriya Gokul Mission (RGM). The Aadhar number \nis also required for registration of farmers on National Digital Livestock Mission (NDLM) portal \nwhere data related to animal productivity, health and vaccination is updated. \n \n(b & c). Animal Husbandry is a  state subject . However, the Department is supporting and \naugmenting livestock health care through the implementation of Livestock Health &  Disease \nControl Programme (LHDCP) which is a Central sector scheme, in all states/UTs, with the aim of \nreducing risk to animal health by prophylactic vaccination against diseases of animals, c apacity \nbuilding of Veterinary services, disease surveillance and strengthening Veterinary i nfrastructure. \nThe major activities supported a re vaccination against Foot and Mouth Disease (FMD), \nBrucellosis, Peste des Petits Ruminants (PPR) and Classical Swine Fever (CSF ), Assistance to \nStates for Control of Animal Disease (ASCAD) for control of state prioritized economically \nimportant exotic, emergent and zoonotic animal diseases  and Establishment and Strengthening of \nVeterinary Hospitals and Dispensaries -Mobile Veterinary Units (ESV HD-MVU). Further, under \nESVHD-MVU, the Central Government has provided 100% financial assistance to the States/UTs \ntowards procurement and customization of MVUs to strengthen veterinary health services . \nAdditionally, there are National Action Plans  and guidelines  on diseases like Avian Influenza, \nAfrican Swine Fever (ASF) , Glanders and Lumpy Skin Disease (LSD)  for control and \ncontainment of diseases within a definite time frame. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |403", "t": "National Fisheries Development Board", "head": "O.I.H. \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 403 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNational Fisheries Development Board \n \n \n403.    SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the aims and objectives of the National Fisheries Development Board; \n(b)  the details of the schemes being implemented by the Government and the \nNational Fisheries Development Board for welfare of fishermen in the country; \nand \n(c)  the amount of funds allocated for development of fisheries during each of the \nlast three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a): The aims and objectives of National Fisheries Development Board are to (i ) \nbring activities relating to fisheries and aquaculture for focused attention and \nprofessional management, (ii) enhance contribution of fish and fisheries towards \nfood and nutritional security, (iii) improve production, processing, storage, transport \nand marketing of the products from capture and culture fisheries, (iv) achieve \nsustainable management and conservation of natural aquatic resources including the \nfish stocks, (v) apply modern tools of research and development including genetics \nand biotechnolo gy for optimizing production and productivity from fisheries, (vi) \nprovide modern infrastructure mechanisms for fisheries, (vii) generate substantial \nemployment and  (viii) train and empower women in fisheries sector.  \n\n \n(b): The Department of Fisheries, Mi nistry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and \nDairying, Government of India is  implementing a flagship scheme namely Pradhan \nMantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) with a highest ever investment of Rs. \n20,050 crore for a period of 5 years with effect from 2020 -21 to 2024 -25 for holistic \ndevelopment of fisheries in the c ountry and welfare of fishermen is at the core of the \nScheme. The Department of Fisheries, Government of India, u nder PMMSY, is \nimplementing various welfare related activities, which include providing livelihood \nand nutritional support for socio -economically backward activ e traditional fishers’ \nfamilies during fishing ban/lean period, providing safety kits to fishers, \nCommunication and/or Tracking Devices for traditional and motorized vessels like \nVHF/DAT/MAVIC/Transponders, providing boats and nets to traditional fi shermen. \nFurther, under PMMSY, the Department of Fisheries, Government of India is also \nimplementing Group Accident I nsurance Scheme to Fishers, Insurance  premium \nsubvention for fishing vessels, training and capacity building programmes, promotion \nof domestic fish consumption and awareness campaign through National Fisheries \nDevelopment Board. In addition , Kisan Credit Card is also provided to fishers and \nfish farmers to meet their short term credit requirement.   \n(c):  The amount of funds allocated for development of fisheries during each  of the \nlast three years  for development of fisheries and welfare of fishermen are detailed \nbelow: \n(Rs. in crore) \nSl.No Year Fund allocated \n(i) 2020-21 910.42 \n(ii) 2021-22 1391.30 \n(iii) 2022-23 1496.09 \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |405", "t": "Veterinary Hospitals", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED ADMITTED QUESTION NO. 405 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n“VETERINARY HOSPITALS” \n405. SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY and DAIRYING  \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of veterinary hospitals per village, State-wise;  \n(b) whether it is true that most vet hospitals suffer from limited staff, unavailability of needed \nmedicines, delayed services etc., forcing the animal keepers to go to private hospitals putting \nextra burden on owners’ pockets as per various reports and claims made by cattle farmers; \n(c) if so, the details of remedial steps taken by the Government; \n(d) whether the Government has undertaken any audit mechanism to keep a check on the \nfunctioning of vet hospitals; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) & (b) Animal Husbandry is a State subject, therefore establishment of veterinary \nhospitals, dispensaries and recruitment of necessary staff in these veterinary institutions for \nproviding veterinary services is under the purview of the State Government. The State -wise \ndetail of veterinary hospitals, dispensaries and Veterinary Aid Centre as Animal Husbandry \nStatistics is enclosed as Annexure-I. \n \n(c) In order to supplement State /UT Government’s efforts, the  Government of India is \nimplementing “ Establishment & Strengthening of Veterinary Hospitals & Dispensaries - \nMobile Veterinary Units (ESVHD -MVU)” a component under Livestock Health & Disease \nControl Programme (LH&DCP). Under the scheme financial assistance of 100% is provided \ntowards non-recurring expenditure on procurement of Mobile Veterinary Units (MVUs), and \ncentral share is provided as recurring expenditure for running of these MVUs for  delivery of \nveterinary services, which includes veterinary medicines by technical staff for treatment of \nanimals at the farmer’s doorstep.  \n \n The Government has released 100% financial assistance of Rs.682.85 Crore to 34 \nStates/UTs for non-recurring expenditure on procurement of 4340 MVUs during 2021-22 and \n2022-23 and Rs.55.45 Crore to 13 States towards recurring expenditure during 2023-24. \n \n(d) & (e) Since, veterinary services of the State Government is State subject, therefore \nfunctioning of veterinary hospitals are under the purview of State Government \n**** \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nNumber of Hospitals, Dispensaries  and No. of Veterinary Aid Centre as on \n31stMarch, 2023 \n \nNumber of Veterinary Institutions (as on 31/03/2023) \nS.No. States / UT Veterinary \nHospitals / \nPolyclinics \nVeterinary \nDispensaries \nVeterinary \nAid Centre \n(Stockman \nCentres / \nMobile \nDispensaries) \nTotal Registered \nVeterinary \nPractitioners  \nas on 31st \nJanuary 2023 \n \n1 Andhra Pradesh 337 1577 1558 3472 5324 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh \n16 183 311 510 \n232 \n3 Assam 21 421 767 1209 3154 \n4 Bihar 1098 39 1595 2732 3464 \n5 Chhattisgarh 350 835 72 1257 1215 \n6 Goa 5 25 50 80 222 \n7 Gujarat 34 741 1057 1832 4447 \n8 Haryana 1048 1815 22 2885 2372 \n9 Himachal \nPradesh \n466 1762 1228 3456 \n1402 \n10 Jammu & \nKashmir \n19 1256 225 1500 \n1057 \n11 Jharkhand 35 424 433 892 927 \n12 Karnataka 697 2156 1381 4234 4786 \n13 Kerala 279 870 15 1164 5172 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 1064 1583 65 2712 3039 \n15 Maharashtra 39 1976 2841 4856 10570 \n16 Manipur 59 151 23 233 561 \n17 Meghalaya 4 126 121 251 433 \n18 Mizoram 11 67 69 147 351 \n19 Nagaland 11 55 100 166 350 \n20 Odisha 30 511 3553 4094 2791 \n21 Punjab 1389 1489 20 2898 4285 \n22 Rajasthan 2975 0 6434 9409 4853 \n23 Sikkim 23 68 63 154 199 \n24 Tamil Nadu 189 2741 3446 6376 6245 \n25 Telangana 107 909 1201 2217 2124 \n26 Tripura 16 65 459 540 451 \n27 Uttarakhand 330 10 779 1119 1156 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 2208 267 2575 5050 6907 \n29 West Bengal 113 613 2609 3335 2661 \n30 A&N Islands 10 13 49 72 59 \n \n \n \n\n31 Chandigarh 5 9 0 14 13 \n32 Ladakh 4 9 127 140 76 \n33 D&N Haveli and \nDD \n1 2 14 17 \n5 \n34 Delhi 49 29 0 78 466 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 9 0 9 34 \n36 Puducherry 0 17 75 92 535 \n Total  13042 22823 33337 69202 81938 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |406", "t": "Agri-Tech Industry", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 406 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nAGRI-TECH INDUSTRY \n \n406.  SHRI T.R. BAALU: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री \nbe please to state:     \n \n(a) whether the Government agrees with the view that the Agri-Tech industry can play a vital \nrole in leading the transformation in food systems and improving the efficiency, quality and \nprofitability of small farms; \n(b) if so, the measures that have been taken or being taken by the Government keeping in \nmind that Agri -Tech has huge transformational potential with capabilities of automating \nagricultural functions, improving traceability of the agriculture supply chain, fighting risks and \nuncertainties through predictive power, directly connecting farmers with consumers, \nAgribusinesses, credit institutions and with the Government policies and many more; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री        (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c) : The government acknowledges the significant potential of the Agri -Tech industry in \ndriving the transformation of food systems and enhancing the efficiency and profitability of small \nfarms. Further, various initiatives and policies have been introduced to promote technological \nadvancements in agriculture, recognizing the role of Agri -Tech in address ing challenges and \nfostering sustainable agricultural practices. The government has laid the initiatives to establish \na Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) for Agriculture, incorporating standardised Registries in the \nagriculture sector, Krishi Decision Su pport System, Agri Data Exchange, Consent Manager, \nSand Box and a Unified Farmer Service Interface (UFSI). This initiative aims to enhance \ninteroperability and convergence of efforts, fostering the development of applications in the \nagricultural sector using emerging digital technologies. Further, to complement these efforts and \ncreate opportunities for public -private partnerships, a Screening Committee has been \nconstituted to invite Expressions of Interest from non -government entities and to recommend \nsuitable Agri-Tech entities for collaborative work with government.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |407", "t": "Adarsh Gram under SAGY", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 407 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nADARSH GRAM UNDER SAGY \n \n407.  SADHVI PRAGYA SINGH THAKUR: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the manner and possibilities for carrying out development of \nAdarsh Gram under the Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY); \n(b)  whether under this Yojana in every Parliament Constituency each \nMember has to adopt one village respectively and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(c)  whether the development of such villages entirely depend upon \nthe intent of the State Government and with no special powers \nand additional funds being given, the exercise of adopting a \nvillage merely remains a formality and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n(d)  whether guidelines have been issued by the Government in this \nregard and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (d) Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY) aims to create \nholistically developed model Gram Panchayats (GPs) across the \ncountry.  Under the Yojana, each Members of Parliament had to \nidentify and develop three Adarsh   Grams during 2014 -2019 and five \nsuch Adarsh Grams (one e ach year) during 2019 -2024.  The Gram \nPanchayats identified under SAGY prepare a Village Development \nPlan(VDP) through participatory process in Gram Sabha, which is a \nroadmap for the holistic development of the GPs.  As per the SAGY \nGuidelines, the responsi bility of implementing the SAGY rests with \nelected representatives of Panchayats and the functionaries of the \n\nprogrammes’ concerned at diferent levels.  The District Collector is the \nnodal officer for implementing SAGY.   Roles and responsibilities of \ndifferent functionaries of SAGY have been described under the Chapter \n10 of Guidelines (https://saanjhi.gov.in/Guidlines.aspx).   \n  \nFurther, the Ministry provided training on SAGY implementation \nprocess to the Gram Panchayat heads. The Ministry has prepared a \ncompendium on SAGY containing supporting materials to help various \nstakeholders in the implementation of SAGY and distributed the same \namongst the participants of the said training programmes. The \nScheme Guidelines containing details of the scheme have been  \nprepared in 14 languages and   are available on the scheme portal, i.e., \nhttps://saanjhi.gov.in/.  \n  \nThe development of Gram Panchayats is envisaged through \nconvergence and implementation of existing Government Schemes \n(Central & State) and other voluntary  programmes under the \nleadership of Member of Parliament without allocating additional \nfunds. SAGY guidelines envisage that SAGY should proactively tap and \nleverage the resources and the strengths of the private, voluntary and \ncooperative sectors.  Amount of funds raised through various sources \nfor the Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana Gram Panchayats is not \nmaintained centrally.  \n  \nAs on 30th November, 2023, there are total 2,51,946 number of \nactivities planned under Village Development Plans under SAGY.   Out \nof which total 2,09,113 number of activities are completed, which \ncomes around 83% of such planned activities.  \n  \n  \n  \n************ \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |408", "t": "Rural Development Projects in Sonipat", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 408 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nRURAL DEVELOPMENT PROJECTS IN SONIPAT \n \n408.  SHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the present status of the various projects related to rural \ndevelopment in the Parliamentary Constituency of Sonipat during \nthe last five years; \n(b)  the details of funds allocated, released and utilized under the \nvarious schemes to the said constituency during the said period; \n(c)  the number of proposals received, approved and rejected for the \nsaid Constituency during the last three years, scheme-wise; and \n(d)  the reasons due to which the proposals were not approved? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) : The Department of Rural Development (DoRD), Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment (MoRD) is implementing a number of schemes, namely \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(Mahatma Gandhi NREGS), Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana–Gramin \n(PMAY-G), Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), Deendayal \nAntyodaya Yojana–National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM), \nDeen Dayal Upadhayay– Gramin Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY), Rural \nSelf Employment Training Institutes (RSETIs) and National Social \nAssistance Programme (NSAP). Further, Department of Land \nResources (DoLR), MoRD is also implementing Watershed Development \nComponent (WDC) of Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) \nwhich is principally for development of rainfed portions of net \ncultivated area and culturable wastelands. \n  \nDuring last 5 years  i.e. from the Financial Year 2018-19 to 2022-23, \n\n  \ni. Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, total 2805 number of works has \nbeen completed in Sonipat district of Haryana (Source: As per \nNREGASoft). \nii. Under PMAY-G, total 968 houses have been completed in \nSonipat. \niii. Under DAY-NRLM, 2139 Self Help Groups (SHGs), 136 Village \nOrganisation (VOs) and 6 Cluster Level Federations (CLFs) \nformed, Rs.190.60 lakh Revolving Fund and Rs.292.00 lakh \nCommunity Investment Fund (CIF) released to the Parliamentary \nConstituency of Sonipat \niv. Under PMGSY, during last five year and current year i.e. since \nApril 2018, 14 roads works of 131 Km length have been \nsanctioned in the Sonipat District. Road length of 128 Km has \nbeen completed during the said period in Sonipat District. \nv. Under DDU-GKY, 663 candidates trained and 400 placed under \nDDU-GKY in Sonipat from 2019 to 2023 [Source: Inputs from \nHaryana State] \nvi. Under RSETIs, 2514 candidates trained and 1831 placed under \nRSETIs in Sonipat [Source: National Centre for Excellence of \nRSETIs (NACER)] \nvii. NSAP is a social security programme.   Hence, it does not have \nany project. \nviii. The activities undertaken under WDC-PMKSY, inter alia, include \nridge area treatment, drainage line treatment, soil and moisture \nconservation, rainwater harvesting, nursery raising, pasture \ndevelopment, livelihoods for asset-less persons etc. WDC-PMKSY, \nthrough these interventions, seeks to ensure sustainable \ndevelopment through improved natural resource management \nand better resilience of farmers to climate change.  The period of \nWDC-PMKSY 1.0 projects (sanctioned during 2009-10 to 2014-15) \nwas over on 31 March, 2022. Under WDC-PMKSY 1.0, 2 projects \nsanctioned in Sonipat district have been completed. \n(b): The Central component of funds under the schemes/programmes \nof this Ministry are released to the States/UTs, which subsequently \nallocate funds to the districts.  However, the details of funds \nallocated, released and utilized under the various rural development \nschemes of MoRD during the last five years in the said constituency, \nwherever applicable/maintained, are  given at Annexure. \n  \n\n(c) & (d): Proposals under the schemes/programmes of this Ministry \nare received through the respective States/UTs and this Ministry does \nnot consider proposals directly from any district/constituency. \n  \ni. Mahatma Gandhi  NREGS is a demand driven wage employment \nscheme and funds are released to the States/UTs on the basis of \n\"agreed to\" Labour Budget (LB) as per the proposal received from \nthem.  Total number of works completed  during the last five \nFinancial years under this Scheme is given at para (a) to the \nreply. \nii. Under PMAY-G, the beneﬁciaries are identiﬁed based on the \nhousing deprivation parameters and exclusion criteria prescribed \nunder Socio Economic Caste Census (SECC)-2011. These \nparameters/criteria are applied on SECC 2011 & Awaas+ 2018 \ndatabase and thus a priority list called the Permanent Wait List \n(PWL) is prepared for the States/UTs. This PWL is further \nsubjected to veriﬁcation by Gram Sabhas and completion of an \nAppellate Process thereafter. Therefore, no proposal for \nselection of households for beneﬁt under PMAY-G is required \nfrom the States/UTs. \niii. Th"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |409", "t": "Material Components Dues under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 409 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nMATERIAL COMPONENTS DUES UNDER MGNREGS \n \n409.  SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details regarding the pending wage dues and material \ncomponent dues under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) till date, State and \ndistrict-wise including Thoothukudi district of Tamil Nadu; \n(b)  whether the entire funds allocated for the MGNREGS have got \nexhausted in the month of October, 2023; \n(c)  if so, the measures taken to disburse funds to the beneficiaries \non time; \n(d)  whether the beneficiaries are given any receipts mentioning their \npending dues in case of delay in wage payment; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the manner in which \nbeneficiaries are notified about their pending dues? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):   Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) is a demand driven wage employment \nscheme. Fund release is a continuous process. Government of India \nreleases material and admin funds to State Govern ment and State \nGovernment releases the fund to its Districts accordingly. Government \nof India does not release material funds directly to the District. \n  \n      State/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for wage component \nunder Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as on  29.11.2023 are given at \nAnnexure-I. \n\n  \n      State/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for material components \nunder Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in the financial year 2023 -24 (as on \n29.11.2023) are given at Annexure-II. \n  \n(b):       No, Sir. \n  \n(c):       Does not arise. \n  \n(d)&(e):   Beneficiaries is eligible for wage payment within 15 days \nafter the completion of work. A beneficiary can find the pending dues \nof wage payment through the report R1.1.4 of Gram Panchayat report \nof the concerned state. Also, a provision has been made in \nJanmanrega App to check the status of wage payment of a beneficiary \nagainst the work done by her/him. The beneficiary can check the \ndetails just by providing Job card number. \n   \n******** \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n409 dated 05.12.2023. \n  \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for wage component \nunder Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as on 29.11.2023. \nSl. No.  States/UTs Wage liability (Rs. in lakh) \n1 Andhra Pradesh  11056.29 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  1848.27 \n3 Assam  9324.32 \n4 Bihar  6495.58 \n5 Chhattisgarh  5954.57 \n6 Goa 22.65 \n7 Gujarat  66.94 \n8 Haryana  1759.06 \n9 Himachal Pradesh  2992.21 \n10 Jammu and Kashmir  8243.87 \n11 Jharkhand  508.44 \n12 Karnataka  17789.54 \n13 Kerala 5194.67 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 5390.17 \n15 Maharashtra 9402.22 \n16 Manipur 0.00 \n17 Meghalaya 399.25 \n18 Mizoram  2784.32 \n19 Nagaland 1080.42 \n20 Odisha  6493.54 \n21 Punjab  332.72 \n22 Rajasthan  35883.86 \n23 Sikkim 58.24 \n24 Tamil Nadu  26185.91 \n25 Telangana  1098.64 \n26 Tripura  1770.82 \n27 Uttar Pradesh  35446.96 \n28 Uttarakhand  3557.55 \n29 Andaman & Nicobar 0.00 \n30 Lakshadweep 0.00 \n31 Puducherry  202.14 \n32 Ladakh 716.54 \n33 Dadra & Nagar Haveli & Daman \n& Diu  0.00 \nTotal  2,02,059.71 \n  \n  \n\n Annexure-II referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n409 dated 05.12.2023. \n State/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for material components under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS in the financial year 2023-24 (as on 29.11.2023) \nSl. No.  States/UTs Material liability (Rs. in lakh) \n1 Andhra Pradesh  22255.29 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  2605.69 \n3 Assam  9785.98 \n4 Bihar  31316.67 \n5 Chhattisgarh  17703.93 \n6 Goa 0.83 \n7 Gujarat  25608.60 \n8 Haryana  2875.04 \n9 Himachal Pradesh  7386.77 \n10 Jammu and Kashmir  4179.71 \n11 Jharkhand  13245.13 \n12 Karnataka  36272.97 \n13 Kerala 9169.77 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 32538.20 \n15 Maharashtra 30153.23 \n16 Manipur 2327.71 \n17 Meghalaya 7917.08 \n18 Mizoram  60.65 \n19 Nagaland 9232.55 \n20 Odisha  46183.59 \n21 Punjab  7706.54 \n22 Rajasthan  32988.99 \n23 Sikkim 1012.57 \n24 Tamil Nadu  10622.48 \n25 Telangana  33128.55 \n26 Tripura  3459.74 \n27 Uttar Pradesh  89338.37 \n28 Uttarakhand  4756.01 \n29 Andaman & Nicobar 21.92 \n30 Lakshadweep 0.14 \n31 Puducherry  0.77 \n32 Ladakh 103.30 \n33 Dadra & Nagar Haveli & \nDaman & Diu  0.00 \nTotal  4,93,958.74 \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |41", "t": "Employment Opportunities for Persons with Disabilities (PWDs)", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*41 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.12.2023 \n  \nCoal Production in the Country \n*41. SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n       MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) the details of the current annual production of coal in the country; \n(b) the details of the annual coal production in the country from 2014 onwards; \n(c) whether the Government is planning to increase the internal coal production in the \ncountry; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) to (d):- A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. *41 for answer on 06.12.2023 asked by Shri Ravi Kishan and MS. Locket \nChatterjee:  \n(a):    In the financial year 2023 -2024 (upto November, 2023), the all India Coal \nProduction is 591.398 Million Tonnes (Provisional). \n(b):    Production of coal in the country from the year 2014 onwards is as under: \n  \n       (In Million Tonnes) \nYEAR Production of Coal \n2013-14 565.765 \n2014-15 609.179 \n2015-16 639.230 \n2016-17 657.868 \n2017-18 675.400 \n2018-19 728.718 \n2019-20 730.874 \n2020-21 716.083 \n2021-22 778.210 \n2022-23(Provisional) 893.190 \n(c) & (d):  Yes Sir. Government is planning to increase domestic coal production. The \nfollowing steps have been taken by the Government to increase the domestic \nproduction of coal :       \ni. Regular reviews by Ministry of Coal to expedite the development of coal blocks. \nii. Enactment of Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Amendment Act, \n2021 for enabling captive mines owners (other than atomic minerals) to sell up \nto 50% of their annual mineral (including coal) production in the open market after \nmeeting the requirement of the end use plant linked with the mine in such \nmanner as may be prescribed by the Central Government on payment of such \nadditional amount. \niii. Single Window Clearance portal for the coal sector to speed up the \noperationalization of coal mines. \niv. Project Monitoring Unit for hand-holding of coal block allottees for obtaining various \napprovals / clearances for early operationalization of coal mines. \nv. Auction of commercial mining on revenue sharing basis launched in 2020. Under \ncommercial mining scheme, rebate of 50 % on final offer would be allowed for the \nquantity of coal produced earlier than scheduled date of production. Also, \nincentives on coal gasification or liquefaction (rebate of 50 % on final offer) have \nbeen granted. \nvi. Auction of commercial mining on revenue sharing mechanism was launched on \n18.06.2020. Terms and conditions of commercial coal mining are very liberal \nwith no restriction on utilization of coal, allowing new companies to participate \nin the bidding process, reduced upfront amount, adjustment of upfront amount \nagainst monthly payment, liberal efficiency parameters to encourage flexibility \nto operationalize the coal mines, transparent bidding process, 100% Foreign \n\nDirect Investment (FDI) through automatic route and revenue sharing model \nbased on the National Coal Index. \n   \nIn addition to above coal companies have also taken following steps to increase the \ndomestic coal production. \n  \n(1).   CIL has identified and initiated actions for fulfilling all the resources required \nlike Environment Clearance/Forest Clearance, land acquisition, evacuation \ninfrastructures such as mechanised loading through Coal Handling Plant (CHP) \n/SILO, Rail Projects etc. for enabling projects to contribute to its target as per 1 BT \nproduction plan.  \n  \n(2). SCCL has planned to produce 70 MT by 2023-24 from the present level of 67 MT. \nRegular liaison is being undertaken for grounding of new projects and operation of \nexisting projects. SCCL has initiated action for developing  infrastructure for evacuation \nof coal like CHPs, Crushers, Mobile Crushers, Pre-weigh-bins etc.  \n  \n**** \n  \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |410", "t": "Functioning of PACS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 410 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nFunctioning of PACS \n \n410. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाǐरता मंğी) be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the steps taken by the Government for strengthening primary level cooperatives; \n(b) the manner in which model bye-laws wouldbe helpful in providing benefits to the farmers in \nruralareas; \n(c) the progress of computerisation of Primary Agricultural Credit Societies (PACS) project; \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to expandthe coverage of cooperative societies across \nthecountry; \n(e) the steps taken to diversify the business of PACS; and \n(f) the manner in which diversification of businessby PACS will help the farmers? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION  \nसहकाǐरता मंğी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n(a) to (f): Primary level cooperative societies, including Primary Agricultural Credit Societies (PACS), \nprovide rural population not just with livelihood opportunities but also with a financial safety net with \na community-based approach. They serve as a crucial link in sustaining the rural economy of the \ncountry, providing short-term and medium-term credit and other input facilities such as fertilizers, \npesticides, seeds, etc. to farmers. Presently, around 13 crore farmers are linked with PACS across the \ncountry. \nIn order toincrease the viability of PACS and diversify their business activities to make them vibrant \neconomic entities at Panchayat level, Model Byelaws for PACS have been prepared by the \nGovernment after consultation with all the stakeholders and circulated to all the States/ UTs on 5th \nJanuary, 2023 for their adoption by PACS after making suitable changes as per respective State \nCooperative Acts.These will enable PACS to diversify their business activities by undertaking more \nthan 25 business activities, including dairy, fishery, floriculture, setting up godowns, procurement of \nfoodgrains, fertilizers, seeds, LPG/CNG/Petrol/Diesel distributorship, short-term & long-term credit, \n\ncustom hiring centers, common service centers, Fair Price Shops (FPS), community irrigation, \nBusiness Correspondent activities, etc. By adopting Model Byelaws, PACS would be able to serve as \nmulti-service centers, catering to varied needs of member farmers in rural areas. They will help in \nimproving the operational efficiency, transparency and accountability of PACS; providing agricultural \ncredit and various non-credit services to the farmer members thus giving them additional sources of \nincome. Model Byelaws have been adopted by 31 States/ UTs. \nFurther, in order to strengthen PACS, project for Computerization of 63,000 functional PACS with a \ntotal financial outlay of ₹2,516 Crore has also been approved by the Government of India, which entails \nbringing all the functional PACS onto an ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) based common national \nsoftware, linking them with NABARD through State Cooperative Banks (StCBs) and District Central \nCooperative Banks (DCCBs).Computerization of PACS will help in improving the operational \nefficiencyof PACS, ensuring speedy disbursal of loans, lowering of transaction costs and increasing \ntransparency. Proposals to computerize 62,318 PACS across 28 States/ UTs have been \nsanctioned.Software has been developed by NABARD and ERP trial run has started in 5,673 PACS in \nthe country. Hardware procurement and digitization of legacy data by the States and Union Territories \nare under process.  \nIn order to expand the coverage of cooperative societies across the country, Government of India has \napproved a plan to set up new multi-purpose PACS or primary dairy/ fisheries cooperativesocieties \ncovering every Panchayat/ village in the country in the next five years, through convergence of various \nGOI schemes, includingAgriculture Infrastructure Fund (AIF), Agricultural Marketing Infrastructure \nScheme (AMI), Sub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM) Pradhan Mantri Formalization \nof Micro Food Processing Enterprises Scheme (PMFME), Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana \n(PMKSY) and Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), Dairy Infrastructure \nDevelopment Fund (DIDF), National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD), PM Matsya \nSampada Yojana (PMMSY), Fisheries & Aquaculture Infrastructure Fund (FIDF). As reported by the \nStates/ UTs, the process for registering 9,961 new PACS/ Dairy/ Fishery cooperative societies in 23 \nStates/ UTs is in various stages. \nA list of initiatives taken by the Government which can be utilized by PACS to diversifytheirbusiness \nactivities, is enclosed as Annexure. \n \nAll these initiatives will provide our farmer members all over the country with requisite forward and \nbackward linkages to market their produce and realise better prices, obtain credit and other services at \nthe Panchayat/ village level itself, generate multiple and stable revenue streams for them, thus \nproviding the"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |411", "t": "Food Processing Projects", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENTOF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 411 \nANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFOOD PROCESSING PROJECTS \n \n411.  SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI: \n        SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \n        DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n \n        Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether in 2021, the Central Government launched seven food processing projects in the  States \nof Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu; \n(b) if so, the details of the status of the projects launched in Andhra Pradesh, including their           \ntentative date of completion, and the extent of financial support provided by the Centre; \n(c) whether there have been any cost and time overruns in the projects in Andhra Pradesh; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(e) the steps taken by the Government to ensure timely completion of these projects? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) does not set up any Food Processing \nIndustries on its own. However, it incentivizes setting up of such industries through its schemes. A \ntotal of 15 food processing projects, located in Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu, have \nbeen approved for assistance in 2021. Out of these, detailed status of 7 projects located in Andhra \nPradesh, is placed at Annexure. \n(c) to (e): There has been no overrun in financial assistance approved by Ministry for these projects. \nAs per related scheme guidelines, such  projects are to be completed within 20 months of the issue \nof approval letter. However, various reasons, including COVID related restrictions, have led to delay \nin completion of some of these projects. \nMoFPI has taken various measures for timely implementation of these projects, which include \n(i) Dashboard for monitoring the progress of the projects; (ii) Regularly review with the promoters \nto help remove bottlenecks in implementation; (iii) Assessing the progress of projects though Virtual \nsite visits to prevent delays in their completion; \n(iv) Issues taken up with the Banks/ Financial Institutions to expedite sanction and disbursement of \nterm loan to the projects; (v) Penal provisions for promoters in case of delayed completion of projects \n. \n \n***** \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 411 ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 REGARDING FOOD \nPROCESSING PROJECTS \nDetails of approved projects under corresponding schemes of Pradhan Mantri Kisan  Sampada   \nYojana located in Andhra Pradesh in 2021 \n(Rs in Cr) \nComponent Scheme- Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure Scheme \nS. \nNo \nProject Date of \nApproval \nDate of \nCompletion \nProject \ncost \nApproved \nGrant-in- \naid \nReleased \nGrant-in- \naid \nStatus \n1 Aparna \nMarine \nExports \n31.03.2021 03.02.2023 40.67 9.53 9.53 Operational \n2 Mulpuri \nAqua \nProcessors \n31.03.2021 23.03.2023 37.07 10.00 9.58 Operational \n3 Mekworld \nMarines and \nExports Pvt \nLtd \n31.03.2021 - 28.22 10.00 6.27 Under \nImplementation \n4 Vaisakhi \nBio Marine Pvt. \nLtd. \n31.03.2021 - 44.16 10.00 0.00 Under \nImplementation \n5 Krishna \nCoastal Foods \nPvt. Ltd \n31.05.2021 - 6.65 1.57 1.06 Under \nImplementation \n6 The \nKrishna \nDistrict Milk \nProducers’ \nMutually \nAided Co- \noperative \nUnion Limited \n31.03.2021 - 80.48 10.00 0.00 Under \nImplementation \n7 Sukshma \nGamma LLP \n31.03.2021 - 24.77 8.38 5.39 Under \nImplementation \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |412", "t": "Free Coaching for Students with Disability", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF EMPOWERMENT OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES  \n  \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 412  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \nFREE COACHING FOR STUDENTS WITH DISABILITY \n  \n412.  SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL:  \n  \nWill the Minister of  SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has modified the terms of the scholarship scheme for providing free \ncoaching to students with disabilities and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the details of the coaching institutes which are providing free coaching to students with \ndisabilities; and \n \n(c) the number of students who have availed the benefits of free coaching for students with \ndisabilities scheme during the last three years? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT  \n(SUSHRI PRATIMA BHOUMIK) \n  \n(a) and (b): The guidelines of ‘Free Coaching for Students with Disabilities’ Scheme have been \nre-framed which are applicable with effect from 01.04.2023. The objectives of the scheme are to \nprovide free coaching facilities to students with benchmark disabilities (having a minimum of \n40% disability) to enable them to appear in competitive examinations for government jobs and \nto seek admission for various professional and technical courses. The reframed Scheme is \nimplemented through the following implementing agencies:- \n \n(i) Empanelled Central University/Institutes under Free Coaching Scheme of Department \nof Social Justice and Empowerment implemented by Dr. Ambedkar Foundation (DAF) under \nMinistry of Social Justice of Empowerment  \n \n(ii) National Institutes / Composite Regional Centers under the administrative control of \nDepartment of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities \n \n(c)  No student has availed the benefit of this scheme during last three years. \n \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |413", "t": "MSP for Kharif Crops", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.413 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nMSP FOR KHARIF CROPS  \n \n413.       PROF. SOUGATA RAY: \n              SHRIMATI PRATIMA MONDAL: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान \nकल्याण मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether most of the State Governments are demanding hike in Minimum Support \nPrice (MSP) for various Kharif Crops;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the details of MSP announced for various Kharif Crops;  \n(d) the details of methodology being followed for fixation of MSP; \n(e) whether the Government has considered the problems of extreme heat, drought \nand lack of enough water along with soil depletion; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याणमंत्री   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n  \n(a) to ( d): Government fixes Minimum Support Price (MSP) for 22 mandated \nagricultural crops (14 Kharif, 6 Rabi & 2 Commercial Crops) on the basis of the \nrecommendations of the Commission for Agric ultural Costs &  Prices (CACP), after \nconsidering the views of State Governments and Central Ministries/ Departments \nconcerned. Before finalizing MSP, CACP holds consultations with State \nGovernments, farmer organizations and other stakeholders, wherein suggestions are \n\nreceived on both price and non-price recommendations. While recommending MSPs, \nCACP considers important factors  like cost of production, overall demand -supply \nsituation of various crops in domestic and world markets, domestic and international  \nprices, inter-crop price parity, terms of trade between agricultural and non-agricultural \nsector, likely effect of price policy on  rest of the economy and a minimum of 50 \npercent as the margin over cost of production.  Details of MSP announced for Kharif \nCrops for the year 2023-24 are given at Annexure. \n \nThe Cost of production  is one of the important determinants of MSP  and its \nestimation is based on the methodology recommended by Expert Committees from \ntime to time. CACP projects cost for ensuing marketing season on the basis of actual \npaid out costs and imputed value of family labour and Composite Input Price Index \n(CIPI).The CIPIs are based on latest prices of major inputs like human labour, bullock \nlabour, machine labour, fertilisers and manures, seeds, pesticides and irrigation. \n(e) & (f) Yes, Sir, problems of extreme heat, drought and lack of enough water along \nwith soil depletion  are considered. Accordingly, various schemes are being \nimplemented which are as follow: \n National Mission for Sustainable Ag riculture (NMSA) to make Indian agriculture \nmore resilient to the climate change.  \n National Innovations i n Climate Resilient Agriculture  (NICRA) to develop and \npromote climate resilient technologies in agriculture.   \n Soil Health Card (SHC) to promote soil test based balanced and judicious \nfertilisers application in the country.  \n Prime Minister’s Program for Restoration, Awareness, Nurturing and \nImprovement of Fertility of Mother Earth (PM -PRANAM) to encourage States to \npromotebalanced use of alternative fertilisers and chemical fertilisers.   \n Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana (PMKSY) for better access to irrigation   \nand to ensure optimum utilization of water in farming. \n  \n \n\n \nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (d ) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 413 due for reply on 05.12.2023. \n \nMinimum Support Prices (MSP)  \n(Rs. per quintal) \nSl. \nNo. \nCommodity Variety  2023-24 \n  KHARIF CROPS    \n1 PADDY Common 2183 \n    Grade 'A' 2203 \n2 JOWAR Hybrid 3180 \n    Maldandi 3225 \n3 BAJRA   2500 \n4 RAGI   3846 \n5 MAIZE   2090 \n6 TUR (ARHAR)   7000 \n7 MOONG   8558 \n8 URAD   6950 \n9 GROUNDNUT   6377 \n10 SUNFLOWER SEED   6760 \n11 SOYABEEN (yellow)   4600 \n12 SESAMUM   8635 \n13 NIGERSEED   7734 \n14 COTTON  Medium Staple 6620 \n    Long Staple 7020 \n  RABI CROPS    \n15 WHEAT   2275 \n16 BARLEY   1850 \n17 GRAM   5440 \n18 MASUR (LENTIL)   6425 \n19 RAPESEED & MUSTARD   5650 \n20 SAFFLOWER   5800 \n21 COPRA (Calendar Year) Milling 10860 \n    Ball 11750 \n22 JUTE    5050 \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |414", "t": "Phase-II of ICJS", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 414 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 14, 1945 \n(SAKA) \n \nPHASE-II OF ICJS \n \n†414.  DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there was any need to implement the Phase-II of the Inter-\nOperable Criminal Justice System (ICJS); \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the prime objectives and the components of the Phase-II of ICJS? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (c) : Yes Sir, the Information Technology (IT) systems for criminal \njustice delivery established in the country primarily consists of the Crime \nand Criminal Tracking Network and Systems i.e. CCTNS (for police), \neForensics (for the Forensic Science Laboratories), eProsecution (for \npublic prosecutors), eCourts(for courts) and ePrisons (for Prisons). These \nIT systems are stand-alone systems functional in all States/ Union \nTerritories. The phase-II of the ICJS Project will inter-link these stand-\nalone IT systems for seamless data exchange and also provide new  \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 414 FOR 05.12.2023 \n \ntechnologies for smart policing. The objectives of ICJS Phase-II are as \nunder:- \ni. Facilitate seamless integration between the various data sets of \nPolice, Prisons, Forensics, Prosecution and Courts. \nii. Enhance data quality by reducing errors in data entry. \niii. Increase effectiveness and timeliness in investigations, and \nconsequently in trials, due to easy access of data between pillars. \niv. Enable effective use of data analytics and modern tools available \nin the investigations. \nv. Reduce dependence on paper records in decision-making. \nvi. Enable a shift towards “SMART Policing”. \nThe components of ICJS Phase-II are as under \ni. Hardware (application servers and client-end systems) \nii. Software update for ICJS \niii. ICJS development and management \niv. Network connectivity \nv. IT infrastructure (dedicated and secure cloud-based) \nvi. Operations & maintenance \nvii. Awareness & capacity building \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |415", "t": "Equality Villages under PMAY-G", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 415 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nEQUALITY VILLAGES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n415.  SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any plan to construct houses under \nPradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G) in rural areas \nwithout any caste division and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the construction of houses on the basis of caste is \nagainst the spirit of the equality clause of the Constitution; \n(c)  whether Tamil Nadu has created more than 200 ‘Equality \nVillages’ and provided accommodation to people belonging to all \ncastes in the equality villages and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  whether the Union Government has any plan to set up such \nequality villages under PMAY-G and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):       In order to achieve “Housing for All”, the overall target is to \nconstruct 2.95 crore houses under Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-\nGramin (PMAY-G) in rural areas. The beneficiaries under the scheme \nare identified on the basis of housing deprivation parameters and \nexclusion criteria prescribed under Socio Economic Caste Census \n(SECC) 2011. These parameters/criteria are applied both on SECC 2011 \nand “Awaas+” survey databases and then they are further subjected to \ndue verification by Gram Sabha and an appellate process thereafter. \nThe houses under the scheme are provided to all the rural areas as per \nthe guidelines without any caste discrimination. \n\n(b):  Under the PMAY-G, minimum of 60% of the target at the national \nlevel is earmarked for SC/ST households. Further, the States are \nallowed to interchange targets between SC and ST if there are no \neligible beneficiaries from either of the category. Also on exhaustion \nof SC/ST beneficiaries, the State/UT by a duly signed letter by the \nChief Secretary can allocate target to the beneficiaries from the \n“Others” category after the approval of the Ministry. \n  \n(c):  It is an initiative of the Government of Tamil Nadu. The State \nGovernment has reported that to promote social justice and to spread \nThanthai Periyar’s message of Social equality, they have launched \nthe  scheme named “Periyar Ninaivu Samathuvapuram” which is an \nintegral part of the Golden Jubilee Celebrations of Indian \nIndependence during the year 1997-98. Under this scheme, an area up \nto an extent of 8 to 9 acres of approachable land is identified wherein \nSamathuvapuram (Equality Village) is established which consist of 100 \nhouses with all basic infrastructure facilities like water supply, Road \nworks, Street lights, play grounds, Parks, Community Hall, Library, \nAnganvadi, PD Shop, School Building and School Toilet etc. All 100 \nhouses are allotted to all communities in the ratio of SC-40, BC-25, \nMBC-25, Others-10. So far, the Government of Tamil Nadu has \nestablished 238 Samathuvapurams during 1997 to 2001 and 2008 to \n2011 in the state where all communities live in unity and brotherhood. \n  \n(d):  There is no proposal to set up \"equality villages\". In fact, PMAY-\nG works on a saturation made for coverage of all households having \nKutcha houses upto two rooms, irrespective of their caste, creed and \nreligion. Basic facilities in those houses viz. toilets, electricity and \nLPG connections thereto are assured through convergence with the \nongoing schemes. The Government of India is already implementing \nvarious Development schemes for poverty alleviation, wage \nemployment, rural infrastructure, skill development, etc. which have \nover the years reduced poverty and improved the quality of life and \ninfrastructure in rural areas.  \n \n ***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |416", "t": "Attacks by Stray Dogs", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying \nDepartment of Animal Husbandry and Dairying \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 416 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER 2023 \n \nATTACKS BY STRAY DOGS \n \n416. SHRIMATI PRATIMA MONDAL:  \n \n Will the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \n मत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \n be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has adopted any scientific management system to control stray \ndog population in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has noticed a large number of attacks by stray dogs in all parts of \nthe country;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof;  \n(e) whether the Government has any proposal to review the present Animal Birth Control \nRules;  \n(f) if so, the details thereof;  \n(g) whether the World Health Organization (WHO) finding is true that India accounted above \n36% of world rabies deaths; and  \n(h) if so, the details thereof and the details of steps taken to check such alarming situation? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) As on date only scientific method adopted for control of Stray Dogs population is through \nsterilization by surgical intervention. \n(b) The Central Government has notified the Animal Birth Control Rules 2023 vide G.S.R.  \n193(E) dated 10 th March, 2023 under Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960 after \nsuperseding the Animal Birth Control (Dog) Rules, 2001 to control the population of Stray \nDogs. The Rules have addressed the direction of Hon’ble Supreme Court of India in writ \npetition no. 691 of 2009 between Animal Welfare Board of India and People for Elimination \nof Stray Troubles, which states that \" Needless to emphasize, no innovative method or \nsubterfuge should be adopted not to carry out the responsibility under the 1960 Act or the \n\n2001 Rules. Any kind of laxity while carrying out statutory obligations, is not countenanced in \nlaw\". \n The AWBI had issued the Revised ABC Module as per the direction of the Supreme \nCourt in the year 2015 for Street Dog Population Management, Rabies Eradica tion and \nReducing Man-Animal conflict.  The module elaborates Scientific process to be adopted for \nSterilization. \n(c) and (d): There are some sporadic reports of dog attacks happening in various parts of the \nCountry. However, the information made available by Ministry of Health and Family Welfare \n(National Centre for Disease Control) there has been reduction in number of dog attacks in \ncomparison to the year 2018. The details are as under: \nYear Number of Dog Bite \n2018 7567811 \n2019 7277523 \n2020 4633493 \n2021 1701133 \n2022 2180185 \n2023(till October \n2023) 2477936 \n \n(e) to (f): The Animal Birth Control Rules have recently been published in the year 2023 after \nreviewing Animal Birth Control (Dogs) Rules, 2001 \n(g) to (h): Yes Sir. As per WHO Association for the Prevention and Control of Rabies in India \n(APCRI)-2004 Study India accounts for 36% of World Rabies Deaths. Ministry of Health & \nFamily Welfare and Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry & Dairying Jointly Launched \nNational Action Plan for Dog Mediate d Rabies Elimination in year 2021.  Ministry of Health \n& Family Welfare implementing National Rabies Control programme Human Health \ncomponent. Funds are being provided to States & UTs through NHM to undertake Human \nhealth Component of National Rabies Contro l program. Funds are provided for following \nactivities \na. Procurement of Rabies Vaccine & Rabies Immunoglobulin through National \nfree drug Initiative \nb. Capacity Building \nc. Awareness Generation \nd. Surveillance & Monitoring \n Also the Local bodies are carrying out Anti rabies vaccination of stray dogs alongwith \nAnimal Birth Control Programme. Funds are also made available to the State Animal \nHusbandry Department for procurement of rabies vaccines as per need. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |417", "t": "PM-Kisan Maan Dhan Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 417 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nPM-KISAN MAAN DHAN YOJANA \n \n417.   SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the number of eligible farmers in Andhra Pradesh who have been enrolled under the Pradhan \nMantri Kisan Maan Dhan Yojana, year-wise;  \n(b)  the number of farmers who have received money under the said Yojana, year-wise;  \n(c)  the amount disbursed under the abovesaid Yojana to the enrolled farmers in Andhra Pradesh, \nyear-wise;  \n(d)  whether the Government has evaluated or assessed the impact a nd effectiveness of the \naforesaid Yojana specifically in Andhra Pradesh; and  \n(e)  if so, the details of the findings along with any subsequent actions taken so far?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c)  The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Maandhan Yojana, a central sector scheme, is a voluntary \nand contributory pension scheme for the Small and Marginal landholder farmers of entry age \ngroup of 18 to 40 years with a provision of Rs. 3000/- monthly pension on attaining the age of 60 \nyears. As on date, total 23,38,309 farmers have been enrolled across the country and in Andhra \nPradesh total 37,429 farmers have been enrolled under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Maandhan \nYojana since inception of the scheme.  \nPradhan Mantri Kisan Maandhan Yojana scheme was launched in 2019, with a provision \nof monthly pension of Rs. 3000/ - after attaining the age of 60. The pension would start to the \nbeneficiaries after attaining the age 60.  \n \n(d) & (e)   The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Maandhan Yojana was launched in 2019, so far no impact \nstudy has been carried out. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |418", "t": "PM-Kisan Bhai Yojana for Marginal Farmers", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 418 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nPM-KISAN BHAI YOJANA FOR MARGINAL FARMERS \n \n418. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is implementing Pradhan Mantri Kisan Bhai Yojana; \n \n(b) if so, the benefits accruing to marginal  farmers therefrom and number of such \nbeneficiaries in the State of Uttar Pradesh; and \n \n(c) the various schemes being implemented by the Union Government for farmers? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b): No Sir, Government of India is not implementing P radhan Mantri Kisan Bhai \nYojana. However, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Government of India has \ncirculated a concept note on PM Kisan -Bhandaran Incentive Scheme, for stakeholder’s \nconsultation to promote storage and eNWR trade via National Agr iculture Market (e-NAM) \nand other e-trading platforms. \n \n(c): The Government of India through Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare is \nimplementing various schemes/ programmes for the welfare of farmers in the country such \nas Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN), Pradhan Mantri Kisan Maan Dhan \nYojana (PM-KMY), Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), Soil Health Card (SHC), \nNational Food Security Mission (NFSM), Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY), \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY), Forma tion & Promotion of 10,000 Farmers \nProducer Organizations (FPOs) scheme, National Agriculture Market (e -NAM) scheme, \nAgricultural Marketing Infrastructure (AMI) scheme, Mission for Integrated Development of \nHorticulture (MIDH), Agriculture Infrastructure Fund (AIF), National Mission on Sustainable \nAgriculture (NMSA), Mission Organic Value Chain Development for North Eastern Region \n(MOVCDNER), Sub-Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM), Per Drop More Crop \n(PDMC-PMKSY), Kisan Credit Card (KCC) and Interest Subvention Scheme.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |419", "t": "Amrit Sarovars Target", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 419 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nAMRIT SAROVARS TARGET \n \n419.  SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI ARUN SAO: \nSHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \nSHRI DILIP SAIKIA: \nSHRI Y. DEVENDRAPPA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of districts which have achieved the target of 75 \nAmrit Sarovars in the country, State/UT-wise particularly in \nJharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Gujarat, North \nEastern States and Karnataka; \n(b)  whether certain States are yet to achieve the target of 75 Amrit \nSarovars per district and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government to encourage mobilization of \ncitizens and non-Government resources for supplementing the \nGovernment’s efforts to achieve the target? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):      State/UT-wise including Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Rajasthan, \nMaharashtra, Gujarat, North Eastern States and Karnataka detail of \ndistricts which have achieved the target of 75 Amrit Sarovars is given \nat Annexure-I. \n \n  \n\n(b):      The number of districts yet to achieve the target of 75 Amrit \nSarovars is 207 out of 735 districts covered under Mission Amrit \nSarovar. State/UT-wise detail of districts yet to achieve the target of \n75 Amrit Sarovars is given at Annexure-II. \n \n(c):      Peoples’ participation has been the key to the entire Mission. \nTo encourage mobilization of citizens and non- Government resources \nfor supplementing the Government’s efforts to achieve the target, \nexplicit provisions have been made in the guidelines of the Mission \nAmrit Sarovars as follows: \n \n(1)  Laying the foundation stone for the Amrit Sarovar to be led by \nfreedom fighter or her/his family member or by the family of martyr \n(post-independence) or a local Padma awardee, and in case no such \ncitizen is available, by the eldest member of the local Gram \nPanchayat. \n \n(2). Provision for people to participate by donating construction \nmaterial, benches and by ShramDaan. During the mission 1,139 \nnumber ShramDaan events have been reported . \n \n(3). If village community so desires, beautification works on the \nSarovar site may mobilize necessary donations through crowd \nsourcing and CSR contributions. A total 1,428 Amrit Sarovars have \nbeen constructed with a contribution of Rs.131.59 crore by people’s \nparticipation including CSR contribution. \n \n(4).  Provision has been made that on the occasion of  Independence \nDay/ Republic Day, Tricolour to be hoisted at each Amrit Sarovar sites, \nby the freedom fighter or his/her family member or by the family \nmember of martyr or a local Padma Awardee. On Amrit Sarovars sites, \nNational event has been celebrated. \n \n(5). Possible users of such water structure, including for irrigation, \nfishery or water chestnut cultivation, should be identified and creation \nof their group encouraged. A total 64,931 users' group are associated \nwith Amrit Sarovars Mission.  \n****** \n  \n\n Annexure-I referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 419 \ndated 05.12.2023. \n  \nState/UT-wise including Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Gujarat, \nNorth Eastern States and Karnataka details of districts which have achieved the \ntarget of 75 Amrit Sarovars \nSI. \nNo. State/ UT \nNumber of districts which have \ncompleted Amrit Sarovar 75 and \nabove \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 23  \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 16  \n3 ASSAM 24  \n4 BIHAR 23  \n5 CHHATTISGARH 26  \n6 GOA 1  \n7 GUJARAT 33  \n8 HARYANA 10  \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 10  \n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 20  \n11 JHARKHAND 22  \n12 KARNATAKA 29  \n13 KERALA 4  \n14 MADHYA PRADESH 46  \n15 MAHARASHTRA 30  \n16 MANIPUR 12  \n17 MEGHALAYA 4  \n18 MIZORAM 11  \n19 NAGALAND 0  \n20 ODISHA 30  \n21 PUNJAB 8  \n22 RAJASTHAN 24  \n23 SIKKIM 0  \n24 TAMIL NADU 13  \n25 TELANGANA 8  \n26 TRIPURA 8  \n27 UTTARAKHAND 12  \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 74  \n29 WEST BENGAL 0  \n30 ANDAMAN & NICOBAR 3  \n31 THE DADRA NAGAR AND HAVELI, \nDAMAN AND DIU 1 \n32 LADAKH 1  \n33 PUDUCHERRY 2  \n  TOTAL 528  \n   \n \n\nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No. 419 dated 05.12.2023. \nSI. \nNo. State/ UT \nNumber of districts which \nhave not achieved the \ntarget of 75 Amrit \nSarovars \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 3  \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 9  \n3 ASSAM 10  \n4 BIHAR 15  \n5 CHHATTISGARH 2  \n6 GOA 1  \n7 HARYANA 12  \n8 HIMACHAL PRADESH 2  \n9 JHARKHAND 2  \n10 KARNATAKA 2  \n11 KERALA 10  \n12 MADHYA PRADESH 6  \n13 MAHARASHTRA 5  \n14 MANIPUR 4  \n15 MEGHALAYA 7  \n16 NAGALAND 11  \n17 PUNJAB 15  \n18 RAJASTHAN 9  \n19 SIKKIM 6  \n20 TAMIL NADU 25  \n21 TELANGANA 26  \n22 UTTARAKHAND 1  \n23 UTTAR PRADESH 1  \n24 WEST BENGAL 20  \n25 THE DADRA NAGAR AND HAVELI, \nDAMAN AND DIU 2 \n26 LADAKH 1  \n  TOTAL 207  \n  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |42", "t": "Teachers in Eklavya Model Residential Schools", "head": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK  SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *42 (OIH) \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.12.2023 \n \n \n \n \nSCHEME TO EDUCATE CONSUMERS ABOUT THEIR RIGHTS \n \n*42. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: SHRI MAHABALI SINGH:  \n(OIH) \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION  \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has implemented any Scheme to edu cate consumers about their rights \nduring the last three years;  \n(b)  if so, the details of the State-wise expenditure incurred under the said Scheme for the last three years \nand the current year;  \n(c)  the number of complaints out of the total number of co mplaints received on which action has been \ntaken by the Government to protect the rights of consumers during the last three years;  \n(d) whether the Government proposes to speed up its resolution mechanism to avoid delays in disposal \nof such complaints; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh ih;w\"k xks;y½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n  (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e)  :   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO  (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.* 42 FOR 06.12.2023 REGARDING SCHEME TO EDUCATE CONSUMERS \nABOUT THEIR RIGHTS. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \n \n(a) to (e)  :  Department of Consumer Affairs is continuously working for consumer protection and  \nempowerment of consumers by enactment of  progressive legislations. With a view to modernize the \nframework governing the consumer protection in the new era of globalizatio n, technologies, e -commerce \nmarkets etc Consumer Protection Act, 1986 was repealed and Consumer Protection Act 2019 was enacted.  \n \nThe Consumer Protection Act, 2019 provides for redressal of the grievances of consumer through \nspecialized three -tier quasi -judicial agencies, now commonly known as ‘National Consumer Disputes \nRedressal Commissions (NCDRC)’ at National level, ‘State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission \n(SCDRC)’ at State level,  and ‘District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission(DCDRC)’ at Di strict \nlevel.  \nThe Act also provides for, inter -alia, simplification of the adjudication process in the Consumer \nCommissions; filing of a complaint by a consumer in the Consumer Commission having jurisdiction with \nrespect to his place of work/residence irr espective of place of transaction and place of business or residence \nof the opposite parties, e -filing and e -payment, video conferencing for hearing, deemed admissibility of \ncomplaints if admissibility is not decided within 21 days of filing; court monitor ed mediation to facilitate \nearly disposal of cases; provision of product liability.  \nProvision for filing cases online through e -Daakhil has been introduced in NCDRC and Consumer \nCommissions of 35 States/UTs at the district and state level for speedy and hassle-free resolution of cases. \nSection 38(7) of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 prescribes that every complaint shall be disposed of as \nexpeditiously as possible and endeavour shall be made to decide the complaint within a period of three \nmonths from the date of receipt of notice by opposite party where the complaint does not require analysis or \ntesting of commodities and within five months if it requires analysis or testing of commodities. \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs has notified 17 Rules and 6 Regul ations under the Consumer Protection \nAct, 2019 for the protection of rights and interests of consumers which, inter -alia, includes E -Commerce \nRules, Direct Selling) Rules, Mediation Rules, Search and Seizure and Compounding of offences by the \nCentral Authority and Crediting of Penalty Rules etc. \nGovernment has also taken several initiatives recently for the protection of consumers from misleading and \ndeceptive advertisements, fake reviews, and unfair trade practices. Under the provisions of the Consumer \nProtection Act, 2019, the Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA) has been established w.e.f. \n24.07.2020 to regulate matters relating to violation of rights of consumers, unfair trade practices and false or \nmisleading advertisements which are prejudicial to the interests of public and consumers as a class.  \nThe CCPA has notified the Guidelines for Prevention of Misleading Advertisements and Endorsements for \nMisleading Advertisements, 2022 on 9th June, 2022. These guidelines inter -alia provide for; (a) con ditions \nfor an advertisement to be non -misleading and valid; (b) certain stipulations in respect of bait \nadvertisements and free claim advertisements; and, (c) duties of manufacturer, service provider, advertiser \nand adverti"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |420", "t": "Contribution of Agriculture in GDP", "head": " \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 420 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nCONTRIBUTION OF AGRICULTURE IN GDP \n \n420. SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \n        DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \n        SHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the contribution of agriculture in the Gross Domestic Product(GDP) and \nthe agricultural growth rate from 1960 to 2013, year-wise; \n(b) whether the contribution of agriculture in GDP has increased between 2014-23; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) if not, the steps taken or being taken to increase the contribution of agriculture in GDP?  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (d):  The details of the contribution of agriculture  and allied sector  in the Gross \nValue Added (GVA) of the country and growth rate of GVA of agriculture and allied sector \nfrom 1960-61 to 2022-23 are at Annexure-I.  \n \nSince 2014,  the Government has adopted and implemented several policies, reforms, \ndevelopmental programmes and schemes for improving the agricultural growth prospects \nand incomes of the farmers. These include: \n                                                                                                                                                                \n(i) Supplementary income transfers under PM -KISAN of ₹ 6000/- per year in three \nequal instalments to eligible beneficiaries.  \n(ii) Increase in Minimum Support Price (MSPs) for all Kharif & Rabi crops ensuring a \nminimum of 50 percent of profit margin on the cost of production. \n(iii) Crop insurance under Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojna (PMFBY). \n(iv) Better access to irrigation under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY). \n(v) Special attention for creation of infrastructure through Agri Infrastructure Fund (AIF) \nwith a size of ₹100,000 crore. \n\n \n(vi) New procurement policy under PM-AASHA in addition to FCI operations. \n(vii) Kisan Credit Cards (KCC) offering production loan to even dairy & fishery farmers \nbesides agricultural crops. \n(viii) Formation and promotion of 10,000 Farmer Producer Organisations(FPO). \n(ix) National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA), which aims to evolve and \nimplement strategies to make Indian agriculture more resilient to the changing \nclimate. \n(x) Adoption of drone technologies in agriculture which has potential to r evolutionize \nthe Indian agriculture. \n(xi) Benefits accruing under Bee -Keeping, Rashtriya Gokul Mission, Blue Revolution, \nInterest Subvention Scheme, Agro -forestry, Restructured Bamboo Mission, \nimplementation of new generation watershed guidelines, etc. \n(xii) Focus on application of digital technology at all stages of agricultural value chain. \n(xiii) Supply of fertilizer to farmer at subsidized price so as to reduce input cost. \n \nThe above efforts have led to a rise in the GVA of the agriculture and allied sector, \nregistering an average annual growth rate of 4 percent over the past five years. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure in reference to reply to part (a) to (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Q.No. 420 \ndue for reply on 5.12.2023 \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nYear-wise details of Gross Value Added (GVA) of Agriculture and Allied Sector \n (at 2011-12 prices) \n (in ₹ crore) \nYear Share of Agriculture & Allied \nSector in total GVA \nGrowth rate of GVA of \nAgriculture & Allied Sector \n1960-61 57 6.74 \n1961-62 55 0.08 \n1962-63 53 -1.99 \n1963-64 51 2.34 \n1964-65 52 9.22 \n1965-66 48 -11.04 \n1966-67 47 -1.42 \n1967-68 50 14.87 \n1968-69 49 -0.16 \n1969-70 49 6.43 \n1970-71 50 7.09 \n1971-72 48 -1.88 \n1972-73 46 -5.02 \n1973-74 47 7.20 \n1974-75 46 -1.52 \n1975-76 47 12.89 \n1976-77 44 -5.78 \n1977-78 45 10.04 \n1978-79 44 2.30 \n1979-80 40 -12.77 \n1980-81 42 12.89 \n1981-82 42 4.60 \n1982-83 41 -0.28 \n1983-84 42 10.12 \n1984-85 41 1.59 \n1985-86 39 0.31 \n1986-87 37 -0.41 \n1987-88 36 -1.59 \n1988-89 37 15.64 \n1989-90 36 1.19 \n1990-91 35 4.02 \n1991-92 34 -1.95 \n1992-93 34 6.65 \n1993-94 34 3.32 \n1994-95 33 4.72 \n1995-96 31 -0.70 \n\nAnnexure in reference to reply to part (a) to (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Q.No. 420 \ndue for reply on 5.12.2023 \n \nYear Share of Agriculture & Allied \nSector in total GVA \nGrowth rate of GVA of \nAgriculture & Allied Sector \n1996-97 31 9.92 \n1997-98 29 -2.55 \n1998-99 29 6.32 \n1999-00 28 2.67 \n2000-01 26 -0.01 \n2001-02 27 6.01 \n2002-03 24 -6.60 \n2003-04 24 9.05 \n2004-05 23 0.18 \n2005-06 22 4.81 \n2006-07 21 2.94 \n2007-08 21 5.51 \n2008-09 20 -0.24 \n2009-10 18 -0.88 \n2010-11 18 8.79 \n2011-12 19 6.40 \n2012-13 18 1.49 \n2013-14 18 5.57 \n2014-15 17 -0.22 \n2015-16 15 0.65 \n2016-17 15 6.80 \n2017-18 15 6.61 \n2018-19 15 2.10 \n2019-20 * 15 6.16 \n2020-21 ^ 16 4.11 \n2021-22 $ 16 3.51 \n2022-23 # 15 3.96 \n \nSource: Ministry of Statistics & Pr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |422", "t": "High Yielding Species of Seeds of Pulses", "head": "  \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nHIGH YIELDING SPECIES OF SEEDS OF PULSES \n \n422.  SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Indian Council of Agricultural Research  (ICAR) has developed certain high \nyielding species of seeds of pulses in the recent past specially for Bihar; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to make new improved varieties of seeds available to \nfarmers for cultivation at the earliest?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE       \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h                               (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) and (b): National Agricultural Research System (NARS) under the aegis of Indian Council \nof Agricultural Research (ICAR) has developed the region specific, high yielding and climate \nresilient varieties of different crops including pulses. Since 2014,  total of 369 varieties of 14 \npulse crops have been released and notified in the country including 24 varieties of seven \npulse crops viz., chickpea (6), fieldpea (6),  pigeonpea (5), fababean (3),  mungbean(2), \nurdbean (1) and lentil (1) for Bihar upto September, 2023. \n \n(c): Number of steps have been taken to make seeds of new improved varieties available to \nfarmers for cultivation at the earliest which include:  \n(i) Production and supply of breeder seed of improved varieties seed. During past five years, \n15.60 lakh q breeder seed of pulses was produced and supplied by ICAR to different public \nand private sector seed production agencies for downstream multiplication of foundation and \ncertified seed. \n(ii) Established of 150 Pulses Seed hubs and 12 centres for enhancing bree der seed \nproduction in 2016 which have produced and supplied 7.09 lakhs quality seed and 21713 q \nbreeder seed during 2016-17 to 2022-23.  \n(iii) Total 1587.74 lakh q quality seed was produced involving 6.39 lakh villages  \n(iv) training of 98.07 lakhs farmers during 2014 -23 under seed village scheme for making \nquality seed available at village level.  \n(v) Distribution of seeds of new high yielding varieties through 6000 Front Line \nDemonstrations and 151873 Cluster Front Line Demonstrations o f pulses during 2018-19 to \n2022-23.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |423", "t": "Modernisation of Rural Areas", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 423 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nMODERNISATION OF RURAL AREAS \n \n423.  SHRI VIJAY KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has formulated any schemes for \nmodernisation of the rural areas of Bihar; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the details and the number of schemes being implemented in \nBihar for development of each of the deprived persons in rural \nareas of Bihar? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Department of Rural Development (DoRD), Ministry of \nRural Development (MoRD) is implementing a number of welfare \nschemes  in all States/UTs including Bihar, to alleviate poverty in rural \nareas and to bring about overall improvement in the quality of life of \nthe people in rural areas, strengthening of livelihood \nopportunities,  providing minimum guaranteed employment, promoting \nself-employment, skilling of rural youths in various useful trades and \nentrepreneurship, infrastructure development and provision of social \nassistance.  These include Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS), Pradhan \nMantri Awaas Yojana – Gramin (PMAY-G), Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak \nYojana (PMGSY), Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana – National Rural \nLivelihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM), Deen Dayal Upadhayay – Gramin \nKaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY), Rural Self Employment Training \nInstitutes (RSETIs) and National Social Assistance Programme \n(NSAP).  Apart from this, Department of Land Resources (DoLR), MoRD \n\nis implementing Watershed Development Component (WDC) of Pradhan \nMantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) which is principally for \ndevelopment of rainfed portions of net cultivated area and culturable \nwastelands. \n \nHowever, this Ministry has not formulated any scheme specifically for \nmodernization of the rural areas of Bihar.   \n                                                                  **** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |424", "t": "Integrated Scheme on Agricultural Marketing", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 424 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 05THDECEMBER, 2023 \n \nINTEGRATED SCHEME ON AGRICULTURAL MARKETING \n \n424. SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the goals of the Integrated Scheme on Agricultural Marketing in the State \nof Andhra Pradesh; \n \n(b) the details of work done under the scheme in the State of Andhra Pradesh during the \nlast three years; \n \n(c) the reasons for which the funds under the scheme utilized in Andhra Pradesh in 2020 -\n21 were a mere Rs.13 crore only; \n \n(d) whether the Government has monitored the work done in 2022 -23 to check if it had \nsustainable benefits thereafter; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं षकसान कल्याण मंत्री (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Government is implementing Central Sector Scheme of Integrated Scheme of \nAgricultural Marketing (ISAM) in the country including in Andhra Pradesh. The main goals \nof ISAM scheme are as under: \n \ni. To promote Creation of scientific storage capacity and non -storage agricultural \nmarketing infrastructure by providing backend subsidy support  to state, cooperative \nand private sector investments.  \nii. To integrate physical wholesale markets to facilitate  online trading of agricultural \ncommodities through transparent price discovery method to enable farmers to realize \nbetter remunerative prices for their produce. \niii. To establish a nation -wide information network system for speedy collection and \ndissemination of market information and data on arrivals and prices for its efficient \nand timely utilization by farmers.  \niv. To support framing of grade standards and quality certification of agricultural \ncommodities to help farmers get better and remunerative prices for t heir graded \nproduce.  \nv. To undertake and promote training, research, education, extension and consultancy \nin the agri marketing sector.  \n \n\n \nISAM have four sub -schemes viz., Agricultural Marketing Infrastructure (AMI), Marketing \nResearch & Information Network (MRIN), National Agriculture Market (e -NAM) and \nStrengthening of Agmark Grading Facilities (SAGF) \n \nThe details of the work done  and fund released/ subsidy released under ISAM in the State \nof Andhra Pradesh during the last three years is at Annexure-I. \n \n(d) & (e): The Development Monitoring & Evaluation office (DMEO), NITI Aayog, had \nsubmitted its report in August, 2020, on evaluation of centrally sponsored schemes in \nAgriculture, Animal husbandry & fisheries sector, which also includes ISAM scheme.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n1. Progress of AMI sub-scheme of ISAM in Andhra Pradesh since 2020-21 \n \nFinancial Year Number of \nProjects \nSanctioned \nTotal Capacity \nSanctioned (in MT) \nTotal Subsidy Released \n(in Rs.) \n2020-21 54 123202.99 9,56,17,181.00 \n2021-22 76 149919.16 6,51,67,240.00 \n2022-23 51 122443.16 4,22,47,558.00 \n2023-24* 31 24814.56 19,66,86,000.00 \nTotal 212              4,20,379.87  39,97,17,979.00 \n \n* - As on 28.11.2023 \n \n2. Progress of e-NAM sub-scheme of ISAM in Andhra Pradesh since 2020-21 \n \nAs on 3 0.11.2023, Total 33 mandis  of Andhra Pradesh  have been integrated on e -NAM. \nThe progress of e-NAM in the state of Andhra Pradesh is as below: \nParticulars FY \n2020 – 21 \nFY \n2021 – 22 \nFY \n2022 – 23 \nTrade Volume \n(MT) 845880 1227226 1134003 \nTrade Value \n(Rs Cr.) 4014 8623 10267 \nFund released \n(In Rs) -- ** -- ** -- ** \n \n** e-NAM is a demand driven scheme. \n \n3. Progress of MRIN sub-scheme in Andhra Pradesh  \n \nThere are 195 Agmarknet nodes (APMC market yards) covered under MRIN scheme in \nthe State of Andhra Pradesh. The year -wise detailed reporting status of Agmarknet \nnodes in the state for last three years are as below;  \n \n \nSource: (Agmarknet portal) \nLink- https://agmarknet.gov.in/pmsnew/consolidatedrep_cat_Year1.aspx) \n***** \nSl. No. Month No. of market reported during last three year \n2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 April 30 29 31 \n2 May 38 27 32 \n3 June 38 27 31 \n4 July 30 27 27 \n5 August 30 25 29 \n6 Sept 31 25 27 \n7 Oct 30 31 25 \n8 Nov 31 31 25 \n9 Dec 30 34 25 \n10 January 29 32 27 \n11 Feb 29 32 25 \n12 March 29 31 24 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |425", "t": "Workers under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 425 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nWORKERS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n425.  SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total amount pending as wages for workers under the \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS) as the Union Government is yet to transfer the \namount in the State of Kerala; and \n(b)  the details of amount sanctioned to State of Keràla during each \nof the last three years? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):  As on date, there is no pending liability for wage component in \nrespect of States of Kerala under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS).  \n  \n(b):    Details of funds released to the State of Kerala under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in the last three financial years from 2020-21 to 2022-\n23 is given below: \n \nFinancial Years 2020-21  2021-22 2022-23 \nCentral fund released to the State of Kerala \n(Rs. In crore) 4,286.77 3,551.93 3,818.43 \n  \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |426", "t": "Beneficiaries under PMAY-G", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n426.  SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of beneficiaries who got the  amount for houses \nconstruction under Pradhan Mantri  Awas Yojana -Gramin (PMAY-\nG) during each of the last five years, State and district-wise; \n(b)  whether it is true that some States constantly  fail to implement \nthe said Yojana and if so, the details of such States; and \n(c)  the details of funds allocated along with their  utilization report \nunder the Yojana during the said period? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a) to (c): In order to achieve the target of “Housing for All” in rural \nareas, the Ministry of Rural Development is implementing Pradhan \nMantriAwaasYojana- Gramin (PMAY-G) with effect from 1st April, 2016 \nto provide assistance to eligible rural households with an overall \ntarget to construct 2.95 crore pucca houses with basic amenities by \nMarch, 2024. Against the mandated target of 2.95 crore houses, more \nthan 2.94 crore houses have already been sanctioned to the \nbeneficiaries by various States/UTs and construction of 2.50 crore \nhouses has also been completed as on 29.11.2023. The scheme has \nbeen able to achieve its major milestones and the Ministry is \ncommitted to a chieve the target of construction of 2.95 crore pucca \nhouses by the deadline prescribed of 31st March, 2024. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nThe Year-wise and State/UT-wise details of houses sanctioned to \neligible beneficiaries under Pradhan MantriAwaasYojana - Gramin \n(PMAY-G) during each of the last five years i.e., from the financial year \n2018-19 to 2022-23 is given in Annexure.   The District level data of all \nStates/UTs related to houses allocated and completed can be seen at \nprogramme website at www.pmayg.nic.in ---->AwaasSoft---->Reports----\n>Houses progress against the target financial year. \n \nThe Central Assistance under PMAY -G is released directly to the \nState/UT considering the State/UT as a unit. Further release of these \nfunds to beneficiaries in various District s/Blocks/GPs is done by the \nrespective State Government/UT Administration.  \n  \nThe amount of Central Share of funds released to the States/UTs \nfor construction of houses under PMAY-G during the last five years i.e., \nfrom the financial year 2018 -19 to 2022 -23 was around Rs.1,60,853.38 \ncrore. The corresponding utilization of funds by the States/UTs was \nRs.2,39,334.02 crore including State/UT Share. \n  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n   \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \nREGARDING BENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n  \nYear-wise and State -wise details of houses sanctioned to eligible \nbeneficiaries under Pradhan MantriAwaasYojana - Gramin (PMAY -G) \nduring in last five years i.e. from the financial year 2018-19 to 2022-23. \n(units in nos.) \nS.No. State Name \nHouses \nSanctioned \nin FY 2018-\n19 \nHouses \nSanctioned \nin FY 2019-\n20 \nHouses \nSanctioned \nin FY 2020-\n21 \nHouses \nSanctioned \nin FY 2021-\n22 \nHouses \nSanctioned \nin FY 2022-\n23 \n1 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 1,063 2,135 19,028 11,042 2,706 \n2 ASSAM 40,378 1,79,831 1,50,038 2,16,318 10,52,922 \n3 BIHAR 4,25,602 10,43,808 6,25,245 8,98,820 1,32,699 \n4 CHHATTISGARH 2,89,909 1,50,952 1,57,531 371 81,374 \n5 GOA 107 53 32 47 18 \n6 GUJARAT 26,308 1,01,288 21,321 1,07,184 1,47,958 \n7 HARYANA 2,485 113 60 3,316 5,092 \n8 \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH 817 1,037 3,996 2,729 792 \n9 \nJAMMU AND \nKASHMIR 17,672 38,612 64,036 55,844 7,810 \n10 JHARKHAND 1,37,291 3,01,972 3,61,540 3,90,135 11,583 \n11 KERALA 926 738 3,329 12,608 1,626 \n12 \nMADHYA \nPRADESH 3,04,707 3,78,325 7,56,530 4,90,045 7,53,869 \n13 MAHARASHTRA 78,766 2,47,512 2,90,972 1,16,781 3,03,589 \n14 MANIPUR 15 167 17,822 1,725 13,845 \n15 MEGHALAYA 3,816 10,968 26,480 3,353 8,866 \n16 MIZORAM 1,708 2,430 7,017 0 6,951 \n17 NAGALAND 3,504 615 4,706 9,750 4,187 \n18 ODISHA 2,31,207 5,48,248 2,90,475 3,418 8,94,376 \n19 PUNJAB 6,794 8,126 1,885 11,047 4,765 \n20 RAJASTHAN 1,82,462 3,75,282 2,64,426 3,86,854 7,451 \n21 SIKKIM 0 0 0 280 48 \n22 TAMIL NADU 93,152 90,679 1,04,296 2,22,791 37,763 \n23 TRIPURA 387 22,533 991 1,57,217 51,872 \n24 \nUTTAR \nPRADESH 3,83,861 1,78,176 7,28,477 4,34,903 8,60,868 \n25 UTTARAKHAND 2,326 32 47 15,390 18,752 \n26 WEST BENGAL 1,00,118 9,62,507 9,41,651 1,66,724 11,06,832 \n27 \nANDAMAN AND \nNICOBAR 0 929 397 0 6 \n28 \nDADRA AND \nNAGAR HAVELI \n& DAMAN AND \nDIU 4,589 0 110 49 941 \n\n29 LAKSHADWEEP 0 0 0 0 0 \n30 PUDUCHERRY* 11 0 0 0 0 \n31 \nANDHRA \nPRADESH 16,251 2,304 1,816 0 1,78,899 \n32 KARNATAKA 15,007 1,000 35,577 3,864 37,923 \n33 TELANGANA* 0 0 0 0 0 \n34 LADAKH 0 379 200 451 1 \n  Total 23,71,239 46,50,751 48,80,031 37,23,056 57,36,384 \n* Telangana & Puducherry are not implementing PMAY"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |427", "t": "Wage Liability for MGNREGS Workers", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 427 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nWAGE LIABILITY FOR MGNREGS WORKERS \n \n427.  SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the amount of wage liability for the Mahatma Gandhi National \nRural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) workers of \nTamil Nadu standing as arrears till date; \n(b)  the time lag between sanction and actual release of amount to \nthe State of Tamil Nadu; and \n(c)  whether delay in wages dampen the spirit of the scheme forcing \nworkers to go out for other avenues of revenue? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (c):   Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven wage \nemployment scheme. This is a fall back option in the absence of \nopportunity of regular income. Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, \nStates/UTs furnish funds release proposals to Government of India. \nThe Ministry releases funds periodically in two tranches with each \ntranche consisting of one or more installments, keeping in view the \n“agreed to” Labour Budget, demand for works, opening balance, pace \nof utilization of funds, pending liabilities, overall performance and \nsubject to submission of relevant documents by the States/UTs.  \n \nAs on 29.11.2023, an amount of Rs. 261.85 crore is pending for wage \ncomponent in respect of State of Tamil Nadu under Mahatma Gandhi \nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS). \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |428", "t": "Khelo India Schemes", "head": "Page 1 of 4 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nKhelo India Schemes  \n \n428. SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.: \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL: \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has taken any step to tap the hidd en talent in \nthe rural areas of the country through Khelo India programme and if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(b) the details o f the Schemes of the Government in the States of Tamil \nNadu, Maharashtra and Odisha to encourage rural spor ts activities under \nthe ‘‘Khelo India’’ programme being run in the country; \n \n(c) whether the Government is emphasizing on developing special facilities \nto encourage meritorious children/youth from rural areas of the country so  \nthat they also get a n opportunity to participate in national/international \ntournaments and represent the country; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof, State and district  wise including Tamil Nadu, \nMaharashtra and Odisha and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(e) whether th e Government has taken steps to ensure the availabili ty of \ntrained professionals for mentoring young talent  in rural areas of the \ncountry; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \n\nPage 2 of 4 \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) & (b):  Sports’ b eing a State subject, the primary responsibility of \ntapping the sports talents , development of special facilities to \nencourage meritorious children/youth  in the country, providing trained \nprofessional for mentoring, including in rural areas, rests primaril y with \nthe respective State/Union Territory Governments. Central Government \nsupplements their efforts.  However, the Ministry of Youth Affairs & \nSports (MYAS) implements the following schemes for the development of \nsports in the country , including the Stat e of Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra \nand Odisha: \n(i) Khelo India - National Programme for Development of Sports; (ii) \nAssistance to National Sports Federations; (iii) Special Awards to \nWinners in International Sports Events and their Coaches; (iv) National \nSports A wards; (v) Pension to Meritorious Sportspersons; (vi) Pandit \nDeendayal Upadhyay National Sports Welfare Fund; (vii) National Sports \nDevelopment Fund; and (viii) Running Sports Training Centres through \nSports Authority of India. \nDetails of the above schemes  are available in the public domain on \nthe websites of this Ministry and the Sports Authority of India.  \n(c) to (f): ‘Khelo India – National Programme for Development of Sports’ \nof this Ministry aims at strengthening the entire sports ecosystem to \npromote the twin objectives of mass participation and promotion of \nexcellence in sports across the country. It focuses on broad -basing of \nsports among the youth and promotion of sports activities throughout \nthe country, including rural areas. One of the components  of this \nScheme, “Promotion of rural and indigenous/tribal games” is specifically \ndedicated to the rural sector of the country.  \n \nApart from above , this Ministry has sanctioned 307  sports \ninfrastructure projects at cost of ₹  2809.13 crore, of various categ ories \nunder the Khelo India Scheme across the country, including the rural \nareas, on the basis of proposals received from the State/UT \nGovernments and other eligible entities, including 05 projects in the \nState of Tamil Nadu, 13 projects in the State of Ma harashtra and 04 \nprojects in the State of Odisha.  \n\nPage 3 of 4 \n \nIn addition , Sports Authority of India (SAI) , an autonomous \norganisation under the aegis of MYAS, has established 23 National \nCentres of Excellence (NCoE) across the Country . These centres  impart \nspecialized training, including expert coaches, boarding and lodging etc.,  \nto promising State and National level pl ayers, from across the country , \nby providing them state of the art infrastructure and training facilities, \nsports science backup, individualized diet  prescribed by trained \nnutritionists under overall supervision of the best coaches and qualified \nsupport staff. The details of NCoE are at Annexure. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nPage 4 of 4 \n \nANNEXURE \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part s (a) to (f)  of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion no.  428 for 05/12/2023 regarding Khelo India Schemes  asked by \nShri C.N. Annadurai, Dr. Subhash Ramrao Bhamre, Dr. DNV Senthilkumar S., \nSmt. Manjulata Mandal, Dr. Amol Ramsing Kolhe, Shri Kuldeep Rai Sharma, \nShrimati Supriya Sule, Hon’ble MPs. \n \n \n \nDetails of National Centres of Excellence (NCoEs) \n \nS. No. State NCoE location \n1 Madhya Pradesh Bhopal \n2 West Bengal Kolkata \n3 Odisha Jagatpur \n4  \nKerela \nTrivandrum \n5 Alleppey \n6 Karnataka Bang"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |429", "t": "15th Instalment of PM-Kisan Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 429 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n15th INSTALMENT OF PM-KISAN SCHEME \n \n429. DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR: \nMS. S. JOTHIMANI: \nSHRI V.K. SREEKANDAN: \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)  whether it is true that the 15th instalment of the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi \n(PM-Kisan) scheme has been released recently and if so, the details thereof, and the total \namount released to the State of Tamil Nadu and total number of farmers benefited under \nthis scheme as on date;  \n(b)  whether there was a delay in the release of the 15th instalment of the PM -Kisan \nscheme to the beneficiaries and if so, the details and the reasons for the delay;  \n(c)  whether there has been a decline in the annual disbursement of funds to beneficiaries \nunder the said scheme and if so, the details and the reasons for the decline;  \n(d)  the number of beneficiaries of PM-Kisan since its inception till 2023; and  \n(e)  whether the Government plan to increase the amount of Rs. 6000/ - under the PM -\nKisan scheme to benefit the farmers and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons \ntherefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \n \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) The PM-KISAN scheme is a central sector scheme launched in February 2019 to \nsupplement the financial needs of land -holding farmers. Under this scheme, the financial \n\nbenefit of Rs 6,000/- per year in three equal instalments every four months is transferred \ninto the bank accounts of farmers' families across the country through Direct Benefit \nTransfer (DBT) mode. A farmer -centric digital infrastructure has ensured the benefits of \nthe scheme reach all the farmers acr oss the country without any involvement of the \nmiddlemen. Maintaining absolute transparency in registering and verifying beneficiaries, \nthe Government of India has disbursed over Rs 2.80 lakh Cr to more than 11 Cr farmers. \nIn the Tamil Nadu State, over Rs.  9,945.69 Cr has been transferred to more than 45.83 \nlakh farmers since inception of the scheme. \n(b) There was no delay in the release of 15th instalment under PM-KISAN. The 15th \ninstalment pertaining to the period August – November 2023, was released on 15/11/2023. \n(c) The annual disbursement of funds to beneficiaries under the PM-KISAN scheme since \ninception is as under: \nPeriod Benefits released (in Rs. Cr.) \nFY 2018-19 6,323 \nFY 2019-20 48,739 \nFY 2020-21 61,931 \nFY 2021-22 67,121 \nFY 2022-23 58,258 \nFY 2023-24 (as on 22/11/2023) 38,660 \n \nThere was slight decline in the FY 2022-23. The reason was the introduction of mandatory \nland seeding provision for the  farmers and seeding of Aadhaar with their active bank \naccount to ensure that the benefits are transferred directly to the int ended beneficiaries’ \naccounts smoothly. \n(d)  Since inception of the PM-KISAN Scheme, more than 11 crore farmers’ families \nhave received benefits of the Scheme. \n(e) There is no such proposal under consideration. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |43", "t": "Dropping Percentage of ST Students for CUET", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.43 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nDropping Percentage of ST Students for CUET \n \n43. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the dropping percentage of the students from ST category for \nthe Common University Entrance Test (CUET) conducted by National Testing Agency (NTA); \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is proposing to find out the reasons of the dropping percentage \nespecially for the ST category students; and \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to improve the percentage for ST students? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (d) In pursuance of the National Education Policy, 2020, the Common University Entrance Test \n(CUET) is being conducted by the National Testing Agency (NTA) for admission in Central Universities \nfrom the academic year 2022 -23 to provide a common platform and equal opportunities to candidates \nacross the country. \n \nAccording to information provided by NTA, the number of candidates from the ST category who \nregistered for CUET (UG) increased from 58,875  for the academic year 2022 -23 to 1,06,287 for the \nacademic year  2023-24, marking a rise of 80.5%  in registrations. ST category candidates who \nappeared in CUET (UG) increased from 19,289 for academic year  2022-23 to 53,645 for the academic \nyear 2023-24, an increase of over 178%. \nSimilarly, the number of candidates from the ST category who registered for CUET (PG) , increased \nfrom 51,171 for the academic year 2022-23 to 71,070 for the academic year 2023 -24, marking an \nincrease of 38.89 %. ST category candidates who appeared in CUET (PG) increased from 25,883 for \nthe academic year 2022-23 to  37,844 for the academic year 2023-24, an increase of over 46%. \nCUET supports the ST category students, as they are able to apply to more than one university with \nsingle entrance test and fee. Moreover, admission in different Cen tral Universities follows reservation \npolicy applicable to Higher Education Institutions. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |430", "t": "Reduction in Budget Allocation under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 430 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nREDUCTION IN BUDGET ALLOCATION UNDER MGNREGS \n \n430.  SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \nSHRI A. RAJA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has received complaints that the \nlabourers have not received wages under the Mahatma Gandhi \nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) \nduring the last four months despite the festival season; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise particularly in Tamil Nadu; \n(c)  the budget provisions made under the said scheme and the funds \ndisbursed to the States during each of the last five years, State-\nwise; \n(d)  whether some funds are withheld in respect of certain States and \nif so, the reasons therefor; \n(e)  whether budget allocation has been reduced under the said \nscheme during the year 2023-24; and \n(f)  if so, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)&(b):    State-wise details of complaints received on Centralized Public \nGrievance Redress and Monitoring System (CPGRAMS) portal in the last four \nmonths (1.8.2023 till 30.11.2023) under the category of delay in payment is \ngiven below: \n  \nState Total complaints \nreceived \nTotal complaints \ndisposed \nTotal complaints \npending \nBihar 6 5 1 \nKarnataka 2 1 1 \nUttar Pradesh 1 0 1 \nWest Bengal 2 0 2 \n\nPunjab 1 0 1 \nTamil Nadu 4 4 0 \nMadhya Pradesh 5 4 1 \nRajasthan 8 7 1 \nTotal 29 21 8 \n   \n(c):     The details of funds allocation at Budget Estimate stage, Revised \nEstimate stage and fund released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS are given \nbelow: \n(Rs. in crore) \nYear Budget Estimate Revised Estimate Release \n2018-19 55,000.00 61,830.09 61,829.55 \n2019-20 60,000.00 71,001.81 71,687.71 \n2020-21 61,500.00 1,11,500.00 1,11,170.86 \n2021-22 73,000.00 98,000.00 98,467.84 \n2022-23 73,000.00 89,400.00 90,810.99 \n  \nState/UT-wise details of funds released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS during \nthe financial years from 2018-19 to 2022-23 are given at Annexure. \n  \n(d):  Except State Government of West Bengal, funds is being released to all \nStates/UTs. The release of funds to the State of West Bengal has been \nstopped as per provision of Section 27 of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Act, 2005 due to non -compliance of directives of \nCentral Government. \n  \n(e)&(f): Financial allocation of Rs. 60,000 crores has been made for Mahatma \nGandhi National Rural Employment Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) for the \nfinancial year 2023-24 at Budget Estimate (BE) stage. Further, an amount of \nRs.10,000 crore has been provid ed by the Ministry of Finance as an advance \nfrom Contingency Fund of India.  \n  \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand -driven wage employment \nScheme. Funds release to States/UTs is a continuous process and Central \nGovernment is committed to making funds available  to States/UTs for the \nimplementation of the Scheme as per the demand for work on the ground. \n \nThe Ministry seeks additional funds under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS \nfrom the Ministry of Finance as and when required for meeting the demand \nfor work on the ground.  \n  \n******* \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to part (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 430 dated \n05.12.2023. \n  \nState/UT-wise details of funds released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS during the \nfinancial years from 2018-19 to 2022-23 (Rs. in lakh) \nS. \nNo States/UTs 2018-19  2019-20 2020-21  2021-22  2022-23 \n1 Andhra \nPradesh 657110.67 720472.46 1030509.79 718267.16 798909.30 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh 19800.64 21020.01 34027.69 45374.43 57757.99 \n3 Assam 103097.37 168752.49 260233.46 222026.01 205234.84 \n4 Bihar 281936.01 325093.78 728423.57 540736.96 639528.76 \n5 Chhattisgarh 289476.07 264031.92 394347.98 389433.98 338355.40 \n6 Goa 48.83 216.76 356.91 3.81 511.53 \n7 Gujarat 102449.67 74758.23 147812.01 161524.32 169207.36 \n8 Haryana 34829.10 33812.58 76355.62 72267.99 37398.62 \n9 Himachal \nPradesh 75090.72 59748.12 94833.37 97575.08 115747.62 \n10 Jammu and \nKashmir 79334.12 118370.31 115231.99 95013.74 105061.12 \n11 Jharkhand 149989.63 127292.89 342408.42 306382.91 270863.73 \n12 Karnataka 297813.10 546271.17 550099.46 602807.73 622528.20 \n13 Kerala 234048.78 352016.95 428677.68 355193.00 381842.70 \n14 Madhya \nPradesh 452909.74 471894.15 908672.51 847908.76 570213.49 \n15 Maharashtra 194892.31 167066.42 160025.70 205645.71 254973.07 \n16 Manipur 28698.66 61074.66 130673.85 56310.74 108663.26 \n17 Meghalaya 78903.38 102443.59 128416.59 112166.07 111691.95 \n18 Mizoram 39783.58 52507.57 59045.38 54891.55 53871.87 \n19 Nagaland 19322.98 33044.70 48382.14 56945.51 89744.90 \n20 Odisha 216328.30 243278.38 521529.26 568015.17 463836.25 \n21 Punjab 57826.49 74886.26 123913.55 125759.36 118213.27 \n22 Rajasthan 524471.10 726747.87 892076.25 986774.85 966299.14 \n23 Sikkim 9586.17 8269.34 11016.55 11241.97 9255.39 \n24 Tamil Nad"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |431", "t": "Wages under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 431 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nWAGES UNDER MGNREGS \n \n431.  MS. S. JOTHIMANI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there have been instances of pending  wages under the \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural  Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS); \n(b)  if so, the details of the amount pending to be  disbursed to \nbeneficiaries, State-wise, particularly Tamil Nadu; \n(c)  the details of beneficiaries who have not  received wages under \nMGNREGS, State and districtwise particularly Tamil Nadu; \n(d)  the data and the number of job cards issued  and active job cards \nseparately under MGNREGS, year and State-wise; \n(e)  the allocation made under MGNREGS since  2019, year and State-\nwise; and \n(f)  whether the Government has any proposal to  provide an extra \n100 days of job for differently abled  people in a household, if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)&(b):   Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) is a demand driven wage \nemployment Scheme. Fund released to the States/UTs is a co ntinuous \nprocess and Central Government is committed in making funds \navailable keeping in view the demand for work. The details of the \npending liabilities for various state UT -wise including Tamil Nadu for \nwage component under  Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as on 29 .11.2023 are \ngiven at Annexure-I. \n(c):     Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, fund is released to the State \nand not to district.   As on   29.11.2023, a total pending liabilities of \n\nwage payment in respect of State of Tamil Nadu is an amount of Rs. \n261.86 crore.  \n(d):    State/UT-wise details of Jobcard issued since inception and \nActive Jobcard as on 29.11.23 under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is given \nat Annexure-II. \n(e):     Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \nis demand drive wage employment scheme. No allocation of fund to \nState/UT wise is made under the Scheme. State/UT -wise details of \nfunds released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS during the financial \nyears from 2019 -20 to 2022 -23 and current financial year 2023 -24 (as \non 28.11.2023) are given at Annexure-III.  \n(f):  No, Sir. \n  \n****** \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to part (a)&(b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No. 431 dated 05.12.2023. \n  \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for wage component \nunder Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as on 29.11.2023. \nSl. No.  States/UTs Wage liability (Rs. in lakh) \n1 Andhra Pradesh  11056.29 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  1848.27 \n3 Assam  9324.32 \n4 Bihar  6495.58 \n5 Chhattisgarh  5954.57 \n6 Goa 22.65 \n7 Gujarat  66.94 \n8 Haryana  1759.06 \n9 Himachal Pradesh  2992.21 \n10 Jammu and Kashmir  8243.87 \n11 Jharkhand  508.44 \n12 Karnataka  17789.54 \n13 Kerala 5194.67 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 5390.17 \n15 Maharashtra 9402.22 \n16 Manipur 0.00 \n17 Meghalaya 399.25 \n18 Mizoram  2784.32 \n19 Nagaland 1080.42 \n20 Odisha  6493.54 \n21 Punjab  332.72 \n22 Rajasthan  35883.86 \n23 Sikkim 58.24 \n24 Tamil Nadu  26185.91 \n25 Telangana  1098.64 \n26 Tripura  1770.82 \n27 Uttar Pradesh  35446.96 \n28 Uttarakhand  3557.55 \n29 Andaman & Nicobar 0.00 \n30 Lakshadweep 0.00 \n31 Puducherry  202.14 \n32 Ladakh 716.54 \n33 Dadra & Nagar Haveli & \nDaman & Diu  0.00 \n  \n\n  \n  \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No. 431 dated 05.12.2023. \nState/UT-wise details of Jobcard issued since inception and Active \nJobcard as on 29.11.23 under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS \nSl. \nNo. State/UTs \nJobcard issued \nsince inception as \non 29.11.23 (in lakh) \nActive Jobcard as \non 29.11.23 (in \nlakh) \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 0.34 0.11 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH 68.53 56.95 \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 3.16 2.99 \n4 ASSAM 63.27 42.14 \n5 BIHAR 159.23 85.2 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 37.96 32.83 \n7 DN HAVELI AND DD 0.04 0.01 \n8 GOA 0.36 0.07 \n9 GUJARAT 43.48 18.23 \n10 HARYANA 13.04 6.56 \n11 HIMACHAL PRADESH 14.49 9.39 \n12 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 13.42 10.48 \n13 JHARKHAND 56.