ndia;\n \n(b) if so, the steps being taken to address the hike;\n \n(c) whether the Government has taken steps including introduction of Udaan\nmodel of flight along with more international flight services for reducing rates\nas rate hikes are affecting the expatriates and their ability to travel and if so,\nthe details thereof;\n \n(d) whether the Government has received any proposals or recommendations\nfrom State Governments especially Kerala  for introduction of a State\nGovernment owned airline or for chartered airline service and if so, the\ndetails thereof; and\n \n(e) whether the Government has accorded permissions for the same and if so,\nthe details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (e)  A statement is laid on the table of the Lok Sabha.\n \n*****\n*70. SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question\nNo.70 regarding \"Hike in Airfare from Middle East Countries\" to be\nanswered on 07.12.2023.\n \n(a) & (b) As per prevailing regulations,\n    (i) The airfare is market driven and is neither regulated nor established by\nthe Government. The airlines are free to fix tariffs under the provisions of\nsub-rule (1) of Rule 135 of the Aircraft Rules, 1937 having regard to all\nrelevant factors, including cost of operation, characteristics of services,\ngenerally prevailing tariff etc. \n    (ii) The airline fare system runs in multiple levels (buckets or Reservation\nBooking Designators) which are in line with practice being followed globally.\nThe fares are fixed by airlines keeping in mind the market, demand,\nseasonality, prevailing international scenarios and other market forces. The\nairfare increases with increase in demand of seats as the lower fare buckets\nget sold out faster and move to higher fare buckets.\n     (iii)  The airlines are required to be compliant with the regulatory\nprovisions under Aircraft Rules, 1937.\n \n(c) At present there is no proposal under consideration for operation of\ninternational flight services between India and Middle East/ Gulf countries\nunder Udan like model.\n \n(d) In the recent past, the Government has received references including from\nthe Chief Minister of Kerala requesting necessary approvals for charter\nflights to be operated for transport of non-resident Keralites from Gulf\ncountries to India.\n \n(e) Charter flights are generally approved by DGCA under the Inclusive Tour\nPackage (ITP) guidelines. Flights not covered under the ITP guidelines are\nexamined on case to case basis by this Ministry. However, there is no specific\nrequest for operation of charter aircraft on India-Gulf sector with this\nMinistry as of 28.11.2023.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |700", "t": "Installation of LED under AMRUT", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 700 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \nINSTALLATION OF LED UNDER AMRUT \n \nNO. 700.  SHRI RAJIV RANJAN SINGH ALIAS LALAN SINGH: \n                SHRI DINESH CHANDRA YADAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether work for installation of LED in place of street lights \nhas been completed by the Government under the AMRUT \nscheme in Bihar; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the details of fund spent on the said scheme? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a & b): So far, for promoting energy conservation, 3.12 lakh \nidentified conventional streetlights have been replaced with energy \nefficient LEDs in State of Bihar against a set target of 1.85 lakhs.  \n(c): No financial allocation is made for replacement of street lights \nwith LEDs because the programme is run without any budgetary \nallocation from Government of India as it is based on a sustainable \nbusiness model where the cost of LEDs is repaid by Urban Local \nBodies (ULBs) from savings in energy and maintenance expenditure \nover a period of time through savings in electricity bill.  \n \n****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |701", "t": "Data of Water Bodies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 701 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nDATA OF WATER BODIES \n701.    DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL  DR. SHR IKANT EKNATH SHINDE \nDR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT   PROF. RITA BAHUGUNA JO SHI \nDR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV   \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has data of all water-bo dies, both natural and man-made in the States of \nMaharashtra, Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh and if so, the detail thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has data of all the wate r-bodies separately in urban and rural areas in the  States \nof Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether the Government plans to ensure that the  rural areas in the State of Maharashtra, Uttar Pra desh \nand Madhya Pradesh continue their reliance on water conservation schemes and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) & (b)      The Ministry of Jal Shakti conducted the first  census of water bodies in collaboration with the \nstates and  in convergence with the sixth minor irr igation census (reference year 2017-18), under the \ncentrally sponsored scheme – “Irrigation Census” wi th the objective of developing a national database for \nall water bodies. The information on important aspe cts of the water bodies like type, condition, statu s of \nencroachments, use, storage capacity etc. were capt ured by the states during the census and the report  was \npublished in March 2023. Information on number of n atural and man-made water bodies in rural and urban  \nareas in the States of Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh a nd Madhya Pradesh as available from the first censu s of \nwater bodies is given in the Annexure I . \n(c)  Water is a State subject and planning, funding, ex ecution and maintenance of water bodies, including \ntheir restoration, rejuvenation and conservation, i s addressed by the State Government concerned. Cent ral \nGovernment supplements the efforts of the States th rough technical and financial support. Central \nAssistance to States are being provided in the area  of water conservation through various Government o f \nIndia programmes like Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchay ee Yojana (PMKSY), Mahatma Gandhi National \nRural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MNREGS), Atal Mi ssion for Rejuvenation and Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT) 2.0, “Jal Shakti Abhiyan: Catch The Rain” (JSA:CTR) campaign, Mission Amrit \nSarovar etc.   \n*** \n\nANNEXURE I  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF  UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO.701 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 RE GARDING “DATA OF WATER \nBODIES”.  \nNumber of natural and man-made water bodies in rura l and urban areas reported in the first Census \nof Water bodies  \n \nState  No. of water Bodies  \nNatural  Man made  Total  \nRural  Urban  Total  Rural  Urban  Total  Rural  Urban  Total  \nMaharashtra 565 9 574 95778 710 96488 96343 719 970 62 \nMadhya \nPradesh \n16600 493 17093 64412 1138 65550 81012 1631 82643 \nUttar Pradesh 218639 4511 223150 21500 437 21937 24 0139 4948 245087 \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |702", "t": "Role of Urban Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 702 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nROLE OF URBAN SECTOR \n \nNO.702. SHRI K. NAVASKANI \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government agrees with the view that to navigate \nthe challenges and identify policy moves that could be put into \npractice, making more resilient and future-  proof cities \nrequires an urban anchor in every State; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the steps that are proposed to be taken by \nthe Government keeping in view that the urban sector would \nplay a critical role in the economy’s structural transformation \nand in sustaining the high economic growth rate in the coming \ndecades; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)    Yes, Government agrees with the view. \n \n(b)  As per the 12\nthSchedule of the Constitution of India, urban planning \nis a State subject. Government of India supplements the efforts of the  \n\n-2- \n \nStates through schematic interventions/ advisories. It provides \nfinancial and technical support to the States. State Town Planning \nDepartments and Urban Development Authorities act as city and state \nlevel urban anchors.   \nMinistry of Housing and Urban Affairs, Government of India has been \ntaking several initiatives for promoting growth and sustainability in \nurban sector. \nSwachh Bharat Mission is accelerating change for safe sanitation, \nwaste management with focus on door to door collection/segregation, \nwaste processing etc. \nSmart Cities Mission aims at promoting cities that provide core \ninfrastructure, clean and sustainable environment and give a decent \nquality of life to their citizens through the application of ‘smart \nsolutions’. The focus is on sustainable and inclusive development by \ncreation of replicable models which act as lighthouses to other \naspiring cities.  \nNational Urban Livelihood Mission aims t o reduce poverty and \nvulnerability of the urban poor households by enabling them to access \ngainful self employment and skilled wage employment opportunities. \nThe mission also aims at providing shelters equipped with essential \nservices to the urban homeless in a phased manner and would also \naddress livelihood concerns of the urban street vendors. \nPM SVANidhi s cheme is a special micro- credit facility launched by \nministry for providing affordable loans to street vendors. \nPradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (Urban) Mission intends to provide \nhousing in urban areas. The Mission addresses urban housing shortage \namong EWS/LIG and MIG categories including slum  dwellers by \nensuring pucca houses. It provides Central Assistance to the \nimplementing agencies through States/Union Territories (UTs) and \nCentral Nodal Agencies (CNAs) for providing houses to all eligible \nfamilies/ beneficiaries. \n…3/ - \n \n\n-3- \nAMRUT Mission,  Under Sub- Scheme on ‘Formulation of GIS based \nMaster Plan for 461 AMRUT Cities selected , final GIS Database \nCreation has been created for 443 towns, Draft GIS based Master Plan \nhas been prepared 330 towns out of that final GIS based Master Plans \nhave been notified for 180 towns. 77 Training programs were \norganised and 2900 officers were trained. Under AMRUT 2.0, a  Sub \nScheme on Formulation of GIS based Master Plans of Class -II Towns \nwith population of 50,000 -  99,999’ was also launched for 675 towns \nwith total outlay of Rs. 631.13 Cr. This Sub-  Scheme is in initial stage \nof implementation. A Pilot Scheme for preparation of Town Planning \nScheme (TPS) and Local Area Plans (LAP) was launched under AMRUT \nfor 25 cities. Total allocation was 50 Cr. All 25 cities have completed \nthe preliminary stage.  \nIn order to incentivize States to undertake urban planning reforms, two \nschemes were launched:  \na) Scheme for Special Assistance to States for Capital Investment \n2022-23 – Part – VI (Urban Planning Reforms) (allocation - Rs. 6000 Cr): \nThe reform components include Modernization of Building Bylaws, \nAdoption of Transferrable Development Rights (TDR), Implementation \nof Local Area Plans (LAP) and Town Planning Schemes (TPS),  \nImplementation of Transit -oriented Development (ToD), Creation of \nSponge Cities, Removing Taxation for running the Buses for Public \nTransport. Funds amounting to Rs. 4093.16 Cr were released by the \nDepartment of Expenditure, Ministry of finance to 12 States. \n \nb) Scheme for Special Assistance to States for Capital Investment \n2023-24 – Part – III (Urban Planning Reforms)  (allocation – Rs. 15000 \nCr) The reform components include Augmentation of human resources \nfor strengthening urban planning ecosystem, Implementation of Town \nPlanning Scheme (TPS)/ Land Pooling Scheme, Modernization of \nBuilding Bylaws, Promoting in-situ slum rehabilitation, Transit-Oriented \nDevelopment (TOD), Transferable Development Rights as planning tool, \nStrengthen"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |703", "t": "Road Safety Audit", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 703 \nANSWERED ON 07TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nROAD SAFETY AUDIT \n  \n†703. SHRI SUNIL BABURAO MENDHE: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)    whether the Government proposes/is likely to propose organising \nof training courses and training workshops for capacity building in Road \nSafety Audit and Road Safety Engineering; \n  \n(b)    if so, the details thereof, State-wise; and \n  \n(c)    if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (c) Indian Academy of Highway Engineers (IAHE) under Ministry of \nRoad Transport & Highways regularly conducts training courses and \nworkshops for capacity building in Road Safety Audit and Road Safety \nEngineering for the officers of Ministry of Road Tra nsport & Highways, \nNational Highway Authority of India(NHAI), National Highways \nInfrastructure Development Corporation Limited (NHIDCL), Border \nRoads Organisation(BRO), State PWDs and Highway Contractors/  \nConsultants. So far, IAHE has conducted 18 certific ation courses on \nRoad Safety Audit wherein 394 Highway professionals have been \ntrained. IAHE has also organized 44 other training programs (under \n\nCalendar trainings, sponsored Programmes etc.) on Road Safety \nwherein 1800 Highway professionals have been tra ined.  In addition to \nthe above, various courses on Road Safety and Road Safety Audit are \nalso conducted by Central Road Research Institute (CRRI), Indian \nInstitutes of Technology (IITs) and National Institutes of Technology \n(NITs).   \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |704", "t": "Status of Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 704 \nANSWERED ON 07TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nSTATUS OF HIGHWAYS \n704.   DR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the details and the present status of the  highways declared as \nNational Highways (NHs) along  with the NHs which are under \nconstruction at present  in the country, State -wise particularly in \nKalaburagi district of Karnataka; \n(b) the details of new NH projects sanctioned for  Karnataka during \nthe last three years and the current year; and \n(c) the action taken by the Government to  complete these projects, \nState-wise?  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) The Ministry is primarily responsible for development and \nmaintenance of National Highways (NHs). Total length of NHs in the \ncountry is about 1,46,145 km at present. State / Union Territory (UT) \n– wise details of notified NHs, including for the State of Karnataka, at \npresent vis-à-vis that in March, 2014 are placed at Annexure-I. 6 nos. \nof NHs in a length of 410 km are passing through Kalaburagi district \nin the State of Karnataka. \n\n State / UT – wise de tails of under construction NHs projects, \nincluding for the State of Karnataka, are placed at Annexure -II. Out \nof this, NHs Projects in 159 km length amounting to Rs. 2,493 Crore \nare under construction in Kalburgi District in the State of Karnataka. \n \n(b) NHs projects in about 2,339 km length amounting to Rs. 55,079 \nCrore have been sanctioned / approved for the State of Karnataka \nduring the last three years and the current year.  \n(c)  The Government has taken number of steps to ensure timely \ncompletion of projects like awarding projects after adequate \npreparation in terms of land acquisition and preconstruction \nactivities, streamlining (i) land acquisition notification process, (ii) \nForest & Environment clearances and (iii) simplifying procedure for \napproval of GAD (General Arrangement Drawing) by Railways, apart \nfrom other initiatives like rationalized exit for equity investors, \npremium re -scheduling, securitization of road sector lo ans, \nrevamping dispute resolution mechanism, relaxations in contract \nprovisions under \"Atmanirbhar Bharat\" to improve liquidity of funds \navailable with contractors/developers, periodic reviews of projects \nat various levels, etc.  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 704 ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 ASKED \nBY DR. UMESH G. JADHAV REGARDING STATUS OF HIGHWAYS \nState / UT – wise details of notified NHs, including for the State of \nKarnataka, at present vis-à-vis that in March, 2014:- \nNH Length in Km \nSl. No. State/UT As on 31.03.2014 As on 31.10.2023 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 4,190 8,683 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 2,027 4,285 \n3 Assam 3,634 4,077 \n4 Bihar 4,467 6,132 \n5 Chandigarh 24 15 \n6 Chhattisgarh 3,031 3,620 \n7 Delhi 80 157 \n8 Goa 269 299 \n9 Gujarat 4,694 8,099 \n10 Haryana 2,050 3,391 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 2,196 2,607 \n12 Jammu and Kashmir  1,513 1,876 \n13 Jharkhand 2,968 3,633 \n14 Karnataka 6,177 8,191 \n15 Kerala 1,700 1,858 \n16 Ladakh  806 806 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 5,116 9,105 \n18 Maharashtra 6,249 18,459 \n19 Manipur 1,452 1,840 \n20 Meghalaya 1,171 1,156 \n21 Mizoram 1,222 1,499 \n22 Nagaland 741 1,670 \n23 Orissa 4,550 5,897 \n24 Puducherry 53 64 \n25 Punjab 1,699 4,239 \n26 Rajasthan 7,646 10,706 \n27 Sikkim 149 709 \n28 Tamil Nadu 4,975 7,000 \n29 Telangana 2,400 4,926 \n30 Tripura 509 889 \n31 Uttar Pradesh 7,986 12,292 \n32 Uttarakhand 2,282 3,664 \n33 West Bengal 2,908 3,910 \n34 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 300 331 \n35 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 31 37 \n36 Daman & Diu 22 22 \n TOTAL 91,287 1,46,145 \n \n\nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 704 ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023  ASKED \nBY DR. UMESH G. JADHAV REGARDING STATUS OF HIGHWAYS \nState / UT – wise details of under construction NHs projects, including \nfor the State of Karnataka: - \nSl. No. State Under Construction NHs Projects \nNos. Length (km) Cost (Rs. Crore) \n1 Andaman and Nicobar 7 216 1,216 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 92 2,389 45,507 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 34 652 9,660 \n4 Assam 53 992 28,625 \n5 Bihar 66 2,187 60,813 \n6 Chhattisgarh 42 1,636 16,235 \n7 Delhi 8 80 12,876 \n8 Goa 15 62 4,675 \n9 Gujarat 67 1,749 43,980 \n10 Haryana 38 942 40,459 \n11 Haryana, Rajasthan 1 15 410 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 41 649 19,099 \n13 Jammu and Kashmir 54 932 48,429 \n14 Jharkhand 35 956 17,532 \n15 Karnataka 92 3,052 63,596 \n16 Kerala 30 912 53,207 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 65 2,283 25,833 \n18 Maharashtra 195 6,617 108,284 \n19 Manipur 38 735 9,716 \n20 Meghalaya 17 325 2,758 \n21 Mizoram 23 638 11,544 \n22 Nagaland 33 679 10,207 \n23 Odisha 63 1,863 30,671 \n24 Puducherry 2 11 99 \n25 Punjab 43 1,222 37,804 \n26 Rajasthan 61 2,099 30,237 \n27 Sikkim 16 232 4,678 \n28 Tamil Nadu 73 1,977 42,345 \n29 Telangana 65 2,076 28,211 \n30 T"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |705", "t": "State Owned Airlines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 705 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  7th December 2023 )  \nSTATE OWNED AIRLINES \n705.    SHRI M. SELVARAJ                         \n             DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR                   \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) whether it is a fact that small neighbouring countries are having \ntheir state owned airlines and our country does not have its own \nairlines, if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the reasons for not operating its State owned airlines; \n(c) whether the Union Government has proposed to start State \nowned airlines; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(e) whether the Union Government is aware of the fact that air \nPassenger traffic reached to over 327 million at airports acorss \nIndia during the fiancial year 2023 and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      \n(GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)) \n(a)&(b): At present, Alliance Air, a  wholly -owned subsidiary of  AI \nAssets Holding Ltd. (AIAHL) , which is a special purpose vehicle \nformed by the Government of India after the disinvestment of Air \nIndia Limited, is in operation.; \n \n(c)&(d): There is no such proposal.; \n \n(e): Details of the passenger traffic handled at the airports in the \ncountry are attached as Annexure. \n****** \n\nAnnexure \nPassengers handled at Indian airports during 2022-23 (In Numbers) \nAirport Domestic International Total \nDELHI (DIAL) 49675490 15652343 65327833 \nMUMBAI (MIAL) 32723077 11207221 43930298 \nBANGALORE (BIAL) 28126165 3785264 31911429 \nHYDERABAD (GHIAL) 17575242 3420785 20996027 \nCHENNAI 13708759 4862634 18571393 \nKOLKATA 15802943 1965919 17768862 \nAHMEDABAD 8597627 1539374 10137001 \nCOCHIN(CIAL) 4637763 4174768 8812531 \nGOA 7891983 467410 8359393 \nPUNE   7865644 141516 8007160 \nLUCKNOW 4347997 872531 5220528 \nGUWAHATI 5039315 12165 5051480 \nJAIPUR 4358994 405388 4764382 \nSRINAGAR 4398074 11591 4409665 \nPATNA   3748635 0 3