) \n(a)  and (b) The Government has approved the Production Linked \nIncentive (PLI) Scheme for Drones and Drone Components with a \ntotal outlay of Rs. 120 crore for a three year period (FY 2021 -22 to \nFY 2023-24). The overview of the performanc e and outcome of the \nPLI Scheme may be seen at Annexure I. ; \nAn amount of Rs 29.43 crores has been disbursed during FY 2022 -\n23. The details may be seen at Annexure II.; \n; \n(c) No Sir. \n****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE I \n \nANNEXURE IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NU MBER 706 \nREGARDING PRODUCTION LINKED INCENTIVE SCHEME DUE FOR ANSWER \nON 07.12.2023 \nPLI Scheme \nfor Drones \nand Drone \nComponents \nInvestment \n(in Crores) \n \nProduction/Sales \n(in Crores) \nEmployment \n82.86 298.47 1830 \nSource: Audited figures for FY 2021-22 received from IFCI Ltd., the Project Management \nAgency \n  \n\nANNEXURE II \n \nANNEXURE IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NUMBER 706 \nREGARDING PRODUCTION LINKED INCENTIVE SCHEME DUE FOR ANSWER \nON 07.12.2023 \nAMOUNT DISBURSED UNDER PLI SCHEME FOR DRONES AND DRONE \nCOMPONENTS DURING FY 2022-23 \nS No Name of the Company Incentive \n1.  Roter Precision Instruments Pvt Ltd 2,03,52,128.6/- \n2.  Dynamake Engineering 11,24,397/- \n3.  Throttle Aerospace Systems Pvt Ltd 30,85,757/- \n4.  Adroitec Information System Pvt Ltd 23,44,170/- \n5.  Ideaforge Technology Ltd 17,41,99,541.2/- \n6.  Raphe mPhibr Pvt Ltd 6,6234,750/- \n7.  Zuppa Geo Navigation Technologies Pvt Ltd 8,79,293/- \n8.  Aarav Unmanned System Pvt Ltd 89,53,554/- \n9.  Servocontrol Aerospace (India) Pvt Ltd 59,89,835/- \n10.  Sagar Defence Engineering Pvt Ltd 64,16,494/- \n11.  Absolute Composites Pvt Ltd 37,30,612/- \n12.  Imaginarium Rapid Pvt Ltd 10,00,928/- \nTotal 29,43,11,460/- \n    Source: IFCI Ltd. \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |707", "t": "Road Projects", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 707 \nANSWERED ON 07TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \n  \nROAD PROJECTS \n  \n707. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of all projects of roads that pass through Kerala, which were \ngiven in-principle approval between May, 2014 and May, 2019 along with \nthe dates of in-principle approvals; \n  \n(b) whether the Pazhani -Sabarimala by Munnar National Highway road \nproject was given in -principle approval during the period between May, \n2014 and May, 2019; and \n  \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) The details of road passing through Kerala, which were given in -\nprinciple approval between May, 2014 and May, 2019 by the Ministry along \nwith the dates of in-principle approvals is Annexed. \n(b)&(c) Yes, Sir. Palani to Kodaighat in Tamil Nadu and Lahai to Pamba near \nSabarimala in Kerala have been approved as In -Principle  National \nHighways (NHs) to connect Pazhani with Sab arimala via Munnar passing \nthrough Kodaighat – Theni – Munnar- Idduki – Kumli – Vandiperiyar – Lahai \n(as part of NH-183, 85, 185 and 183A). \n                                                                     \n\n                               ANNEXURE \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 707 FOR ANSWER ON 07.1 2.2023 ASKED BY \nADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE REGARDING ROAD PROJECTS \n  \n  \n***** \nSl.No. Name of Stretch \nDate of In-\nPrinciple \nApproval \n1 \nRoad connecting Chovva -Mattannur-Koottupuzha- \nValavupra-Makkottam-Virajpetta-Madikkeri- Mysore \n(stretch in Kerala) \n21.11.2016 \n2 \nNew NH from Junction of NH 183 near Vijayapuram to \nJunction of NH85 (old NH49) near    Oonnukal 30.05.2016 \n3 \nStretch of new NH from junction of NH 766 (old NH 212) \nnear Kalpetta connecting Mananthavadi 23.02.2016 \n4 Extension of NH 183A ( Bharanikavu near Adur) up to \nTitanium, Chavara at NH47 (new NH66) 30.05.2016 \n5 \nNew NH connecting NH 183A at Elavunkal near   Laha \nwith Pampa 02.01.2017 \n6 \nRoad connecting International Sea port, Vizhinjam with \nKaramana-Kaliyikkavila stretch of NH 47 and NH by \npass 47 (New NH 66) (Starts from Balaramapuram \npasses through Mukkola - Vizhinjam International Sea \nport (Mulloor)- Pulluvila-Kanjiramkulam and meets the \nNH 47 at Neyyattinkara \n02.01.2017 \n7 Road connecting Thiruvananthapuram-Thenmala 24.03.2017 \n8 Road connecting Vadakkenchery -Pollachi (stretch in \nKerala) \n24.03.2017 \n9 \nRoad connecting Hosdurg Panathur \nBhagamandalam Madikeri in Karnataka State \n(Stretch in Kerala) \n24.03.2017 \n10 Road connecting Cherkala - Kalladka (Stretch \nin  Kerala) \n24.03.2017 \n11 New NH from Kayamkulam (NH 47) to Junction  near \nThiruvalla (New NH 183) \n30.05.2016 \n12 \nNew NH from Alappuzha (NH 47) Changanassery - \nVazhoor -14th Mile (NH 220) 30.05.2016 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |708", "t": "Inter-linking of Rivers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 708 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nINTER-LINKING OF RIVERS \n708.    SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to interlink rivers under National Perspective Plan (NPP); \n(b) if so, the details and the current status of the proposals thereof; and \n(c) the progress of Manas-Sankosh-Tista-Ganga (M-S-T-G) link project? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) to (c)     The Government of India formulated a National Perspective Plan (NPP) in 1980 for transferring \nwater from water surplus basins to water deficit ba sins / areas. National Water Development Agency \n(NWDA) has identified 30 links (16 under Peninsular  Component and 14 under Himalayan Component) \nunder the NPP. Out of 30 Inter-linking of Rivers (I LR) projects, Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) of 11  \nlinks, Feasibility Reports (FRs) of 24 links and Pre-Feasibility Reports (PFRs) of all the 30 links have been \ncompleted. Three links namely, Ken-Betwa Link Proje ct (KBLP), Modified Parbati- Kalisindh-Chambal \n(PKC) Link project duly integrated with the Eastern  Rajasthan Canal Project (ERCP) and Godavari-\nCauvery link project have been identified as priori ty link projects under the NPP. The KBLP is the fir st \nILR project under the NPP, for which the implementa tion has been initiated. The details and the curren t \nstatus of ILR projects under the NPP, including tha t of the Manas-Sankosh-Tista-Ganga (M-S-T-G) link \nproject, is given at Annexure . \n*** \n  \n  \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE   \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)  TO (c)  OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n708 ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING “ INTER-LINKING OF \nRIVERS”.  \nDetails and current status of the ILR Projects under the NPP  \n Sl. No  Name  States benefited  Status  \n1 a.  Mahanadi (Manibhadra) - Godavari \n(Dowlaiswaram) link \nAndhra Pradesh (AP) & \nOdisha \nFR completed  \nb. Alternate Mahanadi (Barmul)  - \nRushikulya – Godavari \n(Dowlaiswaram) link \nAP& Odisha \n  \nFR completed  \n2 Godavari (Polavaram) - Krishna \n(Vijayawada) link  \nAP FR completed \n3        a.   Godavari (Inchampalli) - Krishna \n(Nagarjunasagar) link     Telangana FR completed \nb. Alternate Godavari (Inchampalli) - \nKrishna (Nagarjunasagar) link * Telangana   \nDPR completed  \n4 Godavari (Inchampalli/ SSMPP) - Krishna \n(Pulichintala) link \nTelangana & AP DPR completed \n5        a.   Krishna (Nagarjunasagar) - Pennar \n(Somasila ) link \n  \nAP FR completed \n       b.   Alternate Krishna (Nagarjunasagar) \n- Pennar (Somasila ) link * AP DPR completed \n6 Krishna (Srisailam) – Pennar link AP Draft DPR \ncompleted  \n7 Krishna (Almatti) – Pennar link AP & Karnataka Dr aft DPR \ncompleted \n8             a.    Pennar (Somasila) - Cauvery \n(Grand Anicut) link  \nAP, Tamil Nadu & \nPuducherry \nFR completed \nb. Alternate Pennar (Somasila) -   \nCauvery (Grand Anicut) link * \nAP, Tamil Nadu & \nPuducherry \n  \nDPR completed  \n9  Cauvery (Kattalai) - Vaigai -Gundar link Tamil N adu DPR completed \n \n \n10 a.  Parbati –Kalisindh -  Chambal link Madhya Pradesh ( MP) & \nRajasthan \nFR completed \nb. Modified Parbati – Kalisindh-\nChambal link ( duly integrated with \nERCP) \n  \nMP & Rajasthan \n  \n  \nDraft PFR completed \n11 Damanganga - Pinjal link (As per DPR ) Maharasht ra (only water \nsupply to Mumbai) \nDPR completed \n12 Par-Tapi-Narmada link (As per DPR) Gujarat & Mah arashtra DPR completed \n13 Ken-Betwa link  \n  \nUttar Pradesh (UP) & MP Implementation has \nbeen initiated \n14 Pamba - Achankovil - Vaippar link Tamil Nadu and  Kerala FR completed \n  \n15 Bedti - Varda link Karnataka DPR completed \n  \n16 Netravati – Hemavati link ** Karnataka PFR compl eted \n  \n  \n\n*    Due to pending consensus on Manibhadra and Inchampalli dams, alternate study to divert unutilized \nwaters of Godavari river was carried out and DPRs o f Godavari (Inchampalli/ Janampet) – Krishna \n(Nagarjunasagar) - Pennar (Somasila) – Cauvery (Gra nd Anicut) link projects have been completed. \nGodavari-Cauvery (Grand Anicut) link project have b een prepared, comprising of Godavari \n(Inchampalli / Janampet) - Krishna  (Nagarjunasagar ), Krishna (Nagarjunasagar)- Pennar (Somasila) \nand Pennar(Somasila)-Cauvery(Grand Anicut) link projects. \n**   Further studies are not taken up since after i mplementation of Yettinahole project by Govt. of \nKarnataka, no surplus water is available in Netravati basin for diversion through this link. \nHimalayan Component       \nSl. No.  Name of the  Link  Country/ States benefited  Status  \n1. Kosi-Mechi link Bihar & Nepal PFR completed  \n2. Kosi-Ghaghra link Bihar, UP & Nepal FR completed  \n3. Gandak - Ganga link UP & Nepal FR completed \n(Indian portion) \n4. Ghaghra - Yamuna link UP & Nepal FR completed \n(Indian portion) \n5. Sarda - Yamuna link UP & Uttarakhand FR complete d  \n6. Yamuna-Rajasthan link "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |709", "t": "Pricing Mechanism for Air Fare", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 709 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  7th December 2023 )  \nPRICING MECHANISM FOR AIR FARE \n709.    SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN                  \n             SHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.                   \n             SHRI K. NAVASKANI                        \n             SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN                  \n             SHRI S. VENKATESAN                       \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government has taken any initiatives to ensure that \nthe private airlines publish the correct information on their websites \nregarding fares; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether it has come to the notice of the Government regarding \nhuge increase in airfare of thirty to forty per cent for domestic  \ntravel in 2022- 23 and if so, the reaction of the Government thereto; \n \n(d) whether the tickets to Ahmedabad for World Cup cricket finals \nwent up hundred per cent increase for non -stop and 1000 per cent \nfor one stop or two stops due to dynamic pricing and if so, the steps \ntaken thereon; \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to formulate a pricing \nmechanism for air fares to ensure that passenge rs are not charged \nexorbitantly; \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the initiatives proposed to be taken \nby the Government in this regard and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n\n(g) whether the Government has received any complaints or letters \nin this concern; and  \n \n(h) if so, the details thereof and the measures taken in this regard? \nANSWER \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      \n(GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)) \n(a) & (b) To ensure transparency and aid the traveler make an \ninformed choice, in line with Sub Rule (2) of Rule 135 of The Aircraft \nRules, 1937, Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has issued \nAir Transport Circular 2 of 2010 wherein the scheduled airlines are \ndirected to upload fare sheet displaying fare offered across their \nnetwork on their respective websites and to furnish the copy of \nsuch tariff established to DGCA on every first day of calendar month \nand whenever, there is change in tariff.; \n \nDGCA has set-up Tariff Monitoring Unit (TMU) that monitors airfares \non selected routes on a random basis by using airlines websites on \nmonthly basis to ensure that the airlines do not charge airfares \noutside the range declared by them, under the provision of Sub Rule \n(2) of Rule 135 of The Aircraft Rules, 1937; \n \nThe TMU analysis has sh own that the airfares remained well within \nthe fare bucket uploaded by the airline.; \n \n(c) The domestic airline pricing runs in multiple levels (bucket or \nReservation Booking Designator (RBD)) which is in line with the \npractice followed globally. The lower fare is available for advance \nbooking much earlier (D -31). As time lapses and date of journey \napproaches closer, the fare on higher side is made available (D -7, D-\n1) as per the respective airline policy.; \n \n(d) The fare comparison data for the sectors Delhi -Ahmedabad and \nAhmedabad-Delhi during the world cup as monitored by the TMU is \nplaced at Annexure I; \n \n\n(e) to (h) The issue has been brought to the notice of the \nGovernment. As per prevailing regulation, airfares are neither \nestablished nor regulated by the  Government. With the repeal of Air \nCorporation Act in March 1994, the process of airfare approval by \nGovernment has been dispensed with. Under the provision of Sub \nRule (1) of Rule 135 of the Aircraft Rules, 1937, every air transport \nundertaking engaged i n scheduled air services is required to \nestablish tariff having regard to all relevant factors, including cost \nof operation, characteristic of services, reasonable profit and the \ngenerally prevailing tariff. Airlines are free to charge reasonable air \nfares as per their operation viabilities subject to compliance to \nabove said rule.; \n****** \n\nAnnexure – 1 \n \n \n \nDate of \nTravel \n18Nov.'23 \n(Fare in INR)  \n12Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR)  \n26Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR) \n  \nVariation % of                   \n18 Nov.'23 vs. 12 \nNov.'23 \nVariation % \nof 18 \nNov.'23 vs. \n26Nov.'23 \nDelhi-\nAhmedabad \nAverage 19302  4580  12254 321% 58% \nMin 9178  2819  5796 226% 58% \nMax 39116  13094  26510 199% 48% \nAhmedabad-\nDelhi \nAverage 6325  11652  4987 -46% 27% \nMin 4091  6705  3663 -39% 12% \nMax 10750  20796  8910 -48% 21% \n         \nDate of \nTravel \n19Nov.'23 \n(Fare in INR)  \n12Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR)  \n26Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR) \nVariation % of 19 \nNov.'23 vs. 12 \nNov.'23 \nVariation % \nof 19 \nNov.'23 vs. \n26Nov.'23 \nDelhi-\nAhmedabad \nAverage 17735  4580  12254 287% 45% \nMin 9573  2819  5796 240% 65% \nMax 30506  13094  26510 133% 15% \nAhmedabad-\nDelhi \nAverage 6417  11652  4987 -45% 29% \nMin 3099  6705  3663 -54% -15% \nMax 20796  20796  8910 0% 133% \n          \nDate of \nTravel \n20Nov.'23 \n(Fare in INR)  \n12Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR)  \n26Nov.'23 \n(Fare in \nINR) \n  \nVariation "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |71", "t": "Works/Projects Completed under CSR", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 71 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nLOAN APPLICATIONS UNDER PM SVANIDHI SCHEME \n \nNO. 71. DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S: \n                  SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate:- \n(a) the details and the number of loan applications received, \napproved and loan amount disbursed to street vendors under the \nPM SVANidhi Scheme in the State of Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, \nOdisha and Andaman & Nicobar Islands; \n(b) whether the loan amount is directly disbursed by lending \ninstitutions to the beneficiaries of the PM SVANidhi scheme and \nif so, the details thereof; \n(c) the details and the number of financial literacy and capacity \nbuilding programmes conducted by the Government and the \nnumber of applications received along with the users of web \nportal under PM SVANidhi Scheme in country, State/district -wise, \nparticularly in Tamil Nadu; \n(d) whether the Government has taken any steps to encourage \nparticipation of private banks and enhance their share in the \n\ntotal sanctions and disbursements under the scheme and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(e) the steps taken by the Government for financial inclusion of \nmore and more vendors and also for improvement of their living \nconditions ; \n(f) the total number of vending certificates or Identity Cards (ID \ncards) issued to street vendors in Maharashtra by Urban Local \nBodies (ULBs) during last three years and current financial year, \ndistrict-wise ; \n(g) whether the Government has taken notice of the cases of ULBs \nrefusing to issue vending certificates or ID cards across the \ncountry;  and \n(h) if so, whether any appellate mechanism exist in this regard and \nif so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  to (h): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*****  \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 71 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 07.12 .2023 REGARDING \nLOAN APPLICATIONS UNDER PM SVANIDHI SCHEME.  \n \n(a): The details of the number of loan applications received, \nsanctioned and loan amount disbursed to street vendors under the \nPradhan Mantri Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbharNidhi (PM SVANidhi) in \nthe State of Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Odisha and Andaman & \nNicobar Islands are at Annexure-I.  \n(b): Yes . The loan amount is directly disbursed by lending \ninstitutions to the beneficiaries of the PM SVANidhi scheme. The \ndetails of total amount of loan disbursed lending institution- wise \nparticipating in the PM SVANidhi scheme are at Annexure-II. \n(c):  Digital onboarding of street vendors is an integral component of \nPM SVANidhi Scheme. As such, on-boarded beneficiaries are trained \nin debit and credit transactions by conducting penny drop \ntransaction by the concerned Lending Institutions. So far, over \n31.44 lakh street vendors are digitally active.  \n \nIn addition, the guidelines of “Support to Urban Street Vendor \n(SUSV)” under centrally sponsored scheme of  Deendayal Antyodaya \nYojana-National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NULM) prescribe \nthat Urban Local Body (ULB) will organize sessions on financial \nliteracy for identified street vendors with support of resource \norganization and field staff.  \n \n\nAs on 29.11.2023, a total of 96.34 lakh eligible applications have \nbeen received under the PM SVANidhi Scheme. The details of \nnumber of eligible applications received alongwith the users of web \nportal under PM SVANidhi Scheme across the country including \ndistrict-wise data for the state of Tamil Nadu are at Annexure- III. \nApart from this, there are 2,22,788  institutional users onboarded on \nPM SVANidhi Portal.  \n \n(d):  Yes . Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs (MoHUA) & \nDepartment of Financial Services (DFS) regularly conduct \njoint/separate review meetings with the Lending Institutions \nincluding private sector banks, to improve their performance under \nthe Scheme.  As on November 29, 2023, Private Sector Banks have \ndisbursed 1,26,090 loans amounting to Rs. 153.76 crore. \n \n(e):  The PM SVANidhi Scheme has been made available to all \nstreet vendors vending in urban areas. Further, the lending under \nthe Scheme has been extended beyond 31\nst March, 2022 to \nDecember, 2024. This would help in getting more and more vendors \nunder the ambit of the Scheme. \n \n 'SVANidhi se Samriddhi' component under the PM SVANidhi \nScheme, launched on 04 January, 2021, to build safety net for the \nbeneficiaries’ families for improving their living conditions. \n It aims to link the beneficiaries families to existing eight welfare \nschemes of the Government of India, targeting their holistic \ndevelopment and socio-  economic upliftment. These schemes are \nPM Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana, PM Suraksha Bima Yojana, PM Jan \n\nDhan Yojana, One Nation One Ration Card, PM Shram Yogi \nMaandhan Yojana, Registration under Building and other \nConstr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |710", "t": "DRIP Phase I", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 710 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nDRIP PHASE II \n710.    SHRIMATI APARUPA PODDAR \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the dams selected under the Dam Rehabilitation and Improvement Project (DRIP) Phase  II \nfor improved safety, State-wise; \n(b) the funds that the Government proposes to spend for the DRIP Phase II over next ten years, State-wise; \n(c) the details of functional dams which are more t han 60 years old selected for DRIP Phase II, State- wise \nincluding West Bengal; \n(d) the reasons for not including West Bengal in DRIP Phase II project; and \n(e) whether the Government has any proposal to incr ease capacity or improve infrastructure of the Damo dar \nValley Corporation (DVC) dam, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a)     The State/Agency wise list of 736 number of dam s proposed for physical rehabilitation under Dam \nRehabilitation and Improvement Project (DRIP), Phase II and Phase III is given at Annexure-I.   \n(b)    The Union Government has approved the DRIP, Phase-I I & III Scheme at a total  cost of Rs.10,211 \nCrore [Rs. 5107 Crore for Phase II and Rs 5104 Cror e for Phase III]. This provision is for 19 States a nd 3 \nCentral Agencies, and the funds will be spent over the period of 10 years (2021-2031). The State/ Agen cy \nwise budget outlay under the Scheme is given at Annexure-II.   \n(c)      As per the available information, there are 14 5 dams out of 736 dams proposed in DRIP Phase II & \nIII, which are more than 60 years old. The State /A gency wise detail of these dams is given at Annexure-\nIII . \n(d)      West Bengal is included under DRIP Phase II Sc heme. State of West Bengal is one of the nineteen  \npartner States in the DRIP Phase-II & III Scheme.  \n(e)         Damodar Valley Corporation (DVC) is one of the three  partner central agencies in the DRIP Pha se-\nII & III Scheme. Five dams of DVC have been include d for rehabilitation under the Scheme at a budget \noutlay of Rs 144.7 Crore. The allocation for Phase II & Phase III is Rs 44 Crore & Rs 100 Crore \nrespectively.  \n***   \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SA BHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 710 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING \"DRIP PHASE II\"  \nDetails of Proposed Dams under DRIP, Phase-II & III \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \nSI. No.  State / Agency  No. of dams  \n1. Andhra Pradesh 31 \n2. Chhattisgarh 5 \n3. Goa 2 \n4. Gujarat 6 \n5. Jharkhand 35 \n6. Karnataka 41 \n7. Kerala 28 \n8. Madhya Pradesh 27 \n9. Maharashtra 167 \n10. Manipur 2 \n11. Meghalaya 6 \n12. Odisha 36 \n13. Punjab 12 \n14. Rajasthan 189 \n15. Tamil Nadu 59 \n16. Telangana 29 \n17. Uttar Pradesh 39 \n18. Uttarakhand 6 \n19. West Bengal 9 \n20. BBMB 2 \n21. CWC --- \n22. DVC 5 \nTotal  736  \n\nANNEXURE-II  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SA BHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 710 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING \"DRIP PHASE II\"  \nAgency-wise Financial outlay  \nSl. No.  State/Agency  Union Cabinet allocation (Crore)  \nTotal  DRIP-2  DRIP-3 \n1.   Andhra Pradesh  667 336 331 \n2.   BBMB 230 70 160 \n3.   Chhattisgarh  133 133 0 \n4.   CWC 570 285 285 \n5.   Damodar Valley Corporation 144 44 100 \n6.   Goa  58 58 0 \n7.   Gujarat  400 201 199 \n8.   Jharkhand  238 57 181 \n9.   Karnataka (incl  KPCL) 612 308 304 \n10.   Kerala SEB 150 75 75 \n11.   Kerala WRD 166 166 0 \n12.   Madhya Pradesh  186 100 86 \n13.   Maharashtra  940 379 561 \n14.   Manipur 311 156 155 \n15.   Meghalaya  441 221 220 \n16.   Odisha  804 403 401 \n17.   Punjab  442 196 246 \n18.   Rajasthan  965 503 462 \n19.   Tamil Nadu  1064 530 534 \n20.   Telangana  545 276 269 \n21.   Uttar Pradesh  787 396 391 \n22.   UJVNL 274 171 103 \n23.   West Bengal  84 43 41 \n  Total  10211  5107  5104  \n\nANNEXURE-III  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SA BHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 710 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING \"DRIP PHASE II\"  \nList of proposed dam under DRIP Phase II and III( more than 60 years age)  \nS. No.  \nName of the State/ \nImplementing \nAgency \nS. No. \n Name of the Dams in Phase-II Year of \ncompletion  Age in years  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1 Bhairavanitippa 1961 62 \n2 Owk Reservoir Complex (Palair, \nGollaleru, Timmarervoir) \n1946 77 \n3 Rallapadu Project 1960 63 \n4 Sir Aurther Cotton Barrage (Godavari) 1850 173 \n2 Chhattisgarh 1 Moorumsilli Dam 1923 100 \n3 DamodarVelley \nCorporation \n1 Tilaya Dam 1953 70 \n2 Konar Dam 1955 68 \n3 Maithon 1957 66 \n4 Gujarat 1 Hiran 1 1959 64 \n5 Jharkhand 1 Batre RS 1954 69 \n2 Buchaupa RS 1957 66 \n6 Karnataka 1 Bhadra Reservoir 1962 61 \n2 K.Honamachanahally Minor irrigation \nproject \n1952 71 \n3 Chikkahole 1958 65 \n4 Marconally 1940 83 \n5 Nugu 1959 64 \n6 Tungbhadra 1953 70 \n7 Vani villas sagar dam 1907 116 \n7 Kerla State \nElect"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |711", "t": "Financial Assistance", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 711\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n07.12.2023\n \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE\n \n \n711\n.\n            \nSHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM \nENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it is true that millions of people employed in Micro, Small and Medium Industries in the \ncountry have lost their jobs due to various reasons like cash crunch, import\n-\nexport crisis and escalating price \nrise of \nraw materials and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(b) \n \nthe steps taken by the Union Government to restore the jobs and improve the status of MSMEs, \nespecially in Tamil Nadu;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has provided any financial assistance for revival of MSMEs\n \nin Tamil Nadu \nand if so, the details thereof; and\n \n(d) \n \nthe measures taken/to be taken by the Government to protect the interests of both employer and \nemployees of MSME sector?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU \nPRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) \nto (d)\n:\n \nThe Government has taken a number of steps to resolve the issues faced by the MSME sector \nin the country, including the State of Tamil Nadu. Some of these steps are as follows:\n \n \n1)\n \nBudget 2023\n-\n24 announced the\n \ninfusion of Rs 9,000 crore Credit Guarantee Trust for Micro, Small \n& Medium Enterprises (CGTMSE) \nto enable an additional credit of Rs. 2.00 lakh crore with \nreduced cost of credit\n.\n \n \n2)\n \nPM Vishwakarma Scheme: A Central Sector Scheme with budget outlay of Rs. 1\n3,000 crore for its \nimplementation during the period of 2023\n-\n24 to 2027\n-\n28. The scheme envisages to provide end to \nend holistic support to artisans and craftspeople who work with their hands, using tools, in the 18 \ntrades covered.\n \n \n3)\n \nVivad se Vishwas \n–\n \nRelie\nf for MSMEs: In cases of failure by MSMEs to execute contracts during \nthe Covid period, 95 per cent of the forfeited amount relating to bid or performance security will \nbe returned to them by government and government undertakings.\n \n \n4)\n \nUnder Section 43B of In\ncome Tax Act: Deduction has been allowed for expenditure incurred on \npayments only when payment is actually made to MSMEs.\n \n \n5)\n \nNo global tenders for procurement upto Rs. 200 crore.\n \n \n6)\n \nInclusion of retail and whole sale traders as MSMEs w.e.f. 02.07.2021.\n \n \n7)\n \nNon\n-\nt\nax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of MSMEs w.e.f. \n18.10.2022.\n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \n8)\n \nThe Government of India has taken several measures to provide support of the both employers and \nemployees. Some of them are as under:\n \n \n(i)\n \n(a) \n  \nAdoption of dual criteria based definition of MSMEs on 26.06.2020 to widen the \n  \nambit \nof\n \nthe\n \nMSME\n \nsector\n \nhas been done by\n \nMinistry\n \nof\n \nMSME.\n \n(b) \n \nLaunch of Udyam Portal on 01.07.2020 to facilitate ease of registration for MSME. Since\n \nthen, till 04.12.2023,\n \n2.12 crore MSMEs, with an employment of over 14.25 crore,\n \nhave\n \nregistered\n \non Udyam Portal.\n \n(c) \n \nLaunch of Udyam Assist Platform on 11.01.2023 to bring Informal Micro Enterprises \n(IMEs) under the formal ambit for availing the benefits under Priority Sector\n \nLending. Since\n \nthen, till 04.12.2023, 1.04 crore IMEs with an employment of over 1.22 crore have been \nregistered on UAP.\n \n \n(ii)\n \nTo protect the interest of both the employer and employees of MSME sector during the Covid \n19 crisis, the Government of India launche\nd the Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme in \nMay 2020, which was running till March 31, 2023. Under the Scheme, 1.13 crore loans \namounting to Rs.2.43 lakh crore were extended to MSME borrowers, which has helped \nemployees and employer of these MSMEs.\n \n \n(iii)\n \nAatmanirbhar\n \nBharat\n \nRojgar\n \nYojana\n \nhas\n \nbeen\n \nlaunched\n \non\n \n1\nst\n \nOctober,\n \n2020\n \nto\n \nincentivize \nemployers for creation of new employment and restoration of loss of employment\n \nduring\n \nCovid\n-\n19 pandemic. The terminal date\n \nfor\n \nregistration was\n \n31.03.2022.\n \n \n(iv)\n \nThe \nGovernment is implementing Prime Minister Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbharNidhi (PM\n \nSVANidhi Scheme) since June 01, 2020 to facilitate collateral free working capital loan to\n \nstreet vendors to restart their businesses, which were adversely impacted during the C\novid\n-\n19\n \npandemic.\n \n \n(v)\n \nPradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) was launched by the Government for facilitating self\n-\n \nemployment. Under PMMY, collateral free loans upto Rs. 10 lakh, are extended to micro/small\n \nbusiness\n \nenterprises\n \nand\n \nto\n \nindividuals\n \nto\n \nenable\n \nthem\n \nt\no\n \nsetup\n \nor\n \nexpand\n \ntheir\n \nbusiness\n \n \nactivities.\n \n \n(vi)\n \nPM GatiShakti is a transformative approach for economic growth and sustainable \ndevelopment.\n \nThe approach is driven by seven engines, namely, Roads, Railways, Airports, \nPorts, Mass\n \nTransport, Waterways and \nLogistics Infrastructure. This approach is powered by \nClean Energy\n \nand\n \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |712", "t": "Waste to Energy Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 712 \nANSWERED ON 07/12/2023 \nWASTE TO ENERGY PROGRAMME \n \n712. SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY \n        SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA \n        SHRI C.R. PATIL \n        DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI \n        SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the number of beneficiaries who received central financial assistance under \neach of the three sub-categories of the ‘Waste to Energy Programme’; \n(b) whether any entity from the State of Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, Gujarat and Odisha has been \nprovided central financial assistance under the said programme;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to provide technical support to the beneficiary entities \nthrough any of its agencies; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(f) whether the Government has devised any mechanism to monitor and evaluate the outcomes/ \noutput of the plants which received central financial assistance under the said scheme; and \n(g) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) Details of Central Financial Assistance (CFA) disbursed under Waste to Energy \nProgramme during FY 2018 -19, FY 2019 -20, FY 2020 -21, FY 2021 -22, FY 2022 -23, and \nFY 2023 -24 (as on 30.11.2023) for setting up of Waste to Energy projects, are given at \nAnnexure-I. \n(b) to (c) Details of CFA released to project developers under Waste to Energy Programme \nfor setting up Waste to Energy projects in the State of Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Gujarat and \nOdisha, are placed at Annexure-II. \n(d) &(e) Eight Biogas Development and Training Centers (BDTCs) have been established at \nIndia’s premier Institutions to provide Technical Assistance, R & D, Testing and Validation \nof New Biogas Models / Designs, field inspections of biogas plants, and training and skill \ndevelopment. Details of BDTCs are provided at Annexure-III. \n(f) & (g)  Under the Waste to Energy Programme guidelines dated 02.11.2022, inspection of \nthe plants for performance monitoring for a minimum of 3 consecutive months by designated \ninspection agencies is required before the release of Central Financial Assistance including a \ncontinuous operation of the plant for at least 72 hours, during which the plant must maintain \nan average operational capacity of 80% of its rated capacity. \nIn addition, developers are required to share generation data of the Waste to Energy plants \nthrough SCADA System or a remote monitoring system to Ministry of New and Renewable \nEnergy. \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha unstarred Q. No. 712 to be answered on \n07.12.2023 regarding “WASTE TO ENERGY PROGRAMME”: \nState-wise details of Central Financial Assistance disbursed to project developers under \nWaste to Energy Programme during FY 2018-19, FY 2019-20, FY 2020-21, FY 2021-22, \nFY 2022-23, and FY 2023-24 (as on 30.11.2023), are given as below: \nState \nCFA disbursed to project developers (No. of projects) \nBiogas BioCNG Power Total \nAndhra Pradesh 1 4 2 7 \nGujarat 1 6 - 7 \nHaryana - 2 - 2 \nKarnataka - 1 - 1 \nMadhya Pradesh 1 - - 1 \nMaharashtra 3 - 2 5 \nTamil Nadu - 1 - 1 \nTelangana 1 1 1 3 \nUttar Pradesh - 1 2 3 \nWest Bengal  - - 2 2 \nGrand Total 7 16 9 32 \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nAnnexure in reply to part (b) to (c) of the Lok Sabha unstarred Q No. 712 to be answered \non 03.08.2023 regarding “WASTE TO ENERGY PROGRAMME”: \nDetails of CFA released by this Ministry during FY 2018-19, FY 2019-20, FY 2020-21, FY \n2021-22, FY 2022-23, and FY 2023-24 (as on 30.11.2023), for setting up WTE projects in \nthe State of Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Gujarat and Odisha are given as below: \nState Developer of the project CFA released to project \ndevelopers (in Rs. \nCrores) \nGujarat \nM/s Apex Green Energy Corporation  4.67 \nM/s Turquoise Bio Natural Energy Pvt Ltd  4.67 \nM/s Sayaji Industries Limited 1.00 \nAPMC, Ahmedabad 1.00 \nM/s Shri Hari BioCNG & Fertilizer LLP  4.69 \nM/s Agricultural Produce Market \nCommittee, Surat \n0.83 \nM/s Goverdhannathji Energies LLP  3.00 \nUttar Pradesh \nM/s Purshottam Ram Foods Industries Pvt \nLtd  \n1.80 \nM/s Samagra Agro 0.82 \nM/s. Amit Traders 0.04 \nRajasthan - - \nOdisha - - \nTotal 22.52 \n \n\nAnnexure-III \nAnnexure in reply to part (d) to (e) of the Lok Sabha unstarred Q No. 712 to be answered \non 07.12.2023 regarding “WASTE TO ENERGY PROGRAMME”: \nList of Biogas Development and Training Centers is given as below: \nSl. No Name of BDTC Location of BDTC \n1.  Department of Mechanical Engineering, Indian \nInstitute of Technology \nNorth Guwahati, Guwahati , \nAssam- 781039 \n2.  Department of Agricultural Engineering, \nUniversity of Agricultural Sciences, Bangalore  \nGKVK, Bangalore, \nKarnataka- 560065 \n3.  School of Energy and Env Studies, Devi Ahilya \nVishwavidyalaya \nKhandwa Road, Indore, \nMadhya Pradesh- 4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |713", "t": "Har Ghar Nal Se Jal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO -713 \nANSWERED ON-7/12/2023 \n \nHAR GHAR NAL SE JAL \n713.  SHRI JASWANT SINGH BHABHOR: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to provide  tap water connections to every household in \nthe country under the ‘Har Ghar Nal Se Jal’ initiative of Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) by the year \n2024; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof in Gujarat, district-wise? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n(a) & (b) Since August, 2019, Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) \nin partnership with States to make provision of tap water supply to every rural household  of \nthe country including those in Gujarat.  \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, in Gujarat, 65.16 Lakh (71.46%) rural \nhouseholds were reported to have tap water connections. Since then, 26.02 Lakh additional \nrural households were provided with tap water connections in the State. As on date, all 91.18 \nLakhs rural households in the State are reported to have covered with tap water supply.  District \nwise details of households provided with tap water connection is at Annex \n***** \n \n  \n\nAnnex \nAnnex referred to in part (b) of Lok Sa