.00 \nNarmada           1.51            1.04            0.47            1.51  100.00 \nNavsari           2.80            1.99            0.81            2.80  100.00 \nPanch Mahals           3.56            1.59            1.97            3.56  100.00 \nPatan           2.85            2.63            0.22            2.85  100.00 \nPorbandar           0.64            0.57            0.07            0.64  100.00 \nRajkot           3.23            2.96            0.27            3.23  100.00 \nSabar Kantha           3.46            2.77            0.68            3.46  100.00 \nSurat           3.84            2.92            0.92            3.84  100.00 \nSurendranagar           2.77            2.31            0.46            2.77  100.00 \nTapi           2.39            1.00            1.39            2.39  100.00 \nVadodara           2.95            1.93            1.02            2.95  100.00 \nValsad           3.07            0.98            2.10            3.07  100.00 \nGandhinagar           2.85            2.85                 -              2.85  100.00 \nTotal         91.18          64.94          26.24          91.18  100.00 \nSource: JJM dashboard; HHs: Households \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |714", "t": "Development of Natural Gas Infrastructure", "head": "LOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 714 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7THDECEMBER, 2023 \n \nDevelopment of Natural Gas Infrastructure \n \n714. SHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a)the details on the development of natural gas infrastructure in the country, particularly in the State of \nWest Bengal, including pipelines and distribution networks during the last four years and the current year; \n(b)whether there are plans for expanding the availability of natural gas for domestic, industrial and \ncommercial use; and \n(c)if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वरतेल\u0000  )  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE  IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n  \n(a) to (c) Providing Piped Natural Gas (PNG) connections and establishment of Compressed Natural Gas \n(CNG) station are part of the development of City Gas Distribution (CGD) Network and the same is being \ncarried out by the entities authorised by Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB). After \ncompletion of 11A CGD bidding round,, PNGRB has authorized 300 Geographical Areas (GAs) \n(including 10 GAs in the state of West Bengal) covering about 98% of the population and 88% of total \ngeographical area of the country spread over around 630 districts in 28 states/UTs including the state of \nWest Bengal for development of CGD network across the country. Progress of CGD network including \npipeline during the last four years in the state of West Bengal is as under: \n \nAs on  PNG Domestic Connections \n(Nos.)    \nCNG Stations (Nos.)  Pipeline (Inch-Km)\n  \n  \n31.03.2020 0 0 0 \n31.03.2021 0 15 96 \n31.03.2022 0 37 320 \n31.03.2023 260 68 1702 \n \n\n \n \nLaying of natural gas pipelines is done by entities authorised by PNGRB. As on date, following three \npipelines have been authorised by PNGRB which pass through the state of West Bengal out of which one \npipeline is partially commissioned and two pipelines are under construction. Details are at Annexure-I. \n \nFurther, PNGRB has launched 12 th and 12A CGD bidding rounds covering the states of Arunachal \nPradesh, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, Manipur and remaining parts of Union Territory (UT) \nof Jammu & Kashmir and entire UT of Ladakh. \n \n**** \n \n\nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 714 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF NATURAL GAS INFRASTRUCTURE. \nUnder construction natural gas pipeline passing through the state of West Bengal \nName of \nNatural \nGas \nPipeline \nAuthorised \nEntity  \nDate of \nAuthorization \n \n \nAuthorized \nLength \n(KM) \nAuthorized \nCapacity \n(MMSCMD) \nTarget date \nof \ncompletion \nStates from which \npipeline passes \nNorth-\nEast \nNatural \nGas \nPipeline \nGrid \nIGGL 17.11.2020 1,656 4.75 \nMarch 2025 \n \nAssam, Mizoram, \nManipur, \nArunachal \nPradesh, Tripura, \nNagaland, \nMeghalaya & \nSikkim, West \nBengal \nKanai - \nChhata - \nPanitar \nHPPL 08.07.2019 317 19.20 May 2023 West Bengal \n \nPartially commissioned natural gas pipeline passing through the state of West Bengal \nName of Natural \nGas Pipeline \nAuthorise\nd Entity  \nDate of \nAuthorizatio\nn \n \n \nAuthorize\nd Length \n(KM) \nAuthorized \nCapacity \n(MMSCMD) \nOperatin\ng Length \n(KM) \nUnder \nConst. \nLength \n(KM) \nTarget \ndate of \ncompletio\nn \nStates from \nwhich \npipeline \npasses \nJagdishpur-\nHaldia-\nBokaroDhamra\n-Paradip- \nBarauni-\nGuwahati   \nGAIL 29.01.2018 3,546 23.00 \n \n \n \n \n2922.77 \n \n \n \n \n623.23 \nDecemb\ner 2023 \nUttar \nPradesh, \nBihar, \nJharkhand\n, West \nBengal, \nOdisha, \nAssam \n \nSource: PNGRB \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |715", "t": "Greenfield Airports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 715 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  7th December 2023 )  \nGREENFIELD AIRPORTS \n715.    SHRIMATI SHARDABEN ANILBHAI PATEL            \n             SHRI ANIL FIROJIYA                       \n             SHRI MITESH RAMESHBHAI PATEL (BAKABHAI)  \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) the details of places identified for setting up of greenfield airports \nin the country, State/UT-wise; \n(b) whether it is a fact that the Government encourages private \ninvestment in development of greenfield airports and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c) the number of greenfield airports commissioned during last three \nyears; and \n(d) the details  of airports which have attained viability or running in \nloss as on date? \nANSWER \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      (GEN. \n(DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)) \n(a) GoI has accorded 'In -Principle' approval for setting up of 21 new \nGreenfield Airpor ts namely, Mopa in Goa, Navi Mumbai, Shirdi and \nSindhudurg in Maharashtra, Kalaburagi, Vijayapura, Hassan and \nShivamogga in Karnataka, Dabra (Gwalior) in Madhya Pradesh, \nKushinagar and Noida (Jewar) in Uttar Pradesh, Dholera and Rajkot \n\n(Hirasar) in Gujarat , Karaikal in Puducherry, Dagadarthi, Bhogapuram \nand Orvakal (Kurnool) in Andhra Pradesh, Durgapur in West Bengal, \nPakyong in Sikkim, Kannur in Kerala and Hollongi (Itanagar) in \nArunachal Pradesh across the country. Out of these, 12 Greenfield \nairports viz . Durgapur, Shirdi, Kannur, Pakyong, Kalaburagi,  Orvakal \n(Kurnool), Sindhudurg, Kushinagar, Itanagar, Mopa, Shivamogga and \nRajkot have been operationalised.;; \n \n(b) Ministry of Civil Aviation (MoCA) has taken various steps to attract \ninvestments from priva te investors for development of airport \ninfrastructure in the country including Greenfield Airports. 100% \nForeign Direct Investment (FDI) in Greenfield and Brownfield airport \nprojects under the FDI Policy has been permitted. MoCA has granted \n'in principle'  approval to various State Governments, who have \nundertaken development of Greenfield Airports under Public Private \nPartnership (PPP) mode with private investment including Kannur in \nKerala, Durgapur in West Bengal, Mopa in Goa, Navi Mumbai and \nSindhudurg in Maharashtra, Bhogapuram in Andhra Pradesh and Noida \n(Jewar) in Uttar Pradesh.; \n; \n(c)&(d) During last 3 years, 7 Greenfield airports namely, Sindhudurg, \nOravakal (Kurnool), Kushinagar, Itanagar, Mopa, Shivamogga and \nRajkot(Hirasar) have been operationali sed. Greenfield airports are \ndeveloped with the goal of enhancing connectivity leading to all round \neconomic development of the region. Greenfield Airport Projects are \ncapital intensive with long gestation periods. The viability of \ngreenfield airports depe nds on various factors, including regulatory \napprovals, infrastructure development, airline and route development, \npassenger and cargo demand, market competition, and revenue \ngeneration efforts. The profit/loss details of the above mentioned 7 \nAirports for FY-2020-21, 2021-22 and 2022-23 is at Annexure. \n****** \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure to statement referred in reply of Lok Sabha USQ No. 715 for answer on \n07.12.2023 \nProfit/Loss Statement of 7 Greenfield Airports \n₹ in crore. \nSL. \nNO. NAME OF AIRPORT 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 SINDHUDURG -20.37 -22.49 -40.20 \n2 ORVAKAL* \n(KURNOOL) NA -18.28 -6.5 \n3 KUSHINAGAR -3.58 -2.78 -9.05 \n4 Donyi Polo, Itanagar ** NA NA -3.38 \n5 MOPA*** NA NA -148.34 \n6 SHIVAMOGGA*** NA NA NA \n7 Rajkot*** NA NA NA \n \nNA- Not Applicable \n*Orvakal (Kurnool) Airport was operationalised in March, 2021.  \n**Donyi Polo Airport was operationalised in November, 2022. \n \n***Mopa Airport was operationalised in January, 2023, Shivamogga Airport was \noperationalised in February, 2023 and Rajkot Airport was operationalised in July. 2023.  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |716", "t": "Decrease in Electricity Demand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.716 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n \nDECREASE IN ELECTRICITY DEMAND \n \n†716. SHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether electricity demand has been decreas ed in various parts of the \ncountry during the last one year; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of demand and supply of  electricity across the country \nduring the last two years; and \n \n(c)  the reasons for decrease in demand of electricity in the country? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) :  No, Sir. The demand of power has increased in different parts of the \ncountry during the last one year. The details of Power Supply Position in various \nregions of the coun try during the year 2022 -23 (April, 2022 to March, 2023) as \ncompared to 2021-22 (April, 2021 to March, 2022) and the Power Supply Position \nduring the year 2023 -24 (April, 2023 to October, 2023) as compared to \ncorresponding period of the previous year 2022 (April, 2022 to October, 2022) are \ngiven at Annexure.  \n \n \n*******  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 716 ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 \n************* \nThe details of Power Supply Position in various  regions of the country during the year \n2022-23 (April, 2022 to March, 2023) as compared to 2021-22 (April, 2021 to March, 2022)  \n \n( Figures in MU net ) \nRegion  April,2022 - \nMarch,2023 \nApril,2021 - March,2022 % Change \nEnergy \nRequirement \nEnergy \nSupplied \nEnergy \nRequirement \nEnergy \nSupplied \nEnergy Energy \nRequirement Supplied \n(MU) (MU) (MU) (MU) (%) (%) \nNorthern \nRegion  \n463,088 458,640 417,934 413,915 10.8 10.8 \nWestern \nRegion  \n475,743 475,157 429,065 428,683 10.9 10.8 \nSouthern \nRegion  \n371,467 370,900 350,678 350,421 5.9 5.8 \nEastern \nRegion  \n182,791 180,888 164,054 162,973 11.4 11 \nNorth-\nEastern \nRegion  \n18,758 18,680 18,079 18,033 3.8 3.6 \nAll India  1,511,847 1,504,264 1,379,812 1,374,024 9.6 9.5 \n \nThe Power Supply Position during the year 2023 (Apr il, 2023 to October, 2023) as \ncompared to corresponding period of the previous year 2022 (April, 2022 to October, \n2022) \nRegions \n( Figures in MU net ) \nApril, 2023 - October, \n2023 \nApril, 2022 - October, \n2022 \n% Change \nEnergy \nRequirement \nEnergy \nSupplied \nEnergy \nRequirement \nEnergy \nSupplied \nEnergy Energy \nRequirement Supplied \nNorthern \nRegion 302,137 300,889 297,268 294,208 1.6 2.3 \nWestern \nRegion 300,251 299,936 269,359 268,874 11.5 11.6 \nSouthern \nRegion 244,141 243,915 210,868 210,312 15.8 16.0 \nEastern \nRegion 123,055 122,197 116,275 114,941 5.8 6.3 \nNorth-\nEastern \nRegion 12,649 12,407 11,673 11,615 8.4 6.8 \nAll India  982,233 979,344 905,443 899,950 8.5 8.8 \n \n************* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |717", "t": "Import of Solar Panel", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 717 \nANSWERED ON 07/12/2023 \n \nIMPORT OF SOLAR PANEL \n \n717. SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH ALIAS \n        PAWAN RAJENIMBALKAR \n        SHRI SANJAY JADHAV \n        SHRI VINAYAK RAUT \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the solar panels/equipment used in solar energy generation are being imported; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the number of solar panels imported and the funds spent thereon during each of the last five \nyears including the current year; \n(d) the details of the countries manufacturing solar panels and equipment; \n(e) the details of the countries from which solar panels and equipment are being imported; \n(f) whether the Government proposes to manufacture solar panels and equipment in the country; \n(g) if so, the details thereof; and \n(h) the incentives being provided to the manufacturers for the said purposes? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) Solar panels are manufactured in India as well as imported from various countries. The \ndetails of solar panels imported in the country during last five years including the current year, \ncountry-wise, as per the website pertaining to Export -Import Data Bank of Department of \nCommerce, are at Annexure-I. \n \n(d) Some of the major countries which manufacture solar panels include China, Vietnam, Malaysia, \nIndia, etc. \n \n(e) The details of countries from which solar panels are being imported in India, are mentioned in \nAnnexure-I. \n \n(f) to (h) The Ministry of New & Renewable Energy (MNRE), Government of India, has been \nconsistently bringing out policies to increase the manufacturing of solar panels and associated \nequipment in the country. Some of the initiatives, inter-alia, include those mentioned at Annexure-\nII. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of parts (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 717 for \n07.12.2023 regarding ‘Import of Solar Panel’ \n \n Country Value of Imports in USD million \n2019-20* \n(HS Code \n85414011) \n(Solar PV \nCells & \nModules) \n2020-21* \n(HS Code \n85414011) \n(Solar PV \nCells & \nModules) \n2021-22* \n(HS Code \n85414012) \n(Solar PV \nModules) \n2022-23* \n(HS Code \n85414300) \n(Solar PV \nModules)  \n2023-24* \n(till Sep., 2023) \n(HS Code \n85414300) \n(Solar PV \nModules) \nAnguilla         \nAustralia   0.22 \n \n  \nAustria         \nBangladesh PR         \nBelgium         \nCambodia 5.97 6.69   \n \n0.02 \nCanada 7.92 0.8       \nChad  0.24       \nChile  \n \n    0.2 \nTaiwan 20.97 11.28 0.13     \nChina PRP 1307.03 494.87 3075.32 874.89 501.9 \nCzech Republic         \nDenmark         \nEgypt ARP         \nFinland         \nFrance \n \n0.06   \n \n  \nGermany 3.73 0.03 0.21 0.13 0.02 \nGreece         \nHong Kong 31.3 3.22 229.12 3.04 121.82 \nHungary           \nIndonesia 0.11 0.01       \nIsrael     0.06 \nItaly 0.27 0.16     \nJordan         \nJapan 3.41 0.15   0.01 \nKenya       \nKorea DP RP       \nKorea RP 1.72 0.16     \nLithuania        \nLuxembourg   0.01     \nMalaysia 3.91 7.46 31.43 1.24 43.08 \nMexico         \nMyanmar 0.98 5.78       \nNetherland         \n\n \n \n Country Value of Imports in USD million \n2019-20* \n(HS Code \n85414011) \n(Solar PV \nCells & \nModules) \n2020-21* \n(HS Code \n85414011) \n(Solar PV \nCells & \nModules) \n2021-22* \n(HS Code \n85414012) \n(Solar PV \nModules) \n2022-23* \n(HS Code \n85414300) \n(Solar PV \nModules)  \n2