 objectives and major components of the National Urban \nLivelihoods Mission (NULM) project implemented in different \ndistricts of Kerala; \nb) whether the Government has any schemes to safeguard and \ncreate new public spaces to cater to the needs of the growing \nurban population, ensuring a balance between commercial \ndevelopment and essential public amenities for the well -being of \ncitizens and if so, the details thereof; \nc) whether the current schemes such as the DAY -NULM would \naddress the issues of loss of public space and climate change in \nKerala while fulfilling its objectives; and \nd) if so, the details therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN AFFAIRS \n[SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE] \n \n (a): Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs (MoHUA) is administering \na Centrally Sponsored Scheme named “Deendayal Antyodaya \nYojana-National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NULM)”, \nthrough the respective States/UTs, to reduce poverty and \nvulnerability of urban poor households on a sustainable basis.  \n…2/ - \n\n-2- \nAs informed by the State Govt. of Kerala, Social Mobilization \nand Institutional Development (SM&ID), Employment through \nskills training and placement (EST&P), Self Employment \nProgramme (SEP), Scheme for Shelter for Urban Homeless \n(SUH) and Support to Urban Street Vendors (SUSV) \ncomponents of DAY -NULM are being implemented in all the \ndistricts of Kerala.  \n(b):  Atal Mission for Rejuvenation & Urban Transformation \n(AMRUT) Scheme of Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs was \nlaunched on 25th June 2015 with the objective that includes, \ninter alia, provision of universal water supply, upgrade  of \nsewerage & septage, providing drainage facilities, non-\nmotorized transport and develop ment of parks and green \nspaces, thus improving the quality of life for all citizens, in \n500 cities covering more than 60% population living in urban \nIndia. Further, Hon’ble Prime Minister launched AMRUT 2.0 on \n1\nst October, 2021 extending the scheme to all households in \nall ULBs. \n Further, under the Smart Cities Mission (SCM), to cater to the \nneeds of growing urban population , a total of 1, 377 projects \nworth Rs 12 ,820 crore have been undertaken, which include \ninter alia, riverfront development, Rejuvenation of parks and \npublic spaces,  Open Air Gyms, Rejuvenation of lakes and \ndevelopment, Development of open spaces  and Development \nof Eco Tourism parks.   \n(c)&(d): DAY-NULM Scheme primarily focuses on poverty alleviation \nthrough skill development and livelihood opportunities for the \nurban poor. While it may indirectly contribute to improving the \neconomic conditions of the urban poor, it does  not have \nspecific provisions to address the loss of public space and \nclimate change.  \n…3/ - \n\n-3/- \nThe issues relating to loss of public spaces in urban areas and \nclimate change are being addressed by Missions vis. AMRUT \nand SCM. Under AMRUT, in kerala to enhance public space \nand mitigate climate change effect, 47 acre of green space \narea has been developed. In addition, 0.23 acre of water \nbodies area have been rejuvenated in the State. Moreover, \nunder AMRUT 2.0 in Kerala, 255 Water  bodies rejuvenation \nproject worth Rs. 82.35 crore have been approved.  \n Under SCM, the two cities of Kerala \nvis. Kochi and \nThiruvananthapuram, are developing a total of 139 projects \nworth Rs. 1,833 crore. The projects at Kochi include \ndevelopment of public spaces like Renovation of Calvathy \nCommunity Hall and Customs Boat Jetty in West Kochi, \nRestoration of Rameswaram -Kalvathy canal – Upto Cochin \ncollege, Walkways along the Canal Bank Road, Open Air \nTheatre at Fort Kochi, upgradation of Dutch Palace Entry \nArea, and Renovation of Changambuzha Park. Some of the \npublic spaces being developed in Thiruvananthapuram include \ninter alia  Urban Street haat – Charithra Veedhi including Sree \nChitra Park, Cultural Streets – Manaveeyam Veedhi, \nRedevelopment of Children’s Park, Construction of OAT and \nPark at Putharikkandam Maidanam, and Redevelopment and \nLandscaping of Ponnara Sreedhar Park.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |719", "t": "Duopoly in Aviation Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 719\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nDUOPOLY OF AVIATION SECTOR\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether Boeing and Airbus enjoy a duopoly (more than 50 percent of\nmarket share) in the Indian Civil Aviation sector regarding the designing and\nmanufacturing of civil aircrafts;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the manner in which the Government look\nforward in tackling this duopoly;\n(c) whether the Government has conducted any survey to analyse the impact\nof this duopoly and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether it is true that owing to this duopoly, Boeing and Airbus refused to\nadhere by the force majeure clause normally present in contracts causing\ngrave inconvenience to the Indian Aviation Companies during Covid-19\npandemic and if so, the details thereof;\n(e) whether the Government considers to allow entry to any other players\n(Indian or foreign) in the civil aviation sector to remove the existing duopoly;\nand\n(f) if so, the steps taken by the Ministry to promote indigenous companies to\nremove the duopoly present in the Indian Aviation Sector under the Atma\nNirbhar Bharat scheme, or any other scheme that emphasizes on self-\nreliance?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c) Ministry of Civil Aviation has not conducted any analysis with\nregard to market share of Boeing and Airbus in the civil aviation sector\nregarding designing and manufacturing of civil aircraft. However, globally,\nAirbus and Boeing are the two Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs)\nwho manufacture commercial fixed wing aircraft while other OEMs\nmanufacture regional aircraft.\n \n719. SHRI MANISH TEWARI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n     The extant policy enables domestic manufacturing of aircraft. The\nGovernment has been encouraging the manufacturing of aircraft, including\nregional transport aircraft, and associated equipment by public and private\nenterprises in India.\n \n(d) No such information has come to the notice of Directorate General of Civil\nAviation (DGCA) which is the civil aviation regulator of the country.\n \n(e) and (f) With regard to promotion of design, development and\nmanufacturing in civil aviation sector, Directorate General of Civil Aviation\n(DGCA), has accorded approvals for 16 design organisations. Moreover, as\npart of Atma Nirbhar Bharat, Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL), a\nGovernment of India Public Sector Undertaking (PSU) has been involved in\nmanufacturing the following civil (passenger) aircraft programs:\n(i) Dornier Do-228 (Upgraded) Civil\n(ii) Saras MK-II, 19 seater aircraft\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |72", "t": "Digital Mapping of Ancient Protected Monuments", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nDIGITAL MAPPING OF ANCIENT PROTECTED MONUMENTS \n72. DR. T. SUMATHY THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n  (a) whether the Government is keen on usage of Digital Twin technology and Virtual Reality \ntechnology for digital mapping of ancient protected monuments and the steps taken by \nthe Government for the digital  mapping of ancient protected monuments and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has constituted any expert committee with regard to the \nintroduction and usage of latest technologies and for allocating funds to the same; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) \nto \n(c) \n  \n  \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) in technical collaboration with Bhaskaracharya \nInstitute for Space Applications and Geoinformatics (BISAG) has launched a mobile \napplication 'Indian Heritage' in Sept 2023 containing information on protected \nmonuments/areas with textual content and aided by interactive digital maps. The first \nphase that has been launched incorporates 143 ticketed monuments with the ASI.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |720", "t": "Schemes for Minority Community Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.720 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n  \nSCHEMES FOR MINORITY COMMUNITY CHILDREN \n  \n720.     DR. MOHAMMAD JAWED: \n            SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.: \n            SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n            SHRI BENNY BEHANAN: \n            DR. A. CHELLAKUMAR: \n            DR. AMAR SINGH: \nADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \nSHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \nSHRI ANTO ANTONY: \nSHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN: \nSHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the number of children belonging to the Minority community who have \napplied and been provided scholarships during the last five years, State-wise; \n(b) the reasons for discontinuing the Maulana Azad Fellowship part icularly when all the \nstudents from minority communities are not covered under OBC, SC or ST categories having \nspecific fellowship schemes; \n(c) whether the Government has proposed any policy or scheme for children belonging to the \nMinority Community that provides opportunity to study abroad; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e) the Quantum of funds allocated by the Government to Minority Board or Entity across the \ncountry, State-UT-wise; and \n(f) the time by which the Union Go vernment would increase the amount of fellowship under \nMANF as per new UGC JRF/SRF amount restriction? \n              \n ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a): The Government has been implementing various schemes for the welfare and upliftment \nof every strata, including minorities, especially the economically weaker and lesser \nprivileged sections of the society, through different Ministries/Departments.  \n\nMinistry of Minority Affairs has specifically implemented various wel fare schemes, \nincluding various Scholarship schemes, across the country for socio -economic and \neducational empowerment of the notified minority communities. The details of the number \nof applications received from Minority communities and scholarships provi ded during the \nlast five years, State -wise are available in the website of the Ministry i.e. \nhttps://www.minorityaffairs.gov.in.  \n  \n(b): The Junior Research Fellowship (JRF) Scheme of UGC and CSIR is  open for students of \nall communities/categories. Besides, students from minority communities are also covered \nunder National Fellowship Scheme for Scheduled Castes and OBCs implemented by Ministry \nof Social Justice & Empowerment and National Fellowship Sc heme for Scheduled Tribes \nimplemented by Ministry of Tribal Affairs. As the Maulana Azad National Fellowship \n(MANF) Scheme overlapped with various ongoing fellowship schemes for higher education, \nimplemented by various Central Government Ministries, the Sc heme has been discontinued \nfrom 2022-23. \n  \n(c) and (d): The National Minorities Development & Finance Corporation (NMDFC), a PSU \nunder Ministry of Minority Affairs, provides educational loan for education abroad, at low \nrate of interest, exclusively to stu dents belonging to minority communities. It has also been \nobserved that benefits of interest subsidy that got accrued to the beneficiaries under the \nPadho Pardesh scheme were limited and also that there is an overlap with other similar \nschemes being implem ented by other Ministries viz. Ministry of Social Justice and \nEmpowerment and Ministry Of Tribal Affairs, which are applicable to eligible minority \ncommunity students as well. In view of the aforesaid overlap, limited benefits and ease of \navailing educatio n loans on low rate of interest, it has been decided to discontinue Padho \nPardesh Scheme from 2022-23. \n  \n(e): Under the Scholarship Schemes of this Ministry, funds are not allocated to the Minority \nBoards and entities in the States/UTs. \n  \n(f): All aspects,  including increase in fellowship amount under the Scheme, are looked into \nby the Ministry. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |721", "t": "Delayed Projects of NHs", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 721 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER 2023 \n  \nDELAYED PROJECTS OF NHs \n  \n721. MS. CHANDRANI MURMU:  \n    \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of National Highways (NHs) that failed to be completed on \nschedule, State-wise; \n(b) whether release of funds or administrative lacuna remained the major \nreasons for delay; and \n(c) the cost escalation due to delay, percentage-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)  As on 30 th November, 2023, the construction phase of  667 projects have \nspilled beyond their original completion schedule, without having attained any of \nthe various stages of project completion. State-wise details are at Annexure.  \n \n(b)  No Sir. \n \n(c)  Cost escalation due to delay , if any, is known only on actual completion of \nproject and final settlement of bills.  \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 721 FOR ANSWER ON 07.12.2023 ASKED BY MS. CHANDRANI MURMU REGARDING \nDELAYED PROJECTS OF NHs \nState/UT wise details of projects spilled beyond their original completion schedule: \nSl. No. State/ UT Number of projects \n1. Andhra Pradesh 32 \n2. Arunachal Pradesh 21 \n3. Assam 15 \n4. Bihar 31 \n5. Chhattisgarh 25 \n6. Goa 3 \n7. Gujarat 31 \n8. Haryana 13 \n9. Himachal Pradesh 13 \n10. Jharkhand 15 \n11. Karnataka 37 \n12. Kerala 6 \n13. Madhya Pradesh 15 \n14. Maharashtra 108 \n15. Manipur 29 \n16. Meghalaya 8 \n17. Mizoram 20 \n18. Nagaland 10 \n19. Odisha 22 \n20. Punjab 14 \n21. Rajasthan 28 \n22. Sikkim 10 \n23. Tamil Nadu 33 \n24. Telangana 21 \n25. Tripura 7 \n26. Uttar Pradesh 20 \n27. Uttarakhand 20 \n28. West Bengal 26 \n29. Union Territories (UT’s) 34 \nTotal 667 \n                                                               ***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |722", "t": "Groundwater in Madhya Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA \nREJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 722 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nGROUNDWATER IN MADHYA PRADESH \n†722.    DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of total quantity of groundwater in  Madhya Pradesh and the quantity of water that can be \nused for drinking purpose, district-wise; \n(b) the details of the places in Seoni Balaghat dis trict of Madhya Pradesh where Piezometer/ Automatic \nWater Level Recorders have been installed, location and district-wise; \n(c) the availability and quality of ground water in Seoni Balaghat districts of Madhya Pradesh; and \n(d) the details and the number of villages in Seoni  Balaghat districts of Madhya Pradesh where the \nwork of surface water source and groundwater source based schemes has been completed to provide tap \nwater to every house under Jal Jeevan Mission and t he number of villages in which tap water is being \nsupplied through these schemes, scheme and district-wise? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a)    The Dynamic Ground Water Resources of the countr y are being periodically   assessed jointly by \nCentral Ground Water Board and State Nodal/Ground W ater Department. As per the Ground Water \nResource Assessment-2023 (latest), the assessment i n respect of Madhya Pradesh State is as given \nbelow: -  \ni. the total Annual Ground Water Recharge - 35.47 billion cubic meter (bcm). \nii.  the total Annual Extractable Ground Water Resource  estimated - 32.85 bcm which can be \nutilized for irrigation, drinking, domestic purpose and industrial purpose.  \niii. the total Annual Groundwater Extraction -19.3 billi on cubic meter (bcm), out of which, 1.72 \nbcm ground water is used as drinking and domestic purpose.  \nThe district wise details are given in Annexure -I.  \n(b)    The details of the places in Seoni and Balaghat districts of Madhya Pradesh where Piezometer/ \nPiezometer with Automatic/ Digital Water level Reco rders have been installed by the Government are \ngiven in Annexure -II.  \n \n \n\n \n(c)    Based on the Ground Water Resource Assessment-2023 (latest), the details of total Annual \nGround   Water Recharge, total amount of Annual Ext ractable Ground Water and total Annual \nGroundwater Extraction along with the amount of extraction for drinking and domestic purposes for the \ndistricts of Balaghat & Seoni are given in Annexure -I.  \n         Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) generate s ground water quality data of the country \nincluding Madhya Pradesh on a regional scale as par t of its ground water quality monitoring program \nand various scientific studies. These studies indicate, out of the samples collected and analysed from 79 \nlocations of Balaghat District, the occurrence of contaminants such as Nitrate at 18 locations and Iron at \n12 locations, beyond permissible limits (as per BIS ) for human consumption, has been found; \nSimilarly, out of the samples collected and analyse d from 86 locations of Seoni District, Nitrate at 2 0 \nlocations and fluoride at 5 locations has been found. \n(d)       As reported by State Govt. of Madhya Pradesh on JJM-IMIS portal, the number of villages in \nSeoni and Balaghat districts of Madhya Pradesh wher e water is being supplied through completed \nsurface water source and groundwater source-based schemes are given below:  \n Sr.No.  District  \nWater is being supplied through \nsurface water source-based \nschemes.  \n(No of villages) \n \nWater is being supplied through \nground water source-based \nschemes.  \n(No of villages)  \n1 Balaghat 51 302 \n2 Seoni 181 123 \n  \n***  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nANNEXURE –I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTAR RED QUESTION NO. 722 TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING “GROU NDWATER IN MADHYA \nPRADESH”.  \n DYNAMIC GROUND WATER RESOURCES OF MADHYA PRADESH, 2023 (in HAM)  \nS.NO  Name of District \nTotal Annual \nGround Water \nRecharge \nAnnual Extractable \nGround Water \nResource \nTotal Annual \nGround Water \nExtraction \nCurrent Annual Ground \nWater Extraction for \ndomestic purpose \n1 2 3 4 5 6 \n1 AGAR MALWA 43548.8 40403.21 36336.96 1666.65 \n2 ALIRAJPUR 21490.07 19930.2 8633.15 1910.82 \n3 ANUPPUR 28826.36 26993.74 6349.33 1562.47 \n4 ASHOKNAGAR 39493.05 36824.34 23207.03 1824.21 \n5 BALAGHAT 77153.99 71949.04 16909.36 3851.18 \n6 BARWANI 55841.78 52036.18 37143.14 3727.85 \n7 BETUL 104898.72 97750.31 48528.3 3726.52 \n8 BHIND 98040.22 89953.59 29373.62 3562.49 \n9 BHOPAL 40750.43 37948.75 29712.91 5110.13 \n10 BURHANPUR 36681.41 33962.85 23704.63 1728.73 \n11 CHHATARPUR 86761.39 80899.76 52923.58 3105.27 \n12 CHHINDWARA 104808.74 97605.19 61722.98 5400.1 \n13 DAMOH 39378.78 36576.17 21388.93 2331.94 \n14 DATIA 43075.86 40122.32 14464.77 1766.56 \n15 DEWAS 86549.72 80944.58 64753.63 4300.72 \n16 DHAR 138460.44 127766.67 83557.97 6069.37 \n17 DINDORI 35008.2 33257.8 4164.57 1848.41 \n18 GUNA 81681.71"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |723", "t": "Electrified Flex Fuel Vehicle", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 723 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nELECTRIFIED FLEX FUEL VEHICLE \n \n723.   SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE: \n SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n           \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the world’s first prototype of the  BS-6 Stage -II ‘Electrified \nFlex Fuel Vehicle (FFV)’ aligns with India’s transportation goals; \n(b)  if so, the impact it might have on the  country’s transportation \nlandscape; and \n(c)  the comparison of FFV engine to engines of  other renewable energy \nsources like EVs and Hydrogen? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) and (b) The prototype of the BS-6 Stage-II ‘Electrified Flex Fuel Vehicle \n(FFV)’ is in alignment with the alternate fuel mobility solutions. \n(c) In flex-fuel vehicles (FFVs), the engine is compatible to run on blend of \ngasoline and ethanol of varying percentages. However, in FFVs there are \nvehicular emissions whereas in the case of  EVs and Hydrogen run \nvehicles, there are no CO2 emissions. \n \n \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |724", "t": "Udyam Portal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 724 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON.  07.12.2023 \n \n \nUDYAM PORTAL\n \n \n724. \n \nDR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL:\n \n         \n \nSHRI\n \nSHANKAR LALWANI\n:\n \n \nWill\n \nthe\n \nMinister\n \nof\n \nMICRO,\n \nSMALL\n \nAND\n \nMEDIUM\n \nENTERPRISES\n \nbe pleased\n \nto\n \nstate: \n \n \n(a)\n \nthe number of units that have been registered on the Udyam Portal during the last five years, \nState\n-\nwise;\n \n(b)\n \nthe number of persons who have got employment under these registered u\