742 30.22 23.21 8.37 \n35 Lakshadweep - - - - - - - \n36 Puducherry 15,265 14,299 9,253 9,253 243.17 204.71 152.22 \nSub-total (UT) :- 1,05,688 98,239 70,931 69,693 1,947.71 1,598.41 1,439.39 \nGrand Total* :- 118.63Lakh** 113.42Lakh* 78.15Lakh* 73.89Lakh* 2.00LakhCr. 1.54LakhCr. 1.45LakhCr. \n*Includes grounded (4.01 lakh)/ completed (3.41 Lakh)/ occupied (4.78 lakh) of JnNURM houses during mission period. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |766", "t": "Delhi-Meerut RRTS Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 766 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 7, 2023 \nDELHI-MEERUT RRTS PROJECT \nNo. 766 : SHRI  THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \n Will the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether Delhi-Meerut RRTS Project has become fully \nfunctional; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether the Rapid Rail Transport System (RRTS) Project would \nsave energy and generate ten million units of power annually \nand would also reduce carbon emission and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(d) whether it is also proposed to introduce RRTS in the State of \nTamil Nadu in the near future; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n       THE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n     (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) & (b): The 17 Km priority section of the Delhi -Ghaziabad-Meerut \nRegional Rapid Transit System (RRTS) corridor (Sahibabad to \nDuhai Depot in Uttar Pradesh) has become operational on \n21.10.2023. The entire Delhi-Ghaziabad-Meerut RRTS corridor, \ncovering a total length of 82.15 Km,  is planned to be fully \ncommissioned by June, 2025. \n(c): Yes, sir. National Capital Region Transport Corporation Ltd. \n(NCRTC), the implem enting agency of the Delhi -Ghaziabad-\nMeerut RRTS corridor, has informed that they have employed \nvarious energy conservation measures on the Delhi -\nGhaziabad-Meerut RRTS corridor, like regenerative braking  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \n \nfacility in rolling stock and provision of Solar panels on the \nroof top of Stations/Depot buildings, which is capable to save \nand generate 10 millions of electricity annually , thereby, \nreducing carbon emission by 9200 tonnes.  \n \n(d) & (e): No, sir. The  initiatives for planning, management, funding, \nmonitoring and implementation of urban transport system are \ntaken by respective State Government(s)/Union Territories.  \nCentral Government considers financial assistance to urban \nrail based system based on feasibility of the proposal, \nnecessity and availability of resources, as and when posed by \nthe concerned State/UT Government(s) in accordance with \nthe provisions of the Metro Rail Policy, 2017.  Presently, there \nis no proposal for RRTS in Tamil Nadu submitted by \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu to the Central Government for \napproval. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |767", "t": "Works undertaken for Development of Minorities", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 767 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n  \nWorks undertaken for Development of Minorities \n  \n767 SHRI RAJU BISTA: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \na.  the details of projects/works undertaken for the development of minority \ncommunities by the Government in West Bengal; and \nb. the status and the details of projects/works under which assistance has been provided \nto minority communities in Darjeeling, Kalimpong and North Dinajpur districts, since \n2019? \n  \nA N S W E R \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a):   The Government implements various schemes for the welfare and upliftment of every \nstrata, including minorities, specially the e conomically weaker and lesser privileged sections \nof the society, through various schemes of the Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship, Ministry of Textiles, Ministry of Culture, Ministry of Women and Child \nDevelopment and Ministry of Rural De velopment. Ministry of Minority Affairs specifically \nimplements various schemes across the country for socio -economic and educational \nempowerment of the six (6) centrally notified minority communities. \n  \nPradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK), a Centra lly Sponsored Scheme (CSS), \nis an area development programme under which community infrastructure and basic \namenities are being created in the identified areas. The scheme was earlier implemented \nas Multi -sectoral Development Programme (MsDP), which was re structured and \nimplemented as Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK) with effect from May, \n2018, in 1300 identified blocks, towns and district headquarters of the country. The \nscheme has now been revised with effect from financial year 2022 -23, for \nimplementation in all districts of the country during the period of 15th Finance \nCommission Cycle. The scheme is being implemented under the aegis of the State \nGovernments/ Union Territory Administrations (UTAs) on a fund sharing pattern and \nthe projects are implemented and managed by the concerned State Government/ UT \nAdministration. \n  \n\nSince Inception of the scheme a total number of 141988 units worth of total estimated cost of \nRs 422537.47 Lakh with Central Share of Rs 297205.48 Lakh have been approved under  \nPMJVK. Projects approved includes 163 - School Buildings, 340 - Hostels for school/ ITI/ \npolytechnic,  18300 -Additional Classrooms/ Library/ Lab/ Hall in schools, 1216 -Smart \nClassroom/ Computer Lab/ Teaching Aid in school, 5528 - Other Infrastructure in s chool, \n1650 - Health Projects, 39 -ITI/ Additional Infrastructure for ITI, 8 -Polytechnic/ Additional \ninfrastructure for existing polytechnic, 37 - Skill Centres, 1 - Working Women Hostel, 14674 \nAnganwadi Centres, 342 - Community Service Centre/ Sadbhav Mandap / Community Hall, \n20381- Drinking Water Projects, 73519 IAYs, 2 - Infrastructure in colleges, 731 - Sanitation \nProjects and 5057- Misc infrastructures. \n(b):   The status and details of projects approved in Darjeeling, Kalimpong and North \nDinajpur districts of West Bengal since year 2019 under PMJVK is as under:- \n  \nName of \nDistricts \nName of \nProjects \nTotal \nCost \napproved \nCentral \nShare \napproved \nUnits \nSanctioned  \nCompleted  Work \nnot \nStarted \nDropped  \nDarjeeling \n Hostel for \nschool/ ITI/ \npolytechnic \n224.00 134.40 2 _ 2 _ \nKalimpong \nSchool \nBuildings \n_ _ 1 _ _ 1 \nHostel for \nschool/ ITI/ \npolytechnic \n224.00 134.40 2 _ 2 _ \nHealth \nProjects \n38.72 23.24 4 _ 4 _ \nUttar \nDinajpur \nSchool \nBuildings \n_ _ 2 _ _ 2 \nHostel for \nschool/ ITI/ \npolytechnic \n560.00 336.00 5 4 1 _ \nHealth \nProjects \n164.56 98.74 17 _ 17 _ \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |768", "t": "Calicut Mono Rail/Light Metro Project", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 768 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nCALICUT MONO RAIL/LIGHT METRO PROJECT \n \nNO. 768.   SHRI M K RAGHAVAN : \n          \n Will the Minister of Housing And Urban Affairs be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has received any communication from \nKerala State Government after 2017 on the Calicut Mono Rail/Light \nMetro Project; and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the status of project ? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a) Yes, Sir. The proposal for Light Metro Rail Project in Kozhikode was \nreceived from Government of Kerala. \n(b) This Ministry has requested G overnment of Kerala to revise the \nproposal in consonance with the provision of Metro Rail Policy 2017 \nfor consideration by Government of India.  The revised proposal is not \nreceived from Government of Kerala. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |769", "t": "Funds released under various Educational Schemes for Minorities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 769 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n  \nFunds released under various Educational  \nSchemes for Minorities \n  \n769.     SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the quantum of funds released to Meerut Parliamentary constituency of Uttar Pradesh \nunder various educational empowerment schemes for minority communities during the last \nthree years and the current year; \n(b) whether the funds released under the said schemes have been utilised; \n(c) if so, the details of the schemes under which the set targets have been achieved or likely to \nbe achieved shortly; and \n(d) the reasons for shortfall in achieving the targets thereunder? \n              \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) to (d): The Government has been implementing various schemes for the welfare and \nupliftment of every strata, including minorities, especially the economically weaker and \nlesser privileged sections of the society, through different Ministries/Departments. Ministry \nof Minority Affairs has specifically implemented various welfare schemes, including  various \nScholarship schemes, across the country for socio -economic and educational empowerment \nof the notified minority communities. \nThe Scholarship schemes namely (i) Pre -Matric Scholarship, (ii) Post Matric Scholarship, \n(iii) Merit cum Means based Schol arship, implemented by Ministry of Minority Affairs are \nCentral Sector Schemes wherein, funds are directly transferred to the account of beneficiary, \nhence fully utilised and there is no shortfall in achieving the targets. The district -wise details \nof fund released under various educational schemes for minorities in the country including \nthe State of Uttar Pradesh during the last three year are available in the website of the \nMinistry i.e. https://www.minorityaffairs.gov.in. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |77", "t": "Promotion of Global Competitiveness among Post graduate Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77  \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \n MODEL TENANCY DRAFT BILL \n \nNO. 77. SHRI PARBHUBHAI NAGARBHAI VASAVA: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has circulated the Model Tenancy draft \nbill to State Governments; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the number of States which have enacted the legislation/not \nenacted the legislation till date on Model Tenancy; and \n(d) the time by which the State would enact legislation in this regard \nalong with their action plan as on date, State-wise ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 77*  FOR 07.12.2023 REGARDING ‘MODEL TENANCY \nDRAFT BILL’ \n \n(a) to (d):  Yes, Sir. The relationship between landlord and tenant is a \nstate subject. With a view  however to assist the States/ Union \nTerritories (UTs)  in overhauling their tenancy legislations to \npromote rental housing in the Country, Ministry of Housing and \nUrban Affairs prepared a Model Tenancy Act (MTA) . After the \napproval of MTA by Union Cabinet, it was circulated to all \nStates and UTs on 7\n June, 2021 for adoption by way of either \nenacting fresh legislation or amending existing rental laws \nsuitably.  \n \nStates of Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh  and \nAssam have notified their revised Tenancy laws. \n \n  \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "Export of Petrol and Diesel", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 770 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7 th December, 2023 \n \n Export of Petrol and Diesel \n  \n770. DR. ARVIND KUMAR SHARMA: \n \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies are exporting Petrol (MS) and \nDiesel (HSD) to countries other than Nepal, Bhutan and Sri Lanka, after meeting the \ndomestic demands; \n(b) if so, the details of such exports (both quantity and value) during the last three \nyears, oil company, month and product-wise; \n(c) the number of PSU stand-alone refineries in the country along with the details of \nPetrol (MS) and Diesel (HSD) exports (both quantity and value) by them during the \nsaid period, stand-alone refinery, month and product-wise; \n(d) whether the said exports are made at prices less than or more than Refinery \nTransfer Price (RTP); and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000 ) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL \nGAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c):The details of export of Motor Spirit (MS) (Petrol) and High Speed Diesel \n(HSD) Fuel by Oil PSUs to countries other than Nepal, Bhutan and Sri Lanka during \nlast three years are as under: \n \nMS Exports by Oil PSUs \nProduct 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nIOCL 295.9 750.4 423.6 2233.3 63.9 545.4 \nBPCL 0 0 169.9 1359.4 36.8 250.0 \nHPCL 0 0 0 0 13.1 79.9 \nMRPL* 162.5 515.3 735.4 3927.0 945.4 7429.7 \nTotal 458.4 1265.7 1228.9 7519.7 1059.1 8304.9 \nSource: Petroleum Planning & Analysis Cell; TMT: Thousand Metric Tonne \n*Standalone Refinery \n \n---2/- \n-2- \n\n \n \nHSD Exports by Oil PSUs \nProduct 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nQuantity \n(TMT) \nValue in \n` Crore \nIOCL 1386.0 3543.8 1213.7 5991.7 5.5 39.6 \nBPCL 542.8 1248.2 689.5 4057.1 77.5 561.4 \nHPCL 183.1 474.7 0 0 0 0 \nMRPL* 1187.3 2665.0 2959.8 15618.9 1798.0 15228.8 \nNRL* 54.6 180.4 56.5 303.5 50.3 393.0 \nTotal 3353.8 8112.0 4919.5 25971.2 1931.4 16222.8 \nSource: Petroleum Planning & Analysis Cell; TMT: Thousand Metric Tonne \n*Standalone Refinery \n \n(d)&(e): Profitability of exporting Motor Spirit (MS) and High Speed Diesel (HSD) \ncompared with Refinery Transfer Price (RTP) depends on prices actually quoted by \nthe buyer on export tenders and the RTPs prevalent during export period as RTP \npricing cycle and export price cycles are different. \n \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Guidelines for Seat Booking Portals", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 771\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nGUIDELINES FOR SEAT BOOKING PORTALS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether it is a fact that airlines are displaying every seat as paid on\nbooking portals including their allied vendor's portals and if so, the details\nthereof;\n \n(b) whether specific regulations or guidelines are in place to prevent airlines\nfrom misleadingly showing all seats as paid on booking portals;\n \n(c) if so, the details of action taken in this regard; and\n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the time by which regulations or guidelines\nwould be implemented to address this issue?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (d) No Sir. All airlines are not displaying every seat as paid on booking\nportals.\n \nWith a view to make basic fare more affordable and provide the consumer an\noption of paying for the services which he/she wishes to avail, Government has\nallowed some of the services unbundled and charged separately on \"opt-in\"\nbasis which includes preferential seats too.\n \nAccordingly, airlines are charging only preferential seats on an opt-in basis in\naccordance with Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) Air Transport\nCircular 01 of 2021, titled \"Unbundle of services and fees by scheduled\nairlines\". However, if the passenger doesn?t wish to opt for a preferred seat,\nthe passenger will be assigned seat free of cost before departure through\nAuto-Assigned seat selection mode.\n771. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |772", "t": "Paliyekkara Toll Plaza", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 772 \nANSWERED ON 07TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nPALIYEKKARA TOLL PLAZA \n772. SHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN: \n         \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Paliyekkara toll plaza would be closed down on NH -\n544 in Thrissur district considering that only one toll plaza is allowed \nin 60 km; \n(b)  the revenue collected so far since its operation; \n(c) whether an y financial mis -management by the concessionaire \ncompany operating toll plaza has been noticed and if so, the details \nthereof, and \n(d) whether any violation of agreement has been found from this \ncompany and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) No sir, National Highways Fee Rules, 2008 allows to establish \nUser Fee plaza within distance of 60 Km from other User Fee Plaza, \nif Authority deems it necessary. \n(b) Revenue of Rupees 1299.59 crore has been collected at \nPaliyekkara fee plaza by BOT(Toll) concessionaire till 30.11.2023.  \n\n(c) & (d) As per Concession Agreement of Edapally -Thrissur, \nConcessionaire has to deposit Rs 215 Cr as negative grant to NHAI in \n6 instalments (1st instalment of Rs 15 Cr & 5 instalments of Rs 40 Cr \neach). However, Concessionaire has deposited 1st instalment of Rs \n15 Cr only till date. Concessionaire & NHAI has raised various claims \nand counterclaims before Arbitral Tribunal. NHAI ha s issued \nPreliminary Termination Notice to Concessionaire on 13.04.2023 due \nto various defaults of the Concessionaire including non deposition of \nnegative grant & non maintenance of project stretch which has been \nstayed by Arbitration Tribunal on 21.04.202 3. The Arbitration \nproceedings are in progress. \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |773", "t": "UDAN Scheme for Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 773\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nUDAN SCHEME FOR TAMIL NADU\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to add more airports across the country\nfor improving regional connectivity under Udan Scheme and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) whether it is a fact that development of smaller, existing airports in Tamil\nNadu is yet to take off under Udan Scheme and if so, the details thereof and if\nnot, the reasons therefor;\n(c) the corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard;\n(d) the contribution of UDAN scheme in the overall development of Civil\nAviation Sector;\n(e) whether Airport Authority of India (AAI) has embarked upon\ndevelopment and expansion of airports in the country, if so, the details thereof\nand the amount allocated and utilized for the purpose; and\n(f) the other steps taken by the Government to enhance regional air\nconnectivity from unserved and underserved airports in the country to make\nair travel affordable to the masses?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a)  Under Phase-I of the scheme for  'Revival and Upgradation of unserved\nand underserved Airports/Heliports/Water Aerodromes, the Government has\ntargeted development of 100 Aerodromes. 76 Aerodromes including 2 Water\nAerodromes and 9 Heliports have been operationalised so far. Under Phase-II\nof the scheme, additional 50 aerodromes have been targeted to be developed. \n \n(b) to (c):  Five airports, namely, Vellore, Neyveli, Salem, Ramnad and\nThanjavur have been identified  for development and operationalisation\nunder the UDAN Scheme in the State of Tamil Nadu.  Salem Airport has\nalready been operationalised. Development works at Vellore and Neyveli have\n773. SHRI SELVAM G.\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nbeen completed and licensing is in progress. The works related to the\nestablishment of the Civil Enclave at Thanjavur Airport is being carried out\nby the Airports Authority of India(AAI). The site for Ramnad Airport is to be\nfinalised and handed over by the State Government  to the Airports Authority\nof India for development under the Regional Connectivity Scheme. 24 routes\nhave so far been operationalised in the State under UDAN.\n \n(d) :  Development and revival of  the existing airports has  provided\nconnectivity to the  remote and hilly areas at affordable rates under UDAN\nwhich would contribute to the growth of  the local economy.  517 routes\nconnecting 76 airports, including 9 Heliports & 2 Water Aerodromes, have\ncommenced and more than 130 lakh people have so far availed of the benefits\nof the UDAN Scheme.\n \nVGF amounting to Rs. 3020 crores has been disbursed to Selected Airline\nOperators as of 31.10.2023.\n \n \nAn expenditure of  Rs. 3751 Crores has so far been incurred towards\ndevelopment of airports under the \"Revival/Development of unserved and\nunderserved Airports/Heliports/Water Aerodromes\" Scheme.\n \n(e): Airports Authority of India (AAI) and other airport developers have\ntargeted capital outlay of approximately Rs. 98,000 crore in the airports\nsector during 2019-24 for expansion and modification of existing terminals,\nstrengthening of runways and developing Greenfield Airports among other\nactivities to meet the growing air traffic demands.\n \n(f):  Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS)-Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik (UDAN),\nlaunched on 21.10.2016, aims to stimulate regional air connectivity and make\nair travel affordable to the masses. With a view  to enhance connectivity to\nremote and regional areas of the country and achieve last mile connectivity\nthrough helicopters, UDAN  5.1 has been launched. The ongoing UDAN 5.2\nseeks to enhance connectivity through small aircraft.\n \n \n \n \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Foreign Investment in MSME Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 774\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023\n \n \nFOREIGN INVESTMENT IN MSME SECTOR\n \n \n774. \n \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \n        \n \nMS. DEBASREE CHAUDHURI:\n \n \nWill the Minister \nof MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is aware of the need for foreign investment in the Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterprises (MSMEs) sector in the country;\n \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, whether any study has been conducte\nd on the advantages and disadvantages of foreign \ninvestment in MSMEs sector from competitiveness and viability point of view;\n \n \n(c) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe action taken/proposed to be taken by the Governm\nent to improve viability, income and \nemployment in MSME sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a):\n \nForeign Direct Investment (FDI) is an enabling policy, which is uniformly applicable \nirrespective of scale and size of eligible investee entities. To promote FDI , the Government has put in \nplace an investor\n-\nfriendly policy, wherein most sector/activities \nincluding Micro,\n \nSmall \nand\n \nMedium\n \nEnterprises\n \n(MSMEs) except certain strategically important sectors/activities are open for \n100% FDI under the automatic route, subject to sectoral laws, regulations/rules, security conditions and \nstate/local/laws/regulatio\nns. \n \n \n(b) & (c): \n \nNo specific study has been conducted on\n \nthe\n \nadvantages\n \nand\n \ndisadvantages\n \nof\n \nforeign \ninvestment\n \nin\n \nMSMEs\n \nsector from\n \ncompetitiveness\n \nand\n \nviability\n \npoint\n \nof\n \nview by the Ministry.\n \n \n(d):\n \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MS\nME) implements various schemes \nand programmes aimed at promotion and development of MSME Sector in the country. These \nschemes/programmes include MSME Champions Scheme, Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro and \nSmall Enterprises (CGTMSE), Prime Minister's E\nmployment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Micro \nand Small Enterprises \n-\n \nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP) and Raising and Accelerating \nMSME Performance (RAMP). \n \n \nFurther Government has taken a number of initiatives to support the MSME Sector. Some of t\nhem \nare:\n \n \ni.\n \nCollateral free loan up to a limit of Rs. 500 lakh (w.e.f. 01.04.23) to MSEs with guarantee \ncoverage up to 85 % for various categories of loan through Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for \nMicro and Small Enterprises (CGTMSE) under Credit Guarantee Sch\neme.\n \n\n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \nii.\n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through Self Reliant India Fund. This scheme has a provision \nfor corpus of Rs.10,000 crore from Government of India.\n \n \niii.\n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \niv.\n \nNew registration of MSMEs through \n''Udyam Registration'' for Ease of Doing Business.\n \nv.\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crore. \n \nvi.\n \nInclusion of retail and whole sale trades as MSMEs w.e.f. 02.07.2021.\n \nvii.\n \nNon\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of \nMSMEs.\n \nviii.\n \nRoll out of Raising and Accelerating MSME Performance (RAMP) programme with an outlay \nof Rs. 6,000 crore over 5 years.\n \nix.\n \nIntegration of Udyam Portal and National Career Service (NCS) of Ministry of Labour & \nEmployment, as an outcome registered MSMEs a\nre able to search for jobseekers on NCS.\n \nx.\n \nUnder\n \nVivad se Vishwas \n–\n \nI, relief by way of refund of 95% of the deducted performance \nsecurity, bid security and liquidated damages was provided to MSMEs. Relief was also \nprovided to MSMEs debarred for default in e\nxecution of contracts.\n \nxi.\n \nLaunch of Udyam Assist Platform (UAP) to bring the Informal Micro Enterprises (IMEs) under \nthe formal ambit for availing the benefit under Priority Sector Lending (PSL).\n \nxii.\n \nLaunch of ‘PM Vishwakarma’ Scheme on 17.09.2023 to provide bene\nfits to the traditional \nartisans and craftspeople engaged in 18 trades.\n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |775", "t": "Infrastructure in Urbanized Villages", "head": "   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 775 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \n             INFRASTRUCTURE IN URBANIZED VILLAGES \n \nNO. 775.      SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA:      \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS  be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) whether specific measures are being undertaken by the \nGovernment to improve the infrastructure in urbanized villages \nin Delhi and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the manner in which the Government is prioritizing the \ndevelopment of communal and recreational facilities like parks \nand playgrounds in these areas; \n(c) whether the Government intends to balance the preservation \nof cultural heritage with modern infrastructural development \nin these urbanized villages and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) the budget allocation and timelines for these development \nprojects; and \n(e) whether the Government is ensuring the participation and \nfeedback of local communities in the planning and execution \nof these infrastructure projects and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n       \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n     (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a) to (e ) The Delhi Development Authority (DDA) has informed that \ndevelopment works including infrastructure provisions in urbanized \nvillages is taken up by the Government of National Capital Territory \nof Delhi (GNCTD) and other local bodies.   DDA undertakes the works \nin those areas of urbanized villages, which are declared as \ndevelopment area under Section 12  of the Delhi Development Act \n1957.  \n \nDDA has informed that provisions of facilities in the revenue estate \nof urbanized villages is governed as per Master Plan/ Zonal Plan.  \n \nDevelopment areas of DDA are to be developed through voluntary \nparticipation of land owners by pooling their lands as per prevailing \nnorms.  \n \n \n…2/ - \n\n \n-2- \n  Master Plan for Delhi has provisions for conservation of heritage \nwhich aims at identification of heritage zones & archaeological \nparks and development of special conservation plan for listed \nbuildings and precincts. \n \nMunicipal Corporation of Delhi (MCD) has informed that it undertakes \nthe development works like improvement of Roads, Storm Water \nDrains, Parks, Street Lights etc. in the urban villages under its \njurisdiction.  MCD has developed some parks and play fields on the \nGram Sabha Land in urbanized villages. \n \n MCD has informed that during the current Financial Year  2023- 24, \nRs 3,500/- lakhs has been allocated under Non-Plan head of Account \nfor the works to be executed in the urbanized village in Delhi. \nBesides this, development works are also being carried out from the \ndiscretionary funds on the request of area representatives. \n \nDelhi Cantonment Board has informed that recreational facilities \nsuch as parks, open gyms, schools, roads, drains, sewerage system, \nwater infrastructure and community halls etc. have already been \nprovided by them in some urbanized villages under their jurisdiction. \n \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Bypass at NH-66", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 776 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nBYPASS AT NH-66 \n  \n776. SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n  \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has received any petition from the residents of \n Valiyannur village in Kannur seeking specific solutions to issues in the \n construction of bypass of NH-66; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether there has been a public demand to ensure travel facilities along the \n PC road, specifically from (L150+ 100) chainage, to provide easy access \n from Chalilmota via Palliprom to Kannur; \n \n(d) whether there has been a public demand to alter the construction plan along \n the L150/596 chainage towards Kozhikode, specifically to relocate the \n service road to the lower section instead of the original; \n \n(e) whether there has been a demand to establish a drainage system to ensure \n the flow of rain water collected from the Varam field area to reach Kakkad \n river through Karikkanchira; and \n \n(f) whether these demands have been approved and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n\n \n(a) to (e)     Yes Sir. Residents of Valiyannur village of Kannur District have \nrequested to provide following additional provisions in project for 6 -laning of \nThaliparambha - Muzhapillangad section of NH-66 in Kerala: \n \ni. Extension of service road from Km 150/600  to Km 150/100 (till PC road). \n \nii Extension of Viaduct from Km 149/700 to Km  150+100 (till PC Road). \n \niii To provide cross drain from Km 150/100 to Km 150/600 to ensure the flow \n of rain water. \n \niv Crossing facility at Km 150/100. \n \nv To provide drainage system from Varam field area to reach Kakkad river \n through Karikkanchira. \n  \n(f) No Sir, since concession agreement of this project provides sufficient \n service road, crossing facility & drainage facility along the project stretch. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |777", "t": "Irrigation Projects in Odisha", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 777 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nIRRIGATION PROJECTS IN ODISHA \n777.    SHRI JUAL ORAM \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details and the number of medium, multipurp ose and major projects to be executed in the State of \nOdisha which are pending for the approval of Central Water Commission; \n(b) the total hectares of land that would be brough t under irrigation once each of those projects are \ncompleted; \n(c) the estimated cost of those projects; \n(d) the funds earmarked for those projects and funds released so far; \n(e) the steps taken to expedite the clearance; and \n(f) the present status of each of those projects? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) to(c) & (f)      There are 5 number of medium irrigation projects. 1 number multipurpose (major) project, \nand 2 major irrigation projects presently under app raisal at Central Water Commission (CWC). Details i n \nthis regard are tabulated below.    \nSI No.  Name of the \nprojects  \nArea to be \nirrigated (in \nhectare)  \nEstimated \ncost (Rs. in \ncrore)  \nKey issues and status  \nMedium irrigation projects  \n1 Chandrinala \nirrigation project  \n5,000 Not \nestimated  \nFeasibility report is under examination. \n2 Mankada irrigation  \nproject \n9,850 585.46 -do- \n3 Hidsing Irrigation  \nProject  \n2,958 245.49 -do- \n4 Khadakhai medium \nirrigation \nproject(ph-II) \n5,394 377.26 Feasibility report is under examination. \nMeanwhile, the feasibility report has been \nshared by CWC with other co- basin States of \nJharkhand and West Bengal for their \n\n  \n(d) One of the pre-requisites for inclusion of any majo r/ medium/ multipurpose irrigation project for \nfinancial assistance under any of the ongoing schem es of this Ministry, requires the project to be app raised \nby CWC. Since the above projects are still under ap praisal, no funds have been earmarked / released fo r \nthose projects at this stage. \n(e)  All projects to be appraised in CWC are to be submi tted through online platform, namely, ePAMS. \nThe platform allows real-time monitoring of the app raisal process for each of the projects under appra isal at \nCWC. Further, in the updated guidelines issued in t he year 2017, CWC has laid down indicative timeline s \nfor the appraisal process. This enables measuring of delays against the laid down benchmark.  \n*****  \n \ncomments, if any. \n5 Bankatira barrage \nproject \n7,300 505.90 Feasibility report is under examinatio n. \nMajor/ Multipurpose irrigation projects  \n6 Nabarangpur \nirrigation project \n15,000 278.99 In its present form, the project has not been \nfound acceptable from inter- State aspects. \n Either modified proposal, or NOC from the \nco-basin State of Chhattisgarh, is to be \nprovided by the State Government.  \n7 Middle Kolab \nmultipurpose \nproject \n24,543 1,649.12 -do- \n8 Govindapalli \nintegrated irrigation \nproject \n37,126 4,005.54 The project is presently under examination \nby various expert units in CWC. Comments \non inter- State aspect have been \ncommunicated to Odisha in November, 2023.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "JJM in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-778 \nANSWERED ON-07/12/2023 \nJJM IN TAMIL NADU \n778. SHRI VELUSAMY P: \n          SHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of rural households which have been provided clean tap water connection under \nJal Jeevan Mission (JJM) in the country during the last three years, year, State/UT and district -\nwise for Tamil Nadu; \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any regular monthly/quarterly quality check study of \nwater during the last three years and if so, the details thereof, year and State-wise; and \n(c) the number of rural households which did not have clean tap water connection in the country \nduring the last three years, year, State and district-wise for Tamil Nadu? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n(a) and (c) Since August 2019, Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) – \nHar Ghar Jal, in partnership with States/ UTs  including Tamil Nadu , to enable every rural \nhousehold in the country to have assured potable water through tap connection.  \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, 3.23 Crore (17%) rural households were \nreported to have tap water connections. So far, as reported by States/ UTs as on 05.12.2023, around \n10.49 Crore additional rural households have been provided with tap water connections under \nJJM. Thus, as on 0 5.12.2023, out of 19.24 Crore rural households in the count ry, around 13.7 3 \nCrore (71.37%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes.  \nThe State/ UT-wise and year wise details of water tap connections provided during the last three \nyears and remaining households are at Annex-I. The district-wise details of tap water connections \nprovided during last three years and the remaining rural households to be provided tap water \nconnections in Tamil Nadu are at Annex-II. \n \n  \n\n(b) Under JJM, no study regarding monthly/ quarterly quality check of water has been carried out \nby the Government. However, States/ UTs have been advised to undertake testing of water quality \non a periodic basis i.e. once in year for chemical parameter, and twice in a year for bacteriological \nparameters (pre-monsoon and post -monsoon) and take remedial action wherever necessary, to \nensure that the water supplied to households is of prescribed quality. Further, to enable States/ \nUTs to test water samples for water quality, and for sample collection, reporting, monitoring and \nsurveillance of drinking water sources, an online JJM – Water Quality Management Information \nSystem (WQMIS) portal has been developed.  \nAs reported by States/ UTs, as on 04.12.2023, there are 2,111 drinking water quality testing \nlaboratories at different levels viz. State, District, sub-division and/ or block level in the country. \nOut of 2,111 laboratories, 1,324 laboratories have been National Accreditation Board for Testing \nand Calibration Laboratories (NABL) accredited/ recognized. To encourage water quality testing \nto ensure potable drinking water supply, States/ UTs have opened water quality testing laboratories \nto public for testing of their water samples at a nominal rate. \nTo empower citizens, States/ UTs have been advised to identify and train 5 persons, pr eferably \nwomen, in every village to conduct water quality testing using Field Testing Kits (FTKs)/ \nbacteriological vials at village level and report the same on the Water Quality Monitoring \nInformation System (WQMIS) portal. So far, as reported by states/U Ts, more than 23.33 lakh \nwomen have been trained for testing water using FTKs. \n**** \n  \n\nAnnex \n \nAnnex referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 778 to be answered on 07.12.2023 \nState-wise and year-wise details of rural households provided tap connections during last three years. \n \n(Number in lakhs) \nS. \nNo. State/ UT \nTotal \nrural HHs \nas on date \nRural HHs with \ntap water \nconnection as on \n15/08/019 \nRural HHs provided with tap water \nconnections \nTotal Rural HHs \nwith tap water \nsupply (As on \n05/12/2023) \nBalance \nhouseholds \nNo. In % 20-21 21-22 22-23 23-24* No. In% No. \n1.  A&N Islands 0.62 0.29 46.02 0.33 - - - 0.62 100 - \n2. Andhra Pr. 95.54 30.74 32.18 12.77 9.59 12.12 2.35 68.76 71.97 26.78 \n3. Arunachal Pr. 2.29 0.23 9.91 0.65 0.42 0.34 0.42 2.19 96.01 0.10 \n4. Assam 69.14 1.11 1.63 5.07 16.52 8.75 13.45 45.45 65.74 23.74 \n5. Bihar 166.30 3.16 1.90 103.46 19.86 3.63 1.26 160.34 96.41 5.96 \n6. Chhattisgarh 49.96 3.20 6.38 1.51 4.45 10.81 14.15 35.14 70.34 14.89 \n7. DNH and DD 0.85 0.00 0.00 0.25 0.61 - - 0.85 100 - \n8. Goa 2.63 1.99 75.70 0.33 - - - 2.63 100 - \n9. Gujarat 91.18 65.16 71.46 10.95 8.99 5.03 - 91.18 100 - \n10. Haryana 30.41 17.66 58.08 7.91 3.49 - - 30.41 100 - \n11. Himachal Pr. 17.09 7.63 44.64 3.79 2.87 0.93 0.29 17.09 100 - \n12. J&K 18.70 5.75 30.84 2.16 0.58 0.40 3.10 14.06 75.19 4.64 \n13."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "Kutcha and Pucca Houses", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 779 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nKUTCHA AND PUCCA HOUSES \n \nNO. 779.  SHRI LALLU SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of kutcha and pucca houses in every district of the \ncountry; \n(b) the number of pucca houses constructed by the Government between \n2004-2013; and \n(c) the number of pucca houses constructed by the Government from \n2014 onwards? \n         ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) : During the process of Census, the details of kutcha and pucca houses \nare also collected on decadal basis across the country. As per Census \n2011, State/Union Territory (UT) -wise details of kutcha ( or non- pucca \n/semi-pucca) and pucca houses are at Annexure-I.  \n \n(b) & (c): ‘Land’ and ‘Colonisation’ are State subjects. Therefore, schemes \nrelated to housing are implemented by State/UT Governments in their \nrespective regions. However, Government of India is supplementing the \nefforts of States/UTs by providing Central Assistance to provide pucca \nhouse with basic amenities to all eligible urban beneficiaries across the \ncountry. The number of pucca houses sanctioned, constructed and \ndelivered to the beneficiaries by the Government of India between 2004-\n2013 and 2014 onwards are at Annexure-II.   \n***** \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to LS USQ No. 779 due for answer on 07.12.2023 \n \nState/Union Territory (UT) -wise details of kutcha ( or non-pucca /semi-\npucca) and pucca houses  \n(Figure in ‘000’) \nS. No. States/UTs Total Census \nHouses \nKutcha/Semi- \nPucca Houses \nPucca Houses \n1 Andhra Pradesh 23,636 3,923 19,712 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 340 153 187 \n3 Assam 8,757 2,010 6,747 \n4 Bihar 22,783 8,042 14,741 \n5 Chhattisgarh 6,361 617 5,744 \n6 Goa 443 16 427 \n7 Gujarat 15,083 313 14,770 \n8 Haryana 6,435 1,463 4,972 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 2,576 168 2,408 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 3,276 895 2,381 \n11 Jharkhand 7,324 871 6,453 \n12 Karnataka 16,142 1,865 14,277 \n13 Kerala 9,935 343 9,593 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 17,427 1,786 15,641 \n15 Maharashtra 29,596 2,195 27,401 \n16 Manipur 601 108 493 \n17 Meghalaya 681 124 557 \n18 Mizoram 257 42 215 \n19 Nagaland 492 98 394 \n20 Orissa 12,019 4,279 7,740 \n21 Punjab 7,140 625 6,515 \n22 Rajasthan 16,153 2,323 13,830 \n23 Sikkim 152 9 143 \n24 Tamil Nadu 21,398 3,435 17,963 \n25 Tripura 1,023 121 902 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 42,602 10,744 31,858 \n27 Uttarakhand  3,030 205 2,825 \n28 West Bengal 24,238 3,572 20,666 \n29 A & N Islands 122 10 112 \n30 Chandigarh 277 10 267 \n31 D & N Haveli 90 2 88 \n32 Daman & Diu 75 1 74 \n33 NCT of Delhi 4,053 88 3,965 \n34 Lakshadweep 18 2 16 \n35 Puducherry 349 68 281 \n INDIA 3,04,884 50,526 2,54,358 \n \nSource: Census of India - 2011: Series - H: Table on Houses, Household Amenities & Assets \n\nAnnexure-II referred in reply to LS USQ No. 779 due for answer on 07.12.2023 \n \nNumber of pucca houses sanctioned, constructed and delivered to the \nbeneficiaries by the Government of India between 2004- 2013 and 2014 \nonwards \n \nParticulars 2004-2013 2014 Onward \nHouses Approved for \nconstruction  13.46 Lakh    118.63 Lakh   \nHouses Grounded for \nconstruction  8.58 Lakh    113.42 Lakh   \nConstruction of Houses \nCompleted/Delivered  8.04 Lakh    78.15 Lakh   \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Permission to Kerala for Taking Loan from Different Financial Institutions", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO-*78 \nANSWERED ON- 07/12/2023 \n \nBeneficiaries under JJM \n*78. SHRIMATI APARAJITA SARANGI: \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  the salient features of Jal Jeeven Mission (JJM) and the target set under the said Mission; \n(b)  the details of the funds sanctioned, allocated  and utilised under the said Mission in Odisha \nand Rajasthan so far; \n(c)  the details of the number of tap water  connections provided so far particularly in the rural  \nareas under the said Mission in Odisha and Rajasthan; \n(d)  the measures being taken by the Government  to cover the rest of the rural areas of Odisha \nand Rajasthan at the earliest; \n(e)  whether the Government has set up water  testing laboratories in the country to test water  \nsamples and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f)  the details of the impact of JJM on health and sanitation in Odisha? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n (a) to (f) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n\n2 \n \nStatement referred in reply of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 78 to be answered on \n07.12.2023 \n(a) to (c) To make provision of tap water supply to every rural househo ld in districts across the \ncountry, including those in the States of  Odisha and  Rajasthan, Government of India, in \npartnership with States, is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) since August 2019. The key \nfeatures of the Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) ar e to make provision of potable water to every rural \nhousehold through tap water connection at a service level of 55 litre per capita per day (lpcd), of \nprescribed quality (BIS:10500), on regular and long-term basis.  \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, only 3.11 lakh (3.5%) rural households were \nreported to have tap water connections  in the State of Odisha . So far, as reported  as on \n03.12.2023, more than 57.33 lakh (64.5%) additional rural households have been provided with \ntap water connecti ons. Thus, as on 03.12.2023, out of 88.64 lakh rural households in the State, \nmore than 60.44 lakh (68%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes.  \nSimilarly, a t the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, only 11.74 lakh (11%)  rural \nhouseholds were reported to have tap water connections  in the State of Rajasthan . So far, as \nreported as on 03.12.2023, more than 36.18 lakh (34%) additional rural households have been \nprovided with tap water connections. Thus, as on 03.12.2023, out o f 106.64 lakh  rural \nhouseholds in the State, more than 47.92 lakh (45%) households are reported to have tap water \nsupply in their homes.  \nDetails of Central f und allocated, fund drawn and fund utilization reported by the States of \nOdisha and Rajasthan under Jal Jeevan Mission is as under: \n \nState Year \nCentral share Expenditure \nunder State \nshare \nOpening \nBalance \nFund \nallocated \nFund \ndrawn \nAvailable \nfund \nReported \nutilization \nOdisha \n2019-20     0.78  364.74    364.74    365.52    275.02  255.02  \n2020-21  90.50   812.15   609.11   699.61   688.69   673.00  \n2021-22  10.93   3,323.42   2,492.56   2,503.49   1,306.20   1,289.71  \n2022-23  1,197.29   3,608.62   1,768.73   2,966.02   2,166.47   2,149.96  \n2023-24*  799.56   2,108.54   1,054.27   1,853.83   1,158.46   1,149.67  \nRajasthan \n2019-20  313.67    1,301.71      1,301.71      1,615.38    620.31  698.54  \n2020-21  995.07   2,522.03   630.51   1,625.58   762.04   789.05  \n2021-22  863.53   10,180.50   2,345.08   3,208.61   1,920.16   1,664.02  \n2022-23  1,288.46   13,328.60   6,081.80   7,370.26   3,938.57   4,122.81  \n2023-24*  3,431.69   3,019.94   ND     3,431.69   2,346.67   2,164.35  \n*As on 03.12.2023             Source: JJM-IMIS \n(d) A number of steps have been taken to plan and implement JJM in the whole country including \nthe rural areas of Odisha and Rajastha n, with speed, inter alia, including joint discussions and \nfinalization of saturation plan s and annual action plan s (AAP) of States/ UTs, regular review of \nimplementation, workshops/ confer ences/ webinars for capacity building, training, knowledge \nsharing, field visits by multi -disciplinary team to provide technical support, etc. A detailed \nOperational Guideline for the implementation of JJM; Margdarshika for Gram Panchayats & \nVWSCs to provide safe drinking water in rural households and Guidelines on a special campaign \nto provide piped water supply in anganwadi centres, ashramshalas and schools have been shared \nwith States/ UTs to facilitate planning and implementation of Jal Jeevan Mission. For o nline \n\n3 \n \nmonitoring, JJM –Integrated Management Information System (IMIS) and JJM –Dashboard has \nbeen put in place. Provision has also been made for transpare nt online financial management \nthrough Public Financial Management System (PFMS). \n(e) Government of India is assisting and facili"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Jal Shakti Kendra", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 780 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nJAL SHAKTI KENDRA \n780.    DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Jal Shakti Kendras (J SKs) have been set up in various States of the coun try including \nWest Bengal and if so, the details thereof, district-wise including Jalpaiguri district; \n(b) whether the Government has started any geo-tagging or inventory of all the water bodies in West Be ngal and \nif so, the details thereof; \n(c) the time by which the work of formulating scien tific plans on the basis of geo-tagging is likely t o be initiated \nfor water conservation; and \n(d) the steps being taken by the Government for com pleting the ongoing project under Jal Shakti Abhiya n in the \nState? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a)   Under the Jal Shakti Abhiyan: Catch the Rain (JSA :CTR) campaign, State/ UT Governments have been \nrequested to set up Jal Shakti Kendras (JSKs) in al l the districts of the country including all the di stricts of West \nBengal. As per the information available on the JSA :CTR portal (https://jsactr.mowr.gov.in/), details of districts \nthat have reported setting up of JSK is annexed.  JSK has been established in Jalpaiguri district of West Bengal.   \n(b) to (d)      Enumerating, geo-tagging & making inventory of all water bodies and preparation of scientific \nplans for water conservation based on it, is one of  the focused interventions of JSA:CTR campaign.  Un der this \ncampaign, all State Governments/UTs including West Bengal Government have been requested to enumerate,  \ngeo-tag and create inventory of all water bodies an d to upload the same on JSA:CTR portal. States/UTs have \nalso been advised to prepare scientific water conse rvation plan for each district. As per the informat ion available \non the JSA:CTR portal (https://jsactr.mowr.gov.in/) , till 05.12.2023, 7,47,480 water bodies have been \nenumerated & geo-tagged in the State of West Bengal.  \nMinistry of Jal Shakti has also launched the first Census of Water Bodies in convergence with the Sixt h \nMinor Irrigation Census (reference year 2017-18) un der the ‘Irrigation Census’ scheme with the objecti ve of \ndeveloping a national database of all water bodies in the country. Presently, the report of first Cens us of Water \nBodies has been published and uploaded on the website of the Ministry.  \nJSA:CTR has been implemented across the country on an annual basis and therefore the projects \nundertaken during the campaign are a continuous pro cess. Since water is a state subject, the timelines  for the \nprojects executed under JSA:CTR, including formulat ion of scientific action plans, are defined by the respective \nstates. However, the Central Government undertakes MIS based monitoring to assess the progress of the \nactivities/projects under JSA:CTR. \n***   \n\n ANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTAR RED QUESTION NO. 780 TO \nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 REGARDING “ JAL SHAKTI KENDRA ”. \nDistricts where Jal Shakti Kendra have been set up  \n(as on 04.12.2023) \nS.No.  State  District  \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS NICOBARS \n2 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS NORTH AND MIDDLE ANDA MAN \n3 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS SOUTH ANDAMANS \n4 ANDHRA PRADESH ALLURI SITHARAMA RAJU \n5 ANDHRA PRADESH ANAKAPALLI \n6 ANDHRA PRADESH ANANTAPUR \n7 ANDHRA PRADESH ANNAMAYYA \n8 ANDHRA PRADESH BAPATLA \n9 ANDHRA PRADESH CHITTOOR \n10 ANDHRA PRADESH EAST GODAVARI \n11 ANDHRA PRADESH ELURU \n12 ANDHRA PRADESH GUNTUR \n13 ANDHRA PRADESH KAKINADA \n14 ANDHRA PRADESH KONASEEMA \n15 ANDHRA PRADESH KRISHNA \n16 ANDHRA PRADESH KURNOOL \n17 ANDHRA PRADESH NANDYAL \n18 ANDHRA PRADESH NTR \n19 ANDHRA PRADESH PALNADU \n20 ANDHRA PRADESH PARVATHIPURAM MANYAM \n21 ANDHRA PRADESH PRAKASAM \n22 ANDHRA PRADESH SPSR NELLORE \n23 ANDHRA PRADESH SRI SATHYA SAI \n24 ANDHRA PRADESH SRIKAKULAM \n25 ANDHRA PRADESH TIRUPATI \n26 ANDHRA PRADESH VISAKHAPATANAM \n27 ANDHRA PRADESH VIZIANAGARAM \n28 ANDHRA PRADESH WEST GODAVARI \n29 ANDHRA PRADESH Y.S.R. \n30 ARUNACHAL PRADESH EAST SIANG \n31 ARUNACHAL PRADESH KRA DAADI \n32 ARUNACHAL PRADESH LOHIT \n33 ARUNACHAL PRADESH LONGDING \n34 ARUNACHAL PRADESH LOWER DIBANG VALLEY \n35 ARUNACHAL PRADESH PAPUM PARE \n36 ARUNACHAL PRADESH TIRAP \n37 ARUNACHAL PRADESH WEST KAMENG \n38 ASSAM CACHAR \n39 ASSAM DHUBRI \n40 ASSAM KAMRUP \n41 ASSAM LAKHIMPUR \n42 ASSAM TINSUKIA \n43 BIHAR ARARIA \n44 BIHAR ARWAL \n45 BIHAR AURANGABAD \n46 BIHAR BANKA \n\n47 BIHAR BEGUSARAI \n48 BIHAR BHAGALPUR \n49 BIHAR BHOJPUR \n50 BIHAR BUXAR \n51 BIHAR DARBHANGA \n52 BIHAR GAYA \n53 BIHAR GOPALGANJ \n54 BIHAR JAMUI \n55 BIHAR JEHANABAD \n56 BIHAR KAIMUR (BHABUA) \n57 BIHAR KATIHAR \n58 BIHAR KHAGARIA \n59 BIHAR KISHANGANJ \n60 BIHAR LAKHISARAI \n61 BIHAR MADHEPURA \n62 BIHAR MADHUBANI \n63 BIHAR MUNGER \n64 BIHAR MUZAFFARPUR \n65 BIHAR NALANDA \n66 BI"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |781", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Sahaj Har Ghar Bijli Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.781 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n \nPRADHAN MANTRI SAHAJ HAR GHAR BIJLI YOJANA \n \n781.  SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the salient features of the Pradhan Mantri  Sahaj Har Ghar Bijli Yojana–\nSaubhagya Scheme launched by the Government; \n \n(b)  whether electricity facility to all hous eholds in the country is being \nimplemented as per the schedule and targets; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has any data/information regarding the States \nwhere the said  scheme has achieved considerable progress and  achieved i ts \ntargets in providing electricity to the households; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof along with the extent  of progress made by certain \nStates in this regard? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) :  The Government of India launched Pradhan Mantri  Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar \nYojana–SAUBHAGYA in October, 2017 with the objective of achieving universal \nhousehold electrification,  by providing electricity connections to all un -\nelectrified households in  rural areas and all poor households in urban areas in \nthe country. Salient features of the scheme were: \n \n(i) Providing last mile connectivity and electricity connections to all un -\nelectrified households in rural areas. \n \n(ii) Providing Solar Photo Voltaic (SPV) based standalon e systems for un -\nelectrified households located in remote and inaccessible villages / \nhabitations where grid extension is not feasible or cost effective. \n.......2. \n\n \n \n- 2 - \n \n(iii) Providing last mile connectivity and electricity connections to all \nremaining economically poor un -electrified households in urban areas. \nNon-poor urban households are excluded from this scheme. \n \n(b) to (e)  : Under the aegis of SAUBHAGYA, as on 31.03.2019, all households \nwere reported electrified by the States, except 18,734 households i n Left Wing \nExtremists(LWE) affected areas of Chhattisgarh. Subsequently, seven States \nnamely Assam,  Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Manipur, Rajasthan and \nUttar Pradesh had  reported that around 19.09 lakh un -electrified households, \nidentified before 3 1.03.2019, which were unwilling earlier but later expressed \nwillingness to get electricity  connection. This was also sanctioned. All these \nseven States had reported 100%  household electrification as on 31.03.2021. A \ntotal of 2.817 crore households were  electrified since the launch of \nSAUBHAGYA, up to 31.03.2021.  4.43 lakh additional households, which were \nidentified as un-electrified before 31.03.2019, were given electricity connections \nunder DDUGJY.  As such, a total of 2.86 crore households were electrif ied till \n31.03.2022.  A certificate was taken from all States/ UTs that all eligible \nhouseholds have been electrified.    The state -wise details of number of \nhousehold covered are enclosed at Annexure.   \n \n*********** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPL Y TO PARTS ( b) TO ( e) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 781 ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2023 \n************* \nState-wise electrification of households since launch of Saubhagya Scheme including \nAdditional Households achievement under DDUGJY  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nStates \nNo of \nHouseholds \nelectrified \nfrom \n11.10.2017 to \n31.03.2019 as \nper \nSaubhagya \nPortal \nAdditional Sanction allowed \nunder Saubhagya \nFurther Additional \nHouseholds sanctioned \nunder DDUGJY \nGrand \nTotal(A+B) \nNo of \nHouseholds \nreported \nelectrified \nfrom  \n01.04.2019 to \n31.03.2021 \nTotal HHs \nelectrified \nas on \n31.03.2021 \n(A) \nHouseholds \nSanctioned \nduring 2021-22 \nHouseholds \nelectrified (as \non \n31.03.2022) \n(B) \n1 Andhra \nPradesh* 1,81,930 0 1,81,930     1,81,930 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh 47,089 0 47,089 7859 0 47,089 \n3 Assam 17,45,149 2,00,000 19,45,149 480249 381507# 23,26,656 \n4 Bihar 32,59,041 0 32,59,041     32,59,041 \n5 Chhattisgarh 7,49,397 40,394 7,89,791 21981 2577 7,92,368 \n6 Gujarat* 41,317 0 41,317     41,317 \n7 Haryana 54,681 0 54,681     54,681 \n8 Himachal \nPradesh 12,891 0 12,891     12,891 \n9 Jammu & \nKashmir 3,77,045 0 3,77,045     3,77,045 \n10 Jharkhand 15,30,708 2,00,000 17,30,708     17,30,708 \n11 Karnataka 3,56,974 26,824 3,83,798     3,83,798 \n12 Ladakh 10,456 0 10,456     10,456 \n13 Madhya \nPradesh 19,84,264 0 19,84,264 99722 0 19,84,264 \n14 Maharashtra 15,17,922 0 15,17,922     15,17,922 \n15 Manipur 1,02,748 5,367 1,08,115 21135 0 1,08,115 \n16 Meghalaya 1,99,839 0 1,99,839 420 401 2,00,240 \n17 Mizoram 27,970 0 27,970 \n \n  27,970 \n18 Nagaland 1,32,507 0 1,32,507 7009 7009 1,39,516 \n19 Odisha 24,52,444 0 24,52,444     24,52,444 \n20 Puducherry* 912 0 912     912 \n21 Punjab 3,477 0 3,477     3,477 \n22 Rajasthan  18,62,736 2,12,786 20,75,522 210843 52206 21,27,728 \n23 Sikkim 14,900 0 14,900     14,900 \n24 Tamil Nadu* 2,170 0 2,170     2,170 \n25 Telangana 5,15,084 0 5,15,084     5,15,084 \n26 Tripur"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |782", "t": "Energy Generation Plants based on Plastic", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 782 \nANSWERED ON 07/12/2023 \n \nENERGY GENERATION PLANTS BASED ON PLASTIC \n \n782. SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY \n        SHRI N. REDDEPPA \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up energy generation plants based on plastic; \n(b) if so, the details along with the location thereof; and \n(c) the time by which the energy is likely to be generated therefrom? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) There is no such proposal under consideration. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |783", "t": "Routes under UDAN", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 783\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nROUTES UNDER UDAN\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the total number of routes operationalized under the regional air\nconnectivity scheme UDAN since its inception and the number of persons\nbenefitted from it;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to include more routes under the\nUDAN scheme and if so, the details in this regard;\n(c) whether the Government has identified such routes in the country and if\nso, the details in this regard;\n(d) whether UDAN scheme has contributed significantly in the growth of civil\naviation industry in the country and generated demand for new aircrafts and\nif so, the details in this regard; and\n(e) whether the Government has also amended the rules for commercial pilot\nlicenses to further improve ease of doing business in the aviation sector and if\nso, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a): As on 30.11.2023, 517 RCS routes have been operationalised under UDAN\nsince its inception. More than 130 lakh people have so far benefited from the\nScheme.\n \n(b) and (c): UDAN is an ongoing scheme where bidding rounds are conducted\nfrom time to time for covering more destinations/stations and routes under\nthe scheme. Routes have been awarded under UDAN Round 5.0   to the\n783. SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA\nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK\nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE\nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nSelected Airlines Operators (SAOs) for operation under the scheme. In\naddition to the above,UDAN round 5.1 And 5.2 have been launched to\nenhance the connectivity to remote and regional areas of the country through\nhelicopters and connectivity through small aircraft.\n \n(d): Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS) has contributed to the growth of\ncivil aviation sector by promoting  connectivity of underserved and unserved\nairports. 130 Plus number of city pairs have been created and 2.47 Lakh\nflights have operated under the UDAN scheme so far.\n \nUDAN scheme has encouraged procurement of different type of aircraft in the\ncountry. At present 3 seater Tecnam, 9 seater Cessna 208B, 19 seater Twin\nOtter, 50 seater Embraer 145, 42/72/78 seater ATR and Q-400 as well as\nbigger aircraft like 189 seater Airbus 320/321and B737 are in operation for\nUDAN flights.\n \n13 Airlines have commenced operations under UDAN, including start-ups.\nUDAN scheme has been the cradle for birth of new Airlines like IndiaOne,\nStar Air, FlyBig and Fly91.\n \n(e): The following amendments have been incorporated in Aircraft Rules 1937\nin the recent past to improve ease of doing business in the aviation sector with\nrespect to Commercial Pilot licenses being issued :-\n \n1. Easing out of flying experience requirements for issue of Airlines Transport\nPilots License (Aeroplane/Helicopter) in Section M and N of Schedule II of\nAircraft Rules, 1937 (w.e.f. 07th January 2020) towards enabling co-pilots to\nbecome pilots-in-command.\n \n2.Validity of FRTOL (Flight Radio Telephony License) increased upto the\nvalidity of RTR (Radio Telephony) license and validity of Student Pilot\nLicense (SPL) has been increased from erstwhile 5 years to 10 years w.e.f 08th\nApril 2022.\n \n3.Validity of Commercial Pilot and Airline transport license has been\nincreased from erstwhile 5 years to 10 years w.e.f 10th October 2023.\n \n \n \n \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |784", "t": "Quality of Drinking Water", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-784  \nANSWERED ON-07.12.2023 \n \nQUALITY OF DRINKING WATER \n \n784. SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps to monitor the quality of drinking water and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n  \n(b) whether the Government has carried out periodic tests to ascertain the quality of water in \nindustrial regions and if so, the details thereof; and \n  \n(c) whether the Government has taken the steps to tackle possible health hazards due to \ncontaminated water used for drinking and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL)  \n(a) to (c) The Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) – Har Ghar Jal, since \nAugust 2019, in partnership with States, to make provision of potable tap water supply in adequate \nquantity, of prescribed quality and on a regular & long-term basis to every rural household. States/ \nUTs have been advised to plan and implement piped water supply schemes based on alternative safe \nwater sources for all the villages with water quality issues.  \nAs per operational guidelines of Jal Jeevan Mission, all States/UTs have been advised to carry out \ntesting of water quality on a periodic basis, i.e. once in a year for chemical parameters, and twice in \na year for bacteriological parameters. The Guidelines also provide remedial action is taken wherever \nnecessary, to prevent the use of contaminated water for drinking  and thereby, to tackle possible \nhealth hazards due to such use. \nFurthermore, for assured delivery of safe water, strengthening, up-gradation, and improving the \nfunctioning of drinking water quality testing laboratories technical and financial support is being \ngiven under JJM. As reported by the States/ UTs, as on 05.12.2023, there are 2,111 drinking water \nquality testing laboratories at different levels viz. State, District, sub-division and/ or block level in \nthe country. Out of 2,111 laboratories, 1,324 laboratories have been National Accreditation Board \nfor Testing and Calibration Laboratories (NABL) accredited/ recognized.  \n\n2 \n \n \nIn order to empower t he communities to monitor the water quality, States/ UTs have also been \nadvised to identify and train 5 persons, preferably women, in every village to conduct water quality \ntesting using Field Testing Kits (FTKs)/ bacteriological vials at village level and report the same on \nthe WQMIS portal. So far, as reported by States/UTs, more than 23.34 lakh women have been \ntrained for testing water using FTKs.  \nTo enable States/ UTs to  monitor the quality of drinking water, an online JJM – Water Quality \nManagement Information System (WQMIS) portal has been developed  where the water samples \ncan be registered, the results of the tests can be uploaded , and shared with the concerned \nstakeholders. In case of contamination , an alert i s generated, and the concern authority  has to \nundertake remedial action.  \nAs a result of the above-mentioned efforts, as reported by States/UTs, since the launch of JJM, \nyear-wise, testing of water quality samples in the laboratories has increased from around 39.15 lakh \nsamples in 2018 -19 to more than 62 lakh samples in 2022 -23. Similarly, testing of water quality \nusing FTKs has increased from around 11.01 lakh samples in 2018-19 to 1.07 crore water samples \nduring 2022 -23. During 2023 -24, as on 05.12.2023, 49.53 lakh samples in labs and 80.94 lakh \nsamples using FTKs have been tested for drinking water quality through the WQMIS portal.  \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |785", "t": "Projects for Water Supply", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 785 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \nPROJECTS FOR WATER SUPPLY \nNO. 785.   SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether two projects for water supply, one project for sewage and \nseptage management, two projects for drainage and three projects for \ndevelopment of parks have been sanctioned for Machilipatnam city in \nAndhra Pradesh under AMRUT with an estimated cost of Rs. 80 crores; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the projects have been completed and become operational; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons for delay along with the \ntime by which these are likely to be completed? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a) to (c):  Yes Sir,  under Atal  Mission for Rejuvenation Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT), 02 projects for water supply, 01 project for sewage  \n…2/ - \n\n \n \n-2- \nand septage management, 02 projects for drainage and 03 projects for \ndevelopment of parks have been undertaken in Machilipatnam city in Andhra \nPradesh with an estimated cost of ₹80 crore. As reported by the State, out of \nthe 8 projects, 4 projects have been completed and are operational. The \nremaining 4 projects are under implementation. The present status of the \nabove projects is at ANNEXURE. \n(d): As per the response from the state, the delay in the completion of the 4 \nprojects is mainly due to COVID 19. The likely date of completion of these \nprojects as indicated by the State is as per table below. \nS.No. Projects Status \n1 \n5 MLD STP; including interceptor \ndrains and septage management \nfor entire city \nScheduled to be \ncompleted tentatively \nby July 2024. \n2 \nProviding water supply House \nService Connections, distribution \nlines and Pumping Mains wherever \nsource is available in \nMachilipatnam Municipality under \nAMRUT Scheme \nScheduled to be \ncompleted tentatively \nby February 2024. \n3 Construction of storm water \noutfall drainage network Scheduled to be \ncompleted tentatively \nby July 2024. 4 Construction of storm water \noutfall drainage network - Phase II \n*****\n\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 785 REGARDING “PROJECTS FOR WATER \nSUPPLY”, DUE FOR ANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 07 DECEMBER, \n2023. \nSTATUS OF AMRUT PROJECTS IN MACHILIPATNAM CITY \nS.No. Title Sector Status \nProject \nCost \n(in ₹ cr.) \nPhysical \nProgress \n(in %) \n1 \nDevelopment of Sidimbhi \nAgraharam park \nOthers \n(Parks) \nCompleted & \noperational 0.32 100 \n2 \nDevelopment of Bandar Kota \npark under AMRUT 2016-17 \nOthers \n(Parks) \nCompleted & \noperational 0.8 100 \n3 \nConstruction of new filtration \nplant (22 MLD) providing \nhousehold connections where \npipe lines are laid \nWater Supply \nCompleted & \n22 MLD WTP is \noperational \n17.69 100 \n4 \nDevelopment of Bandar Kota \npark under AMRUT 2016- 17 - \nPhase II \nOthers \n(Parks) \nCompleted & \noperational 1.01 100 \n5 \n5 MLD STP; including \ninterceptor drains and septage \nmanagement for entire city \nSewerage \nand Septage \nManagement \nOngoing 10.87 55 \n6 Construction of storm water \noutfall drainage network Drainage Ongoing 11.09 \n50 \n7 \nConstruction of storm water \noutfall drainage network - \nPhase II \nDrainage Ongoing 7.93 \n8 \nProviding water supply House \nService Connections, \ndistribution lines and Pumping \nMains wherever source is \navailable in Machilipatnam \nMunicipality under AMRUT \nScheme \nWater Supply Ongoing 30.41 94 \nTotal 80.12  \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |786", "t": "Safety Audit of Airlines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 786\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nSAFETY AUDIT OF AIRLINES\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether any deficiencies were found in the recently conducted safety audit\nof airlines, if so, the details thereof;\n \n(b) whether the Government proposes ranking of airlines based on adherence\nto safety parameters so that passengers can make an informed choice; and\n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) In the year 2023, safety audits for 06 scheduled airlines have been carried\nout as per Annual Surveillance Plan 2023. Some of the salient findings\nobserved during the audit are attached at Annexure-I.\n \n(b) & (c) Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) carries out\ncertification of various operators and also carries out surveillance/ audit at\nregular intervals vide published Annual Surveillance Programme (ASP) on\nneed basis to ensure that continued compliance is maintained by the\noperators. However, no airline-wise ranking is done by DGCA.\n \nViolations to the extant Rules & Regulations are dealt as per the Enforcement\nPolicy and Procedure Manual (EPPM) and enforcement actions as deemed fit\nare taken.\n \n \n*****\n786. DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI\nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR DEBEY\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nSALIENT AUDIT OBSERVATIONS  \n \n In case of few airlines, Flight Data Monitoring exceedances were captured during analysis \nhowever there was a delay in taking corrective actions by the organization.  \n In case of few airlines  Internal audits  were not being carried out by the airlines in \naccordance with the approved Flight Safety Manual and the audit findings were not closed \neffectively. \n The work related to Flight Safety Documentation System is l acking supervisory control \nfor checking the amendments of documents as per latest applicable regulations & circulars. \n The Voluntary reports received within the organization are not addressed in time bound \nmanner. \n Training documents of Crew are not timely updated. \n Calibration of various maintenance/ operational tools/ instruments is not as per schedule \napproved by manufacturer. \n Sufficient trained number of manpower is not available in engineering and safety \ndepartments. \n System of follow up action on the finding/ recommend ation of the investigations is not \nfollowed as per timeline prescribed. \n In few of the airlines, it was observed that the Risk assessment for change management \nwas not effective.  \n***END*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |787", "t": "Multi Sectoral Development Programme for the Welfare of Minorities", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †787 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n  \nMulti Sectoral Development Programme for the Welfare of Minorities \n  \n†787.  SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \na. the details of the districts covered under Multi-sectoral Development Programme \n(MsDP) for the welfare of minorities in the country State-wise including district-wise \nin Maharashtra; \nb. the places in Mumbai included under MsDP; \nc. whether the Government has taken effective steps/proposes to take steps to open \neducational institutes, ITIs and Skill Development Centers for the upliftment of \nchildren belonging to Muslim community in Mumbai; and \nd.  if so, the details thereof? \nA N S W E R \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n   \n(a) & (b) : The Government implements various schemes for the welfare and \nupliftment of every strata, including minorities, specially the economically weaker \nand lesser privileged sections of the society, through various schemes of the Ministry \nof Skill Development and Entrepreneurship, Ministry of Textiles, Ministry of Culture, \nMinistry of Women and Child Development and Ministry of Rural Development. \nMinistry of Minority Affairs specifically implements various schemes across the \ncountry for socio-economic and educational empowerment of the six (6) Centrally \nnotified minority communities. \n  \nPradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK), a Centrally Sponsored Scheme \n(CSS), is an area development programme under which community infrastructure and \nbasic amenities are being created in the identified areas. The scheme was earlier \nimplemented as Multi-sectoral Development Programme (MsDP), which was \nrestructured and implemented as Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK) \nwith effect from May, 2018, in 1300 identified blocks, towns and district headquarters \nof the country. The list of these 1300 identified blocks, towns and district \nheadquarters including Mumbai is available on the website of this Ministry i.e. \nwww.minorityaffairs.gov.in.   The scheme has now been revised with effect from \n\nfinancial year 2022-23, for implementation in all districts of the country during the \nperiod of 15th Finance Commission Cycle. \n  \n(c) & (d): The priority sectors under PMJVK are education, health, skill \ndevelopment, women centric projects, etc. Under the education sector project \nproposals relating to construction of schools, additional classrooms, hostels, computer \nliteracy centres/digital education centres, science laboratories in schools, basic support \ninfrastructure, drinking water units, toilets in schools, slow learner centres, Degree \nColleges, higher institutes of technical studies such as Medical, Nursing Colleges, \nAgriculture Colleges, etc are considered and under the skill development sector Skill \ndevelopment centres, ITI, Polytechnics, Hunar Hubs etc. as well as Physical \ninfrastructure facilities relating to arts, crafts, skills, heritage etc are considered under \nthe scheme. The project proposals prepared/drafted by the respective State Govt./UTs \nAdministrations on these priority sectors. The projects under the scheme are \nconsidered and approved on the basis of the proposals received from the respective \nState Govt. and UT Administration. Under the PMJVK 4 smart class rooms are \napproved in Mumbai with the total cost of Rs. 10.16 Lakh having central share of Rs. \n6.10 Lakh. \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |788", "t": "Beneficial works under AMRUT", "head": " \n \nO.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 788 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nBENEFICIAL WORKS UNDER AMRUT \nNO. 788.   SHRIMATI SANDHYA RAY \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of beneficial works done by the Union Government in \nMadhya Pradesh, particularly in Bhind and Datia districts under the \nAMRUT scheme during last four years, district and year-wise; and \n(b) the details of total expenditure incurred under  the AMRUT scheme \nin Madhya Pradesh, particularly  in Bhind and Datia districts during \nthe last four years, district and year-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN \nTHE MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a): Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) \nwas launched by the Central Government on June 25, 2015, for 500 \ncities (485 cities including 15 merged cities) across all States/  Union \nTerritories (UTs) in the country. The mission is in its extended period \ntill 31 March, 2024. The major thrust areas of the Mission are water \nsupply, sewerage & septage management, storm water drainage, \ngreen spaces & parks, non- motorized urban transport and capacity \nbuilding.  \n…2/ - \n\n \n \n-2- \nFor the State of Madhya Pradesh, State Annual Action Plans \n(SAAPs) amounting to ₹6,200.67 crore, including committed Central \nAssistance (CA) of ₹2,592.86 crore have been approved under  Atal \nMission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT). Under \nAMRUT, Madhya Pradesh State has  taken up 216 projects worth \n₹6,766 crore, of which 197 projects worth ₹4,507 crore have been \ncompleted, while 19 projects worth ₹2,259 crore have been grounded \nand are at various stages of implementation.  \nDuring the Mission period, f or the city of Bhind, State \nGovernment has taken up and completed 4 projects costing ₹89.08 \ncrore, which include 02 projects of development of Parks/  Green \nspaces costing ₹1.30 crore, 02 projects costing ₹84.16 crore and ₹3.62 \ncrore in the sectors of Sewerage &  Septage Management and Non-\nMotorized Urban Transport respectively. \nFor the city of Datia , State Government has taken up 02 projects \ncosting ₹77.5 crore, of which 01 project of water supply project \ncosting ₹18.66 crore have been completed and 01 Sewerage and \nSeptage Management project worth ₹58.84 crore is under \nimplementation\n. \nThe district-wise project details in Madhya Pradesh under AMRUT \nis at ANNEXURE.  \n(b): Under AMRUT, Central funds are allocated and released to the \nState as per the Mission guidelines and not city -wise or district –wise. \nFor the State of Madhya Pradesh, CA of ₹2,592.86 crore has been \nallocated for the entire Mission period towards implementation of \nprojects taken up under AMRUT. So far, CA amounting to ₹2,497.06 \ncrore has been released to the Government of Madhya Pradesh for the \nimplementation of the AMRUT projects against which the State has \nsubmitted 100% Utilization Certificates (UCs). \nFurther, as per the information available, ₹82.87 crore and ₹77.45 \ncrore have been utilized for AMRUT projects by the city of Bhind and \nDatia respectively during the Mission period of AMRUT till date.  \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 788 REGARDING “BENEFICIAL WORKS UNDER AMRUT”, DUE FOR ANSWER \nIN THE LOK SABHA ON 07 DECEMBER, 2023. \nDISTRICT WISE PROJECT DETAILS IN MADHYA PRADESH UNDER AMRUT \nS.No. Name of the \nDistrict \nName of the \nCity \nWork Completed Ongoing Total \nNo of \nProject \nProject \nCost \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nNo of \nProject \nProject \nCost \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nNo of \nProject \nProject \nCost \n(In ₹ Cr.) \n1 Betul Betul 3 32.55     3 32.55 \n2 Bhind Bhind 4 89.08     4 89.08 \n3 Bhopal Bhopal 58 1174.36 2 35.45 60 1209.81 \n4 Burhanpur Burhanpur 4 96.73     4 96.73 \n5 Chhatarpur Chhatarpur 2 77.2     2 77.2 \n6 Chhindwara Chhindwara 3 83.88     3 83.88 \n7 Gwalior Dabra 1 0.4 1 56.64 2 57.04 \n8 Damoh Damoh 3 10.18     3 10.18 \n9 Datia Datia 1 18.66 1 58.84 2 77.5 \n10 Dewas Dewas 6 35.41     6 35.41 \n11 Guna Guna 8 20.63 1 81.09 9 101.72 \n12 Gwalior Gwalior 12 776.04     12 776.04 \n13 Hoshangabad Hoshangabad 3 44.11 1 11.17 4 55.28 \n14 Indore Indore 9 712.02 2 301.56 11 1013.58 \n15 Jabalpur Jabalpur 8 169.1 1 362.31 9 531.41 \n16 Khandwa Khandwa 10 55.62     10 55.62 \n17 Khargone Khargone 3 64.45     3 64.45 \n18 Mandsaur Mandsaur 4 63.44 1 0.34 5 63.78 \n19 Morena Morena 4 141.26     4 141.26 \n20 \nMurwara \n(Katni) \nMurwara \n(Katni) 6 57.11 1 90.29 7 147.4 \n21 Ujjain Nagda 2 14.07     2 14.07 \n22 Neemuch Neemuch 5 78.27     5 78.27 \n23 Khandva Omkareshwar 3 4.4     3 4.4 \n24 Dhar Pithampur 5 88.21     5 88.21 \n25 Ratlam Ratlam 4 157.97     4 157.97 \n26 Rewa Rewa 4 65.86 3 237.89 7 303.75 \n27 Sagar Sagar 2 4.5 1 299.1 3 303.6 \n28 Satna Satna 5 87.35 2 190.61 7 277.96 \n29 Sehore Sehore 4 80.19     4 80.19 \n30 Shivpuri Shivpuri 3 23.17     3 23.17 \n31 Singrauli Singrauli 4 72.62 1 95.48 5 168.1 \n32 Ujjain "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |789", "t": "Potable Water Supply in Jharkhand", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-789 \nANSWERED ON-07/12/2023 \n \nPotable Water Supply in Jharkhand \n789. DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  the percentage of households that have been provided potable tap water supply in the rural areas \nin the States of Jharkhand and Bihar; \n(b)  whether the Government would be able to  achieve hundred per cent target under Jal  Jeevan \nMission (JJM) in the States of Jharkhand and Bihar; and \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL)  \n(a) to (c) Drinking Water is a State subject, and as such pl anning, approval, implementation, \noperation and maintenance of drinking water supply schemes, lies with the respective State \nGovernments. Government of India only supplements the efforts of the States by providing technical \nand financial assistance.  \nSince August 2019, Government of India, in partnership with States, is implementing Jal Jeevan \nMission (JJM)-Har Ghar Jal to enable every rural household in the country , including those in the \nStates of Jharkhand and Bihar, to have assured potable water through tap connections.. \nUnder JJM, States/ UTs prepared their ‘State Action Plan’, projecting year -wise target of household \nconnections. The Annual Action Plan  (AAP) for the implementation of JJM is firmed up by every \nState/ UT based  on a detaile d consultative process for emphasizing the need for implementation \nwith speed and on scale and ensure optimal utilization of fund. \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, 3.45 lakh ( 5.6%) rural households were \nreported to have tap water connections  in the Stat e of Jharkhand . S o far, as reported  as on \n03.12.2023, more than 25.08 lakh additional rural households have been provided with tap water \nconnections. Thus, as on 03.12.2023, out of 61.78 lakh rural households in the State, more than \n\n2 \n \n28.53 lakh (46%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes , and as informed \nby the state government, efforts are being made to cover the remaining 33.25 lakh  within the \nMission Period.  \nSimilarly, at the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, 3.16 lakh (2%) rural households were \nreported to ha ve tap water connections  in the State of Bihar . So far, as reported  as on 03.12.2023, \nmore than  157.17 lakh ( 94%) additional rural households have been provided with tap water \nconnections. Thus, as on 03.12.2023, out of 166.30 lakh rural households in the State, more than \n160.34 lakh (96%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes, and as informed \nby the state government, efforts are being made to cover the remaining 5.96 lakh within the Mission \nPeriod.  \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |79", "t": "Central Assistance to Kerala under PM POSHAN scheme (2023-24)", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-79 \nANSWERED ON- 04.12.2023 \n \nCentral Assistance to Kerala under PM POSHAN scheme (2023-24) \n \n79. ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the release of recuring Central assistance under PM POSHAN scheme (2023-24) \nis due for Kerala; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has received request from Kerala to release the balance of first \ninstalment of PM POSHAN scheme (2023-24); and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the status of releasing fund? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) to (d):  Under PM POSHAN Scheme, the State Government of Kerala has submitted the \nproposal for release of 1st instalment of recuring central assistance for the year 2023-24. After \ndue consideration, the Central Government has released central assistance of Rs. 5416.77 lakh \non 22.09.2023 as Part of 1 st instalment and Rs. 5416.77 lakh on 17.11.2023 as balance of 1st \ninstalment of recurring grant in aid for FY 2023-24 to the Government of Kerala. The proposal \nfor release of 2nd instalment of recurring grant in aid for FY 2023-24 under the Scheme has so \nfar not been received from the State. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |790", "t": "Metro Network", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 790 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \nMETRO NETWORK \n \n \nNO. 790 SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n  SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details and the number of cities with a working and an \nunder-construction metro network in the country;  \n(b) the financial assistance provided so far by the Union \nGovernment and the State Government for the metro \nconstruction in Bengaluru so far for each corridor; \n(c) the details of noteworthy expansion of the metro system \nlength and technological advancements in the country during \nthe last nine years, city-wise; \n(d) the manner in which these changes played a role in improving \nurban transportation; and \n(e) the extensions proposed in each of the cities and the status of \ntheir approvals? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a) At present, Metro Rail /Regional Rapid Transit System (RRTS)  \nnetwork is operational in 20 cities across the country namely \nDelhi& 7 NCR cities (Gurugram, Noida, Greater Noida, Ghaziabad,  \n \n \n\n-2- \nFaridabad, Bahadurgarh, Ballabhgarh) , Bangalore, Hyderabad, \nKolkata, Chennai, Jaipur, Kochi, Lucknow, Mumbai, Ahmadabad, \nNagpur, Kanpur and Pune.  Metro Rail /RRTS network is under \nconstruction in 19 cities across the country namely Delhi, \nGurugram, Ghaziabad, Bangalore, Kolkata, Chennai, Kochi, Mumbai, \nNagpur, Ahmedabad, Gandhinagar, Pune, Kanpur, Agra, Bhopal, \nIndore, Patna, Surat and Meerut.  \n(b) Financial assistance provided so far by the Union Government \nand the State Government for metro construction in Bengaluru is as \nunder:  \n(c) to (e) The details of  metro project operationalised in last 9 \nyears are as per Annexure-I.  \nThe details of metro projects  which has been approved by Central \nGovernment as 50:50 Joint Venture (JV) project with State \nGovernment in last 9 years are,as per Annexure II. \nA number of technological advancements have taken place \nduring the last nine years in various Metro Rail operational in the \ncountry. Some of the noteworthy technological advancements are : \n(i) Introduction of Namo Bharat Train - India’s first State of Art \nNamo Bharat train with design speed of 180 kmph and  \noperational speed of 160 kmph  has been introduced on  \n….3/ - \n \n \nParticulars  Financial assistance provided till 28.11.2023 \nin the form of Equity, Subordinate Debt, Pass \nThrough Assistance (PTA) etc.  (` in Cr) \nPhase 1 Phase 2 Phase 2A & 2B \nGovernment of India \n(GoI) \n7,154.61 13,245.95 4,568.15 \nGovernment of \nKarnataka \n5,923.26 12,663.63 5,577.89 \n\n-3- \npriority section between Sahibabad to Duhai  Depot on Delhi - \nMeerut RRTS corridor; \n(ii) European Train Control System(ETCS) – World’s first State of \nArt ETCS level II with Hybrid level -III radio based train \nsignalling system on LTE backbone  has been introduced on \nNamo Bharat trains running on  priority corridor of Delhi - \nMeerut RRTS enhancing passenger safety to a new level. \n(iii) Platform Screen Door(PSD) -   For improved safety and to \nreduce the risk of accidents, PSD has been jointly developed \nby Bharat Electronics  Limited (BEL)  with National Capital \nRegion Transport Corporation (NCRTC); \n(iv) National Common Mobility Card(NCMC) – One Nation-One card \ni.e. NCMC work on all NCMC enabled transport systems in the \ncountry; \n(v) QR based Ticketing – QR based ticketing system has \nfacilitated booking of tickets from Mobile based apps; \n(vi) Unmanned Train Operations (UTO) – For improved efficiency \nand quality of service including better utilisation of resources , \nUTO is functional in certain stretches of Delhi Metro Rail \nCorporation; \n(vii) Indigenous Automatic Train Supervision s ystem (i -ATS) – \nIndia’s first Indigenous ly built Automatic Train Supervision \nSystemdeveloped by the combined efforts of DMRC and Bharat \nElectronics Limited (BEL) has been implemented on Red Line \nof Delhi Metro.  \nThese technological advancements have improved urban \ntransportation drastically which is evident from the ridership which \nhas increased from 28 lakh s in 2014 to about1 crore per day at  \npresent.  \n\nANNEXURE I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) to (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO 790 FOR ANSWER ON \n07.12.2023 REGARDING METRO NETWORK  ASKED BY SHRI \nPRATHAP SIMHA AND SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n \nDETAILS OF EXPANSION OF METRO SYSTEM I N THE COUNTRY \nDURING THE LAST NINE YEARS, CITY-WISE \n \nSr \nNo \nCity Metro system len gth expanded \nduring the last 9 years in the \ncountry as on 30.11.2023 \n1 Delhi & NCR 198 \n2 Bangalore 57 \n3 Mumbai 68 \n4 Kolkata 20 \n5 Hyderabad 69 \n6 Chennai 54 \n7 Jaipur 12 \n8 Kochi 27 \n9 Lucknow 23 \n10 Nagpur 40 \n11 Ahmedabad 39 \n12 Pune 24 \n13 Kanpur 9 \n14 Ghaziabad \n(Delhi-Ghaziabad-\nMeerut RRTS) \n17 \n Total 657 \n \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) to (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |791", "t": "Expenditure on Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 791 \nANSWERED ON 7th DECEMBER, 2023  \n  \nEXPENDITURE ON HIGHWAYS \n  \n791.  SHRI S. MUNISWAMY:         \n  \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) the total expenditure incurred on building highways since May 2019 \ntill date for Kolar Constituency in Karnataka; \n(b) the details of sanctioned highways and the total upgradations of \nexisting highways since the month of May 2019, till date in Kolar, \nKarnataka; and \n(c) the proposed projects for the said constituency? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(Shri NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a)     Expenditure on National Highway works is maintained statewise \nand projectwise but not parliamentary constituency -wise. Total \nexpenditure of Rs 3085 crore was incurred on sanctioned NH works \nfalling fully or  partly in Kolar parliamentary constituenc y since May \n2019.  \n\n(b)     Details of National Highway development / upgradation works \nsanctioned since May 2019 falling fully or partly in Kolar parliamentary \nconstituency in the state of Karnataka are as below :   \nDetails of sanctioned NH works falling fully or partly in Kolar \nParliamentary Constituency \nSl No. Year No of NH development \nprojects sanctioned \nLength in km Sanctioned cost in \nRs Crore \n1 2019-20 1 \n  \n17.5 1047.6 \n2 2020-21 3 53.5 4294.1 \n3 2021-22 1 11.1 97.5 \n4 2022-23 8*** 25.5 649.3 \n5 2023-24 \n (till 04-12-23) \n- - - \n*** Out of 8 works, 6 works are for improvement of black spots. \n  \n(c)                Development and maintenance of National Highways (NHs) \nis a continuous process. Projects to be taken up in successive years are \nfinalized based on the outcome of DPRs, traffic volume, inter-se-priority \nand availability of resources. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |792", "t": "Electricity Connection to Poor Families", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.792 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \n \nELECTRICITY CONNECTION TO POOR FAMILIES \n \n†792. SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has fixed any target  to provide electricity \nconnection to poor families in the country and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(b)  the details of the financial assistance provided  to the State Governments \nfor the said purpose? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) & (b) :  Government of India launched the Pradhan M antri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar \nYojana – Saubhagya in October, 2017 with the objective to achieve universal \nhousehold electrification for providing electricity connections to all willing un -\nelectrified households in rural areas and all willing poor households i n urban \nareas in the country. \n \nUnder the  Saubhagya scheme, as on 31.03.2021 all the States reported \n100% electrification of all the willing un -electrified households. A total of 2.817 \ncrore households were electrified up to 31.03.2021. Further, states reported 4.43 \nlakh additional households have been electrified under DDUGJY. Accordingly, as \non 31.03.2022, a total of 2.86 crore households have been electrified since the \nlaunch of Saubhagya. \n \nUnder Saubhagya scheme and DDUGJY (Additional Households), a tota l \ngrant of Rs. 6,819.4 Crores has been disbursed and the Schemes stand closed. \nAs the arising of new households is a dynamic and continuous process, the \nstates have been asked to take care of the future requirement.  \n \n \n \n\n \nThe Central Government in line with its commitment, is further supporting \nStates for electrification of left -out households, identified before 31.03.2019 \n(period of execution of SAUBHAGYA), under the ongoing scheme of Revamped \nDistribution Sector Scheme (RDSS). Till date, the approval has been accorded for \naround 4.96 Lakh left -out households for electrification for the State of \nRajasthan, Uttar Pradesh and Andhra Pradesh. The details in this regard are as \ngiven below: \n \nState No. of HHs Proposed Approved Cost (Rs. in Cr.) \nRajasthan 1,90,959 459.18 \nUttar Pradesh 2,99,546 338.46 \nAndhra Pradesh 5,577 16.00 \n \n Further, all left out households of particularly vulnerable Tribal Groups will \nbe electrified in mission mode under PM JANMAN.  The funding for this mission \nwill be provided from RDSS. \n**************** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |793", "t": "PM-SVANidhi Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 793 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 7, 2023 \n \nPM-SVANIDHI SCHEME \n \nNo. 793.  SHRI P.C. MOHAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate:- \n \n(a) the number of street vendors who have been sanctioned and \navailed loans for Rs. 10,000, Rs. 20,000 and Rs. 50,000 under the \nPM SVANidhi, State/UT and year-wise; \n \n(b) the number of such loans repaid to the banks along with the \nnumber of such loans that are pending for disbursement; \n \n(c) the number of banks along with their category, which are \nproviding loans under the scheme; and \n \n(d) the steps taken to ensure that all eligible vendors are able to \nsecure loans under the scheme? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OFHOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a):  As on 29.11.2023, more than 78.59 lakh loans have been \nsanctioned and more than 73.97 lakh loans have been disbursed to \n56.23 lakh street vendors under Pradhan Mantri Street Vendor’s \nAtmaNirbharNidhi (PM SVANidhi) Scheme. The State and year wise \ndetails of loans sanctioned and disbursed under the scheme is at \nAnnexure-I.  \n…2/ - \n \n \n\n-2- \n(b): The details of number of loans repaid by the beneficiaries and \nnumber of loans pending for disbursement under PM SVANidhi scheme \nis as Annexure-II.  \n(c): The details of number of banks along with their category, which \nare providing loans under PM SVANidhi scheme is at Annexure-III.  \n(d)  State/ULBs are responsible for identification of eligible street \nvendors and mobilization of new applications under the scheme. \nHowever, in order to increase the number of beneficiaries, Ministry is \ntaking initiatives which inter-alia includes, conduct of regular review \nmeetings with States/UTs/Lending Institutions, awareness campaign \nlike broadcasting of radio jingles, Television and newspaper \nadvertisements are being carried out from time to time. States/UTs \nhave also been provided Information, Education and Communication \n(IEC) material in local languages for outreach and dissemination of the \nbenefits to the vendors to avail the benefits of the scheme. \n**** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 793 TO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nTHE STATE & YEAR WISE DETAILS OF NUMBER OF LOANS SANCTIONED \nAND DISBURSED UNDER PM SVANIDHI SCHEME \n(As on 29.11.2023) \nS. \nNo.  State \nYear 1 (2020-21) \n1st Loan 2nd Loan 3rd Loan \nSanction Disbursed Sanction Disbursed Sanction Disbursed \n1 Andaman And Nicobar Islands 395 387 - - - - \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1,17,843 1,12,746 - - - - \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1,839 1,588 - - - - \n4 Assam 15,816 14,157 - - - - \n5 Bihar 33,890 28,861 - - - - \n6 Chandigarh 2,148 2,051 - - - - \n7 Chhattisgarh 41,020 40,296 - - - - \n8 Daman And Diu AndDadra And \nNagar Haveli 1,121 1,029 - - - - \n9 Delhi 33,069 31,169 - - - - \n10 Goa 1,028 1,017 - - - - \n11 G