Aurangabad section \nof NH – 02 (New NH – 19) 192.40 2848 30.06.2024 \n25 \nConstruction of a new 4 lane \n(Parallel to existing MG Setu) with \nits approaces from km 0.00 to km \n14.500 across river Ganga on NH -\n19 at Patna on EPC basis \n14.5 2926.42 22.09.2024 \n26 \nConstruction of a new 4 lane Major \nBridge across River Kosi at Phulaut \nincluding Rhabilitation and \nupgradation of existing Birpur -\n30 1478.4 06.06.2024 \n\nBihpur Section of NH -106 from km \n106 to 136 to 2 - Lane Paved \nshoulder on EPC mode \n27 \nConstruction of New 4-Lane Bridge \n(Parallel to existing Vikramashila \nSetu)from km 8+920 to Km \n13+375(including approach road \n35m Naugachhia side &53 m \nBhagalpur side)across River Ganga \non newly declared NH -131B at \nBhagalpur in the State of Bihar \n4.5 1110.23 08.05.2027 \n28 \nConstruction of proposed 4_Lane \nRevelgunj bypass NH -19 in the \nState of Bihar on EPC mode \nconnecting NH 19 to 85 origin \n7.155 295.24 09.07.2025 \n29 \nFour Laning of Gaya Hisua -Rajgir-\nNalanda-Biharsharif Section Km \n0.00 to 92.935 on NH-82 in state of \nBihar \n92.935 2138.16 28.02.2024 \n  TOTAL 1223.875 34349.77   \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |907", "t": "Helicopter Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 907 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  7th December 2023 )  \nHELICOPTER SERVICES \n907.    DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL      \n             DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE              \n             DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT              \n             PROF RITA BAHUGUNA JOSHI                 \n             DR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV             \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) the details and total number of ci vil helicopter registered and operational \nin the country;  \n(b) whether the Government has plan to address the concerns raised by the \nindustry regarding the existing rules and regulation that hinder the growth of \nthe civil helicopter market in the country and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government plans to promote the development and \nexpansion of helicopter services through UDAN 5.1 and if so, details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government has undertaken measures to attract more \ninvestments, improve infrastructure and enhance the overall ecosystem for \nhelicopter operations in the country particularly in Uttar Pradesh, Madhya \nPradesh and Maharashtra and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) whether the Government has the details of the specific r egions that \nwould benefit from UDAN 5.1 and if so the detail thereof? \nANSWER \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      (GEN. (DR) \nV. K. SINGH (RETD)) \n(a):  At present, 330 helicopters in various categories, namely, Scheduled \noperator, Non Sc heduled operator, Private operator, State Government, etc. \n\nare registered in the country. An aircraft (helicopter) is considered \noperational as long as it is maintained in the continuing state of \nairworthiness. ; \n \n(b):  Formulation of rules and regulations  and amendment thereof is a \ncontinuous process which is carried out in the interest of promotion of \nhelicopter services keeping in view the norms of the International Civil \nAviation Organisation (ICAO).; \n \n(c) to (d): The UDAN scheme aims to enhance remote and regional \nconnectivity through helicopters. During UDAN -2.0, 4.0, 4.1 & 4.2 bidding \nrounds, a total of 133 helicopter routes/networks across the country have \nbeen awarded to the selected helicopter operators. Presently, the helipads \nare identified for d evelopment only in priority areas of the country, namely,  \nJammu & Kashmir, Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, North Eastern Region, \nAndaman Nicobar Island, and Lakshadweep Islands. So far, 09 helipads and \n48 helicopter routes/networks have been operationalise d under the UDAN \nScheme.   \n Recently, UDAN 5.1  has been launched to further enhance helicopter \noperations and achieve last -mile connectivity exclusively through \nhelicopters.; \n; \n(e): UDAN (Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik) scheme primarily focuses on enhancing \nair connectivity to underserved and unserved airports across all regions of \nthe country. \n \nThe Region -wise details of 76 airports, heliports & water aerodromes \noperationalized under the UDAN scheme is enclosed as Annexure. \n****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nBenefit Region-wise \nRegion-wise details of airports, heliports & water aerodromes operationalized  \nunder the UDAN scheme. \n \nS No. State Airport \n1.  Southern Region Kadapa (Underserved)(Round-1) \n2.   Kurnool (Unserved) (Round-3) \n3.   Belgaum (Underserved)(Round-3) \n4.   Hubli (Underserved)(Round-2) \n5.   Mysore (Unserved) (Round-1) \n6.   Vidyanagar (Unserved) (Round-1) \n7.   Kalaburgi (Gulbarga) (Unserved) (Round-3) \n8.   Bidar (Unserved) (Round-1) \n9.   Shivamogga (Unserved) (Round-4.1) \n10.   Kannur (Unserved) (Round-2) \n11.   Pondicherry (Underserved)(Round-1) \n12.   Salem (Unserved) (Round-1) \n13.  North Eastern Region  Tezu (Unserved) (Round-2) \n14.   Passighat (Unserved) (Round-2) \n15.   Hollongi (Unserved) (Round-4.3) \n16.   Jorhat (Underserved)(Round-2) \n17.    Lilabari (Underserved)(Round-2) \n18.    Tezpur (Underserved)(Round-2) \n19.   Rupsi (Unserved) \n20.   Shillong (Underserved)(Round-1) \n21.   Dimapur (Underserved)(Round-3) \n22.   Pakyong (Unserved) (Round-2) \n23.  Northern Region Hissar (Unserved) \n24.   Shimla (Unserved)  \n25.   Kullu (Underserved) \n26.   Mandi – Heliport(Unserved) \n27.   Rampur – Heliport(Unserved) \n28.   Gwalior (Underserved)(Round-1) \n29.   Adampur (Unserved) (Round-1) \n30.   Bhatinda (Unserved) (Round-1) \n31.   Ludhiana (Unserved) (Round-1) \n32.   Pathankot (Unserved) (Round-1) \n33.   Bikaner (Unserved) (Round-1) \n34.   Jaisalmer (Unserved) (Round-1) \n35.   Kishangarh (Unserved) (Round-2) \n\nS No. State Airport \n36.   Agra (Underserved)(Round-1) \n37.   Allahabad (Underserved)(Round-2) \n38.   Kanpur(Chakeri) (Unserved) (Round-1) \n39.   Hindon (Unserved) (Round-2) \n40.   Bareilly (Unserved) (Round-2) \n41.   Kushinagar (Unserved) (Round-3) \n42.   Pantnagar (Underserved)(Round-1) \n43.   Pithoragarh (Unserved) (Round-2) \n44.   Sahastradhara  -Heliport(Unserved) (Round-2) \n45.   Chinyalisaur –Heliport(Unserved) ("}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |908", "t": "Funds for Sanitation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI  \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-908 \nANSWERED ON-07.12.2023 \n \nFunds for Sanitation \n \n908. DR. UMESH G. JADHAV \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) w hether any financial assistance has been  received from private companies and the \nGovernment Undertakings under the activities being undertaken by  the Government in \nsanitation sector in the rural areas of Karnataka and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) wheth er the Government proposes to invite  Public Private Partnership (PPP) for the \nsanitation sector and drinking water supply in rural areas; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a)  Sanitation being a s tate subject, u nder Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [SBM(G)], \nGovernment of India provides technical and financial support to the States to supplement their \nefforts to improve cleanliness status in the rura l areas.  Operational guidelines of SBM(G) \nPhase-II  provide that  the Corporates/Public Sector Undertakings may take up the issues of \nsanitation through awareness generation and capacity building or through direct targeted \ninterventions in a number of are as related to sanitation such as establishing demonstration \nfields/rural sanitation parks for exposure of various technology options available under SBM, \norganizing exhibitions/sanitation melas, providing necessary exposure to the children in schools \nabout proper sanitation and hygiene, and propagating the programme through mass media and \nGram Panchayat level interventions, etc.  Details of the activities undertaken by the \nCorporates/Public Sector Undertaking in the States are not maintained by the Departme nt of \nDrinking Water and Sanitation.   \n \nSwachh Bharat Kosh (SBK) was set up to facilitate channelization of philanthropic \ncontributions and Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) funds  with the objective of improving \ncleanliness levels in rural and urban ar eas, including in schools. Ministry of Finance is the \ncustodian of funds received under Swachh Bharat Kosh (SBK). A total of Rs. 7 03.88 crore has \nbeen released from SBK to the States including  Rs. 15.73 Crore to Karnataka for \n\nconstruction/re-construction o f dysfunctional toilets a nd for promotion of innovative \ntechnologies in rural sanitation sector. \n \n(b) and (c) The operational guidelines of SBM(G) Phase -II enables the State Governments to \npromote/give emphasis on the use of business model/self -sustainable revenue models including \nPublic Private Partnership (PPP) on the principle of cost sharing, cost recovery and revenue \ngeneration for Community Sanitary Complexes (CSCs) and Solid and Liquid Waste \nManagement (SLWM) in rural areas as well as their operation and maintenance.  \n \nRecognizing the role for private sector in Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen), Department of \nDrinking Water and Sanitation (DDWS) under the Light House Initiative (LHI) has entered into \ncollaboration with the India Sanitation Coalition (IS C) to engage corporates in ODF Plus \nimplementation which aims at creating villages/blocks as Lighthouses for the Districts in \nselected States. The objective of this collaboration is to provide overall supervision and \ntechnical and managerial support from C orporates to the implemen ting agencies in the States \nfor the implementation of ODF plus components and improve the Solid and Liquid Waste \nManagement arrangements.  DDWS/States provide required necessary support for smooth \nimplementation of the Light House Initiative. Phase I of the Light House Initiative was \nlaunched in July 2022 under which 75 Gram Panchayats across 15 states were selected to make \nthem ODF Plus Model Gram Panchayats. Till now, 54 GPs have become ODF Plus Model \nunder the Light House Initiative. \n \nFor drinking water supply in rural areas, Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) is being implemented to \nenable every rural household in the country  to have assured potable water  of prescribed quality \non regular and long -term basis, through tap water connection . Jal Jeevan Mission is being \nimplemented as a decentralized, demand -driven and community-managed programme, wherein \nthe Gram Panchayat and/ or its sub -committee/ user group i.e. Village Water & Sanitation \nCommittee (VWSC)/ Pani Samiti are being empowered to plan, implement, manage, operate \nand maintain in-village water supply system to provide regular and assured tap water supply in \nrural households. \n  \n‘Building partnerships, changing lives’ is the motto of Jal Jeevan Mission. The mission aims to \nchannelize everyone’s energy in achieving the shared goal of ensuring long-term drinking water \nsecurity. There are number of stakeholders working in the water supply sector, viz. Research & \nDevelopment (R&D) institutions, Trusts, Foundations, Non -Governmental Organiza tions \n(NGOs), Village "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |91", "t": "GST Collections for the Financial Year 2022-23", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 8TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n \nIMMUNISATION FOR MEASLES \n \n*91. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU:  \n        SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether as per the report of WHO and US Centres for Disease Control and \nPrevention, an estimated 11 lakh children in India missed their first measles vaccine \ndose in 2022;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, along with the response of the Government thereto;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has fixed any responsibility in this regar d and if not, the \nreasons therefor;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has requested the States to carry out supplementary \nimmunization activities and outbreak response immunization and if so, the response of \nthe State Governments thereon;  \n \n(e) whether the catch -up vaccination age for the administration of MCV has been \nincreased from two to five years in the periodic immunisation intensification activities \nin the country, if so, the details thereof including the Intensified Mission Indradhanu sh \n(IMI) 3.0, 4.0 and 5.0; and  \n \n(f) the steps taken/being taken to ensure that all children receive missed/due doses of \nMCV in the country? \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to ( f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91 FOR 8TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \n(a) to (c): While World Health Organization (WHO) publishes various reports on \nImmunization, under Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), data regarding \nMeasles Containing Vaccines (MCV) administered free of cost to all eligible children is \ncaptured in Health Management Information System (HMIS). As per HMIS, a total of \n2,63,63,270 children received 1 st dose of MCV throughout the country against the \nestimated target of 2,63,84,580 children in the period between April 2022 to March \n2023. \n(d) to (f):  Following steps are taken by the Government in coordination with the \nStates/UTs to ens ure that all eligible children receive missed/due doses of vaccines \nincluding MCV:  \n Guidance has been issued to all States /UTs to  ensure that all children receive \nmissed/due doses of MCV in the country.  \n Special catch up campaigns of Intensified Mission Indradhanush (IMI) are carried \nout in areas of low immunization coverage to vaccinate all unvaccinated/partially \nvaccinated children with missed/due doses of vaccines including MCV.  \n IMI 3.0 and IMI 4.0 were carried out in selected districts with low immuni zation \ncoverage whereas IMI 5.0 was carried out in all districts of the country. Details of \ntotal number of children vaccinated in IMI 3.0, 4.0 and 5.0 are placed at \nAnnexure 1. \n In IMI 5.0, the age for administration of missed dose of MCV was increased from \n2 years to 5 years. \n Additionally, States/UTs have conducted Outbreak Response Immunization \n(ORI) and Supplementary Immunization Activities (SIA) to increase the coverage \nof MCV. \n \n                                                                                             \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure 1 \nTotal number of children vaccinated in Intensified Mission Indradhanush 3.0, 4.0 \nand 5.0: \nS.No. Intensified Mission \nIndradhanush \nrounds \nDistricts \ncovered \nTotal number of \nChildren Vaccinated \n1.  IMI-3.0 \nFeb 2021-April 2021 \n236 9,58,343 \n2.  IMI-4.0 \nFeb 2022-May 2022 \n416 59,99,158 \n3.  IMI-5.0* \nAug-Oct 2023 \n765 95,62,554 \nData source- Immunization Technical Support Unit (ITSU) \n*Data is provisional. \n \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |910", "t": "Road Accidents in India -2022", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 910 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nROAD ACCIDENTS IN INDIA -2022 \n \n910. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.: \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL: \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \nSHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU.: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has recently unveiled Annual report titled \n“Road Accidents in the country-2022” and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Report reveals a worrisome surge in road accidents in \nthe country during the calendar year 2022 and if so, the number of \nroad accidents and the number of persons died/injured  during the \nlast three years, State/UT-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government has taken any  comprehensive measures \nto address this growing crisis and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  the details  of States which have initiated the  installation of AI \ncameras to prevent road accidents  and proper implementation of \ntraffic rules; and \n(e)  the measures being taken to improve road safety, security of riders \nand public awareness? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) &(b)  Ministry publishes “Road Accidents in India” annually based on \ndata received from the Police Departments of States/UTs. Road \nAccidents in India, 2022 has been published by the Ministry and is \navailable on Ministry’s website.  As per the report Number of road \naccidents in the country during the Year 2022 were reported as 4,61,312. \nThe years 2020 and 2021 were affected by the COVID-19 pandemic.  \n\n  \nThe Road Accidents in India Repor t, 2022 gives the following data on road \naccidents in the country:  \n  \nYear No. of Road Accidents  \n2001 4,05,637 \n2002 4,07,497 \n2003 4,06,726 \n2004 4,29,910 \n2005 4,39,255 \n2006 4,60,920 \n2007 4,79,216 \n2008 4,84,704 \n2009 4,86,384 \n2010 4,99,628 \n2011 4,97,686 \n2012 4,90,383 \n2013 4,86,476 \n2014 4,89,400 \n2015 5,05,770 \n2016 4,84,756 \n2017 4,69,242 \n2018 4,70,403 \n2019 4,56,959 \n2020* 3,72,181 \n2021* 4,12,432 \n2022 4,61,312 \n*Covid affected years \n  \nState/UTs-Wise number of road accidents, fatalities and person injured \nin India during the last three (2020-2022) years is attached as Annexure-\nI. \n  \n(c) & (e)  Ministry has formulated a multi-pronged strategy to address the \nissue of road safety based on Education, Engineering (both of roads and \nvehicles), Enforcement and Emergency Care. Accordingly, various \ninitiatives have been taken by the Ministry as detailed below: - \n  \n(1)        Education:  \n  \ni.        Ministry administers Road Safety Advocacy Scheme to provide \nfinancial assistance to various  agencies for raising awareness about \nroad safety and for administering road safety programs. \nii.       Observance of National Road Safety Month/Week every year for \nspreading awareness and strengthening road safety. \n\niii.      Ministry administers a scheme for setting up of Institutes of Driving \nTraining & Research (IDTRs), Regional Driving Training Centres (RDTCs) \nand Driving Training Centres (DTCs) at state/district level across the \nCountry. \n  \n(2)    Engineering (both of Roads and vehicles) \n  \n2.1.    Road engineering: \n  \ni.        Road Safety Audit (RSA) of all National Highways (NHs) has been \nmade mandatory through third party auditors/ experts at all stages i.e. \ndesign, construction, operation and maintenance etc. \n  \nii.       High priority is accorded to identification and rectification of black \nspots /accident spots on NHs. \n  \niii.      Road Safety Officer (RSO) has been designated at each Regional \nOffice of road owning agencies under the Ministry to look after RSA and \nother road safety related works. \n  \niv.     Ministry administers the  electronic Detailed Accident Report (e -\nDAR) Project to establish a central repository for reporting, management \nand analysis of road accidents data across the Country. \n  \nv.       Ministry has issued guidelines for the provision of signages on \nExpressways and National Highways by incorporating best practices and \nInternational standards to offer improved visibility and intuitive guidance \nto the drivers.  \n  \n2.2    Vehicle engineering: \n  \nMinistry has taken various initiatives to make vehicles safer, including \nthe following:-  \n  \ni.        Mandatory provision of an airbag for the passenger seated on the \nfront seat of a vehicle, next to the driver. \nii.       Prescribed norms relat ed to safety measures for children below \nfour years of age, riding or being carried on a motor cycle. It also \nspecifies use of a safety harness, crash helmet and restricts speed to \n40kmph. \niii.      Mandatory provisions for fitment of following listed safe ty \ntechnologies: - \n  \n• For M1 category vehicles: \n• Seat Belt Reminder (SBR) for driver and co-driver. \n• Manual Override for c"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |911", "t": "Privatisation of Airports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 911\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nPRIVATISATION OF AIRPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the number of airports privatised since 2014;\n(b) the details of companies that currently run the airport, airport-wise;\n(c) the names of airports that the Government plans to privatise in the next\nfive years;\n(d) the justification of the Government for privatisation of these assets;\n(e) whether the Government proposes to privatise Kannur International\nAirport; and\n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b): Since 2014,  Airports Authority of India (AAI) has leased out six of\nits airports at Lucknow, Ahmedabad, Mangaluru, Jaipur, Guwahati and\nThiruvananthapuram for better operations, management and development of\nthese airports under Public Private Partnership (PPP). The details of\ncompanies that currently run these airports are as under:\n(1) Chaudhary Charan Singh International Airport, Lucknow - M/s Lucknow\n911. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE\nSHRI ANTO ANTONY\nSHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN\nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN\nADV. ADOOR PRAKASH\nSHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA\nDR. A. CHELLAKUMAR\nSHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.\nSHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN\nSHRI BENNY BEHANAN\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nInternational Airport Limited (LIAL)\n(2) Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel International Airport, Ahmedabad - M/s\nAhmedabad International Airport Limited (AIAL)\n(3) Mangaluru International Airport - M/s Mangaluru International Airport\nLimited (MaIAL)\n(4) Jaipur International Airport - M/s Jaipur International Airport Limited\n(JIAL)\n(5) Lokpriya Gopinath Bordoloi International Airport, Guwahati - M/s\nGuwahati International Airport Limited (GIAL)\n(6) Thiruvananthapuram International Airport - M/s TRV-Kerala\nInternational Airport Limited (TIAL)\n \n(c): As per National Monetisation Pipeline (NMP), 25 AAI airports namely,\nBhubaneswar, Varanasi, Amritsar, Trichy, Indore, Raipur, Calicut,\nCoimbatore, Nagpur, Patna, Madurai, Surat, Ranchi, Jodhpur, Chennai,\nVijayawada, Vadodara, Bhopal, Tirupati, Hubli, Imphal, Agartala, Udaipur,\nDehradun and Rajahmundry have been earmarked for leasing over the years\n2022 to 2025.\n \n(d): Airports of AAI are leased out for their better management, utilising\nprivate sector efficiency and investment. States and passengers are the\nultimate beneficiary of enhanced airport infrastructure and facilities created\nby private partner, who operates, manages and develops the leased airport\nunder PPP. Airports have emerged as a nucleus of economic activities and\nhave multiplier effect on the economy of the State. The revenue received by\nAAI from the leased airports is also utilised for the development of airport\ninfrastructure across the country.\n \n(e) & (f) : Kannur airport in Kerala is an International Greenfield Airport\ndeveloped by State Government of Kerala (GoK) through a Joint Venture\nCompany (JVC) namely, Kannur International Airport Limited (KIAL)\nunder Public Private Partnership (PPP) mode having equity participation\nfrom various private investors, individuals, Banks, Societies, PSUs etc.\n \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |912", "t": "Best Performing Private Companies", "head": "LOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 912 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7th December, 2023 \n \nBest Performing Private companies \n \n912. SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और  \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक  गैस  मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the names of the top ten best performing private companies in the field of Petroleum and \nNatural Gas during the last two years; and  \n \n(b) the names of the private companies which are being probed or have been banned by the \nGovernment for flouting norms during the said period? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वरतेल\u0000  )  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Petroleum & Natural Gas does not maintain such data. However, \nFederation of Indian Petroleum Industry (FIPI), New Delhi has informed that they present \nawards to various companies in the Oil and Gas (O&G) sector in different categories and \nsegments on certain parameters. During 2023, some of the private companies which \nreceived awards in various categories include Think Gas Distribution Private Limited, \nAspenTech Technology, AG&P Pratham, Cairn Oil and Gas, Vedanta Limited, L&T Energy \n– Hydrocarbon, Sun Petrochemicals Private limited etc. \n(b) Action against misfeasance/malfeasance and non-compliance of law by companies is an \nongoing process and appropriately handled by various government ministries/departments \nand agencies in terms of relevant laws. \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |913", "t": "Projects under SCM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 913 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \n                  PROJECTS UNDER SCM \n \nNO.  913 DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL: \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government is successfully  implementing Smart Cities \nMission and if so, the current implementation of the status of \nprojects taken up under the Smart Cities Mission (SCM) in States of \nTamil Nadu, Maharashtra and Odisha during last three years and \nthe current year; \n \n(b) the funds allocated for the projects under  SCM in the said States \nduring last three years and the current year especially for Tamil \nNadu, city-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to extend the Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) to new cities and \ntowns; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n\n(e) the number of cities rejuvenated since the launch of the said  \nscheme; \n \n(f)  whether the Government has also included towns under the said \nscheme and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(g) whether there is provision of separate funds  for this project to \ncover towns and small cities under  the said scheme and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n \n(a ) & (b ) : Government of India launched Smart Cities Mission (SCM) \non 25 June 2015.   100 Smart Cities have been selected through 4 \nrounds of competition from January 2016 to June 2018.   \nAs on 24 November 2023, work orders have been issued in 7,960 \nprojects worth around ₹ 1,71,237 crore; of which 6,271 projects worth \n₹ 1, 16,269 crore have been completed. As on 24 November 2023,          \n₹ 78,749.88 crore has been released; of which  ₹  71,135.70 crore  \n(90%) has been utilized.  \nThe details of funds released/utilized and the progress of projects in \nSmart Cities of  Maharashtra, Odisha and Tamil Nadu is given in \nAnnexure-I. \n(c) to (g) : Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation \n(AMRUT) was launched on 25 June 2015 in 500 selected cities across \nthe country covering around 60% of the urban population. The focus of \nAMRUT Scheme was to provide basic infrastructure for water supply, \nsewerage & septage management, storm water drainage, non-\nmotorised urban transport and development of green spaces & parks \nin Mission cities. \n…3/- \n\n \n-3- \nAMRUT 2.0 was launched in October 2021 with the aim to provide \nuniversal coverage of water supply in about 4,902 towns/cities across \nthe country. The State-wise number of cities under AMRUT and AMRUT \n2.0 is given in Annexure-II. \nUnder AMRUT,  485 cities/towns have been rejuvenated through the \nimplementation of the projects in the domain of water supply, \nsewerage & septage management, storm water drainage, non-\nmotorised urban transport and development of green spaces & parks.  \nUnder AMRUT 2.0, all statutory towns as per Census 2011 are \nincluded. \nUnder AMRUT, funds are allocated to all States/Union Territories  (UTs) \nat the beginning of the Mission and not city -wise or year -wise.  No \nseparate funds have been allocated to the projects undertaken in \ntowns and small cities. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to parts (a) & (b ) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 9 13 for \nanswer on 07.12.2023 regarding Projects under SCM asked by Dr. D NV Senthilkumar S., \nShrimati Supriya Sule, Shrimati Manjulata Mandal, Shri Kuldeep Rai Sharma, Shri C.N. \nAnnadurai, Dr. Subhash Ramrao Bhamre and Dr. Amol Ramsing Kolhe, Hon’ble MPs. \n \nYear-wise release of funds by GoI under SCM for Smart Cities in Maharashtra, Odisha & Tamil \nNadu during the last three years and current year \n(Amount in ₹ Crore) \nSmart City During \n2020-21 \nDuring \n2021-22 \nDuring \n2022-23 \nDuring  \n2023-24 \n(current year) \nTotal Central \nfunds Released \nunder SCM  \nUtilization of \nCentral Funds \nby Smart Cities  \nMaharashtra \nAurangabad 98.00 0.00 98.00 24.50 416.50 379.78 \nKalyan-\nDombivali 0.00 0.00 98.00 49.00 343.00 290.99 \nNagpur 0.00 76.77 49.00 36.75 379.75 353.20 \nNashik 0.00 0.00 49.00 49.00 294.00 245.00 \nPimpri-\nChinchwad 98.00 98.00 98.00 0.00 490.00 490.00 \nPune 49.00 49.00 98.00 0.00 490.00 490.00 \nSolapur 49.00 147.00 24.16 24.84 441.00 416.16 \nThane 0.00 98.00 98.00 24.50 416.50 415.93 \nOdisha \nBhubaneswar 0.00 98.00 0.00 0.00 497.93 490.00 \nRourkela 0.00 49.00 196.00 49.00 490.00 434.91 \nTamil Nadu \nChennai 98.00 98.00 60.96 0.00 558.58 490.00 \nCoimbatore 98.00 98.00 0.00 0.00 490.00 490.00 \nErode 98.00 98.00 98.00 0.00 490.00 463.70 \nMadurai 98.00 98.00 98.00 0.00 490.00 486.40 \nSalem 98.00 98.00 98.00 0.00 490.00 488.83 \nThanjavur 98.00 0.00 196.00 0.00 490.00 488.41 \nThoothukudi 0.00 98.00 196.00 0.00 490.00 480.29 \nTiruchirappalli 56.33 4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |914", "t": "NHLML", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 914 \nANSWER ON 07TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nNHLML \n914.  SHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY: \n          SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \n          SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n  \n            Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क  परिवहन  औि  िाजमार्ग  मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has set up National Highways \nLogistics Management Ltd. (NHLML) and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the agreements have been signed for the development \nof Multi Modal Logistics Parks;    (MMLPs) in some States under \nvarious projects; \n(c) whether these are to be developed under the public -private \npartnership model as to become the largest MMPs; and \n(d)  whether these are to be implemented in the country under the \nPM Gati Shakti National Master Plan and if so, the details thereof till \ndate? \n  \n                                                     ANSWER          \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a)   Yes, Sir. Government has set up National Highways Logistics \nManagement Limited (NHLML), a company 10 0% owned by National \nHighways Authority of India (NHAI) for implementing allied \ninfrastructure works to complement NH network such as construction \nof Multi Modal Logistics Parks (MMLP), Way side amenities, Port \nconnectivity projects etc. \n\n  \n(b)     No, Sir. Agreements have not been signed with State Governments \nfor the development of Multi modal Logistics Parks (MMLPs). However, \nMinistry / NHAI has signed Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with \nthe State Government of following States for development of MMLPs: \n(i) Tamil Nadu, \n(ii) Karnataka, \n(iii) Maharashtra, \n(iv) Madhya Pradesh, \n(v) Andhra Pradesh, \n(vi) Telangana, \n  (vii) Bihar. \n(c)   Yes, Sir.  The development of MMLP is envisaged on Public-Private \nPartnership (PPP) mode based on its viability. \n  \n(d)     Yes, Sir.  Out of 35 locations approved by CCEA, MMLP work for \nfollowing locations have been awarded till date:  \ni. Jogighopa (Assam) \nii. Chennai \niii. Indore \niv. Bengaluru \nv. Nagpur  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|14|UNSTARRED |915", "t": "National Capacity Building Framework", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 915 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL CAPACITY BUILDING FRAMEWORK \nNO. 915 DR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K.: \nSHRI T.R.V.S. RAMESH: \n \n \nWill the Minister for HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) the aim of launching the National Capacity Building Framework \nto achieve garbage free cities under Swachh Bharat Mission-\nUrban (SBM -U 2.0) along with funds allocated and utilized by \nStates; and \n(b) the measures taken/being taken by the Government to make \ncities garbage free? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) : National Capacity Building Framework for Garbage Free Cities \nhas been launched by M inistry of Housing and Urban Affairs \n(MoHUA) under Swachh Bharat Mission- Urban 2.0 to address  a \ncomprehensive approach for capacity building in urban sanitation \nand solid waste management sectors to achieve the vision of SBM-U \n2.0.  \nCentre of Excellence at IIM Indore has been set up to train city \nstakeholders for waste management. Experienced institutions have  \n\n-2- \nbeen empanelled as Swachhata Knowledge Partners (SKPs) to \nprovide support to States and cities in planning and implementation \nof SBM -U2.0.  In addition, the Capacity Building Framework also \nprovides for peer -to-peer learning inter -city exposure visits, \npromoting Start-Ups to fuel innovation, promoting SHGs and informal \nsector into scientific waste management, various training, etc.  \nDetails of States/UTs -wise fund allocated under SBM -U2.0 and \nthe release/utilization are given at Annexure \n(b) : SBM-U 2.0 has been launched on October 1, 2021 for a period of \nfive years with a vision of achieving Garbage Free Status for all \ncities through 100% source segregation, door to door collection and \nscientific management of all fractions of waste including safe \ndisposal in scientific landfills, remediation of all legacy dumpsites \nand safe sanitation. Various measures have been taken and are \nbeing taken by the Union Government to support States and Cities \nfor making cities garbage free interalia, \n(i) Additional Central Assistance (ACA) at varying rates of \n25%, 33% and 50% for different population category of \ncities for setting up of waste processing facilities such as \ncompost, bio- methanation, waste- to-energy, Material \nRecovery Facility (MRF), Construction & Demolition Waste \nprocessing, dumpsite remediation,  etc to increase the \nwaste processing rates. \n \n(ii) Technical support is being provided by way of manuals, \nadvisories, designs, protocols covering all aspects of \nplanning, designing and operation and maintenance of \nwaste management. \n \n(iii) Funds for Capacity Building (CB) are provided to State and \ncities to create institutional capacity to effectively  \n                                                                                                          …3/ - \n \n\n-3- \n \nimplement programmatic interventions to achieve mission \nobjectives. \n \n(iv) Funds for IEC is also provided to State and cities to ensure \nawareness creation alongwith large scale citizen outreach \nto intensify ‘Jan Andolan’ and institutionalize swachh \nbehavior and related set of actions, towards achieving the \nvision of Garbage Free cities. \n(v) The ‘Swachh Survekshan’ launched by Government has \nencouraged a spirit of healthy competition between cities \nto compete for achieving improved cleanliness in cities. In \naddition, annual certification of ODF, ODF+, ODF++ and \nWater+ is done to prevent sanitation slippages. Similarly, \nfor assessment of waste management, Star Rating \nAssessment is also done annually. \n*****\n\nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a ) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 915 due for reply on 07.12.2023 regarding “National Capacity \nBuilding Framework” \n(Rs. in Crores) \nS. \nNo. State/ UT \nAllocation under \nSBM-U 2.0 \n(2021-2026) \nFunds Released/Utilized \n2021-22 2022-23 \n1 A&N Islands 8.60 0.06 0.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1413.30 67.00 231.68 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 129.00 6.15 0.00 \n4 Assam 503.50 5.76 70.40 \n5 Bihar 1204.80 8.58 0.00 \n6 Chandigarh 45.20 11.42 0.50 \n7 Chhattisgarh 727.30 10.14 199.53 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli \nand Daman & Diu 31.2 0.18 0.00 \n9 Delhi 1192.60 174.74 0.00 \n10 Goa 77.80 0.84 6.88 \n11 Gujarat 1918.90 64.45 0.00 \n12 Haryana 645.70 5.58 